<node id="Bassanite">
  <data key="d0">Bassanite</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Bassanite is a calcium sulfate hemihydrate mineral with the chemical formula CaSO4·½H2O. It is scientifically recognized as a stable precursor phase in the precipitation of gypsum. In mineralogical and chemical contexts, bassanite is classified as both a distinct mineral species and a chemical compound that plays a central role in sulfate hydration and crystallization processes.

Within industrial byproducts, bassanite is a primary constituent of phosphogypsum, where it occurs as a major mineralogical species and can also form as a secondary compound through the reaction of phosphogypsum with sulfuric acid. Analytically, the substance is sometimes designated as chemical phase "B" in scientific legends, corresponding to a small diffraction peak observed around 14 degrees. Together, these characteristics define bassanite as a key mineral and chemical phase in both natural precipitation systems and industrial sulfate management.&lt;SEP&gt;A calcium sulfate phase identified within phosphogypsum based on calcium and sulfur content.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792041620</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gypsum">
  <data key="d0">Gypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Gypsum is a naturally occurring monoclinic mineral chemically defined as calcium sulfate dihydrate (CaSO4·2H2O). It typically crystallizes into elongated, prismatic, or needle-like structures that aggregate into dense fibrous forms, exhibiting specific crystal lattice properties. Its composition is verified through strong elemental signals, X-ray diffraction peaks near 20°, and infrared absorption bands around 3400 cm⁻¹ and 1000–1100 cm⁻¹. Thermodynamically, it possesses a standard enthalpy of formation of -1434.5 kJ/mol and exists in dihydrate, hemihydrate, and anhydrite phases, with thermal dehydration yielding semihydrate and anhydrite III. Its solubility profile is complex; it resists acid dissolution but readily dissolves in alkaline treatments like EDTA. During acid leaching, hydrothermal processing, or pre-treatment, gypsum frequently recrystallizes, reacts with silica and aluminum impurities to form complex phases, and reaches its solubility limit, while its crystal growth is heavily modulated by additives and processing conditions. It also functions as a reactant in various synthesis processes.

In industrial and construction applications, gypsum functions as a versatile binder, a standard retarder in Portland slag cement, and an exclusive calcium oxide source in specialized formulations. It enhances road base durability, participates in hydration reactions to form ettringite, influences cement setting times, and recrystallizes in situ to improve brick strength. It reacts with ammonium carbonate in the Merseburg process and is extensively utilized in building materials, carbon dioxide sequestration research, composite development, and plaster and chalk manufacturing. Agriculturally and environmentally, gypsum serves as a soil amendment and fertilizer, particularly for ameliorating acid soils like acid Andosols, improving tillage and root growth, and reducing heavy metal leachability. It acts as a baseline for environmental impact assessments, noting a material savings of one tonne, and like phosphate fertilizers, can release radioactivity, with radium typically adsorbed or incorporated rather than occupying the crystal lattice. Beyond natural deposits, it is synthesized from cleaner phosphogypsum following radionuclide extraction.

Gypsum is intrinsically linked to phosphogypsum, a phosphoric acid manufacturing byproduct, constituting its primary mineral phase and dominant chemical compound across multiple treatment methods. It typically comprises 90 to 95 percent, and in some studied samples exceeds 94 percent or 91 wt%, of the phosphogypsum matrix, which aligns with high sulfur trioxide and calcium oxide concentrations. As the main building blocks of phosphogypsum, gypsum exhibits needle-shaped crystals and agglomerated textures, hosting the majority of trace elements, rare earth elements, cadmium, and uranium via substitution and adsorption. It serves as the primary vector for rare earth elements and a substrate for element adsorption, though certain elements are only weakly bound, influencing their release during leaching. Impurities within the phosphogypsum matrix are unevenly distributed across particle sizes. While natural gypsum is frequently substituted by phosphogypsum in construction due to the latter’s higher purity and favorable conversion conditions, natural gypsum remains the target mineral recovered during phosphogypsum purification for sulfur salvage. Recovered gypsum is actively used in recycling and transport studies, and in analytical contexts, it is often labeled as chemical phase "G" corresponding to the dominant peak, and can constitute a chemical phase in sodium bicarbonate at 76.299%.&lt;SEP&gt;A mineral compound that is the primary subject of the chemical analysis methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Gypsum is a compound recovered from phosphogypsum through novel separation and decomposition processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Gypsum, including waste gypsum and calcium sulfate dihydrate, is studied for its thermal reduction and reaction with carbon in inert atmospheres.&lt;SEP&gt;Gypsum is the common name for calcium sulfate dihydrate, representing the dominant phase in the analyzed sample.</data>
