<node id="Calcium Ions">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium ions are positively charged chemical species, often represented as 3Ca^{2+} in chemical equations, that play multifaceted roles in aqueous solution chemistry, solid-state crystal structures, and various industrial and environmental processes. In aqueous environments, they are frequently mobilized through dissolution and degradation pathways, including the breakdown of calcium-aluminum-silicate phases, hydrolysis reactions, the structural degradation of hydroxyapatite, and the release from complexes such as Ca-NTA. Once dissolved, they act as essential reactants in chemical transformations like the formation of calcium aluminate hydrate and alkaline precipitation reactions. They also readily coordinate with phosphate ions to form hydroxyapatite nuclei, and during hydrothermal synthesis, they interact with amine groups and organic additives to generate stable chelate complexes.

Within solid mineral frameworks, particularly hydroxyapatite and the broader apatite family, calcium ions function as native lattice and channel components. They reside within and border unconnected channels, forming direct bonds with hydroxyl groups to stabilize the crystal lattice, and remain susceptible to ionic substitution by other metal ions under appropriate geochemical conditions. On hydroxyapatite surfaces, their positive charge facilitates the attraction of negatively charged proteins. The concentration and presence of these ions significantly influence material properties; decreasing amounts correlate with lower points of zero charge, while higher solution concentrations enhance the sorption capacity of hydroxyapatite for selenite.

Industrially and environmentally, calcium ions present a complex profile. While they are advantageous for rare earth element recovery, they can simultaneously degrade phosphogypsum quality and contribute to the generation of calcium sulfate waste. Their diverse reactivity, structural integration, and surface interactions underscore their critical importance across geochemical, materials science, and chemical engineering applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Ions that, along with phosphate ions, contribute to supersaturation leading to amorphous calcium phosphate formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Ions are positively charged ions that bind to the hydrophilic headgroups on the inner surface of micelles through hydrogen bonds.&lt;SEP&gt;Ions that interact with phosphate species and are chelated by excess STMP, affecting microsphere aggregation.&lt;SEP&gt;Ions in solution that chelate with glutamic acid and phosphate species to form clusters and eventually HA crystals.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Ions are ions that interact with glutamic acid and phosphate to drive crystal growth and core-shell formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
