</node>
<node id="Hydroxide Ions">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxide Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydroxide ions are negatively charged chemical species that play a multifaceted role across aqueous chemistry, solid-state materials, and spectroscopic analysis. In aqueous environments, they are typically produced during hydrolysis reactions and released into the solution, directly increasing and establishing the alkaline environment. Their concentration serves as a critical driving force in various chemical transformations, most notably promoting the hydrolysis of calcium sulfate into calcium hydroxide.

As essential reactants, hydroxide ions readily combine with calcium and aluminum species to facilitate the formation of calcium aluminate hydrate compounds. Their presence ensures the necessary basic conditions for these mineral and synthetic hydrate syntheses to proceed efficiently.

Beyond solution chemistry, hydroxide ions function as fundamental anionic components within crystalline frameworks, particularly in apatite and hydroxyapatite lattices. Within these solid structures, they exhibit notable substitutional flexibility; they can be replaced by carbonate anions, a modification that enhances the material's acidity, or substituted by chloride ions during specific catalytic reactions.

From a spectroscopic perspective, hydroxide ions are characterized by distinct infrared stretching and bending vibrational modes. The intensity of these characteristic spectral signatures is highly responsive to thermal conditions, increasing significantly upon heat treatment, which provides a reliable analytical method for tracking their presence and structural integration in various materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxide ions are chemical species in the solution that hydrolyze CaSO₄ into Ca(OH)₂ during synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ions that react with STMP to produce phosphate species, influencing shell formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Ions produced by urea hydrolysis that provide an alkaline environment and drive the chemical transformation of STMP.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxide ions are provided by the slow hydrolysis of urea and react with STMP to initiate the synthesis process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
