<node id="Carbonation">
  <data key="d0">Carbonation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbonation is a chemical treatment process applied to solid phases, primarily designed to induce weathering or bubbling reactions that yield modified solid products. In its general form, it serves as a method for altering the physical and chemical properties of solid materials through controlled reaction pathways.

Within industrial and environmental contexts, carbonation is extensively utilized in phosphogypsum management strategies. The process typically involves the reaction of phosphogypsum with reagents such as sodium hydroxide, and is often enhanced by the introduction of ammonia and elevated carbon dioxide pressure. These conditions facilitate the dissolution of the phosphogypsum matrix and drive subsequent chemical transformations.

The primary objective of this phosphogypsum-focused carbonation process is to convert the waste material into valuable compounds, specifically calcium carbonate and ammonium sulfate. A key application lies in indirect rare earth element leaching, where the process is engineered to dissolve phosphogypsum and selectively precipitate calcium carbonate enriched with rare earth elements, thereby enabling their recovery.

Beyond resource recovery, carbonation is also studied for its impact on the microstructure and engineering properties of phosphogypsum-based cementitious materials. By modifying the solid phase through carbonation, researchers aim to improve the durability, strength, and overall performance of construction materials derived from phosphogypsum, aligning waste treatment with sustainable building practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonation is a physicochemical pre-treatment technique applied to increase rare earth element concentration in phosphogypsum prior to extraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792041352</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcination">
  <data key="d0">Calcination</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcination is a high-temperature thermal treatment and synthesis process widely employed in industrial and material science applications to modify raw materials, optimize recycling parameters, and enhance overall reactivity. Typically conducted under controlled conditions, such as in a nitrogen atmosphere, the procedure generally operates between 500°C and 900°C, with specific protocols tailored to target feedstocks and desired chemical outcomes. For example, heating at 550°C for two hours facilitates surfactant evaporation and particle shrinkage, while temperatures around 900°C or above 775°C are utilized to increase crystallinity or transform bone ash into hydroxyapatite. The process serves as a critical downstream step in hydroxyapatite particle production and is applied to a diverse array of substrates, including phosphogypsum, raw material pellets, feldspar pellets, beneficiated solid phases, enriched concentrates, WBS, crushed rock, dried materials, and nHA particles.

In the context of phosphogypsum, calcination functions as a highly effective thermal pretreatment and purification technology designed to remove moisture, organic impurities, fluoride, organic phosphate, and other soluble contaminants prior to acidulation. Specific protocols, such as heating at 600°C for four hours, are employed to eliminate organic waste, while precise temperature control and additives are used to improve material properties. Mechanistically, the thermal treatment induces dehydration, alters impurity profiles through eutectic processes, and facilitates phase changes that transform soluble phosphate into pyrophosphate and phosphorus pentoxide into stable phosphate. This process converts phosphogypsum into recyclable forms such as anhydrite, calcium sulphate hemihydrate, and β-building gypsum powder, and is also used to convert beta-hemihydrate gypsum into anhydrous gypsum. The treated materials are frequently ground into powder for applications including construction-grade bricks, gypsum plaster, enhanced fly ash-lime systems, ceramic molds, and fireproof coatings.

Despite its effectiveness in material modification, waste valorization, and improving reactivity for construction applications, calcination presents certain limitations and environmental considerations. It is recognized as a successful laboratory-scale purification technology for phosphogypsum, though it currently lacks large-scale industrial scalability. Additionally, the process carries inherent environmental risks, notably an increased overall carbon footprint due to its high-temperature requirements, and can release sulfur dioxide when applied to feldspar. Overall, calcination remains a vital thermal process for studying dehydration behavior, managing impurity changes, and enabling the sustainable recycling of phosphogypsum and other industrial substrates.&lt;SEP&gt;A thermal processing method that influences the crystal growth and orientation of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A processing method applied to phosphogypsum crystals to influence their structural properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcination is a thermal treatment process applied at high temperatures to decompose or transform chemical compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcination is a heat treatment process applied to the samples at specific temperatures to alter their crystallinity and phase composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcination is a heat treatment process applied to hydroxyapatite samples at 600 °C and 900 °C to alter their microstructure and crystallinity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002</data>
