<node id="Hemihydrate">
  <data key="d0">Hemihydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Hemihydrate is a hydrated mineral phase and chemical compound of calcium sulfate. It is primarily formed through the dehydration process of calcium sulfate dihydrate, commonly known as gypsum, which involves the precise removal of 1.5 water molecules (1.5H2O). In natural geological settings, hemihydrate manifests as a mineral form found within evaporite deposits derived from gypsum.

Industrially, hemihydrate frequently occurs as an alternative crystalline phase within phosphogypsum, where its formation and structural characteristics are heavily influenced by crystallization temperatures. During manufacturing processes, it crystallizes and can form solid solutions that effectively stabilize the sulfate structure. Owing to its distinct chemical and physical properties, hemihydrate exhibits excellent performance and is widely utilized across various material applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemihydrate is used to prepare filling cementitious materials and influences their properties.&lt;SEP&gt;An intermediate dehydration product of calcium sulfate dihydrate, commonly known as plaster.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemihydrate, also known as plaster, is formed from the dehydration of dihydrated calcium sulfate at 125°C.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemihydrate is a chemical form of gypsum resulting from treatment with sodium carbonate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791917129</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anhydrous Calcium Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Anhydrous Calcium Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Anhydrous calcium sulfate is a calcium sulfate compound that forms when calcium sulfate dihydrate undergoes thermal dehydration, specifically losing two water molecules (2H2O). This processing method is frequently applied to phosphogypsum, as the heat treatment effectively eliminates most harmful impurities while yielding a refined material.

In analyzed phosphogypsum samples, anhydrous calcium sulfate manifests as a distinct crystalline phase, constituting 42.2 wt% of the total composition. Its presence is reliably identified and verified through characteristic absorption bands and X-ray diffraction (XRD) reflections. Owing to its enhanced purity and stable physical properties, anhydrous calcium sulfate is extensively employed in practical applications, particularly as a key component in flooring systems and construction binders.&lt;SEP&gt;Anhydrous Calcium Sulfate is a secondary mineral phase present in the phosphogypsum waste alongside hemi-hydrate gypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
