<node id="Sulfuric Acid">
  <data key="d0">Sulfuric Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄) is a highly corrosive, versatile, low-cost, strong inorganic mineral acid with a standard enthalpy of formation of -814 kJ/mol. It serves as a fundamental chemical reactant and reagent in the wet-process and wet acid methods for digesting and decomposing phosphate rock and natural calcium phosphate ores, such as apatite and fluorapatite, to extract phosphate and manufacture phosphoric acid. During this wet chemical attack, it reacts with phosphate minerals and thorium(IV) phosphate, utilizing a stoichiometric coefficient of five, to yield phosphoric acid and thorium sulfate. While primarily functioning as a critical industrial input, sulfuric acid also operates with a dual status as a highly recoverable resource, an intermediate compound, and a chemical byproduct generated during various synthesis reactions, including the conversion of phosphogypsum, reactions between gypsum and phosphoric acid, interactions with ammonium hydroxide to form ammonium sulfate, and specific synthesis and drying stages. Additionally, it contributes sulfur directly to the phosphogypsum structure.

In phosphogypsum treatment, sulfuric acid is widely employed as an economical leaching agent and chemical modifier to facilitate acid leaching, purification, pre-treatment, valuable metal extraction, phosphorus pentoxide recovery, and the selective extraction of rare earth elements (REEs). It is utilized in conventional techniques and double leaching steps for REE recovery. By adjusting pH levels to precipitate contaminants, it enhances leaching efficiency and gradually enriches rare earths, although recovery efficiencies vary. Within the reaction slurry, the acid typically remains in excess, actively controlling gypsum crystal morphology, imparting a low pH, and facilitating the mobilization of toxic substances and trace elements like cadmium. It also enables radium transfer and induces radionuclide fractionation under concentrated applications.

Chemically, sulfuric acid exhibits the lowest gypsum solubility among tested acids and is frequently evaluated in diluted mediums to determine solubility constants. This characteristic makes it a viable alternative for cyclic leaching processes and a key component in solubility modeling, where it is often compared against gluconic acid-dominated biolixiviants. The recovery and synthesis of sulfuric acid involve cyclical pathways where it shifts between reactant and product depending on the process stage. It can be manufactured from thermally treated phosphogypsum via high-temperature reductive decomposition or derived from calcium sulfate and elemental sulfur through various methods, including microbiological, thermal, wet decomposition, solid-solid reactions, industrial sulfur dioxide treatment, electrodialysis of kiln gas, chemical looping combustion, and hydrothermal conversion to hydroxyapatite. In these operations, it is frequently identified as a main, secondary, or co-product, particularly alongside cement. Advanced recovery technologies, such as co-production and PSC methods, enhance its extraction for direct reuse in phosphoric acid production, generating significant economic revenue and positioning it as a regulated air emission and listed product. Ultimately, sulfuric acid maintains its essential role in phosphate processing while offering substantial potential for resource recovery and industrial reuse.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical reagent specified in the standard for use in gypsum analysis procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;A reagent with a concentration of 12 mol/L used in the experimental procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfuric acid is a product of the traditional process based on phosphogypsum, which suffers from unstable quality due to impurities.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfuric acid is a chemical compound co-produced alongside slag wool in the process based on phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfuric Acid is a chemical product that can be jointly prepared with slag wool based on phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfuric acid is a chemical product synthesized from sulfur recovered through phosphogypsum decomposition processes.</data>
