<node id="Cement">
  <data key="d0">Cement</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Cement is a fundamental binding material and primary construction binder extensively utilized in concrete, mortar, asphalt, and adobe block treatment to meet standard compressive strength requirements. Traditionally composed of calcite and other processed compounds, it is commercially purchased and frequently modified with supplementary substances to optimize structural performance and reduce costs, an objective actively pursued by initiatives like HPG. In specialized mining operations, such as the CPGFM formulation at Qingping Phosphate Mine, cement constitutes a precise portion of the mix and is combined with phosphogypsum (PG), fly ash, and water to prepare cemented filling slurries. PG serves as a direct natural gypsum substitute, a primary calcium oxide source, and a functional set regulator and mineral addition.

The integration of PG into cementitious systems significantly alters material behavior. Cement hydration accelerates PG dissolution, increases matrix compactness, and enhances compressive strength and hardened body density by squeezing surrounding aggregates. However, PG content directly modulates setting time and strength, with higher dosages typically extending the setting period and potentially reducing clinker strength if the PG lacks purity. Experimental mix designs frequently substitute cement with pre-treated PG at ratios between 0% and 18%, with performance rigorously evaluated at a standard 28-day curing age. While cement generally improves water resistance, its combination with PG can decrease antifreeze resilience and hinder weathering resistance. Beyond PG, cement is blended with rice husk ash, fly ash, gypsum, Plaster Of Paris, quicklime, silica fume, sand, and tropical soil to tailor bricks, road bases, and asphalt pavements. Industrial scaling of PG-cement valorization, particularly prominent in India and the Philippines, faces constraints from high decomposition temperatures required for sulfuric acid coproduction, though frameworks like PSC technology recover cement as a co-produced material (e.g., 533.00 kg) to add economic and environmental value. Consequently, broader PG waste valorization through cement remains a promising but evolving field requiring further study.&lt;SEP&gt;Cement is a binding material developed within the phosphogypsum-soil solidified mixes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cement is a traditional product made from phosphogypsum, whose quality is negatively affected by impurities like phosphorus and fluorine.</data>
