<node id="Calcium Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium Sulfate (CaSO₄) is a versatile chemical and mineral compound that naturally occurs in crystalline forms including gypsum, bassanite, and anhydrite, existing as either a dihydrate or hemihydrate depending on crystallization temperature. It is universally recognized as the primary chemical component and major mineral phase of phosphogypsum, an impure industrial byproduct generated by reacting phosphate ores with sulfuric acid, although quartz peaks often dominate spectral analyses. Within this matrix, it functions as the principal host lattice for foreign ions and coprecipitated radionuclides, with its purity frequently compromised by aluminum content. Spectral identification relies on characteristic Calcium-Oxygen bonds, crystalline phase markers aligned with ICDD #072-0916, and verification via green vertical tick marks. As the starting material for various treatment processes, it serves as the primary compound undergoing thermal decomposition and reduction.

The physicochemical behavior of Calcium Sulfate is heavily dependent on its source and processing methods. It exhibits an inversion temperature between 390°C and 450°C, which decreases to 370–390°C after purification, and undergoes a distinct phase transition from Anhydrite III to Anhydrite II at 428°C. Its solubility profile is notably high in ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) but poor in hydrochloric acid, a limitation that restricts extraction efficiency and rare earth element leaching. During phosphogypsum processing, filtration can be impeded by the acicular crystallization of fluorosilicates, while dissolution and hydration may be inhibited by calcium phosphate formation. Additionally, the compound can be encapsulated by calcium silicate, which hinders its reaction kinetics.

Thermally, Calcium Sulfate is present in the initial state of thermal reactions and undergoes gradual, temperature-dependent decomposition, characterized by a decrease in characteristic diffraction peak intensity. It efficiently decomposes under molten conditions and participates in carbothermal reduction and self-decomposition, with its decomposition rate and ratio influenced by temperature and material composition. At high temperatures, it decomposes via redox reactions to yield calcium oxide and sulfur dioxide, producing carbon dioxide when coke is introduced. It can be reduced by iron compounds, acts as an effective oxygen carrier, and frequently represents the formula for thermally treated residues in its common anhydrous form. It also reacts with ammonium carbonate during carbonation, initially transforming into gypsum.

Chemically, Calcium Sulfate acts as a direct precursor for synthesizing crystalline whiskers with large lattice structures and a primary reactant in conversion equations to produce hydroxyapatite. Ongoing research focuses on its valorization using potassium hydroxide and carbon dioxide to recover valuable products for agricultural and industrial applications, as well as on extracting rare earth elements like europium, whose stability within the compound is actively analyzed. Beyond materials science, it is extensively utilized in construction and biomedical engineering as a foundational matrix in composite bone cement, where it dissociates to release calcium ions that facilitate hardening and structural development.</data>
