<node id="Rare Earth Elements">
  <data key="d0">Rare Earth Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Rare Earth Elements constitute a group of chemically similar, highly valuable, and strategically critical metallic elements, primarily characterized by ions with a +3 charge. This group encompasses the lanthanides along with scandium and yttrium, with specific members such as lanthanum, cerium, neodymium, and europium frequently highlighted. Often referred to as industrial vitamins, these elements are indispensable to modern technology, functional materials, and daily life, with global demand steadily rising, particularly for green energy applications. While they occur naturally in phosphate rocks, mineral concentrates, and geographically variable deposits like those in the Philippines, they are also widely recognized as valuable trace components and impurities in various industrial byproducts and waste streams.

In the context of phosphogypsum and associated residues such as phosphoric acid sludge, WBS, TWBS, apatite ore, end-of-life products, shredder feed, synthetic phosphogypsum, marine sediments including the Santos Basin, and fertilizers, Rare Earth Elements are present as mineral inclusions, isomorphous substitutes, dispersed salts, or co-crystallized components within the gypsum structure, typically in dihydrate forms. Their distribution within the phosphogypsum matrix is non-uniform, with yttrium being the most abundant and europium often serving as a model element primarily found on gypsum crystal surfaces. Concentrations typically remain below 0.1 wt% or 0.01 wt%, with specific measurements ranging between 344.32 and 349.14 ppm, representing approximately 70-85% of the Rare Earth Elements originally present in phosphate rock. These elements exhibit low mobility, are primarily trapped in the phosphogypsum residue, and their leaching concentration is limited by phosphogypsum solubility. Removal efficiency during processing increases up to two hours, slightly increases up to four hours, and decreases at eight hours. During extraction, they are frequently co-extracted alongside radionuclides, ultimately remaining in the decontaminated product.

Despite their low concentrations, Rare Earth Elements represent a high-value secondary source for critical raw materials and are heavily targeted for recovery through advanced hydrometallurgical, separation, bioleaching, acid leaching, and ionic liquid techniques. Extraction methodologies include sulfuric acid leaching, mineral acid treatment, resin-in-leach systems, sequential chemical processes, multi-step diffusion and bonding break procedures, raw material beneficiation, digestion, and purification, often leveraging the solubility of calcite in acidic media. Although direct extraction from materials like phosphogypsum is complex and costly due to low concentrations, the overall recovery process is considered cost-effective and carries inherent risks. Ultimately, extracting Rare Earth Elements from these secondary sources facilitates environmental remediation and pollution prevention, secures critical supply chains, reduces reliance on primary natural resource extraction, and transforms previously hazardous impurities into economically beneficial resources, underscoring their substantial value in sustainable resource management and advanced technological applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Valuable elements targeted for recovery from phosphogypsum using microwave treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare earth elements are valuable metals targeted for recovery from phosphogypsum using innovative treatments.</data>
