<node id="Fertilizers">
  <data key="d0">Fertilizers</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Fertilizers are agricultural and industrial products designed to enhance crop growth and supply essential nutrients, specifically calcium, potassium, phosphorus, and silicon. They are manufactured through processes that frequently utilize phosphoric acid, a method inherently linked to the generation of phosphogypsum waste. In addition to standard chemical synthesis, fertilizers serve as a primary application sector for calcite and other processed materials. A notable production approach involves incorporating recycled or allocated phosphogypsum, which is processed to extract and deliver vital nutrients back into agricultural systems.

The application of phosphogypsum-derived fertilizers is geographically concentrated in areas such as Guizhou Province, where they are deployed to improve soil fertility and support agricultural output. Despite their functional benefits, fertilizers are classified as a minor utilization category for phosphogypsum, representing only a 3.2% value and assigned a grey color code in relevant industrial legends. Furthermore, certain fertilizer formulations contain cadmium, a heavy metal that accumulates in soil over time and introduces notable environmental risks. Consequently, while fertilizers play a critical role in bridging industrial byproduct management and agricultural productivity, their composition and application require careful oversight to balance crop enhancement with ecological safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertilizers are an industrial application for the phosphoric acid produced via the wet process.&lt;SEP&gt;Agricultural products that utilize Hydroxyapatite as a component.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertilizers are mentioned as an example of substances in the described chemical process.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertilizers are valuable products that can be produced through the valorization of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Agricultural products that commonly contain dicalcium phosphate dihydrate as a component.&lt;SEP&gt;A product category where a small percentage of phosphogypsum is currently recycled.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-000</data>
