<node id="Hydrothermal Synthesis">
  <data key="d0">Hydrothermal Synthesis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrothermal Synthesis is a versatile aqueous chemical processing technique conducted under controlled thermal and pressure conditions, typically within a sealed autoclave. By facilitating the crystallization and precipitation of materials from dissolved precursors, it serves as a robust pathway for engineering nanomaterials, functional crystals, and hierarchical nanostructures. While generally characterized by elevated temperatures and pressures, it is also recognized in specific contexts as a relatively low-temperature route for producing uniform nanoparticles. The process parameters, including temperature, pressure, pH, and reaction duration, are precisely regulated to dictate crystal growth, nanostructure formation, and particle morphology.

A primary and extensively documented application of Hydrothermal Synthesis is the production of synthetic hydroxyapatite and its various nanostructured forms, including nanocrystalline nanopowders, needlelike rods, porous and hollow microspheres, and phase-pure crystalline samples. The technique is highly advantageous for utilizing diverse starting materials, particularly industrial byproducts and waste streams such as phosphogypsum, calcium phosphate layers, and recycled feedstocks. Through controlled aging, heating (often between 180 °C and 200 °C for 6 to 15 hours), and operation in strong alkaline media (e.g., pH 11), the method promotes controlled hydrolysis and reaction steps that yield well-defined hydroxyapatite structures. Extended durations and slower heating rates enhance crystallinity and promote the growth of hydroxyapatite rods along the c-axis, whereas shorter processing times may result in lower crystallinity. The incorporation of surfactants or complexing agents like EDTA and fluorine compounds further enables the valorization of waste streams, facilitating the coprecipitation of calcium and rare earth elements to produce modified or doped nanostructures.

Beyond hydroxyapatite, Hydrothermal Synthesis is widely applied to synthesize a broad spectrum of crystalline compounds, such as calcium sulfate whiskers and hierarchical nanoflowers including calcium fluoride and lanthanide-doped calcium fluoride. By precisely tuning reaction conditions, the technique effectively converts raw or waste-derived calcium phosphate precursors into fully transformed hydroxyapatite products while simultaneously engineering complex nanostructures. Overall, Hydrothermal Synthesis operates as a highly adaptable and efficient chemical method for manufacturing both conventional and waste-derived nanostructured materials with precisely controlled physicochemical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrothermal synthesis is the chemical process used to produce amorphous calcium phosphate and hydroxyapatite microspheres.&lt;SEP&gt;A one-step high-temperature and high-pressure technique used to fabricate porous hydroxyapatite core-shell microspheres.&lt;SEP&gt;The time-dependent process used to transform ACP microspheres into HA coreeshell microspheres.&lt;SEP&gt;A one-step method used to transform ACP microspheres into porous HA coreeshell microspheres.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrothermal Synthesis is a chemical process used to produce carbonated hydroxyapatite porous microspheres and other hydroxyapatite structures.</data>
