<node id="Ammonium Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Ammonium Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ammonium sulfate is a versatile chemical compound that functions as both a primary industrial raw material and a high-quality agricultural fertilizer, serving as a vital source of nitrogen and sulfur to enhance soil retention and prevent nutrient imbalances. It is particularly effective as a synthetic fertilizer for alkaline soils. Thermodynamically, the compound is characterized by a standard enthalpy of formation of -1180.9 kJ/mol. A significant portion of ammonium sulfate is derived from phosphogypsum waste, effectively bridging industrial waste management with agricultural resource recovery. It is manufactured through various methods, including direct chloride hydrometallurgy, one-step and two-step ammonia-carbonation processes, the specialized Merseburg ammonocarbonation method, and complex decomposition reactions involving phosphogypsum, calcium carbonate, and ammonia. Chemically, it forms when phosphogypsum or gypsum reacts with ammonium hydroxide or ammonium chloride.

In industrial processing, ammonium sulfate frequently emerges as a by-product or co-product during chemical operations. It is notably co-synthesized with nano-crystalline hydroxyapatite from phosphogypsum waste and is generated during the carbonation of phosphogypsum. The compound typically appears in the aqueous phase or as a liquid filtrate following phase separation and product isolation, where it is subsequently recovered by evaporating the remaining solution. Manufacturing operations often incorporate radionuclide flow analysis to monitor and optimize these conversion streams. While it operates as a secondary raw material within phosphogypsum recycling initiatives and gains commercial value through these carbonation processes, its economic competitiveness is occasionally challenged by cheaper alternative products. Ultimately, ammonium sulfate demonstrates a dual role as both a directly synthesized commodity and a recovered by-product, underscoring its broad utility across chemical manufacturing and sustainable agriculture.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium Sulfate is a byproduct present before washing in the synthesis process.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium sulfate is a chemical product whose production environmental impact was assessed using phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium sulfate is a chemical compound synthesized alongside hydroxyapatite, widely used as a fertilizer and in protein purification.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium sulfate is a byproduct material produced from the remainder solution after filtering the hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium Sulfate is a chemical compound whose prepared form is compared against a reference material using IR spectra.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium Sulfate is a prepared compound whose infrared absorption bands match those of a reference material.</data>
