<node id="Kaolinite">
  <data key="d0">Kaolinite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Kaolinite is a clay mineral that serves as the principal constituent of kaolin and frequently occurs as a minor accessory component in phosphate rock and various other rock formations. In industrial applications, it acts as a critical raw material precursor for the production of metakaolin. Additionally, Kaolinite is utilized in thermal processing, where it is added to phosphogypsum reactions to modify melting characteristics.

From an environmental and chemical standpoint, the mineral demonstrates significant adsorption capabilities, effectively removing fluoride and orthophosphate from aqueous solutions, a process likely driven by the development of a positive surface charge. Furthermore, Kaolinite has been extensively studied for its ability to adsorb rare earth ions, particularly under basic pH conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;An initially abundant clay mineral that disintegrates between 450 and 900 °C during the brick baking process.&lt;SEP&gt;Kaolinite is a mineral additive that affects the melting characteristics during the reaction between phosphogypsum and CaS.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-055</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791424027</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cu(II)">
  <data key="d0">Cu(II)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cu(II) is a heavy metal copper ion frequently targeted for removal from aqueous solutions through various adsorption processes. Its adsorption behavior has been extensively investigated across multiple material systems, including phosphogypsum modified with surfactants, specialized nanocomposites, and biochar-based adsorbents such as pure biochar (BC) and hydroxyapatite-biochar (HAP-BC) composites. On BC and HAP-BC, Cu(II) adsorption follows multilayer coverage principles, with detailed analyses conducted on its kinetics, adsorption capacity, and removal mechanisms.

The ion's removal efficiency is highly pH-dependent, increasing sharply as pH rises, though it experiences a slight reduction at high ionic strengths and is notably diminished by the presence of competing Ca(II) and Mg(II) ions. Furthermore, Cu(II) adsorption dynamics are frequently modeled using the Freundlich isotherm, and its interactions have been evaluated in complex ternary metal systems. Collectively, these studies highlight Cu(II) as a well-characterized heavy metal ion whose environmental remediation relies heavily on adsorption-based separation techniques tailored to specific chemical and material conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287728</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-fired Bricks">
  <data key="d0">Non-fired Bricks</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-fired Bricks are construction materials prepared from waste phosphogypsum using hydration and recrystallization processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-fired Bricks are construction materials prepared from waste phosphogypsum using hydration and recrystallization processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-fired Bricks are construction materials prepared from waste phosphogypsum using hydration and recrystallization processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Bricks manufactured without firing, utilizing processes like the Two-step Hydration Process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-014</data>
