<node id="Fe">
  <data key="d0">Fe</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Fe, chemically known as iron, is a chemical element that serves multiple roles across materials science, geochemistry, and analytical spectroscopy. In the context of phosphogypsum processing, Fe is introduced as an additive or catalyst component to investigate the material's thermal decomposition properties under multi-atmosphere control. Its presence specifically functions to reduce the decomposition temperature of phosphogypsum, thereby directly influencing its thermal breakdown behavior.

Beyond its application in thermal processing, Fe is systematically quantified in geochemical studies, where its concentration is measured, reported, and cataloged within geochemical datasets and data tables that specify defined concentration ranges. Furthermore, in metallurgical analysis, Fe refers to iron found in slag samples, with its valence states actively investigated using X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS). Together, these descriptions establish Fe as a critical element for modifying phosphogypsum thermal properties, tracking geochemical concentrations, and analyzing slag composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-012</data>
