<node id="Silicic Acid">
  <data key="d0">Silicic Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A silicon-oxygen-hydrogen species released during the hydrolysis of the solid phase, likely representing orthosilicic acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicic Acid is a dissolved silica compound produced during the reaction, relevant to element leaching.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thorium(IV) Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Thorium(IV) Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A radioactive impurity compound that reacts with sulfuric acid in the context of phosphogypsum chemistry.&lt;SEP&gt;Thorium(IV) Phosphate is a reactant in a second chemical equation involving sulfuric acid.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thorium Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Thorium Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A product formed from the reaction between thorium(IV) phosphate and sulfuric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Thorium Sulfate is a product formed from the reaction between Thorium(IV) phosphate and sulfuric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Thorium sulfate is a soluble salt compound that differs from other sulfates in its solubility characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791313927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphoric Acid">
  <data key="d0">Phosphoric Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphoric acid (H3PO4) is a strategic, water-soluble inorganic chemical compound and a key industrial intermediate that corresponds to the phosphorus pentoxide inventory entry. It forms when phosphorus pentoxide dissolves in water and possesses a standard enthalpy of formation of -1271.7 kJ/mol. The compound is primarily manufactured as a fertilizer-grade solution through the wet-process acidulation of phosphate rock ores, such as apatite and phosphorite, or phosphate concentrates with sulfuric acid, a method widely utilized in industrial complexes and notably in Morocco. Alternative production routes include thermal processes, the dihydrate process, reacting thorium(IV) phosphate with sulfuric acid, and extracting phosphate from phosphogypsum using aqueous citric acid. Regardless of the manufacturing method, the process consistently yields phosphogypsum as a major solid by-product and hydrofluoric acid as a valuable co-product, with sulfuric acid frequently recovered from the phosphogypsum.

Chemically and environmentally, phosphoric acid functions as a partitioning medium that retains significant concentrations of radionuclides, heavy metals, and lanthanides due to the high solubility of U(VI) and disrupted thorium equilibrium. It partitions approximately 70% to 85% of uranium and thorium isotopes, including roughly 70% of uranium-238, alongside 20% to 30% of heavy rare earth elements, lead-210, and other heavy metals. As a residual and interstitial component within phosphogypsum, it is removed by rinsing with pure water, persists in the material's pores, dictates the waste's chemical composition and acidity, and exists primarily as soluble phosphorus ions. It serves as the dominant controlled form of phosphorus in leachates when pH levels fall below 5 and acts as an impurity in phosphate processing. Consequently, rigorous purification is necessary for its safe utilization and accurate radiological assessment.

In industrial and agricultural applications, phosphoric acid serves as a vital precursor for fertilizer manufacturing, white phosphorus synthesis, fluorine separation, and analytical degradation. In chemical engineering, it acts as a primary phosphate ion source for precipitation and neutralization reactions, particularly in synthesizing hydroxyapatite Type 1 and hybrid nano-hydroxyapatite by reacting with calcium hydroxide. It is also utilized as an inorganic phosphorus source alongside waste phosphogypsum and HPA, combines with calcium sulfate dihydrate, and is employed in mixtures with nitric acid or standalone to extract rare earth elements from phosphogypsum and mineral ores, as well as in lime calcination processes. During manufacturing, it operates as a chemical reagent added dropwise to phosphogypsum mixtures to drive synthesis reactions and influences phosphogypsum crystal morphology and filtration flow rates in slurry and conversion vessels. However, it can adsorb onto hemihydrate particles to hinder hydration and act as a harmful free acid that reacts with quicklime to form inert salts. Procured from suppliers such as POCh Gliwice for specific analytical methods, phosphoric acid holds substantial global market demand and strategic economic importance, historically making it a primary focus for Chinese phosphorus chemical companies and a conventional material in soil amendment scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphoric acid is produced via the wet process, during which silicon dioxide contaminates the resulting phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-01