<node id="Thermal Decomposition">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Decomposition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermal Decomposition is a chemical reaction and industrial process in which a compound breaks down into simpler substances when subjected to controlled heating. This phenomenon is typically identified and analyzed through thermogravimetric and differential scanning calorimetry measurements, where it is characterized by a distinct correlation between mass loss and a downward trend in heat absorption data.

In industrial and materials processing contexts, Thermal Decomposition is extensively applied to phosphogypsum. Controlled heating facilitates the breakdown of aluminum-containing phases and converts the material into compounds such as anhydrite. This high-temperature treatment releases sulfur trioxide, promotes mineral release, and enables the recovery of valuable elements. The process is also frequently employed in conjunction with K-feldspar to study reaction mechanisms and optimize resource extraction.

Beyond inorganic waste treatment, Thermal Decomposition functions as a synthesis method in material science, notably for producing hydroxyapatite. In this application, bovine bones undergo boiling, drying, and calcination at 800 °C to effectively remove organic matter. Additionally, the reaction type generally encompasses the thermal breakdown of various compounds into simpler chemical species, such as calcium hydroxide and aluminum hydroxide, underscoring its broad utility across chemical and industrial research.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002844</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chemical Equation">
  <data key="d0">Chemical Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A symbolic representation of chemical reactions, including reactants, products, and reaction arrows, as analyzed in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;A symbolic representation of the decomposition reaction of tricalcium hexaaluminate hydrate.&lt;SEP&gt;The chemical equation mathematically represents the balanced reaction of fluorapatite acidulation and tracks rare earth element distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;A balanced representation of the carbothermal reduction reaction showing reactants and products.&lt;SEP&gt;The chemical equation represents the wet process reaction, detailing reactants and products.&lt;SEP&gt;A balanced reaction equation illustrating the transformation of metaphosphate ions into tripolyphosphate and orthophosphate ions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791985383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Orthosilicic Acid">
  <data key="d0">Orthosilicic Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Orthosilicic acid is the chemically correct form for the silicon-oxygen-hydrogen species released during the hydrolysis reaction.&lt;SEP&gt;Orthosilicic Acid is a specific chemical form of silicic acid, potentially represented by the notation H2SiO4 in the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cementitious Materials">
  <data key="d0">Cementitious Materials</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cementitious materials are binding construction composites and geotechnical building materials primarily synthesized using phosphogypsum (PG) as a core component or admixture, frequently coupled with industrial solid waste. These materials function as essential elements in construction applications, where the incorporation of phosphogypsum enables the transformation of industrial byproducts into viable building composites. Their development and utilization are deeply embedded within industrial and research frameworks, providing a broader context for studying phosphogypsum and its associated chemical reactions.

In practical and experimental settings, cementitious materials are extensively utilized to evaluate critical performance metrics, particularly durability and mechanical properties. Despite their functional role in construction and waste valorization, they are also characterized economically as low-value-added products generated through PCR technology. Collectively, these materials represent a specialized intersection of construction engineering, industrial waste management, and materials science, highlighting ongoing efforts to optimize phosphogypsum-based composites while acknowledging their current market classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Cementitious Materials are construction products enhanced by PG additives to achieve important mechanical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Cementitious materials are binding substances used in construction, frequently modified with additives to achieve specific engineering properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Cementitious materials are incorporated to compensate for impurities in PG and control environmental pollution, increasing CPGFM costs.&lt;SEP&gt;Cementitious materials are composite construction materials that incorporate phosphogypsum, often modified with industrial additives.&lt;SEP&gt;Cementitious materials are binding agents that can be optimized with activators when filled with industrial slags.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791938112</data>
