<node id="Fluorine">
  <data key="d0">Fluorine</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluorine is a non-metallic chemical element that serves as a significant trace constituent and hazardous impurity within phosphogypsum, a byproduct of phosphate acidulation processes, while remaining absent in natural gypsum. Originating from fluoride minerals in phosphate ores, as well as natural sources like volcanoes and various industrial facilities, fluorine is retained alongside phosphorus during production, with reported concentrations in dried phosphogypsum ranging from approximately 1.17 wt% to 1.39 wt%. These levels significantly exceed average Earth's crust concentrations, though they remain lower than those of P2O5. Within phosphogypsum, fluorine exists in both soluble and insoluble forms, often incorporated into hydration products or present as distinct phases, and is commonly associated with fluorapatite where it can release hydrofluoric acid during chemical reactions.

As a persistent environmental contaminant, fluorine readily adsorbs to phosphogypsum surfaces and mobilizes into aqueous solutions, with its leaching and immobilization heavily dependent on pH levels and interactions within backfill materials. It migrates into surrounding soil and aquifer sediments, substantially elevating total and water-soluble concentrations in agricultural landscapes through phosphogypsum application. This contamination pollutes water and soil, enters the human body through environmental cycling, and causes dental and skeletal fluorosis. Industrially, fluorine adversely affects phosphogypsum reuse by reducing crystal interaction forces, shortening cement setting time, and diminishing the initial strength of gypsum and filler bodies. Its geochemical behavior is complex, influencing the coprecipitation of calcium and rare earth elements, substituting oxygen during initial SiO2 dissolution, and migrating across solid and gas phases in processes like chemical looping gasification.

Due to its toxicity and difficulty in immobilization, standard industrial protocols prioritize fluorine removal or stabilization during phosphogypsum pretreatment to prevent secondary environmental release. Common mitigation strategies include fluidized calcination, lime and flotation treatment, citric acid application, and advanced solidification techniques that trap the element within the phosphogypsum matrix for safer long-term storage. Residual fluorine levels are routinely analyzed post-treatment using fluorine ion-selective electrodes. Conversely, when intentionally incorporated into hydroxyapatite matrices, fluorine acts as a beneficial substituent that forms fluorohydroxyapatite ceramics, significantly enhancing their thermal and chemical stability. Additionally, in specific industrial applications, fluorine compounds are deliberately added to enhance the mechanical properties of alpha-hemihydrate gypsum. Consequently, fluorine requires careful management throughout chemical processing to balance its environmental and health risks with its potential industrial utility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorine is a trace chemical element present in phosphogypsum with a measured value of 0.15.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorine is a volatile chemical species analyzed in the phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorine is a major component of the fluorite phase, comprising 43.67 wt.%.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorine represents a mineral phase in phosphogypsum identified by microanalysis that contains a significant concentration of rare earth elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorine shows a very strong, sharp ring structure that closely matches calcium and phosphorus distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorine is an impurity in phosphogypsum that retards cement setting time and reduces early strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-05