<node id="Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfate is a double-charged chemical anion that functions as a discrete ion or integral component of broader compound groups, such as thorium(IV) sulfate. Chemically, it is highly reactive and exhibits strong absorption bands in the 1000–1100 cm⁻¹ range of the FTIR spectrum. In aqueous environments, sulfate predominantly exists in a free state, with its molar fraction routinely calculated using the solution's effective ionic strength. It participates in various chemical processes, including reduction by carbon to release sulfur dioxide and carbon dioxide gases, and acts as a reactive anion in speciation modeling where it interacts with cadmium and influences the type of lead precipitate formed during immobilization. Structurally, sulfate can substitute for phosphate within apatite crystal lattices, enhance the partitioning of uranium into gypsum crystal lattices, and form sludges with radium and barium, directly affecting radium solubility.

Industrially and environmentally, sulfate is a primary constituent of phosphogypsum and gypsum stacks, where it is frequently extracted and measured in different aqueous media. It migrates from these stacks into surrounding environmental compartments, serving as a key component of process water and leachate. Its environmental fate is commonly predicted using GEOCHEM modeling software, and its leaching is routinely estimated in environmental analyses. Groundwater monitoring consistently tracks sulfate concentrations, which are measured as dissolved ions, chemical discharges, and liquid emissions in LCI data for phosphogypsum disposal (often quantified in kilograms), frequently revealing levels significantly elevated above natural background concentrations. Analytical data tables typically catalog these measurements with specific superscript notation, showing a steady downward trend that can increase during neutralization processes.

From a remediation and regulatory standpoint, sulfate can be effectively removed from process water and leachate through interaction with geological materials, with removal efficiency notably enhanced by iron(III) hydroxide and abundant calcium. Despite its prevalence, sulfate is classified as a contaminant whose emissions to soil and water currently lack damage factors in primary impact assessment methods. Consequently, it is subject to established regulatory thresholds, including a general limit of 250 mg/L and a World Health Organization guideline value of 25 mg/L. Collectively, these characteristics underscore sulfate's significance as both a fundamental reactive chemical species and a quantitatively monitored environmental parameter.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate is an ion that acts as a counterbalancing agent associated with Europium within the secondary phase.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical component associated with europium on the surface of gypsum crystals as a counterbalancing ion.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate is a chemical compound that forms insoluble precipitates with lead in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-01