<node id="China">
  <data key="d0">China</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">China is an agricultural nation with a population exceeding 1.4 billion and serves as the primary geographic and national context for the study of phosphogypsum production, pollution management, and utilization. As the world's largest producer of phosphate and phosphogypsum, the country generates approximately 70 to 80 million metric tons annually, accounting for roughly 19.93 percent of global output, with production and accumulation heavily concentrated across its territory. The chemical composition of this phosphogypsum typically contains SO3 levels between 34.00 wt% and 53.72 wt%, and CaO from 28.19 wt% to 40.98 wt%. Environmental analyses of samples from provinces such as Chongqing reveal variable profiles; while some exhibit heavy metals and radioactivity exceeding standard limits, others demonstrate compliant levels. Historically navigating solid waste accumulation challenges since the 1950s, China has implemented a strict regulatory framework and comprehensive policies to address these environmental pressures.

To mitigate the extensive stockpiling that has long been the primary disposal method, the country has strategically shifted focus toward large-scale recycling and the development of low-value utilization products. Consequently, the comprehensive utilization rate improved significantly from 2015 to 2023, reaching 55.6 percent, with national targets aiming for 75 percent by 2025. Although the sector remains in an early developmental stage, it is actively advancing through the commercial deployment of advanced technologies like the Merseburg ammonocarbonation process and innovative applications for treating industrial carbon dioxide-containing tail gas. Comprehensive utilization technologies, including cemented paste backfill and cemented paste gypsum fill mining, are applied across dozens of mines in provinces such as Guizhou, Hubei, and along the Yangtze River Basin.

Beyond its industrial output, China leads global research on phosphogypsum, producing over 800 publications that represent approximately 44 percent of relevant analytical frameworks. It hosts the majority of surveyed recycling companies and serves as the primary region for evaluating disposal practices, lifecycle inventories, and industrial standards. Research and operational sites extend to Guizhou University, Fuzhou for geotechnical studies, and Ordos City for ecological efficiency analyses. Furthermore, China is a major player in phosphate rock production, consumption, and export, while hosting critical rare earth deposits at Bayan Obo and Longnan that frequently serve as case studies for life cycle assessments. Historically managing international scrutiny over supply monopolies and reduced export quotas, the nation is concurrently addressing its status as the world's largest carbon dioxide emitter through comprehensive carbon neutrality initiatives and energy transition policies. Collectively, China’s industrial scale, proactive policy innovations in resource reuse, and extensive documentation of research progress continue to drive global scientific inquiry and shape international standards for sustainable waste management and ecological protection.&lt;SEP&gt;Country where the research institutions and authors are based.&lt;SEP&gt;China is the country where large-scale phosphogypsum discharge occurs and where sulfur shortages drive the research.&lt;SEP&gt;The country where Yunnan Province is located and the source of the phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;China is the geographic location where calcium phosphate rock with ~15% silicon dioxide content is found.</data>
