<node id="PG">
  <data key="d0">PG</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphogypsum (PG) is an environmentally hazardous industrial byproduct and solid waste generated primarily by phosphate chemical plants. Chemically, it consists predominantly of calcium sulfate dihydrate within a complex matrix containing minor compounds like calcium fluoride, fluorine, phosphorus, and soluble phosphorus impurities. The material is notably enriched with rare earth elements, rare and precious heavy metals, and toxic elements, with their distribution governed by specific ionic properties and the host material's crystalline structure. PG exhibits significant radioactive disequilibrium, characterized by deviations from natural isotopic ratios, making it a frequently utilized dried sample for radionuclide concentration measurements. In experimental contexts, PG serves as a baseline material compared against PGI-1.4 during thermal analysis and phase transformation studies, is represented by a solid blue line in spectral graphs, and functions as a starting material containing quartz. Throughout experimental descriptions, PG is consistently used as the abbreviation for phosphogypsum.

During thermal processing, PG is calcined and reduced at various temperatures to study calcium sulfate decomposition and phase transformation. This treatment causes the material to undergo decomposition, releasing impurities like silicon dioxide and generating calcium oxide. Due to its inherent acidity, corrosiveness, and potential for environmental pollution, PG requires careful management and pretreatment. Its leachates initially contain high phosphorus concentrations, and while its use in backfill materials temporarily immobilizes fluoride through calcium-fluoride precipitation, this poses a risk of secondary release if pH levels rise. Researchers routinely analyze its thermal behaviors, elemental composition, and rare earth oxide content to safely extract valuable components and transform it into chemical products through dissolution, decomposition, crystallization, hydrometallurgical conversion, and hydrothermal treatment.

Beyond extraction, PG serves as a precursor and calcium source for synthesizing hydroxyapatite (HAp) powder and adsorbents under controlled conditions, demonstrating superior adsorption performance despite the potential for impurities to affect particle purity and elevate calcium-to-phosphorus ratios. In energy conversion applications, PG operates as an oxygen carrier or foundational support for compound oxygen carriers during the chemical looping gasification of low-rank lignite to produce syngas. Its strong oxygen-carrying capacity is leveraged while grain growth is deliberately inhibited by silicon dioxide to maintain stability, and it reacts with coke during calcination throughout these reduction-oxidation processes. In construction and geotechnical engineering, PG functions as a partial cement replacement, aggregate, and filler in mortar, concrete, and filling materials. It acts as both an activator and a filler, reacting with aluminates to produce ettringite and influencing hydration processes, microstructural development, workability, and compressive strength, although its impurities can occasionally negatively impact cementitious hydration. Additionally, PG serves as a primary component, aggregate, and cementitious material in the production of CPGFM, as well as a base for various engineered mixtures and laboratory mixes used in embankment and pavement applications. It is utilized in combination with CM, where their ratio influences mix performance, and serves as a primary component of the CM-PG-SM-HRB mix used in construction layers. Furthermore, PG is evaluated and utilized as a soil amendment and in road construction, with associated life cycle inventory data available. It is also valorized in brick production to replace clay and sand, though this application results in a higher environmental footprint. Ultimately, PG represents a dual-nature material that remains a central focus of ongoing scientific and engineering research, balancing its status as an environmental challenge with its value as a versatile industrial resource.</data>
