<node id="Apatite">
  <data key="d0">Apatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Apatite is a versatile group of phosphate minerals characterized by variable physical, chemical, and crystallographic properties, primarily occurring as a natural calcium phosphate ore. It serves as the dominant phosphate mineral in phosphate rock deposits, frequently manifesting as carbonate apatite within phosphorite formations, and functions as the principal raw material for phosphoric acid production through sulfuric acid treatment. The apatite crystal structure is highly accommodating to trace elements, which readily substitute for calcium due to similar ionic radii or adsorb onto the lattice. This includes uranium, commonly incorporated in both U(IV) and U(VI) oxidation states, as well as thorium and radium. The substitution of radium for calcium specifically leads to Radon-222 emanation coefficients ranging from 0.5% to 25%. Beyond its role in phosphorus extraction, apatite is recognized as a valuable, non-conventional source for recovering fluorine, strontium, and rare-earth elements. During certain industrial processing stages, however, apatite may remain unreacted and persist as an insoluble impurity. The structural chemistry and synthesis of apatite are central to its wide-ranging applications.

In biomedical contexts, apatite is essential for bioactivity. When implanted biomaterials or composite bone cements are exposed to physiological environments or Simulated Body Fluid (SBF), a bone-like apatite crystal layer precipitates on their surfaces. This precipitation mechanism is fundamental to achieving effective osseointegration and tissue bonding in medical treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of phosphate ore subjected to wet chemical attack to generate phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A crystalline phase that nucleates and grows on the ACP microspheres surface following the dissolution-reprecipitation mechanism.&lt;SEP&gt;Apatite is a mineral component of the phosphogypsum matrix containing yttrium and uranium.&lt;SEP&gt;A mineral that, when completely dissolved during industrial processes, releases rare earth elements that can precipitate as secondary phosphate phases.&lt;SEP&gt;Apatite is a mineral subject to dissolution studies to understand rare earth element release rates across varying pH levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-022</data>
