<node id="Hydroxyapatite">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxyapatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydroxyapatite is a naturally occurring calcium phosphate mineral and a widely synthesized nanocrystalline bioceramic with the chemical formula Ca₁₀(PO₄)₆(OH)₂, commonly abbreviated as HAp, HPA, or HAP. Crystallizing in a hexagonal system, it serves as the primary structural and reinforcing component of vertebrate bones, teeth, and enamel. As a biomineral, its formation and transformation mechanisms are extensively studied in materials science, and it frequently nucleates and grows during synthesis processes. Thermodynamically, it exhibits a standard enthalpy of formation of -4120.8 kJ/mol, remains stable in neutral to alkaline pH environments, and corresponds to reference code ICDD #073-1731. Its flexible crystal lattice readily accommodates anionic substitutions, such as carbonate (forming Type B variants) or fluoride (yielding fluorapatite), which modify its crystallographic structure, texture coefficients, and optical properties.

Hydroxyapatite is extensively synthesized for research and industrial use through methods including co-precipitation, calcination, microwave-driven hydrothermal processing, neutralization, and in situ techniques. Common precursors include phosphogypsum, flue gas desulfurization gypsum, bovine bone, eggshells, and phosphoric acid. Synthesis parameters, reaction agents like CTAB and various surfactants, and post-treatments such as citrate, AL molecule functionalization, HDTMP modification, and heat processing allow precise control over particle size (ranging from 6 to 30 nm for nano-HAp), morphology (including nanoparticles, nanowires, nanorods, flaky structures, microspheres, and nanopowders), crystallinity, specific surface area, and doping profiles. The resulting materials are rigorously characterized using X-ray diffraction, Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, phosphate peak analysis, zeta potential, particle size analysis, thermal evaluation, and density benchmarking to verify phase purity, structural integrity, and electrochemical traits. Researchers frequently synthesize it into porous or specific microspheres to study structural and phase evolution, examining specific crystal planes and lattice fringes.

The material possesses a negatively charged surface that actively initiates apatite formation, promotes cellular integration, and facilitates the adsorption of proteins, drugs, and BMP-7. It exhibits high porosity, structural stability, thermal resilience, modulable acid-base characteristics, exceptional biocompatibility, bioactivity, non-toxicity, and high adsorption and ion-exchange capacities. Due to these versatile properties, Hydroxyapatite is deployed across numerous fields. In biomedical and orthopaedic applications, it is utilized for bone tissue engineering, skeletal repair, dental implants, composite bone cements, nanocomposites, medical device coatings, chromatography media, biosensors, and controlled drug delivery systems. Environmentally, it functions as a non-toxic sorbent and immobilization agent for wastewater and soil remediation, effectively capturing heavy metals, radionuclides, fluoride, selenium, organic dyes, and fulvic acid, frequently in combination with biochar. Industrially and chemically, it serves as a heterogeneous catalyst or support for oxidative and photocatalytic degradation, VOC oxidation, and biodiesel production. Additionally, it is employed as an agricultural fertilizer, a corrosion protection substrate, and a base material for engineered composites and thin coatings, making it a highly adaptable compound in both natural and synthetic contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyapatite is a biocompatible inorganic material synthesized from phosphogypsum, commonly utilized in bone and tooth implants.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyapatite is a calcium phosphate compound synthesized and analyzed through various spectroscopic and diffraction methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyapatite is the target product synthesized from phosphogypsum and phosphoric acid under controlled pH conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyapatite is a prepared material analyzed through thermal and microscopic techniques, showing weight loss steps and morphological changes upon calcination.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyapatite is a material synthesized from phosphogypsum waste, exhibiting rod-like particles when dried and spherical particles when calcined at 900 °C.</data>
