<node id="Temperature">
  <data key="d0">Temperature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Temperature is a fundamental physicochemical quantity and environmental factor that serves as a critical variable across scientific, industrial, and biological applications. Quantitatively measured in Kelvin and degrees Celsius, it is frequently represented by variables such as T1 and T2 in logarithmic ratio equations relating to energy parameters. In thermal analysis, Temperature functions as the horizontal axis variable on graphs, with scales ranging from approximately 0°C to 1500°C, and specific plots often spanning 0 to 1000°C or 100°C to 900°C for thermogravimetric and calorimetric measurements. It marks critical thermal points associated with mass loss and decomposition events.

In materials science, chemical engineering, and mineralogy, Temperature acts as a primary driver of phase transformations, reaction kinetics, and particle formation, directly dictating material properties, crystallinity, crystal growth, product structure, and phase purity. Typical experimental ranges span 373 to 1573 Kelvin, 40 to 400°C, and 100 to 200°C, with specific thermal markers like 66°C, 133°C, and 428°C denoting significant physicochemical changes. It strongly influences the morphology, size, crystalline phases, structure, and composition of hydroxyapatite, with optimized formation often studied at 40°C and empirical adjustments made during protein solution contact. Temperature also drives phosphogypsum dehydration, governs gypsum crystal growth kinetics and morphology, and impacts purification and calcination processes by modulating reaction rates, though its effect on phosphogypsum solubility in chloride solutions remains minor compared to ionic strength. Additionally, it accelerates hydration reactions, increases flow resistance loss in slurries, and is routinely maintained at 180°C for 24 hours in hydrothermal autoclave treatments to affect chemical leaching efficiency and morphological transitions.

In hydrometallurgical and chemical processing operations, Temperature is a crucial controlling parameter that is carefully optimized. During leaching procedures, it is a key optimization factor, with investigations into the TBP-TOPO leaching process conducted at room temperature to evaluate process synergism. For solvent extraction, it is consistently maintained at 50°C to optimize controlling factors, whereas room temperature is identified as optimal for the stripping process to prevent efficiency loss. Temperature also serves as a controlled operating parameter in the MSS2 reactor, reaching up to 260°C, where it functions as a synthesis parameter that, when increased, leads to a reduced specific surface area. Furthermore, it is investigated as a physical parameter influencing the mobility and release rate of trace elements during phosphogypsum leaching.

Beyond inorganic and industrial systems, Temperature functions as a vital biological and environmental regulator. In waste management and composting, it controls microbial reproduction and bacterial distribution, with characteristic temperature trajectories serving as reliable indicators of biological activity. As a physical factor, it also inversely affects the solubility of Radon-222 in water, underscoring its broad influence across geochemical systems. Collectively, these roles highlight Temperature as an indispensable parameter for tracking thermal events, optimizing synthesis, and understanding complex physicochemical and biological dynamics.</data>
