<node id="Heavy Metals">
  <data key="d0">Heavy Metals</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Heavy Metals are toxic, non-biodegradable, and non-radioactive trace metallic elements and ions that function as significant environmental contaminants and soil pollutants across diverse ecosystems. Common examples include lead, cadmium, zinc, copper, arsenic, nickel, chromium, selenium, mercury, and barium. While originating from agricultural inputs, industrial waste, and the natural lithosphere, they are particularly prevalent as adsorbed impurities within phosphogypsum (PG) and industrial phosphoric acid process liquors, typically occurring in very low concentrations within the material. In PG, these metals primarily exist as insoluble solids that alter solubility characteristics compared to natural gypsum and influence the material's overall performance.

During PG storage, disposal, and handling, Heavy Metals can migrate, transform, and leach into the environment, contributing to water and soil contamination. Their environmental behavior and mobility are complex, driven by soil water regimes, matrix potential, thermodynamic states, and the high solubility of PG, which facilitates diffusion into surrounding media. Although acidic seepage waters may experience natural mobility mitigation through buffering from alkaline subsoils and groundwaters, adsorption onto nano-mineral particles can inadvertently increase the risk of broader environmental diffusion. Once introduced, these hazardous substances readily accumulate in soils, groundwater, and biomass, including marine flora and organisms where they bioaccumulate. Agricultural soil concentrations often correlate directly with proximity to PG piles and fertilizer application, posing substantial risks to ecological integrity and human health.

To evaluate and manage these contaminants, researchers assess their environmental availability, leaching potential, speciation, and spatial distribution using techniques such as association coefficients, electrical resistivity tomography, multivariate analysis, and PG carbonation trials. Documented mean contents vary across origins, reflecting diverse industrial and geological pathways. In aqueous solutions, concentrations are routinely monitored against regulatory safety limits, with some regions occasionally exceeding thresholds but generally remaining within acceptable bounds. Comprehensive management and remediation strategies are essential to prevent migration into food webs and ensure environmental safety. Heavy Metals are routinely targeted for removal during pretreatment phases, particularly when extracted from PG, and are effectively reduced through methods such as 0.5 M citric acid leaching, chemical and thermal treatments, solidification, passivation, and advanced immobilization. Adsorbents like hydroxyapatite and HAP-BC are specifically utilized to immobilize polluting metal ions via sorption, often competing with coexisting cations. While PG can assist in soil remediation, its inherent acidity limits direct agricultural application. Continuous monitoring and successful capture of these toxic elements facilitate environmentally sound recycling of contaminated materials and safeguard broader ecological systems and human health.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metals are undesirable elements whose leaching concentrations in the optimal mixes comply with standard specifications.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metals are pollutants targeted for sequestration by hydroxyapatite-based adsorbents.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metals are a category of chemical species in phosphogypsum that exhibit specific leaching behaviors and concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy Metals are pollutants bioaccumulated in marine organisms and leached from phosphogypsum into seawater.</data>
