<node id="Phosphorus">
  <data key="d0">Phosphorus</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorus is a fundamental chemical element that serves as an essential nutrient for plants and animals, primarily sourced from natural phosphates. Structurally, it functions as a central atom in anion configurations, typically bonded to four oxygen atoms in a tetrahedral arrangement, and its chemical reactivity strictly adheres to stoichiometric principles. In agricultural systems, it is a primary component of fertilizers, though its bioavailability is often low and is increasingly addressed through microbial solubilization and integration into nano-hydroxyapatite-based formulations.

While critical for food security, uncontrolled phosphorus releases act as significant environmental contaminants that disrupt the global phosphorus cycle and trigger eutrophication in waterways. Its leaching behavior and transfer dynamics are closely monitored for ecological safety, particularly regarding lake sedimentation and industrial waste management. To mitigate these risks, phosphorus is targeted for removal during pretreatment stages and is effectively immobilized through solidification and stabilization techniques, including coprecipitation with aluminum and iron, incorporation into cemented paste backfill via calcium-phosphate species, and retention during backfill operations. It is notably easier to immobilize than fluorine in CPGFM applications, can be efficiently removed via fluidized calcination, and is leached using citric and oxalic acids.

Industrially, phosphorus is heavily associated with phosphogypsum, a calcium sulfate byproduct of fertilizer production that typically constitutes mass fractions between 0.030% and 2.980%, with geographic variations such as lower concentrations in Brazilian deposits compared to Moroccan ones. Originating from the incomplete decomposition of phosphate ores, it acts as a major, toxic, and acidic impurity that restricts the material’s resource utilization, particularly in construction. When adsorbed onto cementitious materials, phosphorus hinders hydration, prolongs setting times, and reduces initial strength. It is a major element in phosphogypsum samples, following sulfur and calcium in abundance, and may constitute up to 13% by weight in certain industrial samples, appearing as scattered dots or depicted schematically as a yellow ion, with its distribution correlating somewhat with silicon inclusions. Its concentration decreases by 43% in leached samples compared to raw samples.

Analytically, phosphorus is quantified using automated wet chemistry systems and detected via energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy at approximately 2.0 keV, with a prominent peak consistently observed around 2.01 keV. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy is also employed for material characterization, identifying phosphorus as a primary elemental component in hydroxyapatite and calcium phosphate samples, where it is associated with P-O bonding in phosphate groups and P2O5. Additionally, it is detected as the tallest peaks in spectral graphs at approximately 214, 215, and 215.5 nm.

Beyond waste management, phosphorus is integral to resource recovery efforts, successfully extracted from incinerated sewage sludge ash and phosphogypsum. During thermal and chemical treatments like chemical looping gasification, it undergoes sequential phase transitions, ultimately remaining in the solid residue. In specialized chemical processes, it is also recognized as a harmful element that must be removed during the decomposition of potassium persulfate. Collectively, these characteristics underscore the necessity for rigorous handling, precise analytical monitoring, and strategic management of phosphorus across industrial, environmental, and agricultural sectors to balance resource utility with ecological safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorus is an impurity in phosphogypsum that retards cement setting time and reduces early strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-00