<node id="Resource Utilization Mechanism">
  <data key="d0">Resource Utilization Mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Resource utilization mechanism encompasses the processes of dissolution, decomposition, and crystallization for PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource utilization mechanism encompasses the processes of dissolution, decomposition, and crystallization for PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource utilization mechanism encompasses the processes of dissolution, decomposition, and crystallization for PG.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791120685</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fig. 5">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 5</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 5 is not a singular illustration but a generic figure label employed across numerous independent scientific publications, with its specific content varying significantly depending on the source document. Rather than representing a unified image, it denotes distinct figures tailored to diverse research focuses, primarily centered on phosphogypsum processing, materials science, environmental chemistry, and microbiology. In studies addressing phosphogypsum resource utilization, Fig. 5 frequently illustrates application mechanisms, syngas generation via chemical looping gasification, and the environmental and economic impacts of utilizing one ton of phosphogypsum under various scenarios. It also documents chemical process flowcharts for synthesizing nano-hydroxyapatite from phosphogypsum using water as a solvent through high-temperature calcination, tracks calcium carbonate shape transformations across different stirring times, and records mass-loss rate variations under multiple heating rates. Additionally, it displays the mineral phases of phosphogypsum and embedded fillers, as well as the quantitative effect of fluoride addition on the porosity of filling bodies.

Within materials science and cementitious composites, Fig. 5 presents various electron microscopy analyses and structural data. These include transmission electron microscopy images of calcium carbonate shape transformation, low- and high-magnification field-emission scanning electron microscopy images of hydroxyapatite layers fabricated at 180 °C, and scanning electron microscopy micrographs of fracture surfaces for composite and bone cements across compositions ranging from x = 0 to 2.5 wt%. The figure also characterizes porous materials by displaying adsorption isotherm-derived properties such as BET surface area, cumulative pore volume, and average pore width. Furthermore, it illustrates the dissolution rate and strength coefficient of phosphogypsum cementitious materials under dry-wet cycles, the compressibility curve derived from oedometric tests on compacted phosphogypsum, and a compressibility curve depicting the impact of stress-strain on the fractal behavior of particle distribution. It also illustrates the comparative water softening resistance between phosphogypsum-stabilized and unstabilized adobe samples, alongside a diagram showing the effect of the TBP-TOPO molar ratio in kerosene on phosphogypsum leaching.

In environmental chemistry and ecological assessments, Fig. 5 is employed to analyze chemical speciation, surface properties, and microbial dynamics. It displays zeta potentials of biochar and hydroxyapatite-modified biochar across varying solution pH levels, and depicts the mole fraction of water-soluble cadmium in water extracts following phosphogypsum application alongside model-calculated distributions of cadmium across associated ion forms. For rare earth element investigations, the figure provides elemental mapping and spot characterizations of acid-leached phosphogypsum, highlighting mineral inclusion outlines in leaching residues and their spatial overlap with cerium distribution. Beyond inorganic research, it occasionally supports ecological and microbiological assessments by presenting single-factor analyses of sample differences and visualizing microflora networks. Finally, in wastewater treatment contexts, Fig. 5 serves as a composite figure illustrating adsorption kinetics and isotherms, adsorption dynamics, regeneration performance, practical wastewater applications, and material characterization.

The label also encompasses specific analytical plots and mechanistic diagrams. Fig. 5 appears as a graphical plot visualizing Equation 17 for measuring crystallite dimension and anisotropic density, and as a UDEDM plot for synthesized hydroxyapatite nanoparticles and their variants. Additionally, it illustrates the formation mechanism of porous hydroxyapatite core-shell microspheres via a one-step hydrothermal method. Due to its generic labeling convention, some publications merely reference the figure without detailed captions, collectively confirming that Fig. 5 encompasses a wide array of distinct scientific illustrations rigorously adapted to their specific experimental contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 5 presents the deportments of rare earth elements, cadmium, uranium, and chromium in the phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 5 is a visual representation depicting the elemental deportment of specific rare earth elements and trace metals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010</data>
