<node id="Fig. 6">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 6</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 6 is a generic figure label utilized across numerous distinct scientific publications, with its specific content and purpose varying significantly depending on the research domain. In environmental and materials chemistry studies focusing on phosphogypsum (PG), it illustrates toxic impurity solidification and stabilization, specifically depicting cadmium concentration and chemical speciation in soil water extracts following PG application. It also features elemental mapping images comparing cerium with calcium in leached PG to document elemental release and mobility from the gypsum lattice. Within environmental and economic analyses, Fig. 6 presents data on the environmental and economic impact of China’s PG disposal in 2021, projecting changes under optimized management scenarios. In chemical engineering, it functions as a flowchart detailing nano-hydroxyapatite (nHA) production from PG via sulfuric acid decomposition and calcination, as well as a diagram illustrating the effect of reaction temperature on phosphogypsum leaching by TBP-TOPO in kerosene.

In materials science and biomedical engineering, Fig. 6 characterizes synthesized compounds and evaluates structural properties. It displays X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns and field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) images for bone cement (BC) and hydroxyapatite-bone cement (HAP-BC) composites, alongside high-magnification SEM micrographs revealing fracture surfaces, internal microstructure, void distribution, and porosity. Another materials study uses it to present XRD patterns of synthesized powders (Surf, Surf-900C, NoSurf, NoSurf-900C) and illustrate the formation mechanism of hydroxyapatite microcrystals influenced by calcium carbonate shape. Additionally, it outlines the hydration progress of hemihydrated gypsum, noting HG and DG, and illustrates the effect of calcium fluoride on the crystal shape of the filling body. For grain size analysis, it shows grain size evolution under fragmentation for E1, E2, and E3 mixes, and presents a plot used to measure crystallite size.

Across analytical and modification studies, Fig. 6 displays diverse characterization data. In hydroxyapatite (HAp) modification research, it presents attenuated total reflectance (ATR) spectra and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) curves for peeled and AL-functionalized HAp samples, as well as thermogravimetric-differential scanning calorimetry (TG-DSC) patterns for bovine serum albumin (BSA)-loaded and lysozyme (LSZ)-loaded HAp samples. It also graphically represents XRD patterns of materials synthesized with varying Brij-93 surfactant concentrations to evaluate the surfactant's effect on resulting structures. For phosphogypsum characterization, it provides coupled thermal analysis data, including TGA, differential thermal analysis (DTA), and temperature curves for PG alone, alongside XRD patterns of flaking material from phosphogypsum cement mortar (PGCM) specimens after deterioration experiments. Furthermore, it documents scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of brick samples processed through various hydration and drying methods. In nanoparticle synthesis, it illustrates the SSP technique analysis for synthesized HAp nanoparticles and their variants. Finally, in microbial ecology, Fig. 6 displays functional prediction results and abundance differences within bacterial communities to provide insights into microbial functional potential and population dynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 6 presents graphical representations including optical absorption curves, band gap determination, and band gap alignment.&lt;SEP&gt;Illustration depicting band gap alignment of prepared samples relative to water redox potentials.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 6 is a visual representation showing the concentration changes of rare earth elements during leaching.</data>
