<node id="Calcium Sulfate Hemihydrate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Sulfate Hemihydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium sulfate hemihydrate is a solid chemical compound and mineral phase represented by the chemical formula CaSO₄·½H₂O. It forms as an intermediate stage during the dehydration of gypsum through heating and serves as a primary chemical component and predominant mineral form following calcination in phosphogypsum processing. Structurally, it crystallizes into polygonal aggregates composed of sheet- and plate-like formations and exhibits potential gelling activity alongside cementitious properties. Chemically, it acts as a reactive precursor that dissolves in water, reaches saturation, and undergoes a hydration-driven phase transformation to crystallize into calcium sulfate dihydrate (gypsum), a process extensively investigated using advanced microscopy. Notably, its dissolution and hydration kinetics can be hindered by reactions with soluble phosphorus, and it demonstrates a greater propensity to co-precipitate Radium-226 compared to the dihydrate variant.

Beyond its conventional applications in cementitious materials and environmental chemistry, medicine-grade calcium sulfate hemihydrate plays a vital role in biomedical engineering and orthopedic surgery. When precisely blended with hydroxyapatite and sodium chloride, it forms a composite bone cement that facilitates tissue regeneration and structural repair. As a primary reactant in synthesis processes, the compound effectively bridges materials science, environmental chemistry, and medical engineering. Its hydration dynamics, compositional versatility, and multifaceted industrial and clinical utility remain central to ongoing research and practical implementation.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Sulfate Hemihydrate is a secondary chemical component found in phosphogypsum, represented by the formula CaSO4·0.5H2O.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Sulfate Hemihydrate is a reactant used in precipitation and drying processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate hemihydrate is a compound formed when gypsum loses three-quarters of its combined water during sodium carbonate treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate hemihydrate is the primary calcium source reactant converted into HAp during the synthesis reaction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-002</data>
