<node id="Singh M">
  <data key="d0">Singh M</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Singh M is a researcher whose work primarily focuses on the treatment, purification, and industrial utilization of waste phosphogypsum. A significant portion of their research investigates methods for processing waste phosphogypsum for use in cement and plaster manufacturing, as well as for developing polymerised flooring materials. Singh M has also contributed to studies examining the long-term durability of phosphogypsum-based water-resistant anhydrite binders and activated gypsum anhydrite-slag mixtures. Through these efforts, Singh M has established a comprehensive research portfolio centered on transforming phosphogypsum waste into valuable construction and industrial materials.

In addition to application-focused studies, Singh M has developed an improved process for the purification of phosphogypsum. Their work further explores the material science aspects of phosphogypsum derivatives, including detailed investigations into how specific impurities within phosphogypsum influence the mechanical strength and microstructure of selenite plaster. By addressing both purification techniques and performance characteristics, Singh M's research provides critical insights into optimizing phosphogypsum for diverse engineering and manufacturing applications.&lt;SEP&gt;A researcher who published a 2002 study on treating waste phosphogypsum for cement and plaster manufacture.&lt;SEP&gt;Researcher who investigated treating waste phosphogypsum for cement and plaster manufacture.</data>
