<node id="Silica">
  <data key="d0">Silica</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Silica is a chemical compound that serves diverse roles across industrial, geological, and materials science applications. In the context of phosphogypsum, its composition and function exhibit notable variation across sources. Some accounts characterize silica as a primary constituent, comprising over ninety percent of phosphogypsum alongside calcium sulfate dihydrate, while others describe it as a minor impurity present at approximately 1.37 weight percent, potentially introduced during manufacturing or via process water. Within this material, silica can form aluminosilicate phases, reduce the thermal energy required for brick manufacturing at lower concentrations, and be processed using sulfuric acid at varying pH levels to reduce impurities. It is also largely removed through water-washing purification processes. In experimental settings, silica is frequently added to samples, often denoted as "+ SiO₂" in X-ray diffraction analyses, and is utilized as a spherical substrate in prior art for the deposition of hydroxyapatite.

Beyond its industrial and experimental applications, silica plays a significant role in geological and construction materials. It is abundantly present in volcanic ash, where hydration reactions substantially enhance the structural strength of resulting materials. Additionally, silica reacts with lime and fly ash to form strong cementitious bonds. Overall, silica functions as a versatile compound, acting as a major component, a removable impurity, a reactive additive, and a structural substrate depending on the specific application and context.&lt;SEP&gt;Silica is an additive component in the phosphogypsum ingredient system used to meet slag wool production requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;An additive that reacts with calcium sulfate or calcium oxide, contributing to side reactions and eutectic mixture formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Silica reacts with calcium oxide during the thermal reduction process to form calcium silicate.&lt;SEP&gt;Silica is a material dispensed with phosphogypsum to form a eutectic system that promotes efficient decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Silica is the common name for silicon dioxide, representing a minor phase detected in the sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-011</data>
