<node id="Particle Size Distribution">
  <data key="d0">Particle Size Distribution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Particle Size Distribution is a fundamental physical and granulometric property that quantifies the range, proportion, frequency, and uniformity of particle sizes across various micrometer scales. Functioning simultaneously as an analytical characterization technique and a statistical dataset, it provides critical insights into the structural composition and physical behavior of diverse particulate materials. The specific manifestations and analytical applications of Particle Size Distribution vary significantly depending on the material context.

In phosphogypsum, Particle Size Distribution serves as a key analytical metric, typically revealing that the majority of particles fall between 10 and 900 micrometers, with some assessments extending to 1000 micrometers. This property is optimized through sieving processes below 125 mm and directly governs critical material attributes, most notably the permeability of the phosphogypsum matrix. By detailing particle dimensions, it enables accurate evaluation of the material's structural integrity and functional performance.

For hydroxyapatite powders and synthesized nanoparticle systems, Particle Size Distribution is a dynamic property heavily influenced by the synthesis method. It is routinely measured using dynamic light scattering and FESEM to characterize nanoparticle growth, crystallite dimensions, and size uniformity across different hydroxyapatite types. The resulting data provides a statistical breakdown of particle frequency, which is essential for tailoring material properties.

In geotechnical and soil science applications, Particle Size Distribution is analyzed to determine the gradation and fractal characteristics of soil mixes. As a granulometric property, it is not static; it evolves under mechanical stress, typically exhibiting an increase in finer fractions as the soil material content shifts. Across all contexts, Particle Size Distribution remains an indispensable parameter for understanding, optimizing, and predicting the physical and functional behavior of particulate systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-005</data>
