<node id="Magnesium">
  <data key="d0">Magnesium</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnesium is a double-charged chemical ion and metallic element that plays significant roles across solution chemistry, industrial materials, and geochemical matrices. In aqueous solutions, its molar fraction is determined based on effective ionic strength, and its concentration is systematically monitored throughout experimental stages, often exhibiting trends that closely mirror calcium, including a notable decline at the twentieth stage.

Within phosphogypsum, magnesium functions as both a measurable major element and an impurity, typically constituting mass fractions between 0.040% and 1.500%. Although its relative abundance is generally lower than that of other primary constituents, it significantly influences material properties by altering fluorine precipitation mechanisms. Geochemical analyses also reveal regional variations in its distribution, with Brazilian phosphogypsum consistently reporting higher magnesium concentrations compared to Moroccan samples.

In biomaterials science, magnesium acts as a substituent within hydroxyapatite structures. While its incorporation can reduce overall material stability by increasing solubility, it is simultaneously utilized as a dopant in nano-hydroxyapatite particles. When combined with zinc, magnesium doping enhances the nanoparticles' nutrient uptake efficiency, highlighting its dual utility in both modifying structural properties and improving functional performance in advanced material applications.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertilizer component studied for its effects on acid paddy soil improvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium is a chemical element used as a fertilizer component to improve acid paddy soil.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium is an interfering metal ion present in the phosphogypsum leach liquor at a concentration of 150 mg/L.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium substitutes calcium in hydroxyapatite, reducing crystal size and increasing material solubility.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium influences the transformation process of amorphous calcium phosphate during crystallization.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium is a major chemical species analyzed in the phosphogypsum samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
