<node id="Chromium">
  <data key="d0">Chromium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Chromium is a transition metal and trace element with significant environmental, geochemical, and materials science characteristics. Naturally enriched in phosphorites relative to average crustal abundances, it frequently functions as a metal dopant within the apatite lattice, shifting hydroxyapatite's absorption spectrum from the ultraviolet to the visible region. As a heavy metal and toxic contaminant, chromium poses substantial health and ecological risks, particularly through its presence in industrial byproducts like phosphogypsum. In phosphogypsum, chromium is predominantly found within the calcium sulfate and gypsum phases at concentrations typically ranging from 10.8 ppm to 30.00 μg/g, with specific measurements averaging around 20 μg/g. It is routinely quantified using Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass-Spectrometry (ICP-MS) and tracked in analytical datasets and elemental deportment studies.

During phosphogypsum processing and environmental interaction, chromium demonstrates high mobility and exhibits significant liquid-solid fractionation during leaching, where it is fixed via adsorption or weak bonding to gypsum surfaces. While simple water washing can eliminate a portion of the element, comprehensive remediation and removal efforts remain notably inefficient, achieving only approximately a 28 percent removal rate, with processing transfer rates between 3 and 26 percent. Consequently, chromium readily leaches into surrounding environments, contaminating stockyard and amended soils where concentrations frequently exceed the GB 15618-2018 risk control standard, resulting in moderate soil pollution. It is actively measured in leaching toxicity analyses, such as the Toxicity Characteristic Leaching Procedure (TCLP), where concentrations are recorded in milligrams per liter. Regulatory frameworks strictly monitor chromium in surface and irrigation waters, enforcing limits such as 0.05 mg/L, while the US-EPA sets a mixture limit of 5 mg/L. Despite these controls, chromium is often found in mixtures below detection limits and remains below rare effect concentration thresholds in certain contexts.

Beyond terrestrial and aquatic contamination, chromium is actively tracked as a trace metal emission in industrial Life Cycle Inventory data, particularly for brick production and phosphogypsum disposal. It is quantified as a liquid emission and chemical discharge into groundwater, with recorded quantities such as 8.62 × 10⁻⁴ kilograms. Although sometimes referenced as a chemical compound in emission datasets, chromium is fundamentally an elemental heavy metal that manifests in various chemical forms during industrial release and environmental monitoring. Its high mobility and potential for widespread dispersion necessitate continuous environmental tracking and regulatory oversight to mitigate adverse impacts on public health and aquatic ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromium is the chemical element whose percentage content in gypsum is determined by Equation 35.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-025</data>
