<node id="Agglomeration">
  <data key="d0">Agglomeration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Agglomeration is a physical phenomenon characterized by the clustering or clumping of small particles and nanoparticles. In phosphogypsum, this process is driven by moisture content, which causes fine particles to aggregate. Across various material systems, agglomeration significantly impacts scientific analysis and measurement techniques. Specifically, particle clustering can interfere with dynamic light scattering measurements, leading to observed size discrepancies and complicating the accurate determination of particle size distributions.

In hydroxyapatite (HAp) systems, agglomeration manifests as a pronounced tendency for nanoparticles and nanorods to cluster together. The severity of this clumping behavior varies significantly depending on formulation factors, particularly surfactant concentrations. For example, slight particle clustering has been specifically observed in HAp-CTAB-3 samples. Conversely, the addition of specific surfactants, such as Brij-93, effectively prevents the clumping of HAp nanorods. Overall, agglomeration represents a critical physical behavior that influences both the structural properties of materials and the reliability of their analytical characterization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791966653</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dust">
