<node id="Anhydrite">
  <data key="d0">Anhydrite</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Anhydrite is a mineral compound primarily recognized as the anhydrous form of calcium sulfate, though it is sometimes broadly referred to as calcium sulfate or described in certain industrial contexts as a hydrated phase. It is predominantly found in phosphogypsum, a common industrial byproduct, where it constitutes a major component alongside other calcium sulfate species. The formation, crystallization, and chemical behavior of Anhydrite within phosphogypsum are significantly influenced by phosphorus and fluorine impurities, which dictate its specific properties and make it a focal point in the study of calcium sulfate phases and phosphogypsum utilization.

During the thermal decomposition of phosphogypsum at temperatures below 1000°C, Anhydrite emerges as a primary transformation product, often co-occurring with calcium sulfide. It is generated through high-temperature calcination and represents the final crystalline stage resulting from the thermal transformation of anhydrite II. This process yields a dehydrated mineral that crystallizes in the orthorhombic system and exhibits distinct solubility and reactivity characteristics.

In construction and materials engineering, Anhydrite plays a functional role when phosphogypsum is incorporated into building materials. Its proportion increases in brick samples as phosphogypsum content rises, and it similarly accumulates in autoclaved aerated concrete following autoclaving. This increased presence contributes to the structural effectiveness and performance of these concrete applications, highlighting Anhydrite's significance in industrial mineral processing, waste management, and sustainable construction practices.&lt;SEP&gt;A dehydrated crystalline form of calcium sulfate produced during the thermal processing of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Anhydrite is a calcium sulfate phase identified in phosphogypsum alongside bassanite and dihydrate gypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792041755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Egypt">
  <data key="d0">Egypt</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Egypt is a North African country and geographic region that serves as a foundational location for extensive industrial and scientific investigations, particularly those centered on phosphate processing, waste management, and related byproducts. It functions as the geographic origin of key phosphate rock sources and hosts critical operational facilities, most notably the Abu-Zaabal phosphate plant, which is dedicated to phosphoric acid production. The region is also home to phosphate fertilizer plants that have been the subject of targeted environmental and industrial studies. Egypt produces phosphogypsum, contributing to worldwide production statistics with an annual output of 5.4 million metric tons, and serves as the primary origin for phosphogypsum feedstock.

A significant portion of research conducted in Egypt focuses on the comprehensive sampling and chemical analysis of phosphogypsum waste. Samples are systematically evaluated for their chemical composition, impurities, and mean weight percentages of various chemical oxides. Data derived from these analyses are prominently featured in scientific literature and referenced in data tables with research citations, where Egypt is frequently represented with a specific weight percentage measurement of approximately 38%. Beyond chemical profiling, Egypt is a central location for radiological and environmental assessments. Scientific investigations have evaluated the radiological impact of phosphate rocks and phosphogypsum, examining lanthanide recovery processes, radionuclide activity concentrations, and elevated radionuclide levels associated with phosphate materials. Specific studies have also assessed fluoride contamination and overall radioactivity at phosphate fertilizer plants. Additionally, the Red Sea Coast within Egypt serves as a key sample collection site for these investigations.

Beyond phosphate-related research, Egypt operates as a geographic site for analyzing petroleum samples and petroleum waste. These petroleum-related investigations focus on radioactivity measurements and radon emanation phenomena linked to the petroleum industry. Overall, Egypt functions as both the geographic source of essential phosphate resources and the operational base for multidisciplinary studies. It provides a critical testing ground for examining the chemical, radiological, and material characteristics of phosphate and petroleum byproducts, supporting ongoing industrial and scientific advancements in waste management and resource processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Egypt is a country where research on phosphogypsum characterization and beneficiation has been conducted.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
