<node id="Flotation Method">
  <data key="d0">Flotation Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Flotation Method is a traditional separation technique primarily employed in the processing of phosphogypsum. It utilizes water and specialized flotation devices to effectively remove suspended or floating organic matter from phosphogypsum slurry. Despite its established use for organic impurity removal, the method has notable limitations, particularly its inability to thoroughly eliminate phosphorus and fluorine compounds from the material.

Beyond organic matter, the Flotation Method is also applied to extract and remove silicon dioxide from high-silica phosphogypsum. However, this specific application introduces additional operational challenges, as it significantly increases processing costs and generates substantial waste byproducts. Overall, while the Flotation Method serves as a conventional approach for managing organic impurities and silica in phosphogypsum, its effectiveness is constrained by incomplete removal of certain chemical elements and associated economic and environmental drawbacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792098000</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Precipitation Method">
  <data key="d0">Precipitation Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Precipitation Method is a versatile chemical technique applied in distinct industrial and materials science contexts. In the remediation of phosphogypsum, the method is employed to mitigate environmental hazards by reducing the influence of soluble phosphorus and fluorine compounds. Despite these immediate remediation benefits, the process is inherently limited and cannot prevent the long-term dissolution of these soluble elements within the material.

In materials synthesis, the Precipitation Method functions as a simple and alternative route for producing hydroxyapatite and hydroxyapatite nanoparticles. The process is typically conducted at room temperature and operates through an acid-base neutralization reaction between calcium hydroxide and orthophosphoric acid, resulting in hydroxyapatite precipitate with water as a byproduct. The calcium precursor can alternatively be sourced from waste eggshells. This specific room-temperature application is particularly valued for yielding hydroxyapatite structures that exhibit a higher carbonate content.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791854962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soluble Phosphorus">
  <data key="d0">Soluble Phosphorus</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Soluble phosphorus is a chemical form and harmful impurity primarily found within phosphogypsum. As a contaminant, it significantly hinders the industrial reuse of phosphogypsum by increasing the gelling time of processing materials and reducing the overall structural strength of the final products. Its presence introduces multiple operational challenges that must be addressed before the material can be effectively utilized in construction or manufacturing applications.

Beyond its industrial drawbacks, soluble phosphorus poses serious environmental risks. It can readily leach into the surrounding environment, contaminating local ecosystems and contributing to secondary pollution. In aquatic systems, its release is a primary driver of eutrophication, which disrupts water quality and biodiversity. To mitigate these hazards, soluble phosphorus requires thorough removal through pretreatment protocols. Advanced processing techniques, such as calcination-modified treatments, have been shown to effectively decrease its concentration, thereby enabling safer and more sustainable utilization of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Soluble phosphorus is a chemical form of phosphorus found in phosphogypsum that significantly impacts its performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Soluble phosphorus is a phosphate impurity that hinders cement hydration, prolongs setting time, and reduces mechanical performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Soluble Phosphorus is the most significant form of impurity phosphorus in phosphogypsum, mainly composed of phosphoric acid ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Soluble phosphorus is the most significant phosphorus form in phosphogypsum, primarily existing as phosphoric acid ions.&lt;SEP&gt;A soluble impurity in phosphogypsum that is effectively removed during the water washing process.&lt;SEP&gt;Soluble phosphorus is an acidic impurity in phosphogypsum that is effectively removed by lime neutralization and reduced through high-temperature calcination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791770526</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soluble Fluorine">
  <data key="d0">Soluble Fluorine</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Soluble Fluorine is a harmful impurity and chemical contaminant present in phosphogypsum, recovered gypsum, and high-purity gypsum. In industrial applications, its concentration in recovered and high-purity gypsum is strictly limited to less than 0.015 wt%. Chemically, it reacts with lime to form insoluble compounds, although it can slowly dissolve over time, and it is commonly precipitated and removed during chemical treatment processes. From an environmental perspective, soluble fluorine poses a contamination risk as it can leach into surrounding ecosystems alongside soluble phosphorus.

When incorporated into sulfate cementitious materials, filling bodies, or slurry systems, soluble fluorine significantly alters physical and chemical properties. It acts as a chemical component that shortens the slurry setting time, reduces overall density and strength, and weakens the molecular interactions between crystals. These combined characteristics make soluble fluorine a critical impurity that requires careful monitoring and management during gypsum processing and construction material formulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Soluble fluorine is an impurity that weakens intermolecular forces in phosphogypsum and accelerates coagulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Soluble fluorine is an acidic impurity in phosphogypsum that lime neutralization struggles to remove but is reduced by high-temperature calcination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791770526</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium Pyrophosphate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Pyrophosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium pyrophosphate is an insoluble and harmless substance formed when thermal treatment converts soluble phosphorus impurities.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium pyrophosphate is an insoluble and harmless substance formed when thermal treatment converts soluble phosphorus impurities.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium pyrophosphate is an insoluble and harmless substance formed when thermal treatment converts soluble phosphorus impurities.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Pyrophosphate is an intermediate compound suggested to facilitate the formation of beta-tricalcium phosphate during heat treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Pyrophosphate is formed through the condensation of Hydrogen Phosphate during the thermal treatment of Hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hemihydrate Gypsum">
  <data key="d0">Hemihydrate Gypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hemihydrate gypsum is a processed and regenerated gypsum product synthesized primarily through the recycling and thermal treatment of phosphogypsum. Its manufacturing process involves calcination at specific temperatures and controlled residence times, which effectively transforms the raw industrial byproduct into a recyclable material. A critical step in this synthesis is the efficient precipitation and removal of soluble phosphorus impurities, yielding a purified gypsum product that maintains high chemical integrity.

As a distinct mineral phase, hemihydrate gypsum possesses identifiable physical and chemical characteristics that are routinely verified through analytical methods. Most notably, it exhibits characteristic diffraction peaks in X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns, which are consistently observed in phosphogypsum-based mortar and related composite materials. This mineralogical signature not only confirms its presence within recycled construction applications but also highlights its functional role as a sustainably sourced and chemically stable building component.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemihydrate Gypsum is a white powder intermediate produced from phosphogypsum that undergoes hydration.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemihydrate gypsum is the dominant form of phosphogypsum produced via the hemidihydrate-dihydrate process.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemihydrate gypsum is a product manufactured from pretreated phosphogypsum to reduce reliance on natural gypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Target product in phosphogypsum reuse processes, requiring strict content control.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemihydrate Gypsum is a gypsum crystal form whose morphology is influenced by specific dehydration methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792041352</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azouazi">
  <data key="d0">Azouazi</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Azouazi evaluated thermal treatment conditions and reported a significant reduction in radionuclide leaching rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Azouazi evaluated thermal treatment conditions and reported a significant reduction in radionuclide leaching rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Azouazi evaluated thermal treatment conditions and reported a significant reduction in radionuclide leaching rates.&lt;SEP&gt;A researcher who conducted extensive studies on phosphogypsum leaching using various aqueous solutions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
