<node id="Calcium Sulfoaluminate Cement">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Sulfoaluminate Cement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium Sulfoaluminate Cement is a specialized construction material that can be synthesized using phosphogypsum as a sole source of calcium oxide. The manufacturing process permits the substitution of conventional raw materials with treated phosphogypsum. However, the performance of this cement is notably influenced by phosphorus impurities present in phosphogypsum and similar feedstocks.

Beyond structural applications, Calcium Sulfoaluminate Cement serves as a functional matrix for environmental remediation and waste management. Research and practical implementations focus on its ability to incorporate phosphogypsum for the immobilization of hazardous elements. Additionally, the material is utilized in the management and stabilization of radioactivity, underscoring its role in both sustainable construction and nuclear waste containment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791722976</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluoride is a chemical species derived from fluorine that functions as both an element and a compound across environmental, industrial, and agricultural contexts. It originates primarily from phosphate rock and serves as a pervasive, soluble impurity within phosphogypsum, constituting an average mass fraction of approximately 1.27%. In this material, fluoride exists in both soluble and insoluble forms, influencing porosity, crystal interactions, compressive strength, and plaster setting time, though it is largely removed during purification and volatilized during calcination. Industrially, fluoride is released as gaseous hydrogen fluoride and silicon tetrafluoride, particularly during PBG and PSC processes, contributing to atmospheric contamination.

Environmentally, fluoride exhibits high diffusive mobility in soil, readily adsorbing to topsoil and accumulating in plants, which facilitates its entry into the food chain. It frequently leaches into groundwater, with concentrations often exceeding the 4.0 mg/L regulatory limit and the 1.5 mg/L World Health Organization guideline, occasionally reaching up to 32 mg/L. In aqueous systems, it exists as an anion that influences lead precipitate formation during immobilization and complexes with uranous ions under low pH and reduced conditions, with its environmental fate frequently modeled using GEOCHEM software. Leaching characteristics are tracked in Life Cycle Inventory data, with specific quantitative assessments recording chemical discharges such as 0.164 kilograms into groundwater.

As a toxic trace element and primary characteristic pollutant in phosphogypsum, fluoride poses substantial ecological and health risks. Elevated concentrations cause fluorosis in livestock and respiratory issues in humans through oral ingestion or dust inhalation, while also threatening aquatic ecosystems. Due to its pollution potential, fluoride requires strict immobilization and leaching control, particularly in phosphogypsum-based backfill studies and soil amendment applications. Remediation strategies focus on extracting and immobilizing the contaminant using advanced methods like phosphogypsum-derived hydroxyapatite nanoparticles, which achieve over 90% adsorption efficiency dependent on particle size. Alternative approaches include chemical precipitation to form stable compounds such as fluorite, aluminum fluoride complexes, and calcium fluorophosphate, alongside the use of various geological materials. These combined scientific and engineering efforts aim to reduce fluoride's environmental mobility and toxicity, ensuring agricultural and ecological safety.&lt;SEP&gt;A trace element found in phosphogypsum that contributes to its chemical profile.</data>
