<node id="Sulfur Trioxide">
  <data key="d0">Sulfur Trioxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfur trioxide (SO3) is a chemical oxide compound that functions as a vital intermediate in industrial chemistry, specifically during the conversion of sulfur dioxide to sulfuric acid. In industrial processing, its content in slag is also analyzed to calculate decomposition and desulfurization rates.

Sulfur trioxide is a primary and major constituent of phosphogypsum, comprising over 44% of the material's composition. Its concentration varies significantly depending on the geographic origin of the samples, with analytical measurements recording values such as 41.1 wt%, 48.12 ± 9.04 wt%, 51.48 wt% within the gypsum phase, and 53.5 wt%. These quantifications consistently indicate a high sulfur content. The measured concentration generally increases following calcination pre-treatment, making sulfur trioxide the primary compound routinely quantified in analytical procedures to accurately determine the chemical makeup of phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-006</data>
