<node id="J. Environ. Radioact.">
  <data key="d0">J. Environ. Radioact.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d2">J. Environ. Radioact. is a scientific and academic journal dedicated to publishing research on environmental radiochemistry and radioactivity measurements. The publication serves as a key resource for studies examining the radiological properties, behavior, and environmental impacts of various industrial and agricultural materials.

The journal features extensive research on phosphogypsum, including studies on its radiochemistry, the dissolution of radium-226 (226Ra), and the distribution of radionuclides and trace elements within the material. Additional topics covered in the journal include uranium uptake in synthetic gypsum utilized for mine tailings, the radiological impacts associated with phosphate rock processing, and the radiological consequences of applying phosphogypsum in agricultural settings. Furthermore, the journal publishes findings on radon exhalation rates resulting from phosphogypsum recycling processes. Collectively, these publications underscore the journal's comprehensive coverage of environmental radioactivity and radiological safety across diverse material applications.</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516547</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cadmium Ion">
  <data key="d0">Cadmium Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Cadmium Ion (Cd²⁺) is a heavy metal ion that functions as a significant soil contaminant and exists primarily as a free ion within soil solutions. As the active chemical species in this environment, its molar fraction remains lower than the total cadmium content present. The equilibrium concentration and activity of the Cadmium Ion are routinely calculated and analyzed in soil solutions, where its various chemical forms are closely monitored.

In soil environments, the Cadmium Ion frequently binds to form associates, and its overall activity is notably reduced through complexation with other ions and hydroxo complexes. To quantify these interactions, a detailed association coefficient equation is specifically applied to the Cadmium Ion. Furthermore, the environmental mobility and subsequent transfer of the Cadmium Ion to plants are heavily dependent on soil pH levels and the presence of soil carbonates.&lt;SEP&gt;A metal ion that interacts with hydroxyapatite surfaces, as investigated in sorption studies.&lt;SEP&gt;A heavy metal ion whose adsorption behavior and removal efficiency were studied using HAP and 20HPA-HAP adsorbents.&lt;SEP&gt;A heavy metal cation (Cd²⁺) targeted for removal from aqueous solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;A heavy metal ion (Cd²⁺) that exhibits stronger covalent bonding and higher adsorption capacity on HPA-modified hydroxyapatite compared to cobalt ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cadmium Ion is a heavy metal ion targeted for removal from wastewater, showing high removal efficiency with the tested composite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791655004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydroxo Complex">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxo Complex</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Hydroxo Complex (CdOH+) is a form of cadmium that binds prematurely in the soil solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical complex formed by cadmium ions that influences their activity and speciation in soil solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary form of cadmium in soil solution that drives biogeochemical passivation and reduces cadmium penetration into plants.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hydroxo Complex (CdOH+) is a form of cadmium that binds prematurely in the soil solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical complex formed by cadmium ions that influences their activity and speciation in soil solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary form of cadmium in soil solution that drives biogeochemical passivation and reduces cadmium penetration into plants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790950367</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonate Form">
  <data key="d0">Carbonate Form</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Carbonate Form (CdCO3^0) is a chemical species of cadmium present in lower concentrations than the hydroxo complex.&lt;SEP&gt;The Carbonate Form (CdCO3^0) is a chemical species of cadmium present in lower concentrations than the hydroxo complex.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonate form is the initial state of Cerium in phosphogypsum with an oxidation state of +4.</data>
