<node id="Calcium Ion">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Calcium Ion (Ca²⁺) is a positively charged, exchangeable macro-ion that exists as a free species across diverse environmental, geological, and experimental settings. Its concentration is routinely monitored to track chemical dynamics, and it is quantified as a chemical discharge into groundwater, typically measured in kilograms. In soil science, it functions as a key exchangeable cation within Haplic Chernozem soils, residing in the soil solution as a macro-ion that forms associates and actively drives carbonate deposition. The ion is naturally released into aqueous environments during the dissolution of calcium carbonate and is also extracted from phosphogypsum during industrial processing, where it is subsequently liberated by calcium sulfate to drive pozzolanic and hydration reactions.

Chemically and mineralogically, the Calcium Ion is a fundamental structural component of several crystalline lattices, most notably gypsum, where it ensures geochemical stability and reactivity while remaining substitutable by uranyl ions. In hydroxyapatite, it represents the characteristic oxidation state of calcium, identifiable via an XPS peak at 352.8 eV, and its translational vibrational modes are detectable in the 200–350 cm⁻¹ range of Raman spectra. The ion readily reacts with soluble phosphorus or phosphate ions to precipitate as insoluble calcium phosphate. Within simulated body fluid systems, it binds to negative surface charges on hydroxyapatite to nucleate calcium-rich amorphous calcium phosphate, a critical mechanism for biomimetic mineralization. It further supports hydroxyapatite crystallization and growth following calcium citrate decomposition and can be adsorbed onto CTAB monomers during crystal precipitation. Additionally, it facilitates fluoride removal in aqueous solutions and, when introduced to specific solutions, preferentially forms clusters on micellar surfaces.

In advanced material and cementitious systems, the Calcium Ion is highly abundant within the cementitious matrix and calcium silicate hydrate phases. Due to its exchangeable nature, it frequently participates in ion exchange processes within apatite structures and cementitious networks, where it can be readily substituted by other metal ions, including toxic heavy metals. This versatile reactivity, combined with its structural and catalytic roles across geological, biochemical, and industrial domains, underscores the Calcium Ion's significance in both natural cycles and engineered material applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791965930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Magnesium Ion">
  <data key="d0">Magnesium Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnesium ion is an exchangeable cation present in the Haplic Chernozem soil.&lt;SEP&gt;The Magnesium Ion (Mg^2+) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium Ion (Mg²⁺) is a chemical species whose concentration was measured across the experimental conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium ion is an exchangeable cation present in the Haplic Chernozem soil.&lt;SEP&gt;The Magnesium Ion (Mg^2+) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium ion is an impurity removed from the filtrate during the first purification stage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791272396</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Sulfate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Sulfate Ion (SO₄²⁻) is a polyatomic, negatively charged inorganic sulfur compound that functions as an anionic macro-ion across diverse environmental, chemical, and industrial systems. Chemically, it acts as a reactant in various equations and as a spectator ion during stabilization processes. It actively participates in cadmium complexation within aqueous solutions and undergoes anionic substitution for phosphate ions. Spectrally, the ion is characterized by strong absorption bands and distinct vibrational peaks in the 1000–1100 cm⁻¹ range, which are routinely used for its identification via infrared spectroscopy.

In soil environments, the Sulfate Ion exists as a macro-ion within the soil solution, where it readily forms associates and is routinely quantified under various experimental conditions. Industrially, it is a prominent chemical component and characteristic pollutant within phosphogypsum. Released from calcium sulfate, the ion participates in pozzolanic and hydration reactions, drives conversion processes, and is subsequently removed during the phase transition of calcium sulfate in hydrothermal treatments. When present in leachate from phosphogypsum stockpiles, it serves as a notable environmental contaminant. Furthermore, the Sulfate Ion exhibits strong hygroscopic behavior in atmospheric contexts, where it competes for moisture with hydrogen fluoride. This competitive interaction significantly prolongs the atmospheric residence time of phosphogypsum and associated atmospheric mixtures.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical group identified by sharp peaks around 1100 cm⁻¹ and 600 cm⁻¹.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002686</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Carbonate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Carbonate Ion (CO3^2-) is a negatively charged chemical species and macro-ion that plays a significant role in various geochemical and aqueous environments. Primarily found in soil solutions, it frequently forms associates and directly influences both the deposition and dissolution of calcium carbonate. In experimental settings, its concentration is routinely measured alongside that of bicarbonate to monitor solution chemistry.

Beyond soil interactions, the carbonate ion exhibits notable reactivity with metals in aqueous systems. It competes with other ligands for binding sites on cadmium ions and readily reacts with rare earth elements to form precipitates. Furthermore, the ion is a critical substituent in mineral crystallography, particularly within hydroxyapatite structures. By replacing hydroxyl and phosphate ions, the carbonate ion induces the formation of distinct apatite types and significantly alters the physical and chemical properties of the host mineral lattice.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonate Ion is a functional group associated with carbonated Hydroxyapatite, analyzed via FTIR.&lt;SEP&gt;The carbonate ion is adsorbed within the HAp matrix, as indicated by specific C 1s spectral peaks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791966043</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bicarbonate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Bicarbonate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Bicarbonate Ion (HCO3-) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates and participates in calcium carbonate deposition.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bicarbonate Ion is a chemical species whose concentration is tracked as a key parameter in the experimental data.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bicarbonate Ion (HCO3-) is a macro-ion present in the soil solution that forms associates and participates in calcium carbonate deposition.&lt;SEP&gt;The bicarbonate ion is an anion that forms a complex with cadmium ions, influencing adsorption calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;Bicarbonate Ion is a negatively charged anion produced during the hydrolysis of calcium carbonate.&lt;SEP&gt;Bicarbonate ion is a chemical species whose concentration affects carbonate substitution in precipitated hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Bicarbonate ion is a chemical factor investigated for its effects on carbonate substitution in hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791259821</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chloride Ion">
  <data key="d0">Chloride Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Chloride Ion (Cl⁻) is a negatively charged macro-ion that exhibits significant chemical and environmental versatility. In soil science, it functions as a macro-ion within the soil solution, where it readily forms associates. In aqueous and mineral contexts, the chloride ion acts as a competing anion for cadmium binding and possesses the ability to substitute for hydroxyl ions within apatite structures. These properties underscore its active role in geochemical interactions and mineral formation processes.

Beyond its environmental behavior, the chloride ion plays specific roles in surface chemistry and laboratory analysis. When sodium chloride accumulates on surfaces, the release of chloride ions helps attract calcium ions, effectively creating nucleation sites. In synthetic procedures, chloride ions may persist as impurities prior to washing when calcium chloride is utilized as a reactant. Analytically, the chloride ion is a quantifiable chemical species whose concentration is routinely measured across experimental conditions. Its standard quantification method involves silver nitrate titration, which applies a conversion factor of 35.45 g/mol to determine its presence and abundance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792068200</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydroxide Ion">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxide Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Hydroxide Ion (OH-) is a negatively charged anion and macro-ion that serves as a fundamental indicator of basic or alkaline chemical environments. It is frequently represented as OH- in chemical equations and is notably released during the dissolution of calcium carbonate, a process that directly signals alkalinity in aqueous systems.

In chemical reactions, the Hydroxide Ion acts as a key reactant that facilitates hydrolysis processes. Specifically, it breaks pyrophosphate bonds and reacts with pyrophosphate during hydrolysis, while also facilitating the hydrolysis of triphosphate. Its presence drives these bond-breaking and transformation reactions in alkaline conditions.

Within soil solutions, the Hydroxide Ion functions as a macro-ion that forms associates and significantly influences geochemical behavior. It participates in reactions with toxic metal ions to form insoluble precipitates, thereby affecting their mobility. Furthermore, it plays a critical role in pH-dependent leaching behavior and forms stable complexes with cadmium ions, directly impacting their stability constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791984470</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CCE System">
  <data key="d0">CCE System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The CCE System refers to the current state of equilibrium that governs ion binding, adsorption, and salt transfer in the soil.&lt;SEP&gt;The CCE System refers to the current state of equilibrium that governs ion binding, adsorption, and salt transfer in the soil.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790951494</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soil Profile">
  <data key="d0">Soil Profile</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The Soil Profile is the vertical section of soil through which carbonate forms and salts migrate and accumulate.&lt;SEP&gt;The Soil Profile is the vertical section of soil through which carbonate forms and salts migrate and accumulate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790951494</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soil Solution">
  <data key="d0">Soil Solution</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Soil Solution is the aqueous and liquid phase within the soil matrix and micro-basins, serving as the primary medium for dissolved ions, compounds, and microelements. It contains a diverse array of chemical species, including macro-ions, hydroxyl ions, and active cadmium ions, with cadmium concentrations typically remaining significantly lower than those of macro-ions. This liquid phase is fundamentally governed by chemical equilibria, such as the calcium-carbonate equilibrium, and facilitates ion associations, chemical migration phenomena, and continuous interactions with surrounding soil properties. Within this medium, heavy metals and cadmium exist in various chemical forms, exhibit mobility, and directly influence their bioavailability.

Functionally, Soil Solution acts as the critical transport mechanism that delivers essential ions and nutrients directly to plant roots. Because of its complex chemical dynamics, it serves as the essential environment for thermodynamic modeling, particularly regarding cadmium status, and functions as the standard medium for measuring ion activities, heavy metal bioavailability, and macro-ion or hydroxyl ion dynamics. Ultimately, Soil Solution plays a central role in governing soil chemistry, nutrient cycling, and the migration of dissolved substances throughout the terrestrial ecosystem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-000</data>
