<node id="Calcium">
  <data key="d0">Calcium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium is a chemical element and alkaline earth metal that typically exists as a double-charged cation (Ca2+). In analytical and diagrammatic representations, it is depicted as a blue circle and functions as an elemental component where ten atoms are balanced on both sides of a reaction equation. It serves as a fundamental structural and compositional component across geological, materials science, and environmental contexts, including CPB mixtures. Calcium shares significant chemical and biological similarities with radium, influencing radium's environmental behavior; while radium can substitute for calcium in apatite, its larger ionic radius prevents isomorphic replacement in gypsum.

In materials science and biomineralization, calcium is a primary constituent of apatite and hydroxyapatite (HAP) crystal structures. It exists as Ca2+ ions within HAP, participating in electrostatic interactions with proteins, serving as a critical binding target for AL molecules, and acting as a key structural ion. Although often deficient in biological apatites—a condition that directly affects material stability and solubility—calcium is utilized as a chemical precursor in specific molar ratios with phosphate to synthesize bulk and nano-hydroxyapatite nanoparticles. These nanoparticles are routinely identified via EDX and XPS analyses, which reveal various bonds and phases, and they release calcium through dissolution-precipitation mechanisms. Calcium also contributes to the classification of samples as calcium phosphate compounds and is detected alongside sulfur and phosphorus in XRD and EDS analyses, appearing near 0.5 keV in EDS spectra.

Within phosphogypsum (PG), calcium forms the core gypsum matrix alongside sulfur and phosphorus, denoting a major elemental component widely distributed throughout the matrix and comprising approximately 23% by weight in specific samples. It is consistently identified as a predominant element through XRF analysis, with concentrations such as 38.14 ± 1.70 and mass fractions ranging from 2.6% to 28.98%, accounting for over 90% when considering the calcium sulfate matrix and comprising 38 wt.% of the fluorite phase. Analytically, calcium is identified via EDX/EDS through prominent spectral peaks near 3.7 keV (or 3.69 keV), as well as via XRD, XPS, and peaks at approximately 210, 220, 221, and 227 nm. Its presence is confirmed through particle surface distribution, EGTA volumetric methods, and mathematical formulas based on effective ionic strength. Within PG, calcium ions can undergo isomorph substitution, being replaced by rare earth elements, uranium (including U(IV)), thorium, titanium, and strontium, with titanium substitution notably creating oxygen vacancies. The relative proportion of calcium in PG increases when treated with oxalic acid, sodium carbonate, and sodium bicarbonate, and its presence is considered beneficial for generating hydration elements. During acid leaching or acid rain exposure, calcium dissolves from the PG matrix, showing stable initial concentrations followed by a delayed decline that reflects dissolution and recrystallization, with concentrations decreasing by 22% in leached samples compared to raw samples. It can be recovered through vertical-flow electrophoresis or converted into high-value compounds like calcium carbonate and calcium oxide, though it also acts as an interfering metal ion in PG leach liquor, measured at concentrations of 15.45 g/L.

From an agricultural and environmental perspective, calcium functions as a vital plant nutrient and is utilized as a fertilizer component to improve acid paddy soil. When phosphogypsum is applied as a soil amendment, calcium acts as a chemically activated element that adsorbs to the soil complex, reinforcing aggregate structures and functioning as a cation exchange agent. It displaces sodium in sodic soils and hydrogen or aluminum in acidic soils, thereby promoting soil flocculation. However, calcium also interacts directly with soil phosphate, precipitating it into insoluble forms that reduce phosphate bioavailability, though abundant calcium forms complexes with phosphate that effectively mitigate competition with sulfate for adsorption sites. In environmental monitoring and life cycle assessment contexts, calcium is measured as a dissolved chemical ion in groundwater analysis and is recorded as a chemical compound in groundwater emissions, as well as a liquid emission in LCI data for PG disposal. Collectively, these chemical, structural, and ecological properties define calcium's measurable behavior and environmental impact across laboratory and field applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is an element whose content remains low due to silicon aggregation and the thermal decomposition of calcium sulfate.</data>
