<node id="Fig. 3">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 3</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 3 is a generic figure label utilized across numerous distinct scientific studies and analytical contexts, rather than representing a single unified dataset. In soil chemistry and environmental assessments, it visualizes macro-ion and hydroxyl ion (OH⁻) activity in soil solutions following phosphogypsum treatment using the ION-3 model, tracks cumulative phosphogypsum volume over time, and monitors the generation and utilization rates of phosphogypsum in China over the past decade. It also quantifies the proportional environmental impacts of various industrial processes and material flows for comparative ecological footprint analyses. Within chemical engineering and structural applications, it illustrates the effects of agitation time on phosphogypsum leaching using TBP-TOPO in kerosene, details the impacts of acidity, time, phase ratio, and temperature on rare earth element stripping efficiency, and depicts the failure mode of PGEF. Microbiological and ecological research employs it to map microbial diversity, community composition, and species overlap across composting samples.

In material science and nanoparticle characterization, Fig. 3 presents comprehensive physical and structural data, including X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns, density, porosity, linear shrinkage, water absorption, and thermogravimetric data for phosphogypsum in both pellet and powder forms. It displays Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) spectra of samples before and after calcination at 1000 °C, compares hydroxyapatite synthesized via hydrothermal and microwave methods, and provides a schematic of phosphate fertilizer production routes. Advanced microscopy and phase analysis are frequently featured, including scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images revealing the tabular crystalline morphology of raw phosphogypsum grains at 500x and 450x magnification, XRD patterns detailing the phase composition of PPG and prepared hydroxyapatite samples, energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping for 20HPA-HAP composites, UDM plots for synthesized hydroxyapatite nanoparticles, and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) alongside high-resolution TEM (HR-TEM) data detailing the internal structure, lattice fringes, diffraction patterns, and microstructural evolution of porous HA microspheres. Collectively, these varied applications underscore the label’s broad utility across chemistry, materials science, environmental analysis, microbiology, and engineering.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 3 displays EDS maps showing the abundance and distribution of elements in the phosphogypsum samples.</data>
