<node id="X-Axis">
  <data key="d0">X-Axis</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The X-Axis functions as the primary horizontal reference line across a wide array of scientific and technical charts, consistently serving as the framework for the independent variable. In experimental contexts, it is specifically utilized to organize data across four discrete experimental stages. As the foundational horizontal component of coordinate systems, its exact labeling and numerical range are dynamically adapted to match the specific physical quantity or parameter being measured in each graphical representation.

In specialized analytical charts, the X-Axis takes on distinct labels and scales. For Energy Dispersive Spectroscopy (EDS) spectrum charts, it is labeled "Energy / keV" and spans from 0 to 15 keV. In X-ray Diffraction charts, it represents the diffraction angle, while isotherm charts designate it as Relative Pressure. Thermal analysis graphs may label it "Temperature / K", covering a range from 373 to 1573 Kelvin. Spectral graphs utilize it to measure wavenumber, notably displaying a scale that decreases from left to right. Furthermore, in dual-axis line graphs, the X-Axis acts as the horizontal axis whose precise label is explicitly defined within the accompanying chart description. Across all these applications, the X-Axis provides the essential horizontal dimension for accurate data visualization and interpretation.&lt;SEP&gt;X-Axis is the horizontal axis of the charts, representing publications or years.&lt;SEP&gt;X-Axis is a component of the Spectral Graph labeled with Energy(KeV) to represent detected X-ray energy.&lt;SEP&gt;The X-Axis is the horizontal axis of the chart, representing four categorical variables related to stack height.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791878118</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Y-Axis">
  <data key="d0">Y-Axis</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Y-Axis functions as the essential vertical measurement framework across a diverse array of scientific, analytical, and statistical charts, consistently designed to quantify dependent variables and display measured magnitudes. In spectroscopic and analytical instrumentation, it represents signal intensity, such as ion concentration or relative levels on a scale from 0 to 120, or absolute intensity in counts ranging from 0 to 800 on Energy Dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy (EDS) spectrum charts. It similarly tracks intensity in X-ray Diffraction (XRD) charts and measures Absorbance or Transmittance intensity within spectral graphs. In material science and thermodynamic studies, the Y-Axis quantifies the mass fraction as a percentage from 0 to 100, or records the volume adsorbed on isotherm charts. In certain analytical plots, it displays signal intensity without a fixed numerical scale, relying instead on vertical curve offsets to maintain visual clarity.

Beyond laboratory and material data, the Y-Axis is also utilized in bibliometric and statistical visualizations to represent publication categories or total publication counts, typically scaled from 0 to 350 and explicitly labeled as "Publications." Across all these distinct applications, the Y-Axis maintains its core role as the vertical axis that standardizes data interpretation, enabling precise comparison of quantitative trends regardless of the specific chart type or measurement domain.&lt;SEP&gt;The Y-axis represents the relative intensity of diffracted X-rays&lt;SEP&gt;Y-Axis is the vertical scale of the publication graph, ranging from 0 to 300.&lt;SEP&gt;Y-Axis is a component of the Spectral Graph labeled with KCnt to represent X-ray count intensity.&lt;SEP&gt;The Y-Axis is the vertical axis of the chart, labeled "Pt" (Points) with a scale ranging from 0 to 75.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791878118</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legend">
  <data key="d0">Legend</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Legend is a fundamental visualization component used in charts, plots, and maps to decode graphical information by linking visual elements to their corresponding data categories. It typically appears as a designated box, guide, or key positioned at various locations, including the top, the top right corner, or as a vertical guide along the far left side of a plot. Its primary function is to serve as an essential reference tool that ensures accurate interpretation of complex visual data across diverse analytical contexts.

In scientific and technical applications, the Legend identifies specific data series, chemical phases, chemical species, sample groups, or materials by assigning them distinct visual encodings. These encodings commonly utilize specific colors, marker shapes, symbols, patterns, colored squares, or color gradients. Depending on the dataset, it may catalog a defined number of series, such as five or seven, or highlight particular substances like Cobalt and Cadmium ions or the sample material HAP-BC. In cartographic contexts, the Legend quantifies geographic data, for example, by indicating phosphogypsum production volume in million tonnes per year through a color gradient.&lt;SEP&gt;The Legend is a chart component listing 11 environmental impact categories and their associated colors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
