<node id="South Africa">
  <data key="d0">South Africa</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">South Africa is a country and geographic region that hosts the Phalaborwa phosphate rock deposit. It is consistently identified in scientific literature as a primary source of phosphogypsum samples. The phosphogypsum extracted from this nation, particularly from the Lowveld region, has been the subject of extensive geochemical and industrial research.

Studies have investigated the material's cement set retardation properties and analyzed its composition for radioactive nuclides. Comparative analyses indicate that phosphogypsum from South Africa contains significantly higher levels of certain trace elements than samples from other geographic locations. Leveraging these characteristics, South Africa has developed industrial methods for the co-production of sulfuric acid and cement using phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;South Africa is the country containing the phosphoric acid manufacturing plant where samples were sourced.&lt;SEP&gt;South Africa is the country where a high domestic phosphogypsum industry and the largest igneous phosphate deposit in Africa are located.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791915077</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonates">
  <data key="d0">Carbonates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbonates are a versatile class of chemical compounds examined across environmental, nuclear, and materials science disciplines. In soil environments, they significantly affect the mobility and plant uptake of cadmium. Regarding rare earth element chemistry, carbonates act as dispersed, soluble salts, although their dissolution can be restricted by gypsum coatings. In phosphogypsum processing, carbonates bind a portion of the contained uranium and can be refined into high-purity forms for various industrial applications.

In materials research, carbonates interact notably with hydroxyapatite. They are efficiently absorbed by the material, achieving optimal uptake at a pH of 9. Conversely, when carbonates remain as synthesis residues, they decompose during thermal treatment, contributing to mass losses in hydroxyapatite. Furthermore, researchers frequently analyze carbonates for their characteristic calcite crystal structures and distinct spectral properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineral classes highlighted by some authors as potential main rare earth element-bearing minerals in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792039862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
