<node id="Sulfates">
  <data key="d0">Sulfates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfates are chemical compounds and mineral classes that serve important functions in environmental analysis and industrial resource processing, particularly concerning phosphogypsum. In soil analysis, sulfates are routinely examined in soil samples, where their presence is typically detected via barium sulfate (BaSO4) sedimentation and their concentrations are measured using gravimetric techniques.

In the context of phosphogypsum, sulfates present a dual nature that reflects both their natural occurrence and industrial utility. They are commonly described as chemical impurities and insoluble compounds located in the residual phase of phosphogypsum, where they also form complexes with metallic elements such as potassium and sodium. While initially characterized as impurities, sulfates can alternatively be processed into high-purity chemical compounds synthesized from phosphogypsum for diverse industrial applications. Additionally, specific mineral classes of sulfates have been identified by researchers as potential primary carriers of rare earth elements within phosphogypsum, highlighting their significance in both waste management and critical mineral recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792039862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Na+">
  <data key="d0">Na+</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Na+ represents sodium ions whose content is measured using flame photometric detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Na+ represents sodium ions whose content is measured using flame photometric detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Na+ is a monovalent cation that can substitute for divalent cations in apatite structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247568</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cd">
  <data key="d0">Cd</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cd (Cadmium) is a chemical element classified as a heavy metal and heavy metal ion. It is widely recognized as a hazardous pollutant that can cause cancers and impair the immune system, frequently coexisting with other heavy metals in environmental matrices. In industrial and ecological contexts, Cd is commonly detected in soil, terrestrial materials, and phosphogypsum waste, where its presence as a contaminant can significantly hinder the safe utilization of the waste material.

Due to its environmental persistence and health risks, Cd is routinely monitored as a trace element across various scientific and geochemical datasets. Its total and water-soluble concentrations in soil are typically quantified using analytical techniques such as XRF and AAS. Comprehensive concentration data for Cd is systematically measured, recorded in data tables, and reported in geochemical studies to support environmental assessment and research.&lt;SEP&gt;A trace element measured in parts per million in the provided analytical data.&lt;SEP&gt;Cd is a chemical element listed in a tabular dataset with associated numerical measurements.&lt;SEP&gt;Cadmium is a chemical element represented by the symbol Cd.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792039439</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X-ray Fluorescence">
  <data key="d0">X-ray Fluorescence</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">X-ray fluorescence is a spectroscopic method used to determine total cadmium content in soil.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray fluorescence is a spectroscopic method used to determine total cadmium content in soil.&lt;SEP&gt;Analytical technique used to determine micro- and trace elemental composition of samples.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray Fluorescence is an analytical technique used to determine the elemental composition of PG waste.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray Fluorescence is an analytical technique used to measure trace elements in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray fluorescence is an analytical method used to determine the semi-quantitative chemical composition of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;An analytical technique used to determine the chemical composition of solid samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-006</data>
