<node id="Radioactivity">
  <data key="d0">Radioactivity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Radioactivity is a natural phenomenon characterized by the emission of ionizing radiation from materials, functioning as an inherent property of radioactive isotopes that classifies their release into the environment. This property is primarily driven by the decay series of isotopes such as uranium-238, thorium-232, and radium-226, which are naturally present in phosphate ores and related geological formations. As a critical measurable property, radioactivity is systematically quantified in becquerels using specialized detectors calibrated with specific efficiency values, serving as a key environmental parameter, impact category, and emission metric across industrial and ecological assessments. It is routinely evaluated in phosphate rock, fertilizers, dicalcium phosphate, phosphate-derived foods, and industrial byproducts, with phosphogypsum receiving particular attention. In the context of phosphogypsum utilization, radioactivity is assessed alongside hazardous element immobilization and performance in cementitious applications, while also being tracked as a specific activity category in soil amendment inventories, where measured values such as 0.525 Bq are recorded.

The presence of radioactivity in these materials poses potential radiological risks to human populations and ecosystems, particularly when phosphogypsum is repurposed for construction applications like brick manufacturing. Consequently, radioactivity functions as a strict environmental requirement that dictates safety protocols and regional applicability, necessitating ongoing monitoring across mining sites, waste streams, and resource utilization pathways. Extraction and processing techniques are often employed to reduce isotopic radioactivity, ensuring that residues meet established safety standards. Ultimately, radioactivity stands as both a quantifiable industrial characteristic and a vital public health consideration, requiring continuous evaluation to balance material repurposing with environmental protection and occupational safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactivity is a property of raw phosphogypsum primarily caused by thallium and actinium, which is reduced through chemical treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical property of phosphogypsum that was not evaluated in the current study but noted in other literature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-004</data>
