<node id="Lead">
  <data key="d0">Lead</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Lead is a heavy metal chemical element that predominantly exists in the +2 oxidation state, with its environmental solubility primarily governed by lead carbonate and lead sulfate (PbSO4). Naturally occurring as a trace element, it is notably enriched in phosphorites, where it substitutes for calcium within the apatite crystal structure. In environmental and industrial systems, lead leaches from geological and industrial sources, migrates through various compartments, precipitates as lead sulfide (PbS), or adsorbs onto clays and organic materials. In certain industrial and environmental contexts, it is also characterized as a radioactive element or radionuclide. A significant portion of lead is associated with phosphogypsum, a byproduct of the phosphate industry, exhibiting a 27% transfer rate from phosphate rock during wet processing. Documented concentrations in phosphogypsum range from 4.70 to 204.29 μg/g, substantially exceeding the Earth's crust average, with specific measurements recording values such as 6.2 ± 1.7. Lead tends to concentrate in coarser grain size fractions, where enrichment factors increase due to polonium decay and organic matter retention. This contamination extends to soils amended with phosphogypsum, causing slight pollution, and has been extensively measured in soils across the Mississippi Delta and New Orleans regions. It is also a toxic element in phosphogypsum that accumulates preferentially in calcite during conversion, and its presence varies regionally.

Due to its environmental persistence and inherent toxicity, lead poses considerable hazards to ecological systems and human health, frequently prompting investigation alongside cadmium and nickel regarding phytotoxicity and extraction potential. As a divalent heavy metal ion, it is readily absorbed by plants from contaminated soils, initiating bioaccumulation pathways that introduce substantial food safety risks. Consequently, regulatory frameworks commonly enforce a maximum concentration limit of 0.05 mg/L in water quality standards, while the US-EPA sets a limit of 5 mg/L for mixtures. The concentration of lead in phosphogypsum solutions is actively monitored against such limits, and it is frequently measured in milligrams per liter during Toxicity Characteristic Leaching Procedure (TCLP) tests for leaching toxicity analysis. In phosphogypsum, lead is categorized as a trace metal with intermediate mobility, often present as insoluble precipitates with sulfate or carbonates, resulting in a low release rate and limited leaching, which can reach only 0.24 µg/L under specific tested conditions. Its concentrations in phosphogypsum typically remain lower than the rare effect concentration, and it is routinely listed in tabular data for phosphogypsum analysis, sometimes found in mixtures below detection limits.

To mitigate these risks, remediation and environmental management strategies frequently employ apatite-based materials, including natural and synthetic apatite as well as nanocrystalline hydroxyapatite. These phosphate-based sorbents and other low-cost adsorbents rapidly and effectively remove lead ions from aqueous solutions and contaminated matrices through dissolution-precipitation mechanisms. Additionally, lead can be immobilized in contaminated soils using phosphogypsum slag-based cementitious materials, facilitating in situ stabilization, significantly reducing bioavailability, and minimizing environmental dispersion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-01