<node id="Portlandite">
  <data key="d0">Portlandite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Portlandite, an alternative mineral name for calcium hydroxide, is a significant mineral phase encountered across various material science and environmental contexts. It forms as a primary hydration product of clinker phases and appears as a lamellar hydration product within autoclaved aerated concrete when exposed to highly alkaline environments. Additionally, it can be generated from dissolved phosphogypsum. Functionally, Portlandite plays a crucial role in environmental remediation and material stability, notably contributing to fluoride immobilization and effectively capturing carbon dioxide even under harsh conditions.

Analytically, Portlandite is readily identified through X-ray diffraction analysis. It is characterized by specific diffraction peaks, including a prominent peak 3 visible around 47° in PG1 samples and scattered across others, and is often marked by a solid diamond symbol in XRD graphs. Its presence is particularly notable in samples containing 12% calcium carbonate. However, the abundance of Portlandite is sensitive to compositional changes; its diffraction peaks progressively decrease and eventually vanish as phosphogypsum content increases.&lt;SEP&gt;Portlandite is a solid compound (Ca(OH)2) formed during the alkaline dissolution of PG and used as an intermediate for carbonation.&lt;SEP&gt;Portlandite is a synthesized compound formed during the hydrometallurgical conversion of PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Portlandite is a chemical product formed from the conversion and carbonation reactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Portlandite is a calcium hydroxide compound studied for its dissolution and carbonation into calcite.&lt;SEP&gt;Portlandite is a compound introduced into geochemical models to simulate the alkalinity of the CPGFM assemblage.&lt;SEP&gt;Portlandite is a calcium hydroxide compound (Ca(OH)2) identified in the mineralogical composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791938226</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Rock">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Rock</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate Rock is a naturally occurring, finite geological mineral resource and ore, summarized by the USGS, that serves as the foundational feedstock for phosphate manufacturing, phosphorus chemicals, and fertilizer production. Geologically, it manifests as sedimentary, igneous, or metamorphic deposits distributed globally, with substantial reserves located in China, particularly within the Yangtze River Basin, where mining operations face strict regional restrictions. The material is typically available in crude or upgraded forms and is primarily composed of fluorapatite. It inherently contains naturally occurring radioactive materials, including uranium, thorium, and radium-226 isotopes in radioactive equilibrium, alongside various impurities such as rare earth elements, lanthanides, heavy metals, aluminum, and titanium. Global production was estimated at 225 million metric tons in 2014, increasing to 258 million metric tons in 2018, with commercial sourcing extending to regions such as North Africa for industries like Hellenic Fertilizers.

As the primary reactant for phosphoric acid generation, Phosphate Rock undergoes industrial processing via the wet process or acid digestion with concentrated sulfuric acid, often preceded by concentration through flotation. This chemical treatment dictates the profile of its major solid byproduct, phosphogypsum. During acidulation, inherent fluorine reacts to form hydrogen fluoride, while aluminum, titanium, and heavy metal impurities become entrained in the phosphogypsum matrix. The ore's geological composition directly influences the morphology of phosphogypsum samples. Radium-226 persists in the waste stream, contributing to its radioactivity, whereas uranium typically partitions into the phosphoric acid stream. Consequently, Phosphate Rock acts as the original source of toxic substances, including cadmium, found in phosphogypsum, and small amounts of unreacted phosphate rock may remain in the waste. The material transfers elements to phosphogypsum while leaving residual fluorapatite during processing.

Beyond its core roles, Phosphate Rock is integral to resource recovery and regulatory frameworks. Rare earth elements and lanthanides may be extracted from the ore or its byproducts through hydrometallurgical processes, positioning the resource as a bridge to secondary critical mineral recovery. Industrial operations manage phosphogypsum by leaching to recover residual sulfuric acid. Phosphate Rock is strategically regulated under China's 14th Five-Year Plan Period. Its distinct radiological profile establishes it as a standard comparative baseline in radionuclide analysis, and it is closely studied alongside phosphate fertilizer factories for its radiological and chemical characteristics. In environmental applications, the rock is utilized and compared to hydroxyapatite for its capacity to reduce metal bioavailability in soil. Ultimately, Phosphate Rock represents a complex geological resource intrinsically linked to its radiological characteristics, waste management considerations, and the migration mechanisms of associated harmful substances.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-01