<node id="Quartz">
  <data key="d0">Quartz</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Quartz is a silicate mineral chemically composed of silicon dioxide (SiO2) that serves as a standard reference mineral in geological contexts and is frequently concentrated in Gabes sediment. It commonly functions as a typical gangue mineral in phosphate ores, comprising approximately 10% of phosphorite compositions, and is identified within the mineralogical makeup of various raw materials. In phosphate processing, Quartz acts as a persistent, naturally occurring impurity in raw phosphogypsum waste, generally representing a minor constituent that accounts for 0.9% to 5% of the mineralogical composition, although it has also been identified as the primary crystalline component in specific phosphogypsum samples. Due to its high chemical stability and insolubility during standard processing, Quartz co-occurs with aluminosilicates, remains retained in residual Beneficiated Solids, and is closely associated with silicon. Its active removal is managed throughout processing operations to mitigate erosion-corrosion damage to industrial reactors. Despite its general insolubility, Quartz exhibits distinct solubility and solvation behaviors in dilute hydrothermal solutions when temperatures exceed 150 °C.

Beyond its role as an industrial impurity, Quartz demonstrates versatile applications in construction and materials science, serving as a primary mineral phase in formulations such as phosphogypsum-containing bricks, phosphogypsum cement mortar, concrete mixes, and samples incorporating calcium carbonate. Analytically, Quartz is readily identified via X-ray diffraction across diverse materials, including phosphogypsum waste, synthetic hydroxyapatite particles, and composite samples. In graphical XRD plots, it is conventionally represented by an open circle symbol and is specifically characterized by peak 5, which generates a prominent signal around 26° and typically dominates the composition of analyzed samples. The prominence of this crystal phase peak directly correlates with the SiO2/Al2O3 ratio, increasing as the ratio rises, and remains clearly visible in phosphogypsum-based mortars. While often detected with low intensity in XRD patterns, Quartz also appears as a contaminant in hydroxyapatite samples and spectra, attributable to starting materials and identifiable through a specific absorption band.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartz is a minor mineralogical component found within the studied phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartz is present in phosphogypsum in trace amounts alongside calcium sulfate dihydrate.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartz is a mineral component associated with cadmium distribution in the phosphogypsum matrix.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartz represents a minor mineral phase identified in phosphogypsum through mineralogical analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A crystalline mineral identified as a main component within the phosphogypsum sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-002</data>
