  <data key="d3">doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d2">Turkey is a country in Asia that encompasses significant geographic and industrial features, including the major city of Istanbul and Mersin, which houses a research center. As a prominent global industrial hub, Turkey is a major producer of phosphogypsum, generating approximately 3.0 million metric tons annually, and serves as a key location for evaluating gypsum deposits for their economic potential and production viability. In scientific literature and data visualizations, Turkey frequently appears in comparative tables and charts detailing material properties and phosphogypsum composition, often positioned on vertical axes and notably associated with a weight percentage of approximately 43%. The nation is also referenced in data tables alongside research citations, underscoring its prominence in material science datasets.

Extensive environmental and radiological research has been conducted within Turkey, particularly concerning phosphogypsum disposed of by the phosphorus fertilizer industry. Researchers have systematically analyzed the chemical compositions of this substance, including a notable 2008 study by Degirmenci on its chemical components, while also investigating its natural radionuclide content, radiological characteristics, and the resulting public radiation doses. Beyond industrial applications, Turkey's architectural practices reflect adaptation to local climate and social conditions, particularly in adobe construction, which has driven ongoing efforts to improve its engineering properties. Collectively, these attributes highlight Turkey's dual significance as a major industrial producer of gypsum and phosphogypsum and a critical center for scientific research and engineering development in these sectors.</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791734744</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
