<node id="Phosphates">
  <data key="d0">Phosphates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphates are chemical compounds that manifest in two distinct contexts: industrial byproducts and natural soil minerals. In industrial settings, particularly within phosphogypsum waste and solids, phosphates function as primary potentially toxic and harmful impurities distributed throughout the crystals, associated water, and residual phases. Their concentration and variation are heavily dependent on the specific wet production process employed. During phosphogypsum purification, phosphates are systematically targeted for processing and separation; however, they frequently adhere to gypsum crystals, substitute directly into the crystal lattice, and dominate the undissolved particles in leaching residue. Their chemical behavior is complex, as their dissolution is significantly hindered by surrounding gypsum coatings, though their solubility has been explicitly determined in scientific studies. Under certain conditions, these phosphogypsum-bound phosphates undergo microbial reduction to generate phosphine gas. Additionally, they form complexes with metallic elements such as potassium and sodium, and can be effectively immobilized at rates exceeding 90% through the addition of calcium carbonate.

In a separate natural context, phosphates refer to specific green minerals embedded within soil profiles. These naturally occurring phosphate minerals are notably composed of magnesium and potassium, representing a stable, metal-rich mineral component. This dual characterization highlights the entity's varied presence across both industrial waste management and natural geological environments, where it manifests either as a chemically reactive impurity or as a stable geological mineral.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphates are residual chemical compounds found as impurities in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphates are undesirable contaminants in phosphogypsum that are adsorbed, incorporated, and transferred to the solution during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphates are undesirable contaminants adsorbed in phosphogypsum that must be removed for industrial use.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineral classes highlighted by some authors as potential main rare earth element-bearing minerals in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphates are chemical compounds that thorium can form insoluble complexes with, reducing its mobility in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
