<node id="Distilled Water">
  <data key="d0">Distilled Water</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Distilled Water is a highly purified form of water that functions primarily as a solvent and washing agent across various chemical and material processing applications. Its high purity makes it ideal for precise laboratory procedures, such as solubility determinations, where contamination must be strictly avoided. In synthesis and material preparation workflows, it serves as the primary medium for dissolving chemical precursors, including calcium nitrate tetrahydrate in specialized routes like the NoSurf synthesis method. Additionally, in certain chemical reactions, Distilled Water is not only introduced as a reagent but also generated as a byproduct.

Beyond its role as a solvent, Distilled Water is extensively utilized as a washing agent to purify solid materials and remove unwanted components. It is employed to rinse residual solid phases, beneficiated solids, synthesized powders, specific substrates like BS material, and chicken eggshells. The washing process, which sometimes involves incorporating it into boiling mixtures, effectively eliminates undesirable phases, water-soluble impurities, and specific compounds such as nardite. By thoroughly rinsing these materials, Distilled Water facilitates the isolation of clean, washed solids, ensuring the removal of residual contaminants and enhancing the overall purity of the final products.&lt;SEP&gt;Distilled water is used as the solvent for all chemical syntheses and washing procedures in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Distilled water is used as the solvent for all hydrothermal syntheses in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Distilled water is a purified solvent used by Haridasan for leaching radium-226 from phosphogypsum waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Distilled water is used as a solvent and washing agent to remove impurities during the synthesis and purification of HAp.&lt;SEP&gt;Distilled water is used to wash Phosphogypsum powders to remove dirt and impurities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791967640</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Phosphate Ion is a negatively charged polyatomic anion that plays a significant role in various chemical and materials science applications. It is typically represented by the formula PO4 3-, though some descriptions note it as PO²⁻. This ion is generated during the hydrolysis of pyrophosphate ions and can manifest as two orthophosphate ions in reaction products. In chemical environments, it readily reacts with calcium ions to form insoluble calcium phosphate. Additionally, it participates in surface reactions on cement particles, where it actively influences retardation mechanisms. During the precipitation of hydroxyapatite crystals, the ions are known to adsorb onto CTAB monomers.

Structurally, the Phosphate Ion serves as a fundamental sub-lattice component within hydroxyapatite and the broader apatite crystal lattice, where it is subject to anionic substitution. Its presence in these crystalline frameworks dictates specific vibrational characteristics, with translational modes detectable in Raman spectroscopy within the 200–350 cm⁻¹ range. Together, these properties underscore the ion’s importance in driving precipitation reactions, modifying material surfaces, and defining the structural and spectroscopic profiles of phosphate-based systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The phosphate ion is a product formed from the hydrolysis of the triphosphate ion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791984470</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
