<node id="Sulphuric Acid">
  <data key="d0">Sulphuric Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulphuric acid is a key industrial chemical and versatile reagent primarily utilized in phosphate processing and environmental management. It serves as the fundamental chemical agent in the wet process, where it attacks and acidifies both phosphate rocks and phosphate concentrates to synthesize phosphoric acid. This large-scale industrial application inherently generates phosphogypsum as a major byproduct. The chemical is also recognized as a central component of the sulphuric process, underscoring its foundational role in modern phosphate manufacturing and related industrial workflows.

Beyond primary acid production, sulphuric acid is employed to acidify rock phosphate for the synthesis of various fluoride and phosphate compounds. It also features in alternative treatment methodologies for phosphogypsum, where it reacts with the material to form bassanite and effectively reduces residual phosphate and fluoride concentrations. In analytical and regulatory contexts, the compound functions as a standard reagent within the SW-846 testing procedure. Furthermore, specific industrial records document its generation through the PSC process, with tracked production quantities reaching 408.00 kg. Collectively, these applications highlight sulphuric acid's critical function across extraction, byproduct remediation, and standardized testing protocols.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791914247</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium Citrate">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Citrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium citrate is a water-soluble compound formed when fluoride impurities react with aqueous citric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium citrate is a compound believed to perform the same role as citric acid by suppressing HAp nucleation and growth under hydrothermal conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium citrate is an additive used in hydrothermal synthesis to create hierarchical morphology with porosity.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium citrate is used as an additive in hydrothermal synthesis to produce HAP with hierarchical morphology.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium citrate is a water-soluble compound formed when citric acid converts phosphate impurities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720679</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrofluoric Acid">
  <data key="d0">Hydrofluoric Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrofluoric acid is a highly reactive, water-soluble aqueous solution that functions as a significant chemical byproduct and industrial reagent. It is primarily generated during the wet process of phosphoric acid manufacturing, where hydrogen fluoride reacts with silicate impurities present in phosphate rock. The compound can also be produced through the reaction of fluorides with aqueous citric acid. During these formation processes, hydrofluoric acid frequently interacts with silica to yield hexafluorosilicic acid, establishing its role as a reactive chemical product within various reaction pathways.

In industrial applications, hydrofluoric acid is extensively utilized as a chemical reagent to remove quartz phases and convert specific components within phosphogypsum during processing. It also persists as a residual acid within phosphogypsum, directly contributing to the material's highly corrosive and acidic properties. Beyond its involvement in phosphate and gypsum processing, hydrofluoric acid is widely recognized for its practical use as an etching agent for glass and silicon-based materials, leveraging its strong chemical reactivity for precision manufacturing and material treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrofluoric acid is a harmful free acid present in phosphogypsum that reacts with quicklime to form inert salts.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrofluoric acid is one of the initial chemicals listed alongside other acids used in phosphogypsum processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrofluoric acid is a soluble impurity removed from phosphogypsum through chemical reactions and subsequent washing.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrofluoric acid is a gaseous byproduct released during the reaction, derived from fluorine in apatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrofluoric acid is a volatile byproduct released from the fluorine in apatite during the reaction.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrofluoric acid is a water-soluble compound formed when citric acid converts fluoride impurities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015</data>
