<node id="Sodium Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium fluoride (NaF) is a water-soluble fluoride compound formed by the combination of fluorine with sodium cations. It is frequently detected in various studied samples and exhibits distinct chemical and physical behaviors depending on environmental conditions. In aqueous environments, it demonstrates maximum solubility in citric acid, while at elevated temperatures, it transitions into a gas phase compound that begins to appear between 1200 °C and 1300 °C and increases in concentration as temperatures rise.

The compound serves multiple industrial and chemical applications. It is utilized as a chemical additive to influence the synthesis of hydroxyapatite, particularly through the gel method, and functions as a key component in fluoride solutions designed to convert phosphogypsum. However, its presence in cemented paste fill materials has notable structural impacts. Increased concentrations of sodium fluoride lead to higher porosity, coarsened crystal structures, and a significant deterioration of the filling body's pore architecture, ultimately resulting in decreased mechanical strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719139</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Purification">
  <data key="d0">Purification</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Purification is a process that removes impurities from phosphogypsum using aqueous citric acid to form water-soluble compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Purification refers to improved processes and novel methods for cleaning phosphogypsum to render it suitable for various applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Purification is a processing step to isolate rare earth elements after extraction from phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A downstream processing operation required for s-HAp particles due to their irregular morphology and impurities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791559146</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fig. 1">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 1 functions as a generic figure label across multiple independent studies, with each instance representing a distinct entity or dataset rather than a single unified concept. This recurring placeholder is adapted to diverse experimental frameworks spanning phosphate chemistry, materials science, environmental engineering, agricultural research, and geographic analysis. In phosphate chemistry, one instance illustrates the solubility of phosphates in aqueous citric acid at 30°C, while another serves as an ArcGIS-generated geographical map highlighting the locations and production or recycling volumes of representative companies.

Within phosphogypsum research, multiple distinct figures labeled Fig. 1 address different parameters. These include visualizations of rare earth element distribution in phosphogypsum, system boundaries for various disposal methods, and graphical representations of fractional and cumulative grain size distributions. Additional instances present the thermogravimetric and derived curves for JorfLasfar phosphogypsum, alongside X-ray diffraction spectra of phosphogypsum samples.

In materials science, Fig. 1 is frequently utilized to characterize hydroxyapatite (HAP/HAp). One depiction shows the structural framework of hydroxyapatite, sourced from Brunton et al., 2013. Other instances display scanning electron microscopy images of HAP samples subjected to various hydrothermal temperatures and thermal treatments. Furthermore, the label is applied to X-ray diffraction patterns comparing mesoporous HAp particles with standard HAp, as well as X-ray diffraction patterns of HAp powders synthesized at different temperatures.

Environmental and agricultural literature also employs the label for distinct analytical and preparatory diagrams. These include a schematic tracking temperature and water content fluctuations in compost piles during biofertilizer preparation, and a diagram outlining a radioanalytical procedure for uranium separation and radon measurement. Additional applications feature visual representations of prepared cubic and prismatic adobe samples, as well as figures displaying the microscopic morphologies of experimental raw materials. Collectively, these instances confirm that Fig. 1 serves as a context-dependent identifier tailored to specific experimental frameworks rather than a standardized scientific figure.&lt;SEP&gt;A visual diagram illustrating the experimental procedure for hydrothermal HAp synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Visual representation illustrating material flows and system boundaries.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 1 illustrates material flows and system boundaries for the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 1 provides a schematic illustration of the preparation process for nanorod hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 1 displays XRD patterns and FT-IR spectra of samples synthesized at different hydrothermal times.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791984880</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fig. 2">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 2</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 2 functions as a standard, recurring figure label applied across numerous independent scientific publications rather than representing a single unified entity. In each instance, it denotes a distinct visualization tailored to the specific research context, serving as a conventional labeling convention for diverse datasets, comparative analyses, and distribution metrics across multiple independent research domains.

In materials science and chemical characterization, Fig. 2 is frequently employed to display the solubility of fluorides in aqueous citric acid at 30°C, X-ray diffraction patterns of samples collected under varying configurations or reaction times, and the crystallographic peaks of synthesized hydroxyapatite. It also serves as a composite figure integrating Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, X-ray diffraction, and Brunauer–Emmett–Teller analysis results, alongside graphical representations of adsorption-desorption isotherms for hydroxyapatite and 20HPA-hydroxyapatite composites. Additionally, it documents the morphological structures of hydroxyapatite samples synthesized with varying CTAB concentrations, presents FESEM and TEM micrographs with schematic illustrations of sample morphologies and formation processes, and illustrates the morphological evolution of porous HA microspheres and microspheres at various hydrothermal times as characterized by FE-SEM.

Within environmental and resource management research, particularly concerning phosphogypsum, Fig. 2 illustrates a wide array of analytical and assessment data. These include thermogravimetric analysis under both open-system and nitrogen atmosphere conditions, infrared spectra of phosphogypsum derived from Sebaiya ore, and alpha-spectra of uranium isotopes measured in phosphogypsum waste. The label is also utilized for practical and distributional analyses, such as demonstrating the use of phosphogypsum in secondary road construction, presenting particle size curves and stress-strain examination results for raw phosphogypsum, and showing the evolution of grading curves under different stress levels. Furthermore, it supports comparative and distributional assessments, including Life Cycle Assessment results evaluating various disposal methods across environmental impact units, graphical representations of publication trends, geographical distribution, and primary research topics, as well as visualizations of phosphogypsum recycling distribution across different applications in surveyed regions.

In agricultural and soil science contexts, Fig. 2 has been utilized to compare the physical and chemical properties of soil amended with biofertilizer against unamended control soil. Collectively, these varied applications confirm that Fig. 2 operates as a ubiquitous, context-dependent figure designation rather than a fixed scientific object, consistently adapted to present domain-specific visualizations, analytical results, and comparative metrics across independent studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 2 displays the X-ray diffraction pattern of the phosphogypsum raw samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-006</data>
