<node id="Natural Selenite Gypsum">
  <data key="d0">Natural Selenite Gypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Natural selenite gypsum is a reference material compared against phosphogypsum to demonstrate the greater property reduction caused by the latter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790961592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Thermal Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Differential Thermal Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Differential Thermal Analysis is an analytical testing method and technique designed to measure heat flow and temperature variations within a sample to identify endothermic and exothermic thermal events. By detecting these characteristic peaks, the technique determines transformation temperatures and clarifies the nature of thermal changes, including phase transitions and structural transformations. It is frequently utilized as a coupled analytical approach to monitor these dynamic processes.

In practical applications, Differential Thermal Analysis is specifically employed to evaluate the material properties of phosphogypsum, both in its untreated state and following various treatments. The method is used to detect and clarify thermal transformations and phase changes within phosphogypsum samples. Furthermore, it serves to confirm the successful removal of impurities by measuring shifts in the inversion temperatures of exothermic peaks, thereby providing critical insights into the sample's thermal behavior and compositional purity.&lt;SEP&gt;Differential thermal analysis is a testing method used to compare the thermal properties of treated and untreated phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791773399</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DA">
  <data key="d0">DA</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific phosphogypsum sample code characterized by higher impurity levels compared to HDS.&lt;SEP&gt;DA is a specific design or sample of phosphogypsum used in the study, requiring 4.0% citric acid for optimal purification.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790961592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HDS">
  <data key="d0">HDS</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific phosphogypsum sample code characterized by lower impurity levels compared to DA.&lt;SEP&gt;HDS is a specific design or sample of phosphogypsum used in the study, requiring 3.0% citric acid for optimal purification.&lt;SEP&gt;HDS is a gypsum plaster design formulation evaluated in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790961592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exotherms">
  <data key="d0">Exotherms</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Exotherms are thermal events measured by differential thermal analysis to indicate phase changes in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790961592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inversion Temperature">
  <data key="d0">Inversion Temperature</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Inversion temperature is a thermal metric measured by differential thermal analysis, shifting from 390–450°C to 370–390°C after purification.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790961592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DA Design">
  <data key="d0">DA Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">DA Design is a specific Phosphogypsum processing configuration tested against varying acid concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790961592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HDS Design">
  <data key="d0">HDS Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">HDS Design is a specific Phosphogypsum processing configuration tested against varying acid concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790961592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fluoride Ion">
  <data key="d0">Fluoride Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluoride Ion is a negatively charged chemical species that reacts with calcium to form precipitates.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride ion is a chemical component often found in phosphogypsum waste, requiring immobilization to prevent environmental contamination.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride Ion is an anion capable of replacing hydroxyl ions in the apatite crystal structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride ions react with cement particles to form protective coats that influence setting time.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride ion (F⁻) is an ion that can replace hydroxyl ions in C-S-H.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791723719</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Precipitation Reaction">
  <data key="d0">Precipitation Reaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Precipitation Reaction describes the chemical process where soluble ions combine to form an insoluble solid.&lt;SEP&gt;Precipitation reaction is a process step combining adjusted pH and ammonium hydrogen phosphate to form a translucent colloid.&lt;SEP&gt;Precipitation reaction is a chemical process where aqueous ions combine to form an insoluble solid.&lt;SEP&gt;Precipitation reaction is a classification for the chemical process where an acid reacts with a base to form a solid product.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791749368</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrolysis Reaction">
  <data key="d0">Hydrolysis Reaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrolysis Reaction describes the chemical breakdown of polyphosphates through reaction with hydroxide ions.&lt;SEP&gt;The hydrolysis reaction describes the chemical breakdown of triphosphate into phosphate and water using hydroxide ions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791984470</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Fertilizer Production">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Fertilizer Production</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate Fertilizer Production is a specialized industrial process dedicated to manufacturing agricultural fertilizers derived from phosphate rock. Central to this operation is the chemical treatment of phosphate minerals, typically through acidulation, which facilitates the extraction of phosphoric acid for fertilizer formulation. A defining characteristic of this manufacturing activity is the inherent and consistent generation of phosphogypsum (PG), a gypsum-rich solid residue that serves as a major chemical byproduct and waste material throughout the production lifecycle.

The management and disposal of phosphogypsum represent critical operational and environmental considerations, directly tied to the scale and methodology of phosphate fertilizer manufacturing. Additionally, Phosphate Fertilizer Production has been identified as a primary driver behind the significant increase in phosphogypsum generation observed in China.&lt;SEP&gt;An industrial process that generates phosphogypsum as a byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate fertilizer production is the industrial process that generates phosphogypsum as a byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;An industrial procedure that generates phosphogypsum as a byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;An industrial process that generates phosphogypsum as a byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;The industrial process responsible for generating phosphogypsum as a byproduct.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
