<node id="Radioactive Elements">
  <data key="d0">Radioactive Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radioactive Elements are naturally occurring trace impurities found within phosphogypsum and its surrounding environment. Prominent examples include uranium, thorium, radium, radium-226 (Ra-226), lead-210 (Pb-210), and polonium-210 (Po-210). Their concentrations vary geographically, with notably lower levels observed in Chinese phosphate mines and phosphogypsum sources, which establishes more favorable conditions for the safe industrial utilization of this byproduct.

These elements function as persistent and hazardous contaminants that pose significant threats to ecological stability and human health. When released from phosphogypsum, they readily contaminate soil and groundwater, disrupt plant growth, and cause widespread environmental pollution. Human exposure occurs through inhalation, oral ingestion, or direct skin contact, allowing the radioactive materials to be absorbed into the body and result in substantial physiological damage.

Due to their persistent hazards, radioactive elements necessitate rigorous environmental monitoring, systematic removal, fixation, and harmless treatment protocols. Mitigation strategies, such as citric acid treatment, can effectively reduce their concentrations. However, their presence continues to severely hinder the broader industrial utilization and market recognition of phosphogypsum, as stringent safety standards and regulatory concerns limit its commercial application in many regions.&lt;SEP&gt;Trace radioactive elements present in phosphogypsum that pose potential environmental risks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791936400</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranium-238">
  <data key="d0">Uranium-238</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranium-238 is a naturally occurring radioactive isotope of uranium and the foundational parent nuclide of the U-238 decay series. It is naturally abundant in sedimentary phosphates and phosphate rocks, where it is retained during phosphoric acid production and ultimately accumulates as a primary radionuclide in phosphogypsum waste. In undisturbed geological environments, Uranium-238 maintains radioactive equilibrium with its decay products, particularly Radium-226 and Lead-210; however, this equilibrium is frequently disrupted in phosphogypsum and related matrices. Consequently, it serves as a baseline radionuclide for isotopic ratio analysis alongside Uranium-234 and Uranium-235, with its activity often calculated using conversion factors derived from Uranium-235 (typically multiplied by 21.73). Activity ratios relative to Radium-226 and Lead-210 generally remain below unity but can reach unity under specific measurement or environmental conditions. The continuous decay of Uranium-238 generates Lead-210, a common contaminant in phosphogypsum leachate, as well as radium.

Chemically, Uranium-238 exhibits high mobility in the U(VI) oxidation state, allowing it to dissolve and migrate into surrounding water bodies, particularly under saline conditions. Following long-term phosphogypsum amendments, the isotope accumulates in soils, posing ecological and health risks due to its persistence and potential to accumulate in human tissues through multiple exposure pathways. Its concentration in phosphogypsum varies significantly by geographic origin and physical fraction, with measured levels ranging from approximately 9.0 Bq/kg in coarse fractions to 164 ± 12.5 Bq/kg in bulk samples. Due to these risks, Uranium-238 requires removal from phosphogypsum to meet safety standards and is routinely tracked during leaching procedures to evaluate contaminant extraction.

The extraction and stripping efficiency of Uranium-238 from phosphogypsum varies considerably depending on the sample type and process parameters. Developed leaching and stripping procedures have successfully removed the isotope, with reported extraction efficiencies reaching 83.4% and 80.3% for PG-Se and PG-AT samples, respectively. However, in other experimental contexts, Uranium-238 consistently demonstrates the lowest extraction efficiency among target radionuclides, typically ranging from 45% to 60%. Stripping efficiency generally rises steadily over time, plateauing at approximately 49%, while overall removal efficiency increases up to two hours of agitation before declining. Continuous monitoring of Uranium-238’s concentration, mobility, and decay products remains essential for effective phosphogypsum management, environmental assessment, and public health protection.&lt;SEP&gt;Uranium-238 is a specific radioactive isotope monitored within the phosphogypsum analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive isotope measured at 143 Bq in high population contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Uranium-238 is a radioactive isotope measured with a specific activity level in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Uranium-238 is a radioactive emission measured in the LCI data for PG disposal.&lt;SEP&gt;Uranium-238 is a radioactive isotope measured in becquerels within the inventory.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791877169</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radium-226">
  <data key="d0">Radium-226</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radium-226 is a naturally occurring radioactive isotope and the most abundant form of radium, serving as a key member of the uranium-238 decay series generated through the alpha decay of thorium-230. Chemically analogous to calcium, it readily integrates into environmental and biological systems, migrating through groundwater and accumulating in soils, particularly following phosphogypsum applications. Under stable geological conditions, it typically exists in secular equilibrium with uranium-238 and lead-210. Primarily sourced from sedimentary phosphate rocks and uranium mill waste, Radium-226 is notably concentrated in Florida phosphate materials and global geological formations. It functions as the principal radionuclide in phosphogypsum, where it coprecipitates with calcium sulfate and barium, retaining approximately 80–90% within the gypsum phase through surface adsorption and carbonation binding. Its concentration varies significantly across regions and samples, typically averaging around 22 Bq/kg but reaching up to 482 ± 13.2 Bq/kg in specific instances. Activity levels frequently match or exceed uranium-238, often surpassing concentrations in fly ash or natural soil and occasionally violating regulatory standards. Furthermore, Radium-226 exhibits variable enrichment across phosphogypsum particle sizes, with its uranium-238 activity ratio strongly correlating with average grain diameter.

Due to its environmental mobility, Radium-226 readily leaches into water and soil, posing significant public health and ecological risks. Consequently, it is rigorously monitored across drinking water, soil, crops, aquatic systems, and stream sediments, with leaching rates estimated based on established research. Specific activity concentrations are critical parameters in radiation safety assessments, directly influencing total absorbed dose rates and gamma exposure. The isotope is quantified in various environmental and demographic contexts, including high population areas (e.g., 2106 Bq), groundwater (e.g., 41.7 kBq), low population zones, and environmental inventories, with measurements recorded in becquerels. As a gamma emitter with a characteristic energy of 186.2 keV, it is analyzed via gamma spectrometry, often measuring its emission line alongside those of decay products such as radon-222, lead-214, and bismuth-214. Radium-226 also serves as a standard reference material for efficiency determination and is utilized to study fractionation phenomena within the uranium decay chain, though modeling its dissolution, transport, and speciation remains challenging due to incomplete thermodynamic data. Additionally, it is tracked as a radioactive emission in Life Cycle Inventory (LCI) data for phosphogypsum disposal.

In remediation and waste management applications, Radium-226 is frequently targeted for removal from phosphogypsum to comply with safety standards. It is routinely analyzed before and after leaching processes, where removal and extraction efficiencies depend heavily on the methodology and sample composition. Studies report peak stripping efficiencies of approximately 51% at 12 minutes and peak extraction rates of 60% after 2 hours, with specific phosphogypsum samples achieving efficiencies as high as 92.1% and 90.7%. Generally, removal efficiency increases with agitation time up to approximately 2 hours before slightly declining. Notably, Radium-226 consistently demonstrates the highest extraction efficiency among tested species, underscoring its prominence in phosphogypsum waste treatment protocols and radiological safety frameworks.&lt;SEP&gt;Radium-226 is a radionuclide that is primarily transferred into phosphogypsum during phosphoric acid production.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-01