<node id="Wet Process">
  <data key="d0">Wet Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Wet Process is the primary, mainstream, and most prevalent global industrial method for the commercial production of phosphoric acid and phosphate fertilizers. Functioning as a foundational chemical and hydrometallurgical technique, it is widely recognized as an energy-saving and cost-effective procedure. The core mechanism involves the acidulation, digestion, or dissolution of phosphate rock—specifically fluorapatite or apatite ore—using concentrated sulfuric acid at moderate temperatures. This reaction efficiently extracts phosphoric acid while simultaneously partitioning various impurities from the raw material into the final acid product and solid byproducts based on their solubility characteristics.

The process encompasses several distinct operational variants, including hemihydrate, dihydrate, and di-hemihydrate configurations. A major solid byproduct of the reaction is phosphogypsum, whose specific chemical composition is directly dictated by the chosen process type and operational parameters. Beyond the primary extraction, the Wet Process drives several secondary chemical transformations. During acidulation, hydrofluoric acid is generated and subsequently reacts with silica present in the ore to form hexafluorosilicic acid. Furthermore, the technique serves as the simplest and most economical hydrometallurgical method for converting phosphogypsum (PG) into valuable industrial products.

From an environmental and material science perspective, the Wet Process significantly impacts the distribution of trace elements and radionuclides. As a large-scale industrial treatment of phosphate rock, it actively redistributes and fractionates uranium-series radionuclides throughout the entire material stream. This fractionation directly influences both the purity of the extracted phosphoric acid and the radiological profile of the resulting waste products, making the management of these byproducts a critical consideration in modern chemical plant operations.&lt;SEP&gt;The wet process is an industrial method for producing phosphoric acid that generates phosphogypsum waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The wet process is a method of phosphoric acid production where silicon dioxide from calcium phosphate rock enters phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
