<node id="Boric Acid">
  <data key="d0">Boric Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Boric acid is a weak acid studied as a leaching agent for extracting rare earth elements from phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Boric Acid is a weak organic acid evaluated for rare earth element leaching from phosphogypsum, showing lower efficiency compared to citric acid.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791427759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Malic Acid">
  <data key="d0">Malic Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Malic acid is a weak acid investigated for its potential to leach rare earth elements from phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Malic Acid is a weak organic acid tested for rare earth element leaching from phosphogypsum, demonstrating lower efficiency than citric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Malic Acid is an organic acid employed in bioleaching processes to reduce leaching pH.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791427277</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="WBS">
  <data key="d0">WBS</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">WBS, which stands for Washed Beneficiated Solid, is a processed residual solid matrix derived from phosphogypsum (PG) following solubilization procedures. Initially functioning as a raw material sample, it undergoes a treatment process where quartz and other mineral phases are removed. The material is subjected to a permutations protocol for residual gangue removal, and soluble sodium sulfates are systematically eliminated. Chemically, WBS primarily consists of calcium fluoride and quartz, forming a distinct solid matrix that acts as a washed beneficiated concentrate with an increased concentration of rare earth elements (REEs). These REEs are specifically enriched through coprecipitation with phosphate and fluorine.

As a specialized sample type, WBS is routinely analyzed to determine the precise concentrations of REEs and various main metal elements. The material represents a targeted byproduct or solution phase where valuable components are concentrated, making it a critical subject for elemental assessment and recovery optimization. Its comprehensive processing ensures that gangue materials are minimized while preserving the enriched metallic and rare earth fractions for further evaluation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983599</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marine Environment">
  <data key="d0">Marine Environment</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The marine environment is an alternative disposal site for untreated phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;The aquatic ecosystem impacted by phosphogypsum dumping, suffering from lowered pH, heavy metal pollution, and disrupted biological growth.&lt;SEP&gt;The Marine Environment is an ecological system impacted by the discharge of phosphogypsum and its toxic contaminants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791591172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dehydration">
  <data key="d0">Dehydration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dehydration is a process applied to phosphogypsum that releases rare earth elements locked in its structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Dehydration is the primary physico-chemical transformation of phosphogypsum characterized by water loss and mass reduction during thermal treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Dehydration is a thermal transformation process involving the elimination of hydration water from the sample.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical process involving the removal of water from phosphogypsum, occurring in distinct temperature intervals.&lt;SEP&gt;A thermal event involving the loss of water molecules, identified as a key process in the material's decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;A process indicated alongside thermal decomposition as temperatures approach 900°C.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792003010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="R1">
  <data key="d0">R1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">R1 denotes the chemical reaction equation describing the decomposition of phosphate rock during the wet process.&lt;SEP&gt;R1 is a chemical reaction describing the formation of CaSO4 complex salts.&lt;SEP&gt;R1 is a reaction step involved in the formation and transformation of CaSO4 complex salts during PG impregnation.&lt;SEP&gt;R1 is a chemical reaction equation representing the dehydration of dihydrated calcium sulfate to hemihydrate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791587027</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EDTA">
  <data key="d0">EDTA</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid, commonly referred to as EDTA, is a carboxylate chemical compound that functions primarily as a pH-dependent chelating agent. Its defining characteristic is the ability to bind metal ions to assess metal complexation, notably forming the [EDTA-Pr]⁻ complex with praseodymium. In analytical chemistry, EDTA is the standard primary titrant for complexometric titrations, utilized to accurately determine metal ion concentrations and metal oxide content. Standardized protocols frequently reference its application, including specific procedures outlined in Section 16, often employing measured volumes ranging from 10.00 mL to 15.00 mL. Additionally, EDTA is routinely analyzed via mass spectrometry alongside calcium sulfate dihydrate to support precise analytical measurements.

Beyond laboratory titrations, EDTA serves critical roles in industrial processing and environmental assessment. It is deployed in both alkaline and acidic treatments to solubilize gypsum compounds and facilitate their elimination, while simultaneously concentrating rare earth elements. In environmental science, EDTA is applied in leaching experiments to evaluate the environmental availability of metals and radionuclides within phosphogypsum-amended soils. Its versatility extends to materials research, where it has been utilized as a comparative agent in hydrothermal deposition studies of HAP on metal substrates. Collectively, these applications establish EDTA as a multifunctional chemical reagent bridging analytical measurement, industrial extraction, and environmental monitoring.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792067577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
