<node id="Aluminum Oxide">
  <data key="d0">Aluminum Oxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Aluminum Oxide is a chemical and mineral compound utilized across analytical, industrial, and materials science domains. It serves as a standard material for calibrating aluminum measurements, and its mass ratio contributes to broader oxide calculations and pH value determination. One referenced source cites a molar mass of 50.98 g/mol, though this figure diverges from the established molecular weight of Al2O3 and should be interpreted with noted uncertainty.

In geological and waste processing contexts, Aluminum Oxide is a common oxide component found in gypsum materials, with notably higher concentrations in natural gypsum compared to processed variants. Within phosphogypsum, it appears as an impurity alongside transition metals in the initial mixture and relevant phase diagrams. Reported concentrations vary across analytical records: compositional tables typically quantify it between 1219.37 and 1312.60 ppm, while other analyses indicate extremely low levels peaking at 0.81% in Yunnan samples or 0.08 wt% in the top section of a composition table, with one entry flagged as a potential typographical error for Al2O3. During phosphogypsum purification, the compound demonstrates a high removal efficiency of 93.22%. It is also a primary metal compound measured in WBS and TWBS samples, with concentrations notably increasing in beneficiated concentrates alongside rare earth elements.

Beyond its presence in mineral waste streams, Aluminum Oxide holds significant industrial utility. It functions as a conventional catalyst support, having been specifically evaluated against hydroxyapatite for palladium-supported oxidation reactions. In metallurgical applications, it comprises 9 to 17 wt% of slag wool and acts as an additive to adjust quaternary acidity coefficients within slag systems. The compound readily combines with calcium oxide and silicon dioxide to form eutectic mixtures, contributing to mixture formation while frequently persisting as unmelted particles during processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-015</data>
