<node id="Rare Earth Elements (REEs)">
  <data key="d0">Rare Earth Elements (REEs)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A group of chemical elements whose concentrations in samples PG1 and PG2 are quantified in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare earth elements are a group of metals including yttrium, lanthanum, cerium, and neodymium that are dispersed on phosphogypsum surfaces and concentrate during processing.&lt;SEP&gt;A group of chemical elements that occur in nature and are the primary focus of the study regarding their occurrence, speciation, and recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare earth elements are valuable trace elements concentrated in phosphogypsum, particularly from igneous phosphate rocks.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare Earth Elements (REEs) are a group of elements found in phosphogypsum, with varying binding strengths and mobility.&lt;SEP&gt;Critical elements targeted for extraction from phosphogypsum during treatment and purification processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791593333</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Na4EDTA">
  <data key="d0">Na4EDTA</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Na4EDTA is a chemical agent used to solubilize the majority of phosphogypsum mass under alkaline conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Solubilization">
  <data key="d0">Solubilization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Solubilization refers to the chemical process of dissolving quartz from the washed solid using hydrofluoric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;The chemical process by which compounds like CSD and SiO2 dissolve into solutions, influenced by pH and concentration.&lt;SEP&gt;Solubilization is a chemical process used to dissolve specific phases of phosphogypsum and WBS to enrich rare earth element concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrofluoric Acid (HF)">
  <data key="d0">Hydrofluoric Acid (HF)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A chemical reagent used to solubilize quartz from WBS in the hydrometallurgical treatment approach.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical agent used to dissolve SiO2 through a heterogeneous reaction mechanism.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrofluoric acid is a chemical agent used to solubilize WBS, removing silicon dioxide and transforming metal compounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrofluoric acid is a chemical byproduct of the acidulation process that remains in PG due to industrial separation limitations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791023270</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lattice Bound REEs">
  <data key="d0">Lattice Bound REEs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lattice bound REEs represent a mode of rare earth element occurrence where metals are structurally incorporated within gypsum crystal lattices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isomorphism Substitution">
  <data key="d0">Isomorphism Substitution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Isomorphism substitution represents a mode of rare earth element occurrence where metals replace other ions within a mineral structure.&lt;SEP&gt;A process where REEs are incorporated into the calcium sulfate lattice via heterovalent replacement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="XRD Spectrum">
  <data key="d0">XRD Spectrum</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">XRD spectrum is an analytical data representation used to verify the consistency of chemical composition across different matrix samples.&lt;SEP&gt;XRD Spectrum is a diffraction pattern showing characteristic peaks of the crystalline hydroxyapatite form used to calculate crystallite size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791749076</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yttrium (Y)">
  <data key="d0">Yttrium (Y)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Yttrium is a major rare earth element homogeneously dispersed on the surface of phosphogypsum and other concentrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Yttrium is an element found in fluorapatite and phosphogypsum, often complexed by hard ligands like fluoride.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791427277</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lanthanum (La)">
  <data key="d0">Lanthanum (La)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Lanthanum is a major rare earth element homogeneously dispersed on the surface of phosphogypsum and other concentrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Lanthanum (La) is a light rare earth element initially present in phosphate rock.&lt;SEP&gt;Lanthanum is a chemical element represented by a red line that shows the lowest stripping efficiency among the three elements studied.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791813824</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cerium (Ce)">
  <data key="d0">Cerium (Ce)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cerium is a major rare earth element homogeneously dispersed on the surface of phosphogypsum and other concentrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerium is a REE present in phosphogypsum, which undergoes oxidation state changes and forms solid solutions during crystallization.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerium is a chemical element represented by a blue line that exhibits intermediate stripping efficiency compared to other elements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791813824</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Neodymium (Nd)">
  <data key="d0">Neodymium (Nd)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Neodymium is a major rare earth element homogeneously dispersed on the surface of phosphogypsum and other concentrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Neodymium is a chemical element represented by a magenta/pink line that consistently demonstrates the highest stripping efficiency across all phase ratios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791813824</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CWS">
  <data key="d0">CWS</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">CWS is a beneficiated concentrate material with a chemical composition similar to WBS but with slightly increased element concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium Oxide (CaO)">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Oxide (CaO)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium oxide is a major chemical component in PG, WBS, and TWBS, with concentrations varying due to gypsum solubility and processing steps.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium oxide is a chemical compound whose mean weight percentage in PG is analyzed across different countries.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical phase identified by red triangles in the diffraction graph.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium oxide is a product of PSC technology that is subsequently used for Portland cement production.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium oxide is a chemical product formed from the oxidation of calcium sulfide by unreacted calcium sulfate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792097138</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfur Trioxide (SO3)">
  <data key="d0">Sulfur Trioxide (SO3)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfur trioxide is a major chemical component in PG that decreases in WBS due to the high solubility of gypsum in EDTA.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur trioxide is a chemical compound whose mean weight percentage in PG is analyzed across different countries.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791023386</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Silicon Dioxide (SiO2)">
  <data key="d0">Silicon Dioxide (SiO2)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Silicon dioxide is a major component in WBS and TWBS, primarily originating from quartz which resists dissolution during processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon dioxide is a chemical compound whose mean weight percentage in PG is analyzed across different countries.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical phase identified by black diamonds in the diffraction graph.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791090619</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fluorine (F)">
  <data key="d0">Fluorine (F)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluorine is a chemical component that increases in WBS and TWBS due to the formation of stable metal fluorides during processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium Fluorosilicates">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Fluorosilicates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Crystallized compounds identified in TWBS, formed from the transformation of solubilized quartz.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium fluorosilicates are compounds identified in TWBS following hydrofluoric acid treatment of WBS.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphorus Pentoxide (P2O5)">
  <data key="d0">Phosphorus Pentoxide (P2O5)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorus pentoxide is a potential REE-bearing compound whose concentration positively correlates with REE enrichment in WBS and CWBS.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Metal Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Metal Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Metal fluoride is a stable compound formed during PG dissolution, contributing to increased fluorine concentrations in residual solids.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Metal Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Metal Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Metal phosphate is a compound formed during the first enrichment process using EDTA, which may transform into metal fluorides in later steps.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gangue">
  <data key="d0">Gangue</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Gangue refers to undesirable mineral phases like SiO2 that are efficiently solubilized during processing to enrich REEs.&lt;SEP&gt;Gangue is a mining waste material investigated for its dynamic adsorption characteristics toward heavy metals in goaf environments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791655882</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rare Earth Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Rare Earth Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Rare earth phosphate is a compound that may transform into rare earth fluoride during hydrofluoric acid treatment, though this process remains uncertain.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare earth phosphate is a dispersed compound identified in the enriched rare earth concentrates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rare Earth Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Rare Earth Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Rare earth fluoride is a potential product of rare earth phosphate transformation during hydrofluoric acid treatment of WBS.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare earth fluoride is a dispersed compound identified in the enriched rare earth concentrates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Independent Rare Earth Minerals">
  <data key="d0">Independent Rare Earth Minerals</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Independent rare earth minerals represent one of the natural occurrence modes of REEs alongside weathered crust deposits and isomorphism substitution.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural occurrence mode of REEs in nature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weathered Crust Elution Deposits">
  <data key="d0">Weathered Crust Elution Deposits</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Weathered crust elution deposits represent a natural occurrence mode of REEs influenced by ionic radii and hydration energy.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural occurrence mode of REEs in nature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ionic Radii">
  <data key="d0">Ionic Radii</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Ionic radii are a key factor determining the distribution and speciation of REEs in PG and other matrices.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical measurements of cation sizes that influence their likelihood of incorporating into hydroxyapatite's crystal structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247463</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ions Interactions">
  <data key="d0">Ions Interactions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ions interactions influence the spatial distribution and chemical behavior of REEs within the PG crystalline structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012</data>
