<node id="Concentration">
  <data key="d0">Concentration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concentration is a multifaceted concept in chemistry and materials processing that serves both as a quantitative metric and an industrial operation. As a measurement parameter, it quantifies the abundance or amount of specific elements, such as rare earth elements and metals, within samples, starting reactants, and final products. This metric is commonly expressed in parts per million or as a mass percentage, and it functions as a critical variable in mathematical formulas, often representing the concentration of titrants or standard solutions. In practical applications, concentration dictates the strength of chemical solutions, such as citric acid, which must be strictly regulated to optimize treatment outcomes.

Beyond its role as a measurement, concentration also denotes a key hydrometallurgical and processing step designed to enhance material purity and formulation properties. In hydrometallurgical contexts, it acts as a pivotal parameter that influences the removal of quartz and the solubilization of various compounds. Industrially, it refers to specific purification stages, such as the process that increases the purity and strength of phosphoric acid following gypsum removal. Additionally, in materials science, concentration defines the mass percentage of slurry within CPGFM formulations. Together, these applications highlight concentration as a fundamental variable for controlling chemical interactions, optimizing processing efficiency, and ensuring precise compositional control across diverse scientific and industrial domains.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantitative measurements of radioactive materials and rare earth elements expressed in Bq/kg and ppm before and after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Concentration refers to the measured amount of isotopes or elements before and after the treatment process, including standard deviations.&lt;SEP&gt;Concentration is a quantitative measure of solute amount per unit volume of solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792069208</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
