<node id="Silicon">
  <data key="d0">Silicon</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Silicon is a chemical element that functions as both a primary impurity and a trace component within phosphogypsum, a material predominantly composed of calcium sulfate dihydrate. Its presence in phosphogypsum is intrinsically tied to silicate mineralogy, as it commonly manifests as silica and associates with quartz, silicates, and feldspars. In geological and industrial settings, silicon is typically liberated from quartz matrices following the rupture of siloxane covalent bonds and tends to accumulate in distinct localized spots or inclusions. The concentration of silicon in phosphogypsum exhibits substantial variability, heavily influenced by sample preparation and geographic origin. Measured mass fractions range widely from 0.56 wt% in dried samples to as high as 31.000% in bulk materials. Geographic comparisons reveal that Brazilian phosphogypsum consistently contains higher silicon concentrations than Moroccan phosphogypsum.

Analytically, silicon is routinely quantified using Atomic Absorption Spectroscopy and detected via EDX and EDS techniques. In EDS spectra, it characteristically appears in the mid-energy region around 1.74 keV, with its detection in various samples, such as HAP, often attributed to quartz impurities. Industrially, silicon acts as an impurity that can negatively impact machinery and product purity; however, it also provides a beneficial functional role when phosphogypsum is repurposed as an oxygen carrier, where it actively enhances anti-agglomeration capabilities and sintering resistance. Beyond industrial applications, silicon is utilized as a fertilizer component specifically studied for its capacity to improve acid paddy soil conditions. Collectively, silicon remains a chemically significant, geographically variable, and functionally relevant element across both industrial and agricultural contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon is a minor chemical element composing less than 1% of the studied phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon is a major chemical species analyzed in the phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A major element recorded to have a slight decrease in concentration in leached samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon appears as distinct, scattered spots or inclusions rather than a continuous matrix.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element detected as peaks at approximately 213, 222, and 223 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon is an element that aggregates at the melting interface, resulting in relatively low calcium content.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
