<node id="Europium">
  <data key="d0">Europium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Europium is a chemical element classified as a rare earth element and is recognized as a critical rare earth element according to the categorization by Seredin. It is frequently encountered in industrial byproducts, particularly phosphogypsum, where it constitutes a considerable proportion in dried forms. Specific analyses of Idaho phosphogypsum have recorded a mean Europium concentration of 1.2 mg/kg, and its concentration and enrichment levels have been systematically measured and recorded in data tables across various sample sets, including WBS and TWBS samples.

Research into Europium has also focused on its chemical behavior and potential recovery. Its location and stability within calcium sulfate matrices have been extensively investigated to optimize extraction processes from phosphogypsum waste. These studies aim to improve the feasibility of recovering Europium from such waste streams, with reported extraction efforts achieving a recovery rate of approximately 50%. Consequently, Europium is actively targeted for recovery due to its critical status and measurable presence in these industrial materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Europium is a Rare Earth Element used as a model to study surface association with phosphate and sulfate ions.&lt;SEP&gt;A rare earth element used as a model to demonstrate that REEs are found on the surface of gypsum crystals as a secondary phase.&lt;SEP&gt;A rare earth element studied for its location and stability in calcium sulfate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-024</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792039862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Terbium">
  <data key="d0">Terbium</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Terbium is a chemical element whose concentration and enrichment were measured in the samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A critical rare earth element categorized by Seredin, found in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Terbium is a rare earth element measured in WBS and TWBS samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Terbium is a critical rare earth element present in a considerable proportion in dried PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Terbium is a rare earth element analyzed in Idaho phosphogypsum with a mean concentration of 1 mg/kg.&lt;SEP&gt;Terbium is a rare earth element found in phosphogypsum phases, notably in fluorite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007</data>
