<node id="Standard Deviation">
  <data key="d0">Standard Deviation</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Standard Deviation (SD) is a fundamental statistical measure used to quantify the variability, dispersion, or uncertainty of a dataset around its mean. It serves as a critical indicator of how individual data points deviate from the central tendency, providing a numerical assessment of data spread and measurement precision.

In experimental and scientific applications, Standard Deviation is frequently employed to represent the dispersion of elemental concentrations in samples, quantify variability in concentration data, and assess the error margin around mean crystallite size measurements. Visually, it is commonly depicted as vertical error bars on scatter plots and other experimental data graphs, offering an immediate graphical representation of measurement variability. The measure is typically denoted by the ± symbol, explicitly communicating the range of uncertainty associated with reported values. By capturing these deviations, Standard Deviation remains an essential tool for interpreting experimental results, evaluating data reliability, and conveying the precision of quantitative analyses.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-019</data>
