<node id="Phosphate rock">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate rock</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate rock is identified as a primary source material for rare earth element recovery via hydrometallurgical processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate rock is the raw material from which phosphogypsum is derived, containing various chemical impurities.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate rock is a mineral resource primarily composed of apatite, used as the raw material for phosphoric acid production.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate rock is the raw material for phosphogypsum production, with China's rock containing low radioactive elements and high Mg, Al, and Si.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate rock is the primary raw material digested to produce phosphoric acid and phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792039593</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U-238">
  <data key="d0">U-238</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranium-238 (U-238) is a naturally occurring radioactive isotope of uranium that accounts for approximately 99.27% of the element's average environmental abundance. As a parent radionuclide, it initiates a prominent decay series by undergoing alpha decay to form Thorium-234 (Th-234). This sequential decay pathway eventually generates Radon-222 (Rn-222) and subsequently Lead-210 (Pb-210), a process that significantly influences uranium distribution across environmental matrices. In radiological analysis, the minimum detectable activity of U-238 is calculated using Currie's formula, and the isotope frequently serves as a reference baseline for evaluating U-234 enrichment levels in groundwater systems.

The radionuclide is extensively quantified across various industrial, geological, and environmental contexts, particularly within phosphate processing and waste management. U-238 is naturally present in phosphate rock and readily migrates into phosphoric acid during wet processing operations. Consequently, it is routinely measured in phosphate rock samples, phosphogypsum waste, and phosphogypsum process water, with its concentration in phosphogypsum exhibiting variability based on specific manufacturing conditions. Monitoring efforts have documented U-238 levels across multiple industrial plants and geographic regions, including Australia and Florida. Environmental assessments also track the isotope in groundwater, noting instances where concentrations beneath the AMAX site exceed natural background levels. Through these diverse applications, U-238 remains a critical radionuclide for both industrial process monitoring and environmental radiological studies.&lt;SEP&gt;U-238 is a radioactive isotope whose decay series is a primary cause of radioactivity in phosphate rock.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426740</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ra-226">
  <data key="d0">Ra-226</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radium-226 (Ra-226) is a naturally occurring radioactive isotope of radium and a direct decay daughter of uranium-238. It is predominantly found in phosphate rock and phosphogypsum waste, where it partitions primarily into the phosphogypsum matrix. During phosphate fertilizer production, wet-process chemical attacks significantly enhance its concentration in these materials. Consequently, Ra-226 serves as the largest contributor to the total radioactivity of phosphogypsum and is routinely measured across multiple geographic locations in waste samples, phosphate rock, process water, and airborne particulate matter.

As a radionuclide, Ra-226 decays to form radon-222, making it the primary source of indoor radon risk associated with phosphogypsum storage and handling. It is also utilized as a reference standard for measuring radon exhalation rates from soil. The environmental mobility and groundwater concentration of Ra-226 are heavily influenced by local geochemical conditions and bacterial activity. Furthermore, the deposition of airborne Ra-226 particulates from phosphogypsum stacks is estimated to increase soil concentrations by less than 0.1% over a 1,000-year period.

In industrial and environmental monitoring, activity levels and concentrations of Ra-226 are systematically reported and analyzed. These measurements are critical for evaluating radioactive equilibrium in phosphate rock and phosphogypsum, tracking radiological dispersion, and assessing long-term environmental and public health impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Ra-226 is a radionuclide present in sedimentary phosphates with notably high concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;Ra-226 is a radionuclide present in phosphogypsum that undergoes alpha-decay and contributes to radiological hazards.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive isotope found in phosphogypsum and soil, studied for its mobility, bioavailability, and accumulation in crops like rice.&lt;SEP&gt;Ra-226 is a radioactive substance present in PG that restricts its utilization in certain regions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791611692</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pb-210">
  <data key="d0">Pb-210</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Pb-210, also referred to as Lead-210, is a naturally occurring radioactive isotope and a long-lived progeny within the uranium-238 decay series. It is specifically generated as a daughter product from the decay of radon-222 and undergoes beta decay with a half-life of approximately 21 years.

In industrial applications, particularly during the wet processing of phosphate rock, Pb-210 primarily partitions into the resulting phosphoric acid stream. As a result, it is consistently present in both phosphate rock and phosphogypsum waste samples, where its concentration and activity levels are routinely quantified. For example, specific measurements have been documented in Florida, recording a value of 1370. Scientists frequently analyze Pb-210 to assess the secular equilibrium status of these materials. Notably, the parent-to-daughter ratio between Pb-210 and radium-226 is typically observed to be less than unity in processed samples, a characteristic that indicates significant redistribution of the isotope during manufacturing operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Pb-210 is a radionuclide found within sedimentary phosphate ores.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426740</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Th-232">
  <data key="d0">Th-232</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Thorium-232 (Th-232) is a naturally occurring radioactive isotope and parent radionuclide of thorium. It forms part of a naturally occurring radionuclide series that is incorporated into the evaluation of the radium equivalent index. Geologically, Th-232 is commonly found in phosphate rock, with notably higher concentrations in igneous deposits, and is also present in phosphogypsum waste. Despite its terrestrial prevalence, the isotope is scarce in marine sediments and is generally less common in groundwater compared to other thorium isotopes such as Th-228 and Th-230.

The radioactive decay of Th-232 produces Radium-228 as part of its decay chain. Due to its environmental and industrial relevance, its activity concentration is frequently measured across various media and locations. Monitoring efforts have recorded Th-232 in phosphogypsum and phosphate rock samples, as well as in groundwater beneath the AMAX site. Specific field measurements have documented the isotope in Australia and Florida, with recorded values of 10 and 3.7, respectively. Additionally, concentration assessments have been conducted at several industrial facilities, including AMAX, USSAC, Plant A, Plant B, and Plant C, highlighting its presence in both natural geological formations and anthropogenic waste streams.&lt;SEP&gt;Th-232 is a radioactive isotope whose decay series contributes significantly to the radioactivity of phosphate rock.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426740</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="K-40">
  <data key="d0">K-40</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">K-40 is a naturally occurring radioactive isotope of potassium considered in radiation hazard index calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive isotope whose average activity concentration was measured in phosphogypsum waste samples.&lt;SEP&gt;K-40 is a naturally occurring radionuclide measured in phosphogypsum waste and phosphate rock samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A naturally occurring radionuclide included in the evaluation of the radium equivalent index.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium-40 is a radioactive isotope measured for its concentration in PG and PR samples.&lt;SEP&gt;K-40 is a radioactive isotope measured in Australia with recorded values of 10 and 20.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189431</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gamma-Ray Measurements">
  <data key="d0">Gamma-Ray Measurements</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Gamma-ray measurements are the analytical technique used to determine the activity concentration of radionuclides in the samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Analytical technique used to determine parent/daughter isotope ratios in phosphogypsum and phosphate rock samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006</data>
