<node id="UNSCEAR">
  <data key="d0">UNSCEAR</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">UNSCEAR, the United Nations Scientific Committee on the Effects of Atomic Radiation, is an international body dedicated to assessing radiological risks and establishing global safety standards. The organization provides essential guidelines and conversion factors for calculating absorbed dose rates, while also establishing recommended worldwide exposure limits for radiation. Additionally, UNSCEAR sets worldwide average activity concentration benchmarks for naturally occurring radionuclides, specifically uranium, thorium, and potassium-40. It has formally established a recommended safe dose level of 55 nGy/h for workers exposed to gamma radiation and is widely cited for determining the annual effective dose equivalent from natural background radiation.

Throughout its operational history, UNSCEAR has published numerous authoritative reports to disseminate its scientific findings. Key publications include a 1977 report on natural radioactivity, alongside comprehensive assessments on the sources and effects of ionizing radiation released in 1993 and 2000. The committee has also specifically documented the distribution of radium in the human body. Due to its standardized metrics and scientific rigor, UNSCEAR’s data and recommendations are frequently referenced in emission standards reports and serve as a foundational resource for global radiation protection and environmental monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;UNSCEAR is an organization that established average worldwide activity concentrations for uranium, thorium, and potassium-40.&lt;SEP&gt;UNSCEAR is a United Nations committee that published a comprehensive report on the sources and effects of ionizing radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;UNSCEAR is the United Nations Scientific Committee on the Effect of Atomic Radiation, cited as a source for ionizing radiation data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791790789</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Activity Concentration">
  <data key="d0">Activity Concentration</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Activity concentration represents the measured radioactivity levels of radionuclides in becquerels per kilogram.&lt;SEP&gt;Activity concentration is the measured quantity of radioactivity per unit mass, expressed in Bq/kg for the uranium isotopes.&lt;SEP&gt;The measured quantity of a specific isotope in a sample, expressed in Bq·kg.&lt;SEP&gt;Activity concentration is a quantitative measurement of radionuclide presence, estimated using specific emission lines and correction formulas.&lt;SEP&gt;Activity concentration is a quantitative metric measured in Bq kg^-1 to assess the level of natural radionuclides in phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Activity concentration is a quantitative metric measured in Bq kg^-1 used to assess radioactive isotope levels in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Activity concentration measures the radioactivity of isotopes per unit mass, expressed in Bq kg⁻¹.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791312327</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium Sulphate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Sulphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium Sulphate is identified as the main phase composition of PG waste through spectroscopic and diffraction analyses.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulphate constitutes approximately 92% of the main phase composition of phosphogypsum waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary chemical compound in raw phosphogypsum, accounting for 88.46% of its composition.&lt;SEP&gt;The predominant chemical compound in raw phosphogypsum, accounting for 88.46% of its content.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulphate is the chemical compound produced during phosphoric acid generation, which forms phosphogypsum crystals.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulphate is the predominant compound found in raw phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulphate is the majority chemical compound identified in the PPG precursor material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791966758</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
