<node id="Radium Equivalent Activity">
  <data key="d0">Radium Equivalent Activity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Radium Equivalent Activity is a radiological index that combines the hazards of Ra-226, Th-232, and K-40 into a single quantity.&lt;SEP&gt;Radium equivalent activity is a radiation hazard index metric calculated to assess radiological risk in waste samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A weighted sum of natural radionuclide activities used to assess radiation hazards in building materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Absorbed Gamma Dose Rate">
  <data key="d0">Absorbed Gamma Dose Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Absorbed gamma dose rate is a radiological index measuring the total absorbed dose from gamma emissions, found to be approximately four times higher than international safety limits in phosphogypsum waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radon Emanation Fraction">
  <data key="d0">Radon Emanation Fraction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Radiation hazard index used to evaluate radon release from phosphogypsum wastes.&lt;SEP&gt;Radon Emanation Fraction is a calculated parameter representing the proportion of Radon-222 gas released from PG waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Radon emanation fraction is a radiological index used to evaluate the amount of radon gas released from the samples.&lt;SEP&gt;The radon emanation fraction represents the percentage of radon released from phosphogypsum waste, averaging 22%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radiation Hazard Indices">
  <data key="d0">Radiation Hazard Indices</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A set of metrics used to evaluate the environmental and health risks posed by phosphogypsum wastes.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation hazard indices are a set of metrics used to evaluate the radiological risk associated with phosphogypsum waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation Hazard Indices are metrics calculated using formulas like Ra-Eq for radiological safety assessments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coefficient of Variance">
  <data key="d0">Coefficient of Variance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Coefficient of variance is a statistical measure indicating the relative variability of the radiation hazard index measurements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combined Uncertainty">
  <data key="d0">Combined Uncertainty</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Combined uncertainty is a statistical parameter representing the measurement uncertainty for the average values of the radiation indices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation 2">
  <data key="d0">Equation 2</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation 2 is a mathematical formula used to calculate the total absorbed dose rate values from gamma emissions in phosphogypsum waste samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 2 represents the second step of the reaction sequence involving ammonium hydroxide and sulfuric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 2 represents the chemical reaction between ammonium hydroxide and sulfuric acid to form ammonium sulfate and water.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 2 describes the formation of insoluble Ca₃(PO₄)₂ with excellent phosphorus immobilization.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical formula used to calculate average particle diameter based on specific surface area and material density.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 2 is a mathematical formula used to calculate the oxide molar ratio metric M_eta.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792098450</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation 3">
  <data key="d0">Equation 3</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation 3 is a mathematical formula used to calculate the radon emanation fraction to quantify radon gas release.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 3 portrays the dissolution step of hydroxyapatite in the dissolution-precipitation mechanism.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 3 represents the overall combined reaction derived from Equation 1 and Equation 2.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 3 is a mathematical formula used to calculate the pH value of the slag.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792098450</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Half-life">
  <data key="d0">Half-life</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Half-life is the time required for a radioactive isotope to decay to half its initial amount, a key property for evaluating radon gas behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;A temporal property of radioactive isotopes determining decay rates and ingrowth of products.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189670</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="55 nGy/h">
  <data key="d0">55 nGy/h</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">55 nGy/h is the international recommended safe dose level for workers exposed to gamma radiation, established by UNSCEAR.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radium Equivalent Index">
  <data key="d0">Radium Equivalent Index</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A radiological safety metric used to evaluate actual radioactivity in materials containing naturally occurring radionuclides.&lt;SEP&gt;The radium equivalent index is a radiation hazard index calculated for phosphogypsum waste, found to exceed 370 Bq/kg.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alpha-Particle Measurements">
  <data key="d0">Alpha-Particle Measurements</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Analytical technique used to calculate natural isotopic ratios of uranium isotopes in the samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radionuclide Fractionation">
  <data key="d0">Radionuclide Fractionation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The redistribution and separation of uranium series isotopes during the chemical processing of phosphate rock.&lt;SEP&gt;Radionuclide fractionation is the process governing the distribution of radioactive elements during phosphogypsum production.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791314289</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U-238/Ra-226 Ratio">
  <data key="d0">U-238/Ra-226 Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A measured isotopic ratio indicating disequilibrium in phosphogypsum samples compared to phosphate rock.&lt;SEP&gt;The U-238/Ra-226 ratio is a calculated metric used to assess radioactive equilibrium in the samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U-238/Pb-210 Ratio">
  <data key="d0">U-238/Pb-210 Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A measured isotopic ratio showing significant deviation from unity in phosphogypsum waste samples.&lt;SEP&gt;The U-238/Pb-210 ratio is a calculated metric used to assess radioactive equilibrium in the samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U-238/U-235 Ratio">
  <data key="d0">U-238/U-235 Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A stable natural isotopic ratio found to be 21.7 ± 0.1 in both sample types.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U-238/U-234 Ratio">
  <data key="d0">U-238/U-234 Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">An isotopic ratio indicating expected equilibrium between U-238 and U-234 in the processed materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="370 Bq/kg">
  <data key="d0">370 Bq/kg</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The maximum permissible radium equivalent activity limit for safe use in civil applications.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific numerical threshold for Radium Equivalent Activity used to compare against calculated values.&lt;SEP&gt;370 Bq/kg is the maximum acceptable Ra-eq index for safe civil use of materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791791530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Environmental Hazards">
  <data key="d0">Environmental Hazards</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Potential risks to the surrounding environment posed by phosphogypsum solid wastes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U-Series">
  <data key="d0">U-Series</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The uranium decay series that undergoes redistribution and fractionation during the wet processing of phosphate rock.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Table 5">
  <data key="d0">Table 5</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 5 is a generic identifier that appears across multiple distinct scientific publications, each referring to a different data table with specific research focuses. In environmental and nuclear science, one version of Table 5 functions as a reference document detailing radioactive element content, specifically listing U-238 series radionuclides present in significant quantities within phosphogypsum. This table also summarizes reported radionuclide activities in phosphogypsum samples from various global locations, presents the frequencies and attributions for both raw and thermogravimetric analysis-treated phosphogypsum spectra, and outlines the environmental impacts of the phosphogypsum storage process on atmospheric, water, and soil ecosystems.

In a separate nuclear physics context, another instance of Table 5 presents data on the natural isotopic ratios of uranium isotopes, which were calculated based on alpha-particle measurements.

In materials science and catalysis research, a different Table 5 documents the catalytic decomposition results of nitrous oxide when processed over rhodium-supported metal phosphates.

Finally, in agricultural studies, Table 5 summarizes the outcomes of a three-year application of a silica-calcium–potassium–magnesium fertilizer. This data specifically tracks the fertilizer's effects on various crops, with a focus on changes in crop yield and soluble sugar content.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 5 provides phase estimation data via the Rietveld refinement method, including HAp, Alpha TCP, and Beta TCP percentages.&lt;SEP&gt;A data table containing calculated average particle diameters based on specific surface area.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 5 contains data on average particle diameters and crystallite sizes for synthetic hydroxyapatites.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 5 indicates systematic errors introduced by simplifying assumptions in equations.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 5 presents the Life Cycle Inventory data for Phosphogypsum disposal in a stack.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 5 lists the composition and mix proportions of proposed road technique materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791938112</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stadge, 1998">
  <data key="d0">Stadge, 1998</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A scientific reference cited to support the natural U-238/U-235 isotopic ratio range of 21.5–21.7.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Beretka and Mathew, 1985">
  <data key="d0">Beretka and Mathew, 1985</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A scientific reference cited to establish the maximum permissible radium equivalent activity limit of 370 Bq/kg.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zaidi et al., 1991">
  <data key="d0">Zaidi et al., 1991</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A scientific reference cited to establish the maximum permissible radium equivalent activity limit of 370 Bq/kg.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="A0">
  <data key="d0">A0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A0 represents the background concentration or reference value of an element in the Enrichment Factor equation.&lt;SEP&gt;A0 represents the initial or background concentration of an element in scientific notation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="N">
  <data key="d0">N</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">N is a variable representing the count rate of Rn-222 at the second measurement after radioactive equilibrium is attained.&lt;SEP&gt;N represents the concentration or activity of a specific element or radionuclide in a sample within the Enrichment Factor equation.&lt;SEP&gt;N is a variable added to the background value to calculate a specific normalization model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enrichment Factor">
  <data key="d0">Enrichment Factor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Enrichment Factor (EF) is a metric used to quantify the concentration of elements or radionuclides in samples compared to a natural background.&lt;SEP&gt;Enrichment Factor is a standard ratio or specific index used to model radionuclide migration or leaching.&lt;SEP&gt;The Enrichment Factor is a quantitative parameter used to evaluate isotope fractionation by comparing activities in different material fractions.&lt;SEP&gt;Enrichment Factor is a calculated metric comparing isotope activity in fractionated material to that in non-fractionated bulk material.&lt;SEP&gt;Enrichment Factor is a metric used to quantify the concentration of isotopes like lead-210 and radium-226 in specific phosphogypsum fractions.&lt;SEP&gt;Enrichment Factor is a metric calculated to quantify the concentration of specific substances in a fraction relative to the total sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791313927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normalized Index">
  <data key="d0">Normalized Index</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Normalized Index is a mathematical form bounded between 0 and 1, characteristic of saturation curves.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parent/Daughter Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Parent/Daughter Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Parent/Daughter Ratio is a metric comparing radioactive isotope ratios across different sample groups.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PR">
  <data key="d0">PR</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">PR is a sample group where radioactive ratios remain close to the natural equilibrium ratio.&lt;SEP&gt;PR represents a sample type, likely Phosphoric Acid Residue, used for radionuclide concentration measurements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Natural Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Natural Ratio is a baseline reference value of approximately 1 used for comparison.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U-238/Ra-226">
  <data key="d0">U-238/Ra-226</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">U-238/Ra-226 is an isotope ratio measured in samples, showing deviation in PG groups.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U-238/Pb-210">
  <data key="d0">U-238/Pb-210</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">U-238/Pb-210 is an isotope ratio measured in samples, showing deviation in PG groups.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ra-Eq">
  <data key="d0">Ra-Eq</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ra-Eq is Radium Equivalent Activity, a weighted sum used to calculate radiation hazard indices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
