<node id="NORM">
  <data key="d0">NORM</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">NORM refers to naturally occurring radioactive materials, which are subjects of radioactivity evaluation in several studies.&lt;SEP&gt;NORM refers to Naturally Occurring Radioactive Material, a classification applied to phosphogypsum due to its contamination with trace radionuclides.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791314057</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranium Isotopes">
  <data key="d0">Uranium Isotopes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranium isotopes are radioactive elements measured in environmental samples and intercomparison runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995813</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radon">
  <data key="d0">Radon</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radon is a radioactive, chemically inert gas that belongs to the uranium decay series and serves as a prominent contributor to natural background radiation. It is primarily generated through the radioactive decay of Radium-226 within soil, from which it naturally emanates into the surrounding environment. Beyond soil, Radon is also released from Naturally Occurring Radioactive Material (NORM)-contaminated substances, various building materials, petroleum samples, and notably from phosphogypsum, phosphogypsum stacks, and phosphogypsum bricks. The exhalation rate of Radon from phosphogypsum decreases in coarse fractions due to reduced surface area, and its immobilization potential and emanation characteristics in construction materials and waste are actively studied.

For accurate environmental monitoring and assessment, the emanation coefficient and exhalation rate of Radon from sources like phosphogypsum and petroleum are routinely measured. A key characteristic in measurement protocols is that Radon establishes equilibrium with its decay progenies over a period of at least seven weeks. Due to its radioactive emissions and the hazards posed by these progeny, Radon presents significant health, environmental, and residential risks, primarily through internal exposure via inhalation. Consequently, it remains a central subject in radiation hazard and exposure assessments, where scientific studies routinely measure concentrations and evaluate potential safety thresholds.

Monitoring efforts in environments utilizing phosphogypsum, such as rooms constructed with phosphogypsum bricks and industrial phosphogypsum stacks, indicate that Radon concentrations typically align with standard indoor levels and remain significantly below recommended or remedial action limits. Despite these generally acceptable levels, the pervasive presence of Radon across various natural and industrial contexts continues to warrant careful, ongoing monitoring and comprehensive risk evaluation to ensure public and environmental safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Radon is a gas produced from radium chloride and used in cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive gas emitted from bricks over the life cycle of a constructed wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Radon is a radioactive gas whose emission was estimated to show no significant change compared to conventional materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791878577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gamma-Ray Spectrometry">
  <data key="d0">Gamma-Ray Spectrometry</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Gamma-ray spectrometry is an analytical technique used to determine natural radioactivity in building materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Gamma-ray spectrometry is the measurement technique used to quantify radionuclide concentrations in prepared phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Gamma-ray spectrometry is an analytical technique used to determine isotopic activity ratios for radionuclides with long half-lives.</data>
