<node id="Ettringite">
  <data key="d0">Ettringite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ettringite is a crystalline calcium-aluminum double sulfate mineral compound and a primary hydration product formed through the chemical interaction of calcium and sulfate ions, particularly during the reaction of phosphogypsum with tri-calcium aluminates or slag. Recognized as one of the main chemical substances generated in cementitious studies, it frequently co-occurs with C-A-S-H and C-S-H gels. Morphologically, ettringite typically manifests as needle-like, needle-column, or fibrous crystals that surround phosphogypsum particles, interlace with C-S-H gel, and bond with other hydration products to form agglomerates. These structures actively fill pores within cement paste matrices, mortar gels, and cemented paste backfill systems, thereby reinforcing microstructures and significantly improving the densification, consolidation, and mechanical strength of filling materials and concrete.

The formation and evolution of ettringite are highly sensitive to compositional and environmental conditions. It is actively stimulated by acidic and alkaline solutions during early-stage chemical erosion, as well as by phosphogypsum and Plaster of Paris, with robust needle-column frameworks developing notably when Plaster of Paris content reaches 20%. Conversely, decreased aluminum oxide levels can inhibit its creation. Analytically, it is identified through distinct X-ray diffraction patterns, including a characteristic peak near 9° and specific markers like a star with a dot inside, with peak intensities varying according to phosphogypsum concentration, curing duration, and sample ratios. Functionally, ettringite plays a pivotal role in governing the hydration kinetics of calcium oxide and fluorgypsum-activated binders, accelerating early strength evolution, and enhancing the overall durability of conventional cementitious engineering. Additionally, it facilitates environmental applications such as fluoride immobilization and phosphate removal, though its structural impact remains matrix-dependent, occasionally disrupting geopolymer architectures. Overall, ettringite serves as a critical phase in both sustainable industrial waste utilization and advanced binder performance.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical phase developed during hydration that enhances the strength behavior of the solidified material.&lt;SEP&gt;Ettringite is a mineral phase that develops during early hydration, contributing to the strength of the solidified material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791937342</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fluorite">
  <data key="d0">Fluorite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluorite is a sparingly soluble fluoride mineral that frequently occurs as a minor accessory component within various geological formations, including phosphate rock and general rock strata. Its presence in industrial byproducts such as phosphogypsum has been confirmed through advanced analytical techniques, including scanning electron microscopy and electron probe micro-analysis, which identify it as a distinct mineral phase. Characterized by its low solubility, fluorite typically persists as an insoluble impurity during material processing and represents a compound to which fluoride ions readily precipitate, particularly in cemented paste backfill mixtures.

In environmental and geochemical contexts, fluorite exhibits a dual behavior: it acts as a host phase that restricts the mobility of rare earth elements, yet simultaneously contributes to fluorine leaching within phosphogypsum. From a mineral processing standpoint, fluorite can be effectively isolated from associated minerals like scheelite by utilizing sodium silicate as a depressant in solutions containing calcium and gypsum. Collectively, these characteristics define fluorite as a low-solubility mineral with significant implications for resource extraction, waste management, and geochemical element migration.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorite is a mineral form of fluorine whose proportion decreases during long-term leaching, accompanied by rising fluoride concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorite is a mineral component of the phosphogypsum matrix containing yttrium, lanthane, cerium, cadmium, and uranium.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792039439</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geochemical Modeling">
  <data key="d0">Geochemical Modeling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Geochemical Modeling is a computational tool used to estimate pollutant release and explain PTEs retention mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;A computational technique used to predict mineral solubilities and trace element controls in mining area waters.&lt;SEP&gt;Geochemical modeling is a computational approach used to simulate radionuclide release pathways and elution mechanisms from Phosphogypsum stacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Geochemical modeling is an analytical technique used to simulate radionuclide complexation and mobility in stack fluids.&lt;SEP&gt;Geochemical modeling is a computational approach used to simulate brine remediation processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-015</data>
