<node id="Fluorapatite">
  <data key="d0">Fluorapatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluorapatite is a crystalline calcium phosphate fluoride mineral and the definitive chemical form of apatite, characterized by the formula Ca5(PO4)3F. While primarily defined by its fluoride composition, mineralogical analyses also identify it as a calcium phosphate carbonate mineral in certain contexts, reflecting the apatite group's capacity for anion substitution. Its presence is consistently indicated by the overlapping spatial distribution of fluorine, calcium, and phosphorus. Geologically, Fluorapatite serves as the primary constituent of both igneous and sedimentary phosphate rock ores, functioning as the main natural source of fluorine in phosphogypsum and acting as a concentrated reservoir for critical materials. In specific geological and operational systems, it can contain substantial concentrations of rare earth elements, yttrium, and scandium, comprising up to one percent of its mass and occasionally accounting for one hundred percent of target rare earth elements within localized deposits.

Industrially, Fluorapatite operates as the principal raw material and key reactant in the wet chemical process for phosphoric acid production, a foundational step in manufacturing fertilizers and gypsum while generating potentially toxic byproducts. It is also utilized as a standard reference material for calibrating phosphorus measurements. During synthesis, its biomimetic growth, self-assembly properties, and morphological development can be actively controlled through factors such as citrate ion influence. In environmental and waste processing contexts, Fluorapatite frequently appears as a residual mineral on gypsum surfaces, originating from unreacted phosphate rock. Within these systems, it plays a dynamic role in geochemical cycling: it actively contributes to fluorine leaching, regulates fluorine solubility in phosphogypsum, and governs fluoride ion leaching behavior in backfill systems. Furthermore, it serves as the primary compound for fluoride precipitation in cemented paste backfill mixtures and forms a stable phosphate phase in neutral environments during immobilization processes. This multifaceted profile underscores Fluorapatite’s critical importance across chemical manufacturing, resource extraction, and environmental geochemistry.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-025</data>
