</node>
<node id="Phosphate Ores">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Ores</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate Ores are raw mineral resources excavated for modern agriculture and fertilizer production.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate ores are raw geological materials from which phosphogypsum is derived, containing varying gangue minerals and fluoride content.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate ores are the raw materials excavated worldwide, with data tracked by the USGS database.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate ores are the raw materials processed in the phosphoric acid industry, containing trace amounts of rare earth elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate ores are mineral deposits characterized by high natural radioactivity and trace element enrichment.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate Ores are raw materials that undergo incomplete decomposition by sulfuric acid, leaving impurities in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate ores, specifically apatite, are the raw materials processed to produce phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791936023</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="USGS Database">
  <data key="d0">USGS Database</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The USGS database provides worldwide excavation data for phosphate ores from 1994 to 2022.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791022627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Drinking Water">
  <data key="d0">Drinking Water</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Drinking water resources are affected by fluoride accumulation originating from phosphogypsum stockpile sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Drinking water is a medium from which lead and cadmium ions are removed using biochar adsorbents.&lt;SEP&gt;Drinking water is a pathway through which phosphogypsum pollutants like fluoride can enter the human body.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791900466</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Global Health Concern">
  <data key="d0">Global Health Concern</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Global health concern refers to the widespread impact of fluoride accumulation in drinking water, potentially affecting 180 million people.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791022627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Agricultural Use">
  <data key="d0">Agricultural Use</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Agricultural use is a phosphogypsum recycling pathway that yields mixed results due to safety concerns regarding potentially toxic element leachability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791022627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heavy Metal">
  <data key="d0">Heavy Metal</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Heavy metals are toxic elements frequently associated with industrial waste, targeted for immobilization in cementitious materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metal refers to toxic metallic ions whose sorption and remediation are studied using mineral apatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metal is an impurity removed from the filtrate during the first purification stage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791272396</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biochar">
  <data key="d0">Biochar</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Biochar is a carbon-rich solid material derived from the pyrolysis of biomass, particularly agricultural biomass, and is widely recognized as a renewable, low-cost, and sustainable resource. It functions primarily as a soil amendment that enhances soil properties and attenuates toxic elements, effectively preventing the transfer of harmful substances into the food chain. In agricultural and remediation contexts, it is frequently applied alongside phosphogypsum or utilized in systems involving gypsum pond wastewater augmented with potassium, phosphorus, and sulfur to mitigate toxic element mobility.

Beyond soil applications, biochar serves as an economical and versatile adsorbent and sorbent. It is extensively utilized in water treatment processes to remove both organic and inorganic contaminants, and it is commonly employed in experimental research for heavy metal adsorption studies. In the construction and materials sectors, biochar acts as a green additive in cement-based composites, often utilized in conjunction with carbon dioxide curing techniques, and functions as a key carbon-rich component in specialized formulations such as the HAP-BC composite. To evaluate its structural and chemical properties, biochar is routinely characterized using analytical techniques including X-Ray Diffraction and Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy, which are used to identify its mineral composition and functional groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Biochar is a porous material prepared from agricultural by-products used to adsorb heavy metal ions from drinking water and wastewater.&lt;SEP&gt;Biochar is a carbon-rich material combined with hydroxyapatite nanoparticles to form a nanocomposite for heavy metal removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Biochar is a carbon-rich material derived from agricultural by-products used to adsorb lead and cadmium from drinking water.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791654123</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sodic Soil">
  <data key="d0">Sodic Soil</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodic soil is a type of degraded soil characterized by high sodium content, often treated with gypsum or lime for reclamation.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of soil targeted for reclamation through calcium amendment to reduce sodium saturation and control erosion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423626</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Poly-Aluminum Chloride">
  <data key="d0">Poly-Aluminum Chloride</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Poly-aluminum chloride is a chemical activator used to enhance the solidification and stabilization of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Poly-Aluminum Chloride is a chemical activator used synergistically with cemented paste backfill for fluoride retention in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791714337</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="A.M. Rashad">
  <data key="d0">A.M. Rashad</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A.M. Rashad is a researcher who reviewed the use of phosphogypsum as a construction material.&lt;SEP&gt;A researcher who investigated the potential use of phosphogypsum in alkali-activated fly ash under thermal stress.&lt;SEP&gt;A.M. Rashad is a researcher who investigated the potential use of phosphogypsum in alkali-activated fly ash under thermal stress.&lt;SEP&gt;A.M. Rashad is a researcher who studied the use of phosphogypsum as a construction material.&lt;SEP&gt;A researcher who authored studies on phosphogypsum as a construction material and its potential use in alkali-activated fly ash.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
