<node id="Aspergillus Niger">
  <data key="d0">Aspergillus Niger</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Aspergillus niger is a fungus utilized alongside geological fluorapatite for the immobilization of lead.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus Niger is a fungal species inoculated into press mud to enhance maize yield and potassium use efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791141872</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Ore">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Ore</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate ore is a natural geological raw material and mineral resource primarily processed to extract phosphoric acid, a procedure that inherently generates phosphogypsum as a byproduct. The ore serves as a foundational industrial input and can be processed using treated mother liquid derived from rare earth recovery. In industrial and regulatory frameworks, phosphate ore is formally recognized as a raw material category featured in industrial statistics charts, while also being associated with waste management classifications that oversee the extraction and processing of phosphate rock.

A defining characteristic of phosphate ore is its direct and comprehensive influence on the properties of the resulting phosphogypsum. The ore's inherent chemical composition, mineralogical makeup, and natural compositional variations dictate the color, chemical profile, and overall mineralogical characteristics of the phosphogypsum produced. Furthermore, crystal structure substitutions present in the original ore are transferred into the phosphogypsum during processing. Notably, phosphate ore acts as the primary natural geological source of fluorine found within the phosphogypsum waste stream. Consequently, the specific geological and compositional attributes of the phosphate ore fundamentally shape both the industrial handling of the material and the environmental management of its processing byproducts.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate ore is a raw material reacted with sulfuric acid in the wet process to produce phosphoric acid and phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate ore is the raw material treated with sulfuric acid to produce phosphoric acid and phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A raw material input used in both the wet and kiln phosphoric acid processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate ore is a raw material dissolved in phosphoric acid to produce phosphoric acid and phosphogypsum waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792001629</data>
