<node id="Eutrophication">
  <data key="d0">Eutrophication</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Eutrophication is an ecological and environmental process characterized by severe water quality degradation and nutrient overload in aquatic systems. It is primarily triggered by excessive phosphorus and phosphate enrichment, which frequently originates from the export of phosphogypsum (PG) into natural water bodies. This phosphorus runoff commonly stems from soil leaching or agricultural sites treated with phosphogypsum, introducing soluble phosphorus and impurities directly into surface waters. In certain documented instances, the process is also associated with elevated fluorine levels contributing to the chemical imbalance.

The resulting nutrient surplus fundamentally alters the aquatic survival environment, particularly disrupting phytoplankton populations and cascading through the ecosystem to affect overall biodiversity. Consequently, eutrophication poses a severe threat to aquatic organisms and ecosystem health, manifesting as a widespread environmental impact driven by anthropogenic phosphorus discharge. By degrading water quality and overwhelming natural waterways, this condition represents a critical ecological consequence that highlights the environmental risks associated with phosphogypsum-related runoff.&lt;SEP&gt;Eutrophication is an environmental impact addressed using an alternative assessment method due to data limitations.&lt;SEP&gt;Eutrophication is an environmental impact category where PG use as soil amendment ranks lowest in footprint and brick production ranks highest.&lt;SEP&gt;An environmental condition caused by nutrient pollution from phosphogypsum, leading to excessive algae growth in water bodies.&lt;SEP&gt;Eutrophication is an ecological condition caused by excessive phosphorus in water bodies, leading to rapid algae growth and oxygen depletion.&lt;SEP&gt;Eutrophication is a process potentially triggered by algal blooms in restricted water bodies due to phosphorus contamination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-001</data>
