</node>
<node id="Iron">
  <data key="d0">Iron</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Iron is a chemical element and transition metal that serves multifaceted roles across environmental science, industrial processing, and materials engineering. In agricultural and soil chemistry contexts, it actively binds with phosphate to precipitate into insoluble ferrous or ferric phosphate compounds, which substantially reduces phosphate bioavailability and plant uptake while also contributing to fluoride accumulation in topsoil layers. In water quality management, iron is subject to regulatory standards, featuring a specified concentration limit of 0.3 mg/L and a World Health Organization guideline value of 300 mg/L.

Within phosphogypsum processing, iron functions as both a major chemical species and a metallic impurity. Its concentration varies geographically, appearing in lower amounts in Brazilian samples compared to Moroccan ones. Reported measurements include trace contents ranging from 10.7 to 30.00 μg/g, approximately 0.31 wt% of dried phosphogypsum, 0.21 ± 0.01, 12.5 ppm within calcium sulfate phases, and 60 mg/L as an interfering ion in leach liquor. During extraction procedures, iron co-leaches as an impurity, with efficiency increasing under prolonged processing times or elevated temperatures. This presence complicates downstream purification efforts, particularly in rare earth element recovery, and the element is also utilized as Fe3+ in synthetic solutions for extraction experiments.

Industrially, iron is recovered as a valuable metal from solid waste and red mud through magnetic separation following high-temperature sintering and co-sintering techniques. In advanced materials science, it acts as a functional metal dopant and carrier. It can be impregnated into synthetic nanohydroxyapatite for agricultural applications, where it alters the material's optical absorption properties into the UV-visible range. Furthermore, iron is strategically combined with phosphogypsum or nickel to synthesize compound oxygen carriers that lower reaction temperatures during chemical transformations, thereby supporting clean energy development and efficient industrial processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron is an element whose chemical state on the reduced sample surface is determined by XPS analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-006</data>
