<node id="Density">
  <data key="d0">Density</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Density is a fundamental physical characteristic of various materials, typically quantified in units such as grams per cubic centimeter. As a core metric, it plays a critical role in material science, quality control, and engineering applications. In the context of phosphogypsum, density is a defining physical trait that directly influences transportation logistics and application methodologies. Notably, the intrinsic density of phosphogypsum increases when subjected to higher calcination temperatures.

When phosphogypsum is utilized as an additive, its impact on the density of composite materials varies by application. For general construction materials and cement paste, the incorporation of phosphogypsum typically reduces overall density. Conversely, when phosphogypsum is introduced into bricks at specific substitution rates, the resulting density increases. These divergent trends highlight how compositional ratios and base material matrices dictate the final physical properties.

In specialized biomedical applications, such as composite bone cement, density exhibits a slight decrease as sodium chloride additive concentration rises. Within this specific matrix, density also maintains a linear relationship with compressive strength, serving as a relevant indicator for mechanical performance. Beyond construction and biomedical composites, density remains a standard parameter evaluated in the quality analysis of biodiesel, where it functions as a key metric for fuel specification and consistency.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical property of the nano-hydroxyapatite types measured using a helium pycnometer.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical property of the nanopowders ranging from 2.86 to 3.04 g/cm³, influenced by synthesis time and pressure.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical property representing mass per unit volume, measured in g/cm³ for the synthesized nanopowder samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Density is a physical property of the nanopowder that correlates directly with particle diameter.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical property measured during the analysis, expressed in grams per cubic centimeter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-000</data>
