<node id="United States">
  <data key="d0">United States</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The United States is a developed nation and a major global hub for the production, consumption, and export of phosphate rock. As the second-largest annual producer of phosphogypsum, generating approximately 30.0 megatonnes per year, the country manages this industrial byproduct primarily through large-scale stockpiling. This disposal method is driven by anticipated future demand and existing regulatory restrictions. Although the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency currently classifies phosphogypsum as valueless waste, the nation has initiated research into its potential utilization, building upon early studies conducted in Japan. Furthermore, the United States has formally designated rare earth elements as strategic resources to address perceived supply chain vulnerabilities.

The phosphogypsum produced in the United States is scientifically notable for its elevated radionuclide levels, a characteristic that has been extensively documented and measured in peer-reviewed literature, including studies by Luther et al. (1993) and Rutherford et al. (1995). To mitigate potential health risks associated with these radioactive materials, the United States has established a specific drinking water standard that sets a combined concentration limit of 0.19 Bq/L for radium-226 and radium-228. These regulatory and scientific frameworks underscore the country's approach to managing the environmental and public health implications of its phosphate industry.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is a major producer of phosphogypsum where stockpiling is the predominant management method, with limited agricultural use.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States predominantly stockpiles phosphogypsum, with limited agricultural use, and faces radioactivity constraints in Florida.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-003</data>
