<node id="Fertilizer">
  <data key="d0">Fertilizer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fertilizer is primarily recognized as an agricultural product manufactured from phosphoric acid, typically formed into granular pellets for crop cultivation. As the principal downstream product of phosphoric acid processing, it accounts for the vast majority of global phosphate rock consumption. Industrial production of this fertilizer often occurs at integrated chemical complexes, such as the facility in Huelva, where it is manufactured concurrently with phosphoric acid.

In alternative production pathways, fertilizer is also produced through the conversion of phosphogypsum (PG), a byproduct of phosphate acid manufacturing. This PG-derived fertilizer serves as a viable agricultural application for phosphogypsum, helping to mitigate industrial waste while improving soil conditions. Research initiatives, including evaluations by Mohammed et al., have assessed its viability, and it has been frequently compared against conventional fertilizers in life cycle assessment (LCA) studies to evaluate its environmental and economic impacts.

Additionally, fertilizer represents a distinct agricultural application for hydroxyapatite, a naturally occurring calcium phosphate mineral. In this specific context, the material is utilized for its calcium and phosphate composition to enhance soil fertility and support plant growth, demonstrating the versatility of phosphate-based compounds in agricultural formulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-024</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
