<node id="Carbide Slag">
  <data key="d0">Carbide Slag</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbide slag is an industrial byproduct primarily utilized as a reactive modifier and cement retarder. Its most common application involves combining it with phosphogypsum to alter the material's properties for use in cement formulations. This modification process is frequently optimized by incorporating various types of fly ash, such as circulating fluidized bed fly ash and biomass fly ash, which work in tandem with carbide slag to enhance the final product's performance. Additionally, carbide slag is employed to react with impurities present in phosphogypsum, thereby improving the overall efficiency of CPGFM systems.

Beyond its role in cement and phosphogypsum processing, carbide slag serves several other specialized industrial functions. It is mixed with biomass fly ash and phosphogypsum to produce high-strength CCM, and it is combined with potassium sulfate to increase calcium and sulfate ion concentrations, facilitating targeted crystal formation. In environmental and energy applications, carbide slag also operates as a carrier material within gasification and carbon capture processes. Collectively, these diverse applications demonstrate carbide slag's versatility as a multifunctional additive across construction materials, chemical processing, and emission control technologies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791772206</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
