<node id="Reaction Temperature">
  <data key="d0">Reaction Temperature</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Reaction Temperature is a fundamental experimental and process variable that dictates the thermal conditions necessary for various chemical and physical transformations. Its specific value and operational role vary significantly depending on the targeted application, spanning from low-temperature leaching operations to high-temperature thermal processing and hydrothermal synthesis.

In leaching processes, Reaction Temperature serves as a critical control parameter that directly influences extraction efficiency. It is typically maintained within a moderate range, such as 25°C to 75°C or specifically at 55°C, to maximize the removal of radioactive materials and rare earth elements. Experimental evaluations also test its performance at 30°C and ambient conditions, consistently confirming its substantial impact on overall leaching yields.

For high-temperature thermal and redox applications, including chemical looping gasification and catalytic reduction-oxidation cycles, Reaction Temperature provides the essential thermal energy required to drive gasification reactions efficiently. Optimal conditions for specific catalysts like PG-CuFe2O4 fall between 750°C and 850°C. Furthermore, when catalysts are employed, the parameter can be strategically reduced from 910°C to 590°C for reduction stages and from 1100°C to 1000°C for oxidation stages, demonstrating its adaptability in optimizing catalytic performance and energy consumption.

In materials synthesis, particularly hydrothermal methods, Reaction Temperature is applied within a 60°C to 150°C range and functions as a dominant factor over reaction time. It critically governs nucleation and growth dynamics, thereby controlling the crystal morphology and particle size of synthesized compounds, such as hydroxyapatite. Across all these distinct domains, Reaction Temperature remains an indispensable variable for precisely tuning process outcomes and material properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Reaction temperature is a key parameter that influences reaction products and desulfurization rates during carbothermal reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Reaction Temperature is the thermal condition of the reduction test, significantly impacting the melting state and desulfurization rate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
