<node id="Barium">
  <data key="d0">Barium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Barium is an alkaline earth chemical element that frequently occurs as a trace component or patchy impurity within various geological and industrial materials, exhibiting a notably heterogeneous distribution. It is particularly prominent in phosphogypsum by-products, where it is consistently measured at approximately 0.54 wt% in dried samples across multiple studies, including those of South African deposits, though it is depleted in phosphorite deposits relative to typical crustal abundances. Chemically, barium demonstrates poor solubility and primarily precipitates as barium sulfate within the phosphogypsum crystal lattice. This insoluble sulfate phase co-precipitates or co-crystallizes with radium, including the radioisotope Ra-226, to form sulfate compounds and solid solutions. This behavior is highly significant in uranium mining environments, where it contributes to the formation of (Ba, Ra) sulfate sludges, and in waste management systems, where it critically influences radium nucleation, solubility, and overall mobility.

In experimental and analytical contexts, barium (Ba2+) is routinely introduced as an ion in synthetic solutions for extraction experiments, where its extraction efficiency has been observed to be higher than that of radium. When subjected to leaching processes, barium acts as a major element with low mobility, showing a decreasing release rate over time and a slight decrease in concentration in leached samples. Its leaching concentrations typically remain below 10 µg/L. Spectroscopic analysis can detect barium via characteristic peaks at approximately 230 and 232 nm. Due to its widespread presence and low removal rate, barium is routinely analyzed alongside radionuclides and strontium in phosphogypsum leachates and monitored during groundwater assessments.

Given its environmental distribution and potential health implications, barium is subject to regulatory oversight. While chemically classified as an alkaline earth element, it is frequently monitored in industrial and environmental frameworks alongside heavy metals, with its concentration in phosphogypsum solutions tracked against established limits. Regulatory standards include a World Health Organization guideline value of 0.1 mg/L for drinking water quality, reflecting the need to manage its mobility and exposure in both natural and engineered systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792036298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strontium">
  <data key="d0">Strontium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Strontium is an alkaline earth chemical element that occurs naturally as a trace component and metallic impurity, primarily originating from phosphate rocks. It exhibits heterogeneous distributions within phosphorite deposits and phosphogypsum by-products, where it is notably enriched. Additionally, strontium is one of the most abundant elements in the gypsum phase and is also found in fluorite and quartz phases. Due to its ionic radius similarity to calcium, strontium readily substitutes for calcium within the apatite crystal structure in phosphorite and into the calcium sulfate dihydrate lattice in phosphogypsum, directly linking it to the gypsum matrix.

In phosphogypsum, strontium typically constitutes approximately 0.49 to 0.54 wt% of dried material, with mass contents ranging from 47.00 to 469.71 μg/g and specific measurements recorded at 709 ± 115. It is routinely characterized using Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass-Spectrometry and spectroscopically identified by a small peak around 1.5 keV. Despite its significant concentration, strontium is highly retained within the phosphogypsum matrix and exhibits poor solubility, requiring strong chemical dissolution for release. It is categorized as having intermediate mobility in phosphogypsum, and its release rate decreases as a function of leaching time. While strontium can be leached by citric acid, its relative proportion increases when other reagents are utilized. In phosphogypsum leach liquor, it acts as an interfering metal ion, recorded at concentrations such as 17 mg/L, and is present as a Sr2+ ion in synthetic solutions used for extraction experiments.

Geochemically, strontium shares properties with radium and barium, tending to co-precipitate and co-crystallize with them, particularly as sulfate compounds and solid solutions, which is critical for understanding radium mobility in industrial waste systems. Although the element itself is stable, its radioactive isotopes are recognized as radionuclides that can be efficiently removed from contaminated environments by hydroxyapatite. Conversely, when strontium acts as a substituent in hydroxyapatite, it can reduce the material's stability by increasing its solubility. The environmental behavior and mobility of strontium are actively studied, particularly regarding its presence in phosphogypsum leachates, their subsequent impact on soil systems, and its leaching characteristics under simulated acid rain conditions. Overall, strontium's geochemical mobility, substitution patterns, and interaction with mineral matrices make it a significant focus in environmental and industrial waste management research.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007</data>
