<node id="Drying Process">
  <data key="d0">Drying Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Drying Process involves heating dried PG at 70 °C for over 24 hours to determine free water content.&lt;SEP&gt;A thermal process stage in PSC technology that accounts for nearly 40% of coal use, particularly for high-moisture raw materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Drying process involves heating PG at specific temperatures, which influences its effect on compressive strength.&lt;SEP&gt;A processing step that breaks down larger crystals into smaller, jagged fragments.&lt;SEP&gt;The Drying Process is a treatment method applied to wet Phosphogypsum sludge to remove moisture, resulting in physical cracking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791734744</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Table 3">
  <data key="d0">Table 3</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 3 functions as a generic data presentation label utilized across numerous independent scientific studies, with its specific content and purpose varying significantly depending on the research context. In phosphogypsum utilization and mineral processing research, it documents the mass fractions of major elements in samples from various phosphate fertilizer factories, alongside operating conditions and extraction efficiencies for leaching rare earth elements, as well as rare earth element content in dihydrate phosphogypsum. It presents radionuclide activity concentrations, including Radium-226 activity deviations across different phosphogypsum fractions and average enrichment factors for various isotopes, often supplemented by literature values for terrestrial materials as comparative benchmarks. Additionally, Table 3 lists the P2O5 and Fluorine content in typical phosphogypsum samples from China and other countries, and presents global data on the generation, disposal, and utilization of phosphogypsum across multiple nations. It further documents the primary benefits and environmental risks associated with phosphogypsum applications across different sectors, while also presenting temperature values and the nature of each studied phosphogypsum transformation. The table also outlines the existence forms and harmful effects of various impurities in phosphogypsum, details the composition of major species and impurities before and after treatment, and records chemical composition changes following treatment with various leaching reagents.

In construction and materials science applications, Table 3 summarizes literature data on mortar setting time under varying phosphogypsum replacement levels and substitution materials. It provides comprehensive data on composite cement formulations treated with sodium chloride, detailing average grain size, mechanical properties, water absorption rates, microstructural characteristics, and performance across different sodium chloride concentrations. The table also presents quantitative results from shrinkage, softening in water, and dry unit weight tests, specifically summarizing experimental drying shrinkage results at 28 days.

Beyond materials and chemical analysis, Table 3 serves as a reference for spectroscopic and biological data. It lists distinct infrared vibrations for PVA-peeled HAP and AL-functionalized HAP, records the Ca/P molar ratios determined by ICP-OES measurements for HAp Types 1 through 6, and lists measured optical band gap values for various synthesized HAp samples. In agricultural and botanical studies, it documents physiological measurements, including chlorophyll and vitamin C content across plant specimens subjected to various experimental treatments, supporting analyses of plant nutritional status and treatment efficacy. Across all disciplines, Table 3 functions as a standardized reference point for presenting empirical data, literature comparisons, and experimental outcomes tailored to the specific objectives of each study. It also presents Life Cycle Inventory data for phosphogypsum use as a soil amendment and compiles national-level policies promulgated regarding phosphogypsum utilization since 2018.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 3 recapitulates the design criteria and quality monitoring parameters for the mixes.&lt;SEP&gt;A data table listing chemical species examined using LA-ICP-MS in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 3 presents the trace element composition data of minerals in phosphogypsum obtained via LA-ICP-MS.</data>
