<node id="Radium">
  <data key="d0">Radium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radium is a naturally occurring radioactive chemical element and radionuclide, with 226Ra being a particularly notable isotope. It occurs naturally in association with uranium and thorium within phosphate materials, phosphate rock, and uranium mine wastes. Due to its large ionic radius, radium cannot fit into the apatite crystal lattice in phosphorite deposits; instead, it co-precipitates or co-crystallizes with barium and strontium.

During industrial phosphate processing, radium becomes prominently concentrated in phosphogypsum, a byproduct whose radium content varies by region and is routinely measured in the material and related substances. During phosphate decomposition, approximately 80% of radium migrates into the processing stream, with roughly 89% ultimately retained within the phosphogypsum crystal structure. Within this matrix, it preferentially incorporates into sulfate minerals such as gypsum and accumulates in smaller grain size fractions. Chemically, radium demonstrates a slight preference for retention in phosphogypsum over lead, exhibits lower extraction efficiency compared to barium (yielding a theoretical separation factor), and shows a high transfer factor into calcite. Its stripping and extraction efficiencies have been actively measured in processing contexts.

Despite being largely trapped within the phosphogypsum crystal structure, radium retains the potential to leach into surrounding soil systems and leachates, creating a documented risk of permeation into nearby water bodies. Given its radioactive nature and environmental mobility, the presence of radium in phosphogypsum stockpiles requires careful monitoring and the implementation of effective removal processes to mitigate ecological impacts. Historically, radium was also utilized in paints and medical applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Radium is a naturally occurring radionuclide found in phosphate rock and subsequently concentrated in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A radionuclide that is predominantly transferred into phosphogypsum during the production process.&lt;SEP&gt;Radium is a naturally occurring radionuclide in phosphate ore that is removed during production and ends up in phosphogypsum waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000</data>
