</node>
<node id="EDS">
  <data key="d0">EDS</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">EDS is an analytical technique used to identify elemental composition and confirm the successful production of HAP, CaS, and NaCl.&lt;SEP&gt;EDS is an abbreviation for Energy Dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy, used for elemental analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;An analytical technique used to confirm the elemental composition and successful incorporation of HPA into HAP.&lt;SEP&gt;EDS is an alias for Energy Dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy, a technique used to determine elemental composition.&lt;SEP&gt;EDS is an energy dispersive spectroscopy system used for chemical microanalysis and imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;Electron dispersive spectroscopy is an analytical attachment used with SEM for element distribution and migration analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792098909</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SEM">
  <data key="d0">SEM</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is a high-resolution microscopic imaging and analytical technique primarily utilized to visualize and characterize the physical structure, morphology, and microstructural features of various materials. By scanning a focused electron beam across a sample's surface, SEM enables the detailed observation of surface topography, particle architecture, distribution, and arrangement, as well as internal characteristics such as fracture surfaces, grain size, porosity, and internal hydration products. This methodology serves as a fundamental and indispensable tool for microscopic characterization, providing critical insights into structural evolution and morphological transformations across diverse scientific and industrial domains.

In practical applications, SEM is extensively employed to evaluate the physical characteristics and morphological properties of specific compounds, composites, and synthesized samples. It is frequently used to analyze the architecture, dimensions, and aspect ratios of hydroxyapatite (HAp/HAP) nanoparticles, including peeled and rod-shaped structures, as well as to monitor morphological changes under varying experimental conditions such as stirring. The technique effectively reveals distinct morphological differences, characterizing synthetic hydroxyapatite as randomly distributed platelets and bone ash as homogenous nanoparticles. Furthermore, SEM is applied to examine the surface morphology of HAP and HPA-HAP composites, assess the physical structure of PG and Ni/PG carriers, and investigate internal hydration products within PGCM. Through these varied applications, SEM remains a vital analytical method for understanding material properties at the microscale.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is a microscopy technique used to validate the average nanocrystal size and morphology of the synthesized product.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is a microscopy technique used to image the morphology, agglomeration, and porous structure of dried and calcined hydroxyapatite samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A microscopy technique used to study the microstructures and element distribution of polished slag sections.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM is used to examine the microstructure of the PGI-1.4 sample reduced at 1250 °C.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792097742</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Salt Leaching Methods">
  <data key="d0">Salt Leaching Methods</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Traditional fabrication techniques that, when combined with nucleation and crystallization methods, greatly improve scaffold interconnectivity.&lt;SEP&gt;Salt Leaching Methods are fabrication techniques used to create porous structures in scaffolds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791060605</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nucleation and Crystallization Methods">
  <data key="d0">Nucleation and Crystallization Methods</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Nucleation and Crystallization Methods are techniques used to prepare scaffolds with improved interconnectivity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791060605</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compression Tests">
  <data key="d0">Compression Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Compression Tests are mechanical testing procedures used to determine the compressive strength of the composite bone cement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791060605</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Average Grain Size">
  <data key="d0">Average Grain Size</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Average Grain Size is a microstructural metric that increases with higher NaCl modifier concentration.&lt;SEP&gt;Average grain size is a microstructural metric that increases with NaCl modifier concentration in the composite cements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791060605</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bending Strength">
  <data key="d0">Bending Strength</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanical property investigated using a universal testing machine and ASTM guidelines to assess fracture resistance.&lt;SEP&gt;Bending Strength is a mechanical property that decreases alongside compressive strength with higher NaCl content.&lt;SEP&gt;Bending strength ($\sigma_f$) is a mechanical property measured in MPa, exhibiting an exponential relationship with porosity.&lt;SEP&gt;Bending strength is a mechanical property that reaches its maximum value when phosphogypsum content is 75%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Modulus">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Modulus</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Elastic Modulus is a fundamental mechanical property that quantifies a material's stiffness. It is typically expressed in megapascals (MPa) and can be determined through empirical equations or direct mechanical testing. In engineering and materials science, particularly concerning road construction and stabilized mixes, this parameter is critical for evaluating performance and conditioning pavement use. The testing process often involves measuring the property at specific curing intervals, such as 7, 90, and 360 days, to track stiffness development over time.

The value of the Elastic Modulus, frequently denoted as the E value, is significantly influenced by material composition and physical characteristics. It exhibits an exponential relationship with grain size and tends to decrease as the concentration of sodium chloride (NaCl) additives increases. In specific applications like the P12 Mix, the addition of CM significantly enhances stiffness, with measurements reaching 2864 MPa at just 7 days of curing. Overall, Elastic Modulus serves as a vital metric for assessing how material formulations and curing conditions affect structural rigidity and long-term durability in civil engineering contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791936533</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
