<node id="Biomaterials">
  <data key="d0">Biomaterials</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Biomaterials refers to two distinct but related concepts: a prominent peer-reviewed scientific journal and a broader category of materials used in clinical and biomedical contexts.

As a scientific journal, Biomaterials is dedicated to publishing research on the synthesis, processing, characterization, and biological interactions of advanced materials for medical applications. The publication frequently features studies on hydroxyapatite and related compounds, including the controlled synthesis of bone-related materials, porous hydroxyapatite ceramics derived from natural bone, hydroxyapatite coatings for medical implants, and the fabrication and characterization of porous hydroxyapatite granules. It also covers bone substitutes, calcium phosphate cements, detailed analyses of apatite structures such as carbonate substitution in sodium-bearing apatite, and the application of micro-Raman and FTIR spectroscopy for material characterization. The journal has a history of publishing significant research, including an early paper by K.D. Groot and a 2000 paper authored by A. Cüneyt Tas. Within this academic domain, biomaterials represent the primary application field for hydroxyapatite, particularly as bone replacement options.

In a broader scientific and medical sense, biomaterials encompass both natural and synthetic substances specifically engineered for clinical use. These materials are designed to interact with biological systems, serving as replacements, supports, or enhancers for damaged tissues, organs, or physiological functions. A critical aspect of their development is that their surface properties often require precise modification to establish efficient and stable interfaces with biomolecules. Together, these definitions highlight how the term biomaterials bridges specialized academic publishing with the fundamental engineering of materials for human health.&lt;SEP&gt;A scientific journal publishing research on biomaterials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792003010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
