<node id="Solid-State Reaction">
  <data key="d0">Solid-State Reaction</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Solid-State Reaction is a chemical decomposition pathway and process mechanism primarily investigated for the processing and decomposition of phosphogypsum. Research utilizing this approach focuses on analyzing the reaction kinetics and underlying mechanisms, often conducted under controlled conditions such as inert atmospheres. The technique is evaluated through comprehensive kinetic and reaction mechanism frameworks to understand how phosphogypsum breaks down under solid-state conditions.

In materials synthesis, Solid-State Reaction serves as a traditional chemical-processing route employed to produce hydroxyapatite. While it has been historically utilized for this purpose, the method is recognized for several operational drawbacks. These limitations include excessively long reaction times and material agglomeration, which can affect the efficiency and quality of the synthesized compound. Despite these challenges, it remains a foundational chemical-processing technique referenced in the synthesis of hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792097138</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
