<node id="Figure 4">
  <data key="d0">Figure 4</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 4 serves as a scientific illustration whose specific content varies across distinct research contexts due to the generic nature of figure numbering in academic publications. In materials science and characterization studies, Figure 4 presents comprehensive analytical data, including X-ray diffraction patterns for samples S2–S4 alongside their thermal treatments, as well as X-ray diffraction patterns for materials synthesized at 200 °C under varying pH and time conditions. It also facilitates the characterization of PG through scanning electron microscopy and X-ray diffraction, while providing thermogravimetric and derivative thermogravimetric curves that detail the thermal distributions of hydroxyapatites. Within this same context, Figure 4 displays the distribution of crystallite sizes determined from the X-ray diffraction patterns, specifically covering HAp Types 1 through 6.

In process and reactor engineering applications, Figure 4 functions as a schematic illustration outlining the experimental procedure and reactor setup. It also acts as a visual representation that maps the dependencies between observed activity ratios and average grain diameter, providing insights into process performance and material behavior.

In fluid dynamics and computational modeling research, Figure 4 presents a mathematical model describing natural convection in PGEF. Because these descriptions originate from independent studies, Figure 4 refers to separate experimental setups, material characterizations, and theoretical models rather than a single unified dataset. Each variation is accurately represented within its respective disciplinary framework, ensuring that all reported analytical, procedural, and modeling aspects are comprehensively accounted for.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4 is a visual representation showing the XRD pattern of the prepared ammonium sulfate sample.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4 presents phase diagrams calculated by FactSage Software for multi-component oxide systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792096852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 5">
  <data key="d0">Figure 5</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 5 illustrates the utilization of oxygen resources within PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5 illustrates structure refinement from XRD data of an HAp sample synthesized at 200 °C, pH 11 for 15 h.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure displaying the infrared spectrum and mineral phases of PGEF.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5 illustrates how BSA adsorption changes the thermal stability of hydroxyapatites.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5 displays SEM micrographs of Type 1 through Type 6 HAp powders.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5 is a visual representation showing the EDX spectrum of the as-prepared hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 5 is a graphical representation showing the ash fusion temperatures of phosphogypsum samples with varying quaternary acidity coefficients.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792097275</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 6">
  <data key="d0">Figure 6</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 6 is a non-unique identifier utilized across multiple independent scientific studies, with each instance representing a distinct visual data presentation. In one research context, Figure 6 displays the effects of various reaction factors on the composition of syngas. In another study, it presents a graphical representation plotting the relationship between temperature and the leachability of radioactive materials and rare earth elements. A third instance illustrates the covalent bond characteristics, electron localization, and bond order density specifically for O-Cd and O-Co bonds.

Regarding material and structural characterization, Figure 6 depicts the FTIR spectra of hydroxyapatite samples designated as HAP1, HAP2, and HAP3, alongside samples with adsorbed protein. It also serves as a visual representation detailing the main chemical composition of phosphogypsum. In the realm of crystallographic and microscopic analysis, Figure 6 displays the XRD patterns of hydrothermally synthesized samples, comparing those prepared with and without the Brij-93 surfactant. Additionally, it presents bright field TEM images that illustrate the morphology of Type 1 HAp structures, a detail consistently noted across the provided descriptions. Collectively, these separate instances of Figure 6 encompass a broad spectrum of analytical techniques, spanning gas composition dynamics, thermal leachability trends, electronic bond properties, spectroscopic material analysis, chemical composition mapping, and microscopic structural imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 6 is a visual representation showing the FTIR spectra of hydroxyapatite powders before and after heat treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 6 illustrates the evolution of trace element leaching concentrations over varying time and temperature conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 6 is a graphical representation illustrating the desulfurization rates and slag properties at different reaction temperatures and times.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 6 is a graphical representation illustrating the effects of reaction conditions on slag sulfur content and desulfurization rate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792097138</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 7">
  <data key="d0">Figure 7</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 7 serves as a recurring label across multiple independent scientific studies, with its specific content and analytical purpose varying significantly depending on the research domain. In investigations involving phosphogypsum, this figure illustrates common modification techniques applied to the material and graphically demonstrates how successive leaching steps affect the removal efficiency of target elements. Within the specific application of chemical looping gasification of low-rank lignite using phosphogypsum, Figure 7 depicts the resulting solid-phase products.

In analytical and materials characterization contexts, the figure presents Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy data used to verify the removal of surfactant residues and identify chemical functional groups. It also displays laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy (LIBS) results that map the identified spectral line wavelengths for various elements. From a computational chemistry perspective, Figure 7 visualizes bond order density and natural adaptive orbital analysis to elucidate underlying interaction mechanisms.

Finally, in nanomaterials research focusing on Type 2 hydroxyapatite (HAp), the figure provides a dark-field transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image paired with a particle size distribution histogram that quantifies the nanoparticle dimensions. Collectively, these distinct representations highlight how the same figure label is utilized across diverse experimental, computational, and analytical frameworks to convey domain-specific data.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 7 is a graphical representation showing thermal analysis curves for PGI-1.4 across different C/S molar ratios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792097138</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium sulfate dihydrate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium sulfate dihydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium sulfate dihydrate is the primary chemical compound composing the PG sample used in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate dihydrate is the primary chemical compound composing the PG sample before the gasification process.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate dihydrate is the specific chemical compound that constitutes the majority of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate dihydrate is the main chemical component of phosphogypsum that participates in hydration reactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-000</data>
