</node>
<node id="Equation E(2)">
  <data key="d0">Equation E(2)</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation E(2) describes the kinetics of the growth of oxygen carrier grains, accounting for drag force effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791090726</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ca10(PO4)6F2">
  <data key="d0">Ca10(PO4)6F2</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ca10(PO4)6F2 is a solid-phase compound containing fluorine and phosphorus present at temperatures up to 500 °C.&lt;SEP&gt;Ca10(PO4)6F2 is the chemical formula for the phosphate rock raw material digested in the wet process.&lt;SEP&gt;Ca10(PO4)6F2 is a precipitate that forms during fluorine immobilization and dissolves at high pH levels, increasing fluoride concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ca4Si2F2O7">
  <data key="d0">Ca4Si2F2O7</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ca4Si2F2O7 is a solid-phase compound formed from the conversion of CaF2 at temperatures between 400 and 500 °C.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791090726</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HF">
  <data key="d0">HF</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">HF is a gas-phase compound that begins to appear when the temperature reaches 600 °C.&lt;SEP&gt;HF is a gaseous compound formed during the acidulation process and from phosphogypsum processing.&lt;SEP&gt;HF is a gaseous byproduct released during the chemical reaction of phosphate rocks with sulfuric acid.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791314176</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Drag Force">
  <data key="d0">Drag Force</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Drag force is the resistance exerted by inert support material on grain boundary motion, influencing growth kinetics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791090726</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surface Energy">
  <data key="d0">Surface Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Surface energy is a physical property of the oxygen carrier and inert material that affects grain boundary dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791090726</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="P">
  <data key="d0">P</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">P represents the phosphorus element whose transformation and migration are analyzed during the CLG process.&lt;SEP&gt;P is the dependent variable representing net or differential pressure in the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791209442</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FD">
  <data key="d0">FD</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">FD denotes the maximum resistance per unit area of the grain boundary to grain boundary motion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791090726</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Grain Boundary">
  <data key="d0">Grain Boundary</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The grain boundary is the interface where grain growth occurs and is subject to drag forces and resistance.&lt;SEP&gt;Grain boundaries are interfaces between different crystalline grains in a material.</data>
