<node id="Cement Retarders">
  <data key="d0">Cement Retarders</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Cement retarders are industrial additives and conventional construction materials primarily utilized in the building sector. Historically, their production relied on natural gypsum; however, contemporary manufacturing increasingly incorporates recycled phosphogypsum (PG), transforming these retarders into a dominant, low-value application for this industrial byproduct. This shift is largely driven by small-scale integrated companies that combine phosphogypsum production with recycling operations. Due to logistical and economic factors, the commercial distribution of cement retarders typically features a limited sales radius, generally confined to within 100 kilometers of the production facility.

From an environmental and technical standpoint, the integration of phosphogypsum into cement retarders plays a critical role in sustainable construction practices. The substitution rate of PG in these formulations is a pivotal variable that significantly influences Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) outcomes. Consequently, this substitution rate serves as a key sensitivity factor when evaluating the overall environmental impact of phosphogypsum cement recycling (PCR) technology. By repurposing industrial waste into functional building products, cement retarders bridge conventional construction material standards with emerging circular economy initiatives, despite currently being categorized as low-value applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Cement Retarders are construction additives manufactured using Anhydrite III.&lt;SEP&gt;A construction material product manufactured using phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Cement retarders are low-value-added construction products constrained by transportation costs and recommended for gradual reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;A low-value-added product derived from phosphogypsum that faces economic constraints due to transportation costs.&lt;SEP&gt;Cement retarders are a current main application for phosphogypsum in China, representing low-value utilization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
