<node id="Impurities">
  <data key="d0">Impurities</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d2">Impurities refer to minor foreign substances and non-target contaminants, primarily consisting of elements such as silicon, aluminum, sodium, iron, magnesium, strontium, potassium, and fluorine, which are present in lower concentrations within phosphogypsum. These contaminants are characterized by their slight to insolubility, their tendency to decompose during heating, and their behavior of leaching alongside rare earth elements during processing.

Because they negatively impact the material's utility, impurities require careful removal or separation throughout industrial processing, a step often highlighted by red circles in process diagrams. Specifically, precipitation is employed to isolate slightly soluble and insoluble impurities during hydrolysis. The presence of these impurities significantly deteriorates the hydration properties of cementitious materials, thereby restricting the allowable phosphogypsum content in construction mixtures. Furthermore, they degrade the performance of calcium phosphogypsum fiber-mixed materials (CPGFM), which necessitates the implementation of additional treatment protocols and supplementary materials to mitigate their adverse effects.</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791721844</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
