<node id="Zinc">
  <data key="d0">Zinc</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Zinc is a chemical element classified as both a trace element and a heavy metal, typically existing as a divalent ion. Naturally, it is highly concentrated in phosphorites, where it commonly associates with organic matter or metallic sulfide precipitates. In industrial contexts, particularly within phosphogypsum, zinc is a prominent trace component with average concentrations of 8 ± 1, significantly exceeding the Earth's crust average and demonstrating a 20–49 percent transfer rate during processing. It is routinely quantified in phosphogypsum minerals and analyzed mixtures using techniques such as LA-ICP-MS, with concentrations typically measured in milligrams per liter and recorded in associated tabular data.

Due to its environmental persistence and ecotoxicity potential, zinc is formally regulated in water quality standards with a documented concentration limit of 5 mg/L. Its environmental behavior is notably pH-dependent, as aquatic concentrations decrease substantially alongside cadmium when pH levels rise. During Toxicity Characteristic Leaching Procedure (TCLP) testing, zinc is measured as a heavy metal whose leaching concentration often approaches or slightly exceeds USEPA limits, particularly in optimal mixes and specific formulations like P12 and P13. Despite these exceedances in certain formulations, zinc is generally present in phosphogypsum at concentrations lower than the rare effect concentration, with some analyses recording leaching levels as low as 1.47 µg/L. Consequently, it is actively monitored in phosphogypsum solutions and lifecycle inventory data as a liquid emission, with specific groundwater discharge quantities documented, such as 5.71 × 10⁻³ kilograms.

In agricultural and environmental applications, zinc acts as a potential contaminant that can cause slight soil pollution when phosphogypsum is used as an amendment, notably in regions like the Mississippi Delta and New Orleans. To mitigate this contamination, zinc is actively targeted for removal from aqueous solutions. Pre-conditioned phosphogypsum serves as a low-cost adsorbent with high capacity, a process heavily controlled by pH levels. Alternatively, nanocrystalline and synthetic hydroxyapatite effectively removes zinc via an ion-exchange mechanism, which is similarly hindered by low acidity.

Beyond its environmental roles, zinc serves valuable functional applications in material science and agriculture. As a metal dopant, it produces absorption features in the UV-vis region that closely resemble those of pure titanium dioxide. In agricultural settings, zinc is specifically doped into or impregnated within nano-hydroxyapatite particles. This integration reduces overall particle size, significantly enhances fertilizer performance by leveraging zinc's properties as an essential trace element for optimized crop delivery, and enables controlled protein release.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-02