<node id="Trace Elements">
  <data key="d0">Trace Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Trace Elements are minor chemical constituents and impurities found within phosphogypsum and its source materials, typically quantified in parts per million. This category encompasses a diverse range of metallic and non-metallic components, including lead, copper, cadmium, nickel, strontium, zinc, arsenic, uranium, chromium, iron, fluorine, barium, scandium, and selenium. Their concentrations vary regionally and are frequently documented in chemical composition tables. Because these elements are incorporated into phosphogypsum in various chemical forms, accurately determining their concentrations and assessing their environmental availability presents significant challenges. Consequently, research efforts focus on partitioning these components, analyzing their solubility, and identifying the specific solubility-controlling solids that govern their presence and behavior within the material.

The environmental and biological dynamics of Trace Elements are heavily influenced by the acidic conditions often associated with phosphogypsum, which can cause these constituents to remain dissolved and highly mobile. This mobility directly impacts the material's environmental behavior and extraction potential, necessitating thorough analysis of their distribution across different ecosystems. To evaluate their leaching behavior and trends, researchers routinely apply the Toxicity Characteristic Leaching Procedure (TCLP) test to raw phosphogypsum, phosphogypsum mixes, and leached samples. Understanding the solubility, mobility, and bioaccumulation of these elements is critical, as they readily enter ecological pathways by being taken up by plants from the soil and subsequently ingested by humans and animals. Therefore, comprehensive analysis of Trace Elements is essential for accurately evaluating both the environmental impact of phosphogypsum and its associated ecological and biological risks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013</data>
