<node id="CaCO3">
  <data key="d0">CaCO3</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">CaCO3 is the chemical formula representing calcium carbonate.&lt;SEP&gt;CaCO3 is formed as a final byproduct when Ca2+ reacts with HCO3- during the extraction process.&lt;SEP&gt;CaCO3 is a chemical compound used in the thermal decomposition process to recover potassium from K-feldspar.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium carbonate, a compound used as a primary reactant in the described experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791593110</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ca2+">
  <data key="d0">Ca2+</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ca2+ is the chemical formula representing the calcium ion.&lt;SEP&gt;Ca2+ is a divalent cation that typically occupies the Me site in apatite structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Ca2+ is a calcium ion that combines with phosphate ions to form Ca9(PO4)6 clusters.&lt;SEP&gt;Ca2+ is a calcium ion that rapidly reacts with phosphate species to form insoluble calcium phosphate precipitates.&lt;SEP&gt;Ca2+ is a calcium ion present in the PG leach liquor at a concentration of 15.45 g/L.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791813191</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
