<node id="Absorbed Dose">
  <data key="d0">Absorbed Dose</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Absorbed Dose refers to the average density of energy absorbed by a mass of cells from radiation exposure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791183363</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gray">
  <data key="d0">Gray</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Gray is the SI unit for absorbed dose, equivalent to one joule of energy per kilogram of mass.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791183363</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dose Equivalent">
  <data key="d0">Dose Equivalent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dose Equivalent measures both the energy absorbed by tissue and its biological impact from radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;A calculated value obtained by multiplying radiation exposure by a weighting factor that accounts for organ radiosensitivity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791183363</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sieverts">
  <data key="d0">Sieverts</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Sieverts are the units used to measure dose equivalent, accounting for the biological impact of different radiation types.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791183363</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gamma Radiation">
  <data key="d0">Gamma Radiation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gamma Radiation is a form of external, aerial radioactive exposure primarily originating from phosphogypsum storage stacks and gypsum stacks. This radiation is emitted directly from these facilities due to the presence of Radium-226 and its subsequent decay products. Characterized by a small quality factor, the absorbed dose of Gamma Radiation typically equals the equivalent dose in humans. As a direct exposure risk, it poses potential hazards to both humans and animals. To manage this risk, Gamma Radiation levels are routinely measured, often expressed as a safety metric in millisieverts per year (mSv y-1), to evaluate radiological exposure against established public dose limits.

In industrial applications, particularly during brick sintering, phosphogypsum is incorporated into construction materials, resulting in external Gamma Radiation emitted by the finished bricks. When phosphogypsum is utilized at concentrations up to 30%, the resulting external radiation exposure is reduced to less than one mS. This demonstrates that while Gamma Radiation presents a direct radiological hazard during storage and handling, its emission can be effectively quantified and mitigated through regulated industrial practices and controlled material composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Gamma radiation is a form of external exposure originating from radionuclides in phosphogypsum building materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791424749</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphoria Formation">
  <data key="d0">Phosphoria Formation</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The Phosphoria Formation is a specific geological deposit where trace element enrichment patterns differ from average phosphorites.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphoria Formation is a geological formation containing phosphorites, studied for its compositional features.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002</data>
