<node id="Wet Phosphoric Acid Process">
  <data key="d0">Wet Phosphoric Acid Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Wet Phosphoric Acid Process is an industrial chemical manufacturing method primarily utilized for the production of phosphoric acid. A central feature of this procedure is the generation and subsequent treatment of phosphogypsum as a byproduct. The process fundamentally influences the major elemental composition of this byproduct, resulting in phosphogypsum that exhibits high impurity levels and a distinct, typical morphology.

Additionally, the Wet Phosphoric Acid Process disrupts natural radioactive equilibrium by partitioning various radionuclides across different phases. Specifically, the method directs Lead-210 (Pb-210) into the phosphoric acid stream, while Polonium-210 is selectively partitioned into the phosphogypsum. Through these mechanisms, the process dictates both the physical structure and the chemical and radiological profile of its outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;The wet phosphoric acid process is the industrial method that generates phosphogypsum as a byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;The Wet Phosphoric Acid Process is an industrial chemical procedure that generates phosphogypsum as a byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;The industrial process that produces phosphogypsum as a by-product, currently relying on a dihydrate method.&lt;SEP&gt;A traditional method for producing phosphogypsum, characterized by a blue background and involving phosphate ore and sulfuric acid.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791770406</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Po-210">
  <data key="d0">Po-210</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A radionuclide that partitions primarily into phosphogypsum, with up to 99% appearing in the gypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Po-210 is a radioactive isotope measured in Florida with recorded values of 1030 and 1480.&lt;SEP&gt;Polonium-210 is a relatively long-lived radioactive daughter product formed from the decay of Radon-222.&lt;SEP&gt;A relatively long-lived alpha emitter formed from the decay of Lead-210.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188410</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dihydrate">
  <data key="d0">Dihydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">A calcium sulfate form whose recrystallization is slowed by the presence of stabilized hemihydrate sulfate structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Dihydrate is a crystalline form of calcium sulfate produced during phosphogypsum formation depending on crystallization temperature.&lt;SEP&gt;Dihydrate is a hydrated mineral phase of calcium sulfate found in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Dihydrate is a crystalline form of phosphate produced during wet processes for phosphate extraction.&lt;SEP&gt;Dihydrate is the most common crystal type of calcium sulphate found in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791917129</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Central Florida Rock">
  <data key="d0">Central Florida Rock</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A source of phosphate rock known for producing phosphogypsum with the highest reported Ra-226 activities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188559</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Idaho">
  <data key="d0">Idaho</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Idaho is a geographic location representing a source of phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Idaho is a U.S. state serving as a primary source for phosphogypsum and phosphate rock samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A geographic location associated with phosphate rock that yields phosphogypsum with potentially high radionuclide activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Idaho is a geographic region referenced in the dataset for radioactive isotope measurements.&lt;SEP&gt;Idaho is a geographic region where phosphogypsum samples were analyzed and compared for chemical composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425570</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Syria">
  <data key="d0">Syria</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Syria is a country and geographic region prominently featured in environmental and radiological research, particularly concerning the production and analysis of phosphogypsum. As a significant global production site, Syria generates approximately 0.35 million tons of phosphogypsum annually, contributing substantially to worldwide production statistics. The country serves as the origin for one of the phosphorite ores that are mixed to produce this industrial byproduct. The phosphate rock extracted in Syria yields phosphogypsum characterized by potentially high radionuclide activities, making it a focal point for radioactive isotope measurements in scientific datasets.

Extensive environmental and radiological assessments have been conducted across Syria. Researchers have investigated the radiological impacts and gamma radiation emissions associated with phosphogypsum piles within the country. Additionally, phosphogypsum samples sourced from Syria have been rigorously analyzed to determine the distribution of trace metals and measure Polonium-210 concentrations. Beyond industrial sites, the country's coastal regions near phosphogypsum-related industrial facilities have been evaluated for ecological risks and heavy metal contamination, highlighting comprehensive efforts to monitor the environmental footprint of phosphogypsum production and handling in Syria.&lt;SEP&gt;Syria is a country in Asia with an annual phosphogypsum production of 0.35 Mt.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720918</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Th-228">
  <data key="d0">Th-228</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A radionuclide measured in phosphogypsum samples from Australia with a mean activity of 5.8 Bq kg-1.&lt;SEP&gt;Th-228 is a radioactive isotope measured in Australia and Florida with varying concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;A thorium isotope commonly found in groundwaters.&lt;SEP&gt;Th-228 is a radioactive isotope measured at AMAX, USSAC, Plant A, Plant B, and Plant C.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022</data>
