<node id="Phosphate Groups">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Groups</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Tetrahedral structural units that form the compact framework of the hydroxyapatite crystal lattice.&lt;SEP&gt;Hard Lewis base components within hydroxyapatite that facilitate cation binding through surface complexation.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate groups are chemical moieties in hydroxyapatite that stabilize gold nanoparticles at lower temperatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate groups are chemical functional groups identified by strong absorption dips around 1000 cm⁻¹ and 600 cm⁻¹ representing symmetric stretching and bending modes.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate groups are functional components of the AL molecule structure that facilitate binding to calcium.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791469856</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydroxyl Groups">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxyl Groups</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydroxyl Groups are chemical moieties and functional components, typically existing as OH⁻ ions, that play significant structural and chemical roles, particularly within the crystal lattice of hydroxyapatite. Structurally, these groups reside in specific crystal channels and are directly bonded to calcium ions, where they serve to balance the positive charge of the surrounding matrix. Functionally, they act as hard Lewis bases that facilitate cation binding through surface complexation. In catalytic and nanomaterial applications, hydroxyl groups assist in the activation of formaldehyde and help stabilize gold nanoparticles at elevated temperatures.

Spectroscopically, hydroxyl groups are characterized by distinct O–H stretching vibrations and intramolecular stretching modes. These vibrational features manifest in infrared spectra as a sharp dip around 3600 cm⁻¹, as well as broader absorption bands and stretching features in the 3400–3500 cm⁻¹ range. Together, these structural, functional, and spectroscopic attributes define the multifaceted role of hydroxyl groups in hydroxyapatite-based systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical groups commonly found in hydrated minerals like gypsum, responsible for broad absorption bands in the spectrum.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyl Groups are structural components present in minimal quantities in bone apatite but higher in tooth enamel.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical bonds (O-H) identified at 533.6 eV binding energy in the phosphogypsum spectrum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791967022</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
