<node id="Synthetic Hydroxyapatite">
  <data key="d0">Synthetic Hydroxyapatite</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Synthetic hydroxyapatite is a manufactured form of the compound characterized by higher purity and specific surface area compared to natural sources.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic hydroxyapatite is a nano-scale material studied for its defluoridation chemistry and equilibrium properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic Hydroxyapatite is a manufactured material produced from phosphogypsum, designed to mimic natural bone ash for industrial applications.&lt;SEP&gt;A synthetic material produced via a hydrothermal method from phosphogypsum, characterized for potential use as a bone ash substitute.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791558350</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Hydroxyapatite">
  <data key="d0">Natural Hydroxyapatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Natural hydroxyapatite is derived from bioresources and differs from synthetic forms due to lower purity, higher substitution, and lower specific surface area.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural hydroxyapatite is a biomineral extracted from Corbicula moltkiana freshwater clam shells for synthesis purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural Hydroxyapatite is the dominant structure in natural bone ash after calcination, serving as a benchmark for synthetic particles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-001</data>
