<node id="Heterogeneous Catalysis">
  <data key="d0">Heterogeneous Catalysis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Heterogeneous catalysis is a scientific field where hydroxyapatite has been previously studied, though not the focus of this review.&lt;SEP&gt;Heterogeneous Catalysis is a field where solid catalysts' acid-base properties play a key role in driving catalytic performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Heterogeneous Catalysis is a field where the acid-base properties of solid catalysts drive the activity and selectivity of chemical reactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Heterogeneous catalysis involves using solid catalysts, such as hydroxyapatite-supported materials, to drive chemical reactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Heterogeneous catalysis is a chemical application domain where hydroxyapatite is utilized due to its structural properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791655882</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FTIR">
  <data key="d0">FTIR</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy, commonly abbreviated as FTIR and occasionally referred to as Fourier Transformed-Infrared Spectroscopy, is a fundamental spectroscopic and analytical characterization technique. By measuring the absorption of infrared light, FTIR identifies specific chemical bonds, molecular vibrations, and functional groups, thereby providing detailed insights into the chemical composition and structural properties of diverse inorganic and organic materials. The method is widely utilized across materials science and biomedical research to evaluate and characterize complex substances.

A primary application of FTIR involves the detailed analysis of hydroxyapatite (HAp) and apatite-based systems, including carbonated hydroxyapatite powders and various synthesized HAp samples. The technique is routinely employed to verify crystal structures, distinguish specific HAp phases through characteristic phosphate (PO4) and hydroxyl (OH) ion peaks, and detect organic functional groups within the HAp matrix. Furthermore, FTIR facilitates the study of ion accommodation mechanisms, such as carbonate ion substitution within apatite crystal lattices, and has been shown to yield results consistent with X-ray diffraction (XRD) findings regarding the inhibitory effects of HPA on crystal growth.

Beyond apatite characterization, FTIR serves as a versatile analytical tool for examining protein structures and confirming the successful loading or binding of biomolecules onto HAp surfaces. It is also applied to analyze the spectra of specific compounds like the Brij-93 surfactant and to investigate chemical interactions between adsorbents and adsorbed metal ions. Through these diverse applications, FTIR remains an indispensable method for comprehensive material and biochemical analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR is an analytical technique utilized to characterize the synthesized hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR is an analytical method used to confirm the presence of specific chemical bonds within the samples.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR is an analytical technique used to confirm the presence of oxygen vacancies on the O-H group of HAp.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791966043</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biodiesel">
  <data key="d0">Biodiesel</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Biodiesel is an alternative fuel source produced to replace depleting fossil fuels and support sustainable energy production.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodiesel is a fuel whose production yield is evaluated using hydroxyapatite and other catalysts.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodiesel is a renewable fuel produced using heterogeneous catalysts derived from waste materials like chicken bones.&lt;SEP&gt;A renewable fuel produced using hydroxyapatite-supported CaO-CeO catalysts.</data>
