<node id="HapD">
  <data key="d0">HapD</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">HapD is a calcium-deficient variant of hydroxyapatite characterized by a higher density of acid sites due to abundant HPO4 2- groups and OH- vacancies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247343</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ca/P Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Ca/P Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The Ca/P Ratio is a chemical and stoichiometric metric that quantifies the atomic or molar proportion of calcium to phosphorus within synthesized hydroxyapatite and related biological materials. This quantitative measure plays a critical role in evaluating the structural integrity, acid-base properties, and industrial applicability of hydroxyapatite particles, while also serving as a key indicator for assessing sample purity against established bone hydroxyapatite standards. During the synthesis process, the ratio is typically maintained at a theoretical value of 1.67 to ensure proper stoichiometry and optimal material formation.

To determine the actual elemental composition, the Ca/P Ratio is commonly measured using Inductively Coupled Plasma Optical Emission Spectroscopy (ICP-OES). When comparing synthetic hydroxyapatite and bone ash samples, empirical measurements often reveal values that deviate from the theoretical target, with reported figures of approximately 2.46 for synthetic hydroxyapatite and 2.78 for bone ash. Despite these numerical differences, the ratios are generally considered comparable in terms of structural similarity, highlighting the metric's utility in characterizing both synthetic and naturally derived calcium phosphate materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Ca/P Ratio is the stoichiometric calcium-to-phosphorus ratio set to 1.67 in the sample preparation solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ca/P ratio is a chemical parameter of hydroxyapatite that influences amino acid and protein adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ca/P ratio is a stoichiometric metric that varies depending on synthesis time and conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Ca/P Ratio is the molar ratio of calcium to phosphorus in the starting materials, set at 1.67 for the synthesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791857078</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Brönsted Acid Sites">
  <data key="d0">Brönsted Acid Sites</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Brönsted Acid Sites are a type of acid site in hydroxyapatite derived from HPO4 2- species.&lt;SEP&gt;Brönsted Acid Sites are surface locations on apatites associated with hydroxyl vacancies where adsorption occurs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006</data>
