<node id="Crystallinity">
  <data key="d0">Crystallinity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Crystallinity is a fundamental structural and material property that quantifies the degree of structural order within a substance, specifically representing the proportion of its crystalline phase relative to the amorphous phase. In the context of hydroxyapatite and related samples, including those designated as CC, it serves as a critical parameter for evaluating material behavior and performance. The degree of crystallinity is typically assessed quantitatively, with values such as 90.4% reported in synthesized hydroxyapatite, and is characterized through analytical techniques including X-ray diffraction, Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, and other spectroscopic methods that monitor features like hydroxyl group peak intensity.

This property significantly influences the physicochemical characteristics of the material. Higher crystallinity exhibits a direct correlation with ion adsorption capacity and photocatalytic activity under ultraviolet irradiation, while simultaneously demonstrating an inverse relationship with specific surface area and overall sorption capacity. Additionally, increased crystallinity is associated with a slower rate of ion release and contributes to greater regularity in particle shape. The structural order is highly sensitive to compositional and processing variables. It is generally enhanced by heat treatment, calcination temperatures, phase breakdown conditions, and decreasing citrate concentrations. Conversely, crystallinity is typically reduced by the incorporation of carbonate, increased a/c ratios, and the presence of foreign phases. However, the effect of surfactant addition presents conflicting observations, with some studies indicating a reduction while others report an increase, suggesting that the outcome may depend on specific experimental conditions or surfactant types. Overall, crystallinity remains an essential structural parameter that provides vital insights into the synthesis, characterization, and functional performance of hydroxyapatite-based materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Crystallinity represents the degree of structural order in the synthesized materials, which increases with longer reaction durations.&lt;SEP&gt;A material property confirmed by the FFT pattern in Panel (e).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791984880</data>
