</node>
<node id="HAp">
  <data key="d0">HAp</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">HAp, the standard abbreviation for hydroxyapatite, is a synthesized nanocrystalline material extensively studied in materials and biomaterials science. It predominantly exists as rod-shaped particles, nanorods, or microcrystals that feature specialized hierarchical structures and mesoporous characteristics. The synthesis of HAp typically involves hydrothermal conversion, where it develops as the final crystalline phase after 15 hours of reaction at a pH of 11. Its structural evolution and crystallization are significantly influenced by the shape transformation of CC, while its growth and final morphology can be precisely regulated through the addition of CTAB and citrate.

In research contexts, HAp is frequently characterized as a target material, with its morphology and functional groups undergoing detailed analysis. Due to its unique physicochemical properties, it serves multiple practical applications, functioning primarily as a biomaterial substrate for protein adsorption and as an effective mesoporous drug carrier. These combined attributes establish HAp as a versatile and highly regulated nanomaterial in biomedical and chemical engineering studies.&lt;SEP&gt;HAp is hydroxyapatite, the target material synthesized from PPG, characterized by its crystalline structure and [Ca]/[P] ratio.&lt;SEP&gt;HAp is a synthesized hydroxyapatite material analyzed for its chemical composition and textural characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Pristine hydroxyapatite semiconductor sample used as a baseline for photocatalytic performance.&lt;SEP&gt;HAp is a hydroxyapatite material synthesized for photocatalytic applications, exhibiting specific flat band potentials and charge separation properties.&lt;SEP&gt;HAp is the standard abbreviation for Hydroxyapatite, the target ceramic material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-024</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791965185</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Citrate">
  <data key="d0">Citrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Citrate is a bioorganic micromolecule used as an additive to regulate the nucleation and crystal growth of hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrate is a molecule that chelates calcium ions, provides nucleation sites for HAp, and modifies crystal aggregation modes.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrate is a chelating agent that controls calcium ion release and promotes the orderly self-assembly of HAp building blocks.&lt;SEP&gt;Citrate is a chemical compound used synergistically with CTAB to control the morphology of hydroxyapatite microcrystals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791967780</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium Citrate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Citrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium Citrate is a compound formed when citrate chelates calcium ions, serving as unstable colloid nanoparticle clusters that provide nucleation sites.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003</data>
