<node id="Zeta Potential">
  <data key="d0">Zeta Potential</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Zeta Potential is a physicochemical property that quantifies the effective surface charge of dispersed materials, including nanoparticles, biochars, and hydroxyapatite particles or dispersions. It is measured in millivolts and serves as a critical indicator of a material's electrochemical characteristics and surface reactivity. The value is typically determined through electrophoresis and is frequently utilized as the dependent variable plotted on the vertical axis of two-dimensional line graphs.

A defining characteristic of Zeta Potential is its strong dependence on environmental conditions, most notably pH. As pH levels increase, the potential generally decreases, becoming more negative, a shift that directly promotes electrostatic interactions and enhances adsorption processes. In addition to pH, the measurement varies with solution concentration and is heavily influenced by the adsorption and desorption of ions. Specific formulation parameters also play a role; for instance, slightly lowering citrate concentration can reduce the potential, while the synthesis method significantly impacts the electrochemical profile of hydroxyapatite.

In practical applications, Zeta Potential is extensively used to study the surface charge properties of materials suspended in various media, such as hydroxyapatite particles in simulated body fluid solution. By tracking how these surface charge characteristics shift under different conditions, researchers can effectively predict colloidal stability, evaluate adsorption capacities, and optimize material performance across diverse scientific and industrial contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791747660</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stirring Time">
  <data key="d0">Stirring Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stirring Time is a parameter that regulates the final morphology of crystals, driving the transformation from spheres to nanorod clusters.&lt;SEP&gt;Stirring time is a critical experimental parameter that influences the shape transformation of CC and the resulting morphology of HAp products.&lt;SEP&gt;The duration of contact between the two phases, with 40 minutes identified as sufficient for maximum extraction.&lt;SEP&gt;A process parameter set at 40 minutes during the extraction experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791813073</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcining">
  <data key="d0">Calcining</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcining is a thermal treatment applied to the samples that preserves phosphate bands but reveals carbonate existence forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcining is a thermal treatment process applied at 800 °C or 1000 °C to analyze sample structure and remove organic components.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791259821</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nanorods">
  <data key="d0">Nanorods</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Nanorods are elongated, rod-like, and needle-like nanoscale structures or crystals that typically measure between 20 and 40 nanometers in width. Functioning as fundamental structural and nanoscale building blocks, they pack in an orderly manner or radiate outward from a central core to assemble into larger hierarchical architectures. These assembled structures commonly manifest as spherical agglomerates, microspheres, clusters, or starburst formations that exhibit varying degrees of opacity. When examined via transmission electron microscopy, nanorods appear as distinct elongated particles that frequently form tightly packed, agglomerated clusters.

In both biological and synthetic contexts, nanorods are primarily identified as the characteristic morphological structures of hydroxyapatite crystals, existing in naturally occurring dental enamel as well as in laboratory-synthesized materials. Their physical properties, including particle dimensions and agglomeration states, are directly governed by the specific synthesis methodology and the concentration of surfactants used during production. Because of these variable synthesis parameters, the precise definition and morphological traits of nanorods can differ across studies. Furthermore, these nanostructures are not always static; they can undergo structural evolution during the assembly process, eventually transforming into nano-needles as the hierarchical organization progresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanorods are a specific crystalline morphology of hydroxyapatite characterized by high length-to-diameter ratios.&lt;SEP&gt;The rod-like particle morphology adopted by HAp crystals, particularly when synthesized with Brij-93 surfactant.&lt;SEP&gt;Aggregated or individual rod-shaped HAp structures whose agglomeration is prevented by surfactant.&lt;SEP&gt;One-dimensional nanostructures formed in P123-modified samples that facilitate distinct electron transport pathways.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791967780</data>
