<node id="Fourier Transform Infrared">
  <data key="d0">Fourier Transform Infrared</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A spectroscopic technique used to generate spectral curves representing molecular vibrations and chemical bonds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791260619</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure A">
  <data key="d0">Figure A</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A stacked line graph presenting six distinct spectral curves arranged vertically.&lt;SEP&gt;A grouped bar chart with line overlays that visualizes the relationship between HPA doping amount and metal ion adsorption capacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791656147</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Curve A">
  <data key="d0">Curve A</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The bottom-most spectral curve in the stacked line graph, exhibiting the most complex pattern with multiple distinct peaks and troughs.&lt;SEP&gt;Curve A is the blue line on the graph representing the particle size data for the Surf sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Curve B">
  <data key="d0">Curve B</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A spectral curve located above Curve A, showing a similar general shape but with less pronounced peaks.&lt;SEP&gt;Curve B is the orange/red line on the graph representing the particle size data for the NoSurf sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Curve C">
  <data key="d0">Curve C</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A spectral curve positioned above Curve B in the stacked line graph.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791260619</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Curve D">
  <data key="d0">Curve D</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A spectral curve positioned above Curve C in the stacked line graph.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791260619</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Curve E">
  <data key="d0">Curve E</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A spectral curve showing sharper, more defined peaks, particularly in the 1000–1400 cm⁻¹ range.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791260619</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Curve F">
  <data key="d0">Curve F</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The top-most spectral curve displaying distinct peaks similar to Curve E.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791260619</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C-H Stretch">
  <data key="d0">C-H Stretch</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A chemical vibration feature indicated by arrows pointing to small peaks around 2900 cm⁻¹ in Curve A.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791260619</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="V3 CO3">
  <data key="d0">V3 CO3</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A chemical annotation referring to the asymmetric stretching vibration of carbonate ions, located in the 1400–1500 cm⁻¹ range.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791260619</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="O-H Stretching">
  <data key="d0">O-H Stretching</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A broad, shallow dip around 3400–3500 cm⁻¹ in Curve A, characteristic of water or hydroxyl groups.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791260619</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfate Groups">
  <data key="d0">Sulfate Groups</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Chemical groups responsible for a significant, broad absorption feature around 1000–1100 cm⁻¹ across all spectral curves.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate groups are chemical components used as a reference denominator to normalize the carbonate signal in the calibration equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate Groups are chemical groups characteristic of PPG that are absent in the synthesized HAp.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791965346</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonate Ions">
  <data key="d0">Carbonate Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbonate ions are chemical species (CO3 2-) that originate from the dissociation of carbonic acid and readily react with divalent cations to form solid precipitates. In various material and environmental contexts, they function as atmospheric components or trace impurities that significantly influence the chemical composition of target structures. Within crystalline frameworks, particularly apatite and hydroxyapatite lattices, carbonate ions frequently substitute for phosphate or hydroxyl groups during synthesis. This substitution occurs both within the bulk lattice and on the surfaces of synthesized powders, with adsorption being notably enhanced on surfactant-produced powders due to their increased surface area.

Analytically, carbonate ions are routinely quantified as a percentage impurity in samples such as phosphogypsum, utilizing derived calibration equations for precise measurement. Spectroscopically, they manifest as a distinct chemical group exhibiting two vibrational modes within the sample structure. Their asymmetric stretching vibrations are particularly characteristic, becoming increasingly sharp and distinct under specific analytical conditions, such as those documented in curves E and F. Together, these properties define their behavior across synthesis, structural integration, and analytical characterization.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonate ions are adsorbed onto the surface of Hydroxyapatite, with adsorption capacity increased by the presence of CTAB.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonate Ions partially substitute phosphate groups in the Hydroxyapatite lattice due to atmospheric carbon dioxide capture.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791965185</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
