<node id="Grinding">
  <data key="d0">Grinding</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Grinding is a processing step applied after calcination to produce the final powder.&lt;SEP&gt;Grinding is a mechanical treatment used to reduce particle size and improve the recovery of rare earth elements from phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical treatment investigated to enhance rare earth element leaching from phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Grinding is a physical modification technique used to improve the particle size and distribution of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Grinding is a physical preparation method applied to phosphogypsum to reduce particle size and enhance leaching efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Grinding is a physicochemical pre-treatment technique applied to increase rare earth element concentration in phosphogypsum prior to extraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792041352</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cake">
  <data key="d0">Cake</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Cake is the solid residue obtained after filtration, which is processed into the final product.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791272396</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Drying">
  <data key="d0">Drying</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Drying is a thermal and physical processing step primarily employed to remove moisture from various materials following synthesis, washing, or filtration. It functions as a critical downstream and final preparation operation designed to solidify, stabilize, and prepare the resulting products for subsequent use. The fundamental purpose of this procedure remains consistent across applications, serving as an essential phase in material processing workflows.

The apparent variations in temperature and duration do not represent conflicting definitions but rather reflect the application of drying to distinct materials and synthesis pathways. For example, when processing a cake or general synthesized materials, the process is typically conducted at 80 °C, occasionally extending for 24 hours as a controlled synthesis condition. In the recovery of calcium carbonate after filtration, drying is applied at 100 °C, whereas phosphogypsum requires a physical preparation step at 110 °C for two hours. Additionally, the technique is frequently utilized as a final step to solidify and prepare hydroxyapatite (HAp) particles or powder. The exact procedural approach and thermal conditions can vary significantly across different methodologies, notably differing from established protocols such as the one described by Smolen et al., which highlights the adaptable nature of drying across diverse chemical and material processing contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791856141</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Evaporation">
  <data key="d0">Evaporation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaporation is applied to the filtrate to recover ammonium sulfate as a byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;A processing step used to recover sulfate compounds from the supernatant liquid.&lt;SEP&gt;Evaporation is a process step used to decrease solution volume and increase rare earth element concentration during indirect leaching.&lt;SEP&gt;A concentration technique used to increase the initial concentration of rare earth elements in the leach solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Evaporation is the technique used to concentrate the stripping solution to a volume of 200 mL.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791811934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Potassium Dihydrogen Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Potassium Dihydrogen Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Potassium dihydrogen phosphate (KH₂PO₄) is a chemical compound that serves as a fundamental reactant and precursor in various material synthesis pathways. It is consistently utilized alongside phosphogypsum, functioning either as a starting material mixed directly with the gypsum or as a co-reactant within the synthesis mixture. The compound is integral to alternative synthesis routes, particularly those employing hydrothermal conditions, where it facilitates the controlled formation of target materials.

In the specific context of hydroxyapatite (HAp) production, potassium dihydrogen phosphate plays a dual and complementary role. It is characterized as both a primary phosphate source and a secondary precursor, a distinction that reflects its versatile function depending on the exact stoichiometry and synthesis protocol. As a primary phosphate source, it supplies essential potassium and phosphate ions, while its designation as a secondary precursor highlights its supportive role in balancing the calcium and phosphate contributions derived from phosphogypsum. Through these combined mechanisms, potassium dihydrogen phosphate acts as a critical chemical reactant that, when processed hydrothermally with phosphogypsum, enables the efficient and alternative synthesis of hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium Dihydrogen Phosphate is a chemical reactant utilized in precipitation and reflux synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium dihydrogen phosphate is a phosphate source purchased for the synthesis process.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate precursor added to the CTAB surfactant solution to initiate calcium phosphate layer formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium Dihydrogen Phosphate is a chemical compound introduced into the CTAB surfactant solution to facilitate electrostatic interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical compound serving as the primary source of Phosphorus in the reaction equation.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical compound ($\mathrm{KH_2PO_4}$) used as a primary component in buffer preparation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792069104</data>
