<node id="BC">
  <data key="d0">BC</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">BC, which stands for biochar, is a carbonaceous adsorbent material primarily utilized for the removal of heavy metal ions from aqueous solutions, including mixed and competitive adsorption environments. In its pristine form, it functions as a base adsorbent, though specific variants can be derived from rice straw to serve as a porous substrate for hydroxyapatite nanoparticles. Throughout various scientific studies, BC is consistently analyzed alongside the HAP-BC nanocomposite to evaluate its adsorption performance and surface characteristics.

When compared directly to the HAP-BC nanocomposite, BC demonstrates that its adsorption capacities are surpassed by the composite material. Physicochemically, BC exhibits distinct surface charge properties, becoming significantly more negatively charged at higher pH levels relative to HAP-BC. In graphical representations of zeta potential data, BC is typically denoted by a black line with square markers. These combined attributes highlight its role as a foundational carbonaceous material in adsorption research and nanocomposite development.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287451</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HAP-BC">
  <data key="d0">HAP-BC</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HAP-BC is a hydroxyapatite-biochar nanocomposite synthesized for environmental remediation and heavy metal adsorption applications. Structurally, the material consists of hydroxyapatite loaded onto the surface of biochar, a configuration that yields distinct X-ray diffraction (XRD) and Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectral patterns. Designed as a modified biochar adsorbent, HAP-BC is specifically engineered to enhance ion adsorption capabilities and demonstrates superior uptake efficiency and removal performance compared to both unmodified biochar and its individual constituent components. The composite is highly effective at extracting heavy metal ions, including Pb(II), Cu(II), and Zn(II), from mixed aqueous solutions, and its practical utility is validated through sorption and regeneration experiments that highlight its competitive adsorption capacity.

Physicochemically, HAP-BC exhibits favorable surface charge characteristics, maintaining a higher zeta potential than pure biochar across increasing pH levels. In analytical representations, such as zeta potential graphs, the material is distinctly plotted as a sample represented by a red line with circular markers, underscoring its consistent electrostatic behavior and structural stability. Overall, HAP-BC functions as a novel, highly efficient adsorbent that successfully integrates the properties of hydroxyapatite and biochar to deliver enhanced remediation performance for heavy metal contamination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Langmuir Isotherm Model">
  <data key="d0">Langmuir Isotherm Model</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Langmuir Isotherm Model is a mathematical framework used to calculate maximum adsorption capacity and interpret monolayer adsorption data.&lt;SEP&gt;Langmuir isotherm model is a mathematical framework used to calculate and predict the maximum adsorption capacity of adsorbent materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-000</data>
