  <data key="d5">1791656147</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Freundlich Isotherm Model">
  <data key="d0">Freundlich Isotherm Model</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Freundlich Isotherm Model is a mathematical framework used to interpret multilayer adsorption data on heterogeneous surfaces.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287728</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adsorption Capacity">
  <data key="d0">Adsorption Capacity</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Adsorption Capacity is a quantitative metric representing the maximum mass of adsorbate retained per unit mass of adsorbent.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption capacity indicates how much adsorbate can be adsorbed, represented by the Freundlich constant.&lt;SEP&gt;A quantitative measure of the amount of heavy metal ions adsorbed per gram of adsorbent.&lt;SEP&gt;A quantitative metric measuring the amount of heavy metal ions adsorbed per gram of adsorbent, used for comparative analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791654288</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pb(II)">
  <data key="d0">Pb(II)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Pb(II) is a lead ion classified as a heavy metal ion that has been extensively studied for its targeted removal from aqueous solutions. Environmental research has systematically evaluated its adsorption behavior onto various nanocomposite materials, with particular emphasis on biochar (BC) and hydroxyapatite-modified biochar (HAP-BC). As a critical target pollutant, Pb(II) serves as a benchmark for assessing the efficacy of these adsorbents in heavy metal remediation.

The adsorption process for Pb(II) is characterized by monolayer coverage principles and closely follows the Langmuir adsorption model. Experimental studies have thoroughly analyzed its adsorption kinetics, removal capacity, removal percentage, and underlying removal mechanisms. Notably, Pb(II) exhibits the highest removal capacity among all metals tested in these comparative studies. Additionally, its competitive adsorption dynamics have been rigorously evaluated across varying pH levels, demonstrating consistent affinity and stability under different environmental conditions. Collectively, these findings underscore Pb(II)'s significance in advancing efficient, model-based strategies for heavy metal ion sequestration.&lt;SEP&gt;Pb(II) is a heavy metal ion targeted for adsorption using organophosphonate-modified hydroxyapatites.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791655882</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zn(II)">
  <data key="d0">Zn(II)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Zn(II) is a zinc ion classified as a heavy metal that is frequently targeted for removal from aqueous solutions. Its adsorption behavior has been extensively investigated on biochar (BC) and hydroxyapatite-biochar nanocomposites (HAP-BC). The adsorption process on these biochar-based materials adheres to multilayer coverage principles and is effectively described by the Freundlich adsorption isotherm model. Researchers have thoroughly evaluated the adsorption kinetics, equilibrium, removal capacity, and underlying mechanisms of Zn(II) on these substrates, often comparing its performance alongside other metals.

In competitive adsorption experiments, Zn(II) is commonly studied in conjunction with Pb(II) and Cu(II). However, its removal efficiency is notably influenced by environmental and chemical factors. The presence of competing divalent cations, particularly Ca(II) and Mg(II), significantly reduces its adsorption capacity. Additionally, elevated ionic strengths have been observed to slightly diminish the overall removal capacity of Zn(II) from solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Zn(II) is a heavy metal ion targeted for adsorption using organophosphonate-modified hydroxyapatites.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791655882</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Water Treatment">
  <data key="d0">Water Treatment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Primary environmental application benefiting from the high surface area and porosity of HAP-BC.&lt;SEP&gt;Water Treatment is an industrial and environmental application field utilizing adsorbent materials for pollutant removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Water treatment is an application area where PG and its derivatives are used to retain metals and anions.&lt;SEP&gt;An application area for calcium hydroxide as a common base.&lt;SEP&gt;Water treatment is an application area for sustainable photocatalysts derived from the synthesized Hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791967640</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="q_e">
  <data key="d0">q_e</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">q_e represents the equilibrium adsorbed amount of metal ions per unit mass of adsorbent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287728</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C_e">
  <data key="d0">C_e</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">C_e represents the equilibrium concentration of metal ions in the solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287728</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="K_L">
  <data key="d0">K_L</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">K_L is the Langmuir constant related to the energy of adsorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287728</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="q_max">
  <data key="d0">q_max</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">q_max is the maximum adsorption capacity representing monolayer coverage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287728</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="K_F">
  <data key="d0">K_F</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">K_F is the Freundlich adsorption capacity constant.&lt;SEP&gt;K_F is the Freundlich constant representing adsorption capacity, determined from the intercept of the linearized equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287728</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="n">
  <data key="d0">n</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">n is the adsorption intensity constant in the Freundlich model.&lt;SEP&gt;n is a parameter related to adsorption intensity, determined from the slope of the linearized equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287728</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Monolayer Adsorption">
  <data key="d0">Monolayer Adsorption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Monolayer adsorption describes the formation of a single layer of adsorbate molecules on the surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Monolayer adsorption is a simple adsorption mechanism that the Freundlich model indicates is not the primary mechanism here.&lt;SEP&gt;Monolayer adsorption is a mechanism ruled out by the Freundlich model, indicating a more complex adsorption process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791653869</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multilayer Adsorption">
  <data key="d0">Multilayer Adsorption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Multilayer adsorption describes the formation of multiple layers of adsorbate molecules on a heterogeneous surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Multilayer Adsorption is a process where ions form multiple layers on an adsorbent surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287728</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Competitive Adsorption">
  <data key="d0">Competitive Adsorption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Competitive Adsorption refers to the simultaneous sorption of multiple heavy metal ions in contaminated water.&lt;SEP&gt;Competitive adsorption refers to the simultaneous adsorption of multiple ions, studied by introducing coexisting cations into the heavy metal solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Competitive adsorption is the process studied to understand how multiple heavy metals interact with adsorbents in mixed solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Competitive adsorption refers to the simultaneous uptake of multiple metal ions by an adsorbent.&lt;SEP&gt;Competitive Adsorption is the process where multiple heavy metal ions simultaneously compete for binding sites on the adsorbent surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Competitive adsorption describes the process where multiple substances compete for binding sites on an adsorbent surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287728</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heterogeneous Biochar Surface">
  <data key="d0">Heterogeneous Biochar Surface</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The heterogeneous biochar surface provides varied binding sites that facilitate multilayer adsorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sylvite">
  <data key="d0">Sylvite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sylvite is a mineral identified in biochar through XRD peaks at 28° and 41°, corresponding to KCl.&lt;SEP&gt;Sylvite (KCl) is a mineral whose diffraction peaks disappeared from the BC sample during the adsorption process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288099</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scherrer's Formula">
  <data key="d0">Scherrer's Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Scherrer's Formula is a mathematical method used to calculate the average crystalline size of hydroxyapatite as 3.85 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;Scherrer's formula is applied to XRD data to calculate the average crystalline size of hexagonal pyromorphite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288099</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonate Group">
  <data key="d0">Carbonate Group</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbonate Group is a chemical group identified by a peak at 1467 cm⁻¹ in the HAP-BC spectrum.&lt;SEP&gt;The Carbonate Group is a chemical substituent that partially replaces phosphate groups in the Hydroxyapatite lattice.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonate Group is a chemical component (CO3 2-) that partially substitutes phosphate groups in the HAp lattice due to atmospheric carbon dioxide entrapment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Carbonate Group (CO3^2-) substitutes phosphate groups in HAp due to atmospheric CO2 adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;The carbonate group is an ionic species produced by urea hydrolysis that incorporates into the hydroxyapatite lattice.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791984880</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="KCl">
  <data key="d0">KCl</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">KCl is a chemical compound identified as sylvite in the biochar XRD pattern.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium chloride, a chloride solution tested at 25 degrees.&lt;SEP&gt;KCl is a chemical compound used as a reactant in the double decomposition reaction to produce potassium sulfate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791587763</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CaMg(CO3)2">
  <data key="d0">CaMg(CO3)2</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">CaMg(CO3)2 is a chemical compound identified as dolomite in the biochar XRD pattern.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Space Group P63/m">
  <data key="d0">Space Group P63/m</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Space group P63/m defines the symmetry of the hydroxyapatite crystal structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Space group P63/m is the crystallographic symmetry classification determined for the synthesized hydroxyapatite structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791542236</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JCPDS 89-6440">
  <data key="d0">JCPDS 89-6440</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">JCPDS 89-6440 is a standard reference card used to index the diffraction peaks of hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="OCH Group">
  <data key="d0">OCH Group</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The OCH group is an organic functional group identified by a peak at 1093 cm⁻¹ in the BC spectrum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CH2 Group">
  <data key="d0">CH2 Group</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The CH2 group is an organic functional group identified by a peak at 1434 cm⁻¹ in the BC spectrum.&lt;SEP&gt;A molecular group within the extractant cation exhibiting specific stretching vibration modes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-008</data>
