<node id="FESEM">
  <data key="d0">FESEM</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Field Emission Scanning Electron Microscopy used to characterize particle morphology and nanoparticle distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;FESEM is a microscopy technique used to visualize the surface morphology and particle assemblies of modified biochar.&lt;SEP&gt;Field Emission Scanning Electron Microscopy used to characterize the morphology of HAP-BC after recycling.&lt;SEP&gt;FESEM is a microscopy technique used to observe particle morphology, size, and agglomerates in the synthesized powders.&lt;SEP&gt;FESEM is a microscopy technique used to observe the nanoscale morphology of HAp.&lt;SEP&gt;FESEM is an imaging technique used to capture images for measuring the average crystallite size of samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791966881</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hexagonal Pyromorphite">
  <data key="d0">Hexagonal Pyromorphite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hexagonal pyromorphite is a crystalline mineral compound formed on HAP-BC during the adsorption of Pb(II).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chemisorption">
  <data key="d0">Chemisorption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Chemisorption is the adsorption mechanism indicated by the pseudo-second-order kinetic model for metal ion uptake on biochars.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Electrostatic Interactions">
  <data key="d0">Electrostatic Interactions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Electrostatic interactions are the primary removal mechanisms for Cu(II) and Zn(II) by HAP-BC and for all three ions by BC.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrostatic interactions are attractive forces promoted by negative zeta potential that enhance adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrostatic interactions are the primary mechanism driving hydroxyapatite-protein binding.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrostatic Interactions are molecular forces responsible for binding macromolecules to the adsorbent surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791748680</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adsorption Kinetics">
  <data key="d0">Adsorption Kinetics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A study examining the influence of time intervals on the adsorption rate of heavy metals onto biochar.&lt;SEP&gt;The study of the rate and time-dependent behavior of heavy metal ion uptake by adsorbents.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption kinetics describes the rate and mechanistic pathway of heavy metal ion uptake by BC and HAP-BC in mixed solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption kinetics refers to the rate and mechanism of protein adsorption on HAp and other biomaterials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791486417</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adsorption Isotherms">
  <data key="d0">Adsorption Isotherms</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adsorption isotherms describe the equilibrium adsorption capacities and selectivity ordering of BC and HAP-BC for ternary metal solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption isotherms are experimental curves used to derive BET surface area, cumulative pore volume, and average pore width for the powders.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption Isotherms are experimental curves analyzed to determine surface area, pore volume, and mesoporosity of the powders.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption isotherms refer to the study of adsorption equilibrium at constant temperature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791655701</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pseudo-first-order Kinetic Model">
  <data key="d0">Pseudo-first-order Kinetic Model</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The pseudo-first-order kinetic model is a mathematical framework used to analyze adsorption kinetics, though it provided a poorer fit than the pseudo-second-order model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
