<node id="Ternary Metal System">
  <data key="d0">Ternary Metal System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The ternary metal system is the experimental setup containing Pb(II), Cu(II), and Zn(II) simultaneously.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ternary Metal System refers to the mixed solution containing Pb(II), Cu(II), and Zn(II) ions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288449</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="XRD Patterns">
  <data key="d0">XRD Patterns</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">XRD Patterns, or X-ray diffraction patterns, are graphical representations and analytical datasets derived from experimental diffraction data. They function as essential characterization outputs designed to evaluate the crystalline structure, phase composition, and phase purity of synthesized materials. By identifying characteristic peaks and analyzing peak intensity, these patterns provide critical insights into the crystal structures and structural evolution of various compounds.

In specific material science applications, XRD patterns are extensively used to characterize bacterial cellulose (BC) and hydroxyapatite-bacterial cellulose (HAP-BC) composites, as well as to assess the crystalline structure and nanoparticle size of hydroxyapatite powders. The visualizations frequently depict peak intensity enhancements that correspond to crystallite growth, while also enabling the precise indexing of the hexagonal structure of hydroxyapatite (HAp). Additionally, they facilitate the detailed analysis of crystallite size variations along the c direction, making them indispensable for understanding the microstructural properties of advanced synthesized materials.&lt;SEP&gt;XRD Patterns are diffraction results used to confirm the apatite structure of HAP samples before and after heat treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;XRD Patterns provide structural data revealing the crystalline or amorphous nature of the resulting slag phases.&lt;SEP&gt;Diffraction patterns used to show the phase composition and decomposition rate of calcium sulfate in PG and PGI-1.4.&lt;SEP&gt;XRD patterns are utilized to track phase transformations and diffraction peak intensities of compounds at different temperatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792097626</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FESEM Image">
  <data key="d0">FESEM Image</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">FESEM images visualize the surface morphology of HAP-BC after treatment with the mixed metal solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Microscopic imaging data that evidences the presence of larger particles in surfactant-free powders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
