<node id="Na(I)">
  <data key="d0">Na(I)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A monovalent cation that shows minimal competitive interference during heavy metal adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Na(I) is a monovalent ion that causes steric hindrance and competes for surface adsorption sites on HAP-BC at high concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288449</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="K(I)">
  <data key="d0">K(I)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A monovalent cation that shows minimal competitive interference during heavy metal adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;K(I) is a coexisting cation in water that competes with heavy metals during the adsorption process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288449</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ca(II)">
  <data key="d0">Ca(II)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A divalent cation that reduces the removal percentage of Cu(II) and Zn(II) through competitive uptake.&lt;SEP&gt;Ca(II) is a coexisting cation in water that competes with heavy metals during the adsorption process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288449</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mg(II)">
  <data key="d0">Mg(II)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A divalent cation with stronger hydration that exhibits weaker competitive uptake than Ca(II) but stronger than Na(I) and K(I).&lt;SEP&gt;Mg(II) is a coexisting cation in water that competes with heavy metals during the adsorption process.</data>
