<node id="Adsorbent">
  <data key="d0">Adsorbent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material, specifically HAP-BC, used to remove heavy metals from wastewater through adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorbent refers to the material, such as phosphogypsum, that captures metal ions from a solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A material, such as phosphogypsum, used to remove pollutants from a solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorbent describes the function of hydroxyapatite in capturing heavy elements, organic dyes, and fluoride ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorbents are materials that utilize hydroxyapatite to capture and remove heavy elements, organic dyes, and fluoride ions from solutions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791626951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ternary system">
  <data key="d0">Ternary system</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An experimental condition involving a mixed solution of three components used to test HAP-BC adsorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288603</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acid sites">
  <data key="d0">Acid sites</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Functional groups on the HAP-BC surface that are protonated by HCl to facilitate heavy metal desorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288603</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydration">
  <data key="d0">Hydration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A chemical property of Mg(II) ions that increases their interaction with water and reduces competitive adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydration is the chemical reaction process in cementitious materials that is influenced by Phosphogypsum addition.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical process in phosphogypsum that is hindered by the presence of phosphorus impurities.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical processing method affecting the crystal structure and texture of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A processing method applied to phosphogypsum crystals to influence their structural properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydration is a chemical process through which residual sulfides in molten slag affect slag wool performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydration is a chemical process that sulfides undergo, leading to reduced performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792098242</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adsorption Isotherm">
  <data key="d0">Adsorption Isotherm</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adsorption isotherm is a graphical representation showing the relationship between adsorbate concentration and adsorption capacity.&lt;SEP&gt;A scientific chart used to analyze the porosity and surface area of solid materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288603</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Removal Efficiency">
  <data key="d0">Removal Efficiency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Removal Efficiency is a quantitative metric expressed as a calculated percentage that represents the fraction or proportion of a target substance successfully removed or extracted from a solution, wastewater, or source material. It serves as a standardized, objective measure across various chemical and environmental engineering contexts to evaluate the effectiveness of separation and purification processes.

In water treatment and environmental remediation, Removal Efficiency specifically measures the percentage of heavy metal ions removed from wastewater or aqueous solutions, typically utilizing adsorbents. This metric can be tracked over multiple operational cycles to assess the durability and sustained performance of the treatment materials. In resource recovery and nuclear waste management applications, Removal Efficiency quantifies the successful extraction or reduction of radioactive materials, radionuclides, and rare earth elements from source materials such as phosphogypsum. In these specialized contexts, the metric is often calculated as a leaching percent to evaluate the effectiveness of specific extraction or leaching processes. Together, these applications demonstrate how Removal Efficiency functions as a versatile indicator of process success across diverse industrial and scientific domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791790264</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
