<node id="BET Surface Area">
  <data key="d0">BET Surface Area</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">BET Surface Area is a quantitative physical property and measured metric used to determine the specific surface area of materials, typically expressed in square meters per gram (m²/g) as surface area per unit mass. Derived from nitrogen adsorption isotherms, this metric is primarily employed to quantify the surface area of synthesized powders and microspheres, while simultaneously characterizing the textural properties and physical attributes of prepared samples. Researchers routinely tabulate BET Surface Area values to evaluate and compare the structural and textural characteristics of various material formulations.

In practical applications, BET Surface Area effectively tracks morphological and compositional differences across diverse synthesized systems. For hydroxyapatite-based materials, it reveals significant textural variations, such as the marked increase observed in HAP-BC compared to standard BC, and identifies specific variants like the Surf Sample as exhibiting the highest measured values. Furthermore, the metric is instrumental in monitoring synthesis parameter effects; for instance, in hydrothermal processing of microspheres, BET Surface Area values have been documented to rise substantially from 1.0225 m²/g at one hour to 49.2412 m²/g with extended reaction times. Collectively, these measurements underscore the utility of BET Surface Area as a critical analytical parameter for assessing the physical and textural evolution of particulate and composite materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791985624</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pore Volume">
  <data key="d0">Pore Volume</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Quantitative measurement of total pore volume, indicating enhanced porosity in HAP-BC.&lt;SEP&gt;A calculated metric representing the total volume of pores in the powder.&lt;SEP&gt;Pore Volume is a quantitative measure of the cumulative void space in the powders, expressed in cubic centimeters per gram.&lt;SEP&gt;Pore volume is a textural property of mesoporous hydroxyapatite that allows carrying high dosages of drugs.&lt;SEP&gt;The pore volume of the synthesized HAp sample is 0.27 cm^3/g.&lt;SEP&gt;Pore Volume quantifies the void space within the microspheres, varying across samples T1 to T10 as listed in Table 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791985624</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heavy Metal Ions">
  <data key="d0">Heavy Metal Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Heavy metal ions are industrial pollutants and trace contaminants that pose significant ecological and health risks. They are commonly discharged into the environment through wastewater and exhaust gases, and are frequently detected in wastewaters, sediments, aqueous solutions, and industrial byproducts such as phosphogypsum, where they substantially contribute to overall environmental risk profiles. Representative examples of these ions include lead, copper, zinc, cadmium, and cobalt.

Due to their hazardous nature, heavy metal ions serve as primary target analytes and pollutants in environmental studies, necessitating continuous monitoring and effective removal from contaminated water sources. Remediation strategies predominantly rely on adsorption techniques utilizing diverse materials, including agricultural waste adsorbents and hydroxyapatite composites. In these treatment processes, functional groups present on composite surfaces actively facilitate the complexation and precipitation of heavy metal ions, thereby enabling their efficient capture and elimination from aqueous systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791900466</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Type IV Isotherm">
  <data key="d0">Type IV Isotherm</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adsorption isotherm type indicating mesoporous structures in BC.&lt;SEP&gt;A classification of adsorption isotherm exhibited by the HAp particles according to IUPAC standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Type IV isotherm is a classification of N2 sorption isotherms indicating mesoporous features in the samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791966148</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Type H3 Hysteresis Loop">
  <data key="d0">Type H3 Hysteresis Loop</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Hysteresis loop type associated with mesopores assembled by HAP nanoparticles.&lt;SEP&gt;Type H3 Hysteresis Loops are attributed to slit-shaped pores formed by self-assembled clusters in the microspheres.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791985624</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Microporous Structure">
  <data key="d0">Microporous Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural feature of HAP-BC indicated by Type I isotherm behavior at low relative pressures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mesoporous Structure">
  <data key="d0">Mesoporous Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural feature of BC and HAP-BC indicated by Type IV isotherms and Type H3 hysteresis loops.&lt;SEP&gt;Mesoporous structure is a pore architecture found in hydroxyapatite powders, which transforms into a meso- plus macroporous structure upon heat treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Mesoporous structure refers to the irregular pore architecture that enhances the drug loading capacity of hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791486667</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nanocomposite">
  <data key="d0">Nanocomposite</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A composite material consisting of nanoparticles (HAP) on a substrate (BC), specifically referring to HAP-BC.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
