<node id="Algerian-Tunisian Phosphorite Blend">
  <data key="d0">Algerian-Tunisian Phosphorite Blend</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A mineral mixture used as the source material for deriving phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A geological blend of Algerian and Tunisian phosphorite used to produce PGB.&lt;SEP&gt;A raw mineral blend used as the source material for PGB.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791311453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tunisian Phosphorite">
  <data key="d0">Tunisian Phosphorite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A natural mineral source from which phosphogypsum is derived.&lt;SEP&gt;Tunisian Phosphorite is the raw material source used to produce the purely Tunisian PGC phosphogypsum sample.&lt;SEP&gt;Geological phosphorite from Tunisia used to produce PGC.&lt;SEP&gt;A raw mineral source used to produce PGC.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791311453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PGB">
  <data key="d0">PGB</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">PGB is a phosphogypsum sample derived from a 1:1 (w/w) blend of Algerian and Tunisian phosphorites. This material is primarily utilized in scientific analyses focusing on the physicochemical and radiological properties of phosphogypsum fractions.

In analytical studies, PGB is extensively evaluated for its particle size distribution and grain size correlations. It is specifically noted for contributing 46.4% to the F02 particle fraction. Furthermore, the sample exhibits distinct radium and lead concentration ranges across various particle fractions, and it is systematically assessed for isotopic activity ratios to characterize its radiological behavior.

In graphical representations of phosphogypsum fraction analyses, PGB is consistently depicted using red bars in grouped bar charts. These visualizations serve to contextualize its proportional contribution and radiological characteristics relative to other phosphogypsum samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791311453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PGC">
  <data key="d0">PGC</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">PGC is a specific type of phosphogypsum sample derived exclusively from pure Tunisian phosphorite. This material has been extensively characterized through various analytical methods, particularly focusing on its physical and geochemical properties. Particle size distribution analyses reveal that PGC exhibits the highest contribution within the F01 fraction, accounting for 39.3% of the total sample. Additionally, it demonstrates the narrowest enrichment factor range among comparable materials and has been specifically evaluated for isotopic activity ratios alongside grain size correlations.

In scientific data visualization, PGC is consistently represented by blue bars in grouped bar charts designed to analyze phosphogypsum fractions. Collectively, these characteristics establish PGC as a well-defined reference sample for studying the physical, isotopic, and enrichment behaviors of phosphogypsum originating from Tunisian sources.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791311453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
