</node>
<node id="1.5 to 2.0 micrometers">
  <data key="d0">1.5 to 2.0 micrometers</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">1.5 to 2.0 micrometers is the measured width range of the field of view in the SEM image.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HA">
  <data key="d0">HA</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">HA refers to hydroxyapatite, the base material of the synthesized powders, which undergoes shrinkage and structural transformation from mesoporous to meso- plus macroporous upon heating.&lt;SEP&gt;HA is the abbreviation for Hydroxyapatite, a crystalline mineral phase.&lt;SEP&gt;HA is a categorical row label within a statistical data table.&lt;SEP&gt;HA refers to a crystalline phase identified by lattice fringes with 0.344 nm interplanar spacing and diffraction rings corresponding to (002) and (211) planes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791985489</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mesoporous HA Structure">
  <data key="d0">Mesoporous HA Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mesoporous HA structure is the initial structural state of the hydroxyapatite powders before heat treatment, characterized by fine pores.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Meso-Plus Macroporous Structure">
  <data key="d0">Meso-Plus Macroporous Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Meso-Plus Macroporous Structure is the resulting structural state of the powders after heating at 900°C, indicating the formation of larger pores alongside mesopores.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TEM Analysis">
  <data key="d0">TEM Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">TEM analysis is a technique used in cited studies to evidence the formation of nano-sized pores along HA rods after calcination.&lt;SEP&gt;TEM analysis is a microscopy technique used to observe the structure of HAp.&lt;SEP&gt;TEM Analysis is an imaging technique used to verify the formation of elongated Hydroxyapatite nanorods.&lt;SEP&gt;TEM Analysis is a characterization technique used to propose the formation mechanism based on evolutionary information.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791985383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Particle Growth Inhibition">
  <data key="d0">Particle Growth Inhibition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Particle growth inhibition is a mechanism during powder synthesis where surfactants act as templates to restrict crystal expansion, resulting in higher surface area.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Process Temperature">
  <data key="d0">Process Temperature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Process temperature is a critical synthesis parameter that controls the surface area of hydroxyapatite crystals, independent of the surfactant type.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CTAB Surfactant">
  <data key="d0">CTAB Surfactant</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A chemical agent used during synthesis to produce pure hydroxyapatite powders, alter particle morphology, and increase surface area.&lt;SEP&gt;CTAB Surfactant forms assemblies that interact with phosphate ions and provide nucleation sites for Hydroxyapatite growth.&lt;SEP&gt;An organic molecule mentioned in the sample name that may contribute to the detected Carbon peak.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-024</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791966377</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FTIR Analysis">
  <data key="d0">FTIR Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">FTIR Analysis is a spectroscopic method used to identify functional groups such as phosphate, hydroxyl, and carbonate in calcium apatites.&lt;SEP&gt;An analytical technique used to identify vibrational modes and confirm the predominant hydroxyapatite phase in synthesized powders.&lt;SEP&gt;A spectroscopic technique used to analyze post-adsorption surface changes and identify coordination reactions.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR Analysis is a spectroscopic technique used to identify vibrational modes of chemical bonds in the prepared samples.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR analysis is an analytical technique used to support conclusions regarding the chemical composition of the HAp-CTAB-9 sample.&lt;SEP&gt;Analytical technique used to confirm the presence of oxygen vacancies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791964406</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vibrational Modes">
  <data key="d0">Vibrational Modes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Specific atomic movement patterns of chemical bonds identified through spectroscopic analysis, including stretching and bending modes.&lt;SEP&gt;Vibrational Modes are specific atomic bond movements, such as symmetric stretching and bending, characteristic of phosphate tetrahedra.&lt;SEP&gt;Vibrational modes such as bending, stretching, and rocking describe the infrared spectral behavior of chemical groups like nitrate and water.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791813449</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Powder Surf-900C">
  <data key="d0">Powder Surf-900C</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A heat-treated version of the surfactant-synthesized powder, showing reduced surface area and increased crystallinity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Powder NoSurf-900C">
  <data key="d0">Powder NoSurf-900C</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A heat-treated version of the non-surfactant powder, analyzed alongside other samples in FTIR spectra.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Beta-Tricalcium Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Beta-Tricalcium Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Beta-Tricalcium Phosphate is a calcium phosphate phase detected in minor amounts in specific powder samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A secondary calcium phosphate phase that was successfully avoided in the synthesis process to ensure pure hydroxyapatite powders.&lt;SEP&gt;A secondary calcium phosphate phase indicated by specific diffraction peaks in the chart.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta-Tricalcium Phosphate is a product formed from the partial conversion of Hydroxyapatite at 775-1000°C due to dehydroxylation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792003010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="V3 Asymmetric Stretching Mode">
  <data key="d0">V3 Asymmetric Stretching Mode</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The v3 (P-O) asymmetric stretching mode is assigned to the 564 cm^-1 peak in the vibrational analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="V1 Symmetric Stretching Mode">
  <data key="d0">V1 Symmetric Stretching Mode</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The v1 (P-O) symmetric stretching mode is assigned to the 474 cm^-1 peak in the vibrational analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="V4 Bending Mode">
  <data key="d0">V4 Bending Mode</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The v4 (O-P-O) bending mode is a vibrational mode of the phosphate group resolved by Raman spectroscopy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="V2 Bending Mode">
  <data key="d0">V2 Bending Mode</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The v2 (O-P-O) bending mode is a vibrational mode of the phosphate group resolved by Raman spectroscopy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="60 mM CTAB Concentration">
  <data key="d0">60 mM CTAB Concentration</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A concentration of 60 mM CTAB surfactant was used to synthesize HA crystals with specific dimensions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="10-25 nm Diameter">
  <data key="d0">10-25 nm Diameter</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The diameter of HA crystals produced using 60 mM CTAB surfactant ranges from 10 to 25 nm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="75-100 nm Length">
  <data key="d0">75-100 nm Length</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The length of HA crystals produced using 60 mM CTAB surfactant ranges from 75 to 100 nm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cetyltrimethyl Ammonium Bromide">
  <data key="d0">Cetyltrimethyl Ammonium Bromide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cetyltrimethyl Ammonium Bromide is a surfactant added to direct the formation of hydroxyapatite nanorods or needles.&lt;SEP&gt;A biocompatible cationic surfactant used to control HA morphology and act as a nucleating site.&lt;SEP&gt;Cetyltrimethyl ammonium bromide is a chemical agent used as a template to synthesize mesoporous hydroxyapatite particles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791486667</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydroxyapatite Synthesis">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxyapatite Synthesis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydroxyapatite Synthesis is a chemical co-precipitation procedure involving precursor mixing, pH adjustment, heating, precipitation, and calcination.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyapatite Synthesis is the production process that might be the source of acidic group contamination on the surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791749218</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surf Route">
  <data key="d0">Surf Route</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Surf Route is a synthesis variant that incorporates a surfactant to control crystal morphology under specific conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A synthesis pathway for HA powders conducted with the addition of a surfactant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NoSurf Route">
  <data key="d0">NoSurf Route</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The NoSurf Route is a baseline synthesis variant conducted without surfactants, maintaining identical chemical and thermal conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A synthesis pathway for HA powders conducted without any surfactant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jeol JSM-6490A">
  <data key="d0">Jeol JSM-6490A</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Jeol JSM-6490A is a scanning electron microscope instrument utilized for the morphological analysis of all synthesized powders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ammonium Nitrate">
  <data key="d0">Ammonium Nitrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ammonium nitrate is an inorganic chemical compound that functions as a critical reagent in various chemical processing and extraction methodologies. It can originate as a chemical byproduct formed during the synthesis of hydroxyapatite. Within experimental and industrial workflows, it is primarily deployed as a salting-out agent and salting-out marker during solvent extraction processes. When introduced into synthetic solutions, the compound is added to maintain ionic strength and ensure consistent extraction efficiency. Throughout these procedures, ammonium nitrate remains present in the aqueous phase, where it actively facilitates the chemical environment necessary for effective separation and purification.

The compound is specifically utilized to enhance the extraction and facilitate the loading of rare earth elements. It operates synergistically alongside nitric acid to significantly amplify the salting-out effect, thereby optimizing overall recovery rates. To achieve maximum performance and maintain constant efficiency, ammonium nitrate is consistently applied at an optimum concentration of 3 mol/L. Through these targeted applications and precise concentration controls, it plays an indispensable role in streamlining solvent extraction operations and improving the yield of valuable inorganic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium Nitrate is a byproduct or impurity present before washing in the synthesis of calcium phosphate compounds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791857078</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Silicon Wafer">
  <data key="d0">Silicon Wafer</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Silicon Wafer is the substrate onto which dispersed powder samples are placed for FESEM analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conductive Carbon Tape">
  <data key="d0">Conductive Carbon Tape</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Conductive Carbon Tape is used to mount powder samples for SEM analysis without ultrasonic dispersion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cationic Surfactant">
  <data key="d0">Cationic Surfactant</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cationic Surfactant is a chemical agent used as a template to direct the morphology and size of hydroxyapatite nanopowders.&lt;SEP&gt;Cationic surfactant is used as a template in the hydrothermal synthesis of hydroxyapatite nanopowders.&lt;SEP&gt;Cationic surfactant acts as a template in the hydrothermal synthesis of hydroxyapatite nanopowders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791543956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Template">
  <data key="d0">Template</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Template refers to a structural guide or medium utilized in chemical synthesis to control the shape and size of precipitated materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Microemulsion">
  <data key="d0">Microemulsion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Microemulsion is a liquid dispersion technique employed as a route for processing and synthesizing hydroxyapatite particles.&lt;SEP&gt;A technique used to obtain phosphate materials like hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Microemulsion is a chemical-processing route mentioned for synthesizing hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791664401</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Polyethylene Glycol">
  <data key="d0">Polyethylene Glycol</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A surfactant utilized to control HA morphology and reduce particle size.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene Glycol is a polymer additive used alongside templates to facilitate the biomimetic synthesis of spherical nanohydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;A polymer added to calcium phosphate solutions during spray pyrolysis to form nano-sized hydroxyapatite powders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791470547</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cetyltrimethylammonium Bromide">
  <data key="d0">Cetyltrimethylammonium Bromide</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Cetyltrimethylammonium Bromide is a specific cationic surfactant used as a template in the hydrothermal synthesis of dandelion-like hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;A surfactant compound used as a capping agent in the synthesis of gold nanorods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791967780</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ibuprofen">
  <data key="d0">Ibuprofen</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Ibuprofen is a pharmaceutical compound whose drug delivery behavior is studied when incorporated into hydroxyapatite powders.&lt;SEP&gt;A poorly water-soluble pharmaceutical drug referenced in literature for delivery using hydroxyapatite-based systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Ibuprofen is a drug whose loading and release profiles from silica matrices were studied in a 2004 publication.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-011</data>
