<node id="Ethanol Amine">
  <data key="d0">Ethanol Amine</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ethanol amine is a chemical compound utilized as a surfactant to control HA morphology and reduce particle size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Beta-TCP (b-TCP)">
  <data key="d0">Beta-TCP (b-TCP)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A secondary crystalline phase that appears in HA powders synthesized without surfactant, particularly at lower pH levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chemical Precipitation Method">
  <data key="d0">Chemical Precipitation Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A synthesis technique employed to produce HA powders at controlled pH and temperature levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synthesis Parameters">
  <data key="d0">Synthesis Parameters</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Reaction conditions including pH, temperature, stirring rate, and reflux time that dictate phase purity.&lt;SEP&gt;Factors such as time, pressure, and temperature that influence the density and SSA of the powders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791857346</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ca9(PO4)6 Clusters">
  <data key="d0">Ca9(PO4)6 Clusters</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Tetrahedral structural clusters formed by calcium and phosphate ions that facilitate HA particle growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Hexagonal-shaped clusters that form on rod-shaped micellar surfaces and act as nucleating points.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791664527</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CTAB Micelles">
  <data key="d0">CTAB Micelles</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rod-like structures formed by CTAB cations that act as separate reactors to uniformly distribute reaction conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stirring rate">
  <data key="d0">Stirring rate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stirring rate is a reaction parameter that affects the synthesis conditions of calcium phosphates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reflux time">
  <data key="d0">Reflux time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Reflux time is a processing condition that influences the addition of ions to clusters and HA particle growth.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CTA+">
  <data key="d0">CTA+</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">CTA+ is a cationic surfactant ion derived from CTAB that forms tetrahedral structures and micellar rods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PO4 3-">
  <data key="d0">PO4 3-</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">PO4 3- is a tetrahedral phosphate ion that binds to CTA+ nuclei due to charge and stereochemistry complementarity.&lt;SEP&gt;PO4 3- is a phosphate ion whose bending and stretching vibrations are identified in the ATR spectra.&lt;SEP&gt;PO4 3- is the phosphate ion responsible for the strong P-O stretching vibration bands observed in the FTIR spectrum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791664401</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Particle size">
  <data key="d0">Particle size</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Particle size is a physical characteristic of the synthesized powders that is regulated by the presence of CTAB.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Crystallinity degree">
  <data key="d0">Crystallinity degree</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Crystallinity degree is a material property determined alongside average crystallite size in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331814</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Peak width">
  <data key="d0">Peak width</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Peak width represents the width at half maximum intensity of the (002) XRD reflection used in calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791332008</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Particle agglomeration">
  <data key="d0">Particle agglomeration</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d2">CTAB micelles act as separate reactors that prevent particles from growing or agglomerating.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791332029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ball-Milling Treatment">
  <data key="d0">Ball-Milling Treatment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Ball-milling treatment reduces particle size, increases specific surface area, and improves fluidity and strength while lowering water requirements for phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791361860</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rashad">
  <data key="d0">Rashad</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Rashad calcined phosphogypsum at 850 degrees Celsius for 2 hours.&lt;SEP&gt;Rashad conducted experiments determining optimal calcination temperatures for phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791361860</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Potgieter et al.">
  <data key="d0">Potgieter et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Potgieter et al. demonstrated that treating phosphogypsum-based cemented paste backfill with sulfuric acid or ammonium hydroxide reduces set retardation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791361860</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wu et al.">
  <data key="d0">Wu et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wu et al. are researchers who investigated the application of calcium carbide slag for phosphogypsum neutralization.&lt;SEP&gt;Wu et al. reported that ball-milling treatment efficiently reduces phosphogypsum particle size and improves compressive strength.&lt;SEP&gt;Wu et al. documented the forms of phosphate impurities in phosphogypsum and their release during hydration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719139</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Du et al.">
  <data key="d0">Du et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Du et al. utilized ball-milling combined with reverse flotation to eliminate microfine particles and organic materials from raw phosphogypsum ore.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers who summarized the composition of PG, including various impurities and contaminants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791450734</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dodecylamine">
  <data key="d0">Dodecylamine</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Dodecylamine is a reagent used to float gypsum concentration after reverse flotation removes microfine particles and organic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362169</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Flexural Strength">
  <data key="d0">Flexural Strength</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Flexural strength is a mechanical property that increases by 46% for alpha-hemihydrate produced from treated phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical property measured for hemihydrate phosphogypsum composite containing recycled waste glass.&lt;SEP&gt;Flexural Strength is a mechanical property of mortar that remains relatively stable as Plaster Of Paris content increases from 40 to 55%.&lt;SEP&gt;Flexural strength is a mechanical property investigated in previous research on phosphogypsum-based composite materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Flexural strength is a mechanical property measured to assess the structural integrity of stabilized adobe samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Flexural strength is a mechanical property measured on prismatic adobe samples using a one-point bending test.&lt;SEP&gt;Flexural strength is a mechanical property assessed to determine the bending resistance of the adobe samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791734588</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Water Usage">
  <data key="d0">Water Usage</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Water usage for phosphogypsum drops to a minimum of 60% after ball-milling treatment, representing a 21.5% reduction compared to unmilled material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362318</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Organic Contaminants">
  <data key="d0">Organic Contaminants</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Organic contaminants are impurities present in phosphogypsum that are removed through flotation and calcination processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362318</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alpha-Hemihydrate">
  <data key="d0">Alpha-Hemihydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Alpha-hemihydrate is a processed form of phosphogypsum whose compressive and flexural strengths improve significantly after purification.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362318</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Effluent">
  <data key="d0">Effluent</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Effluent is the wastewater generated during phosphogypsum washing that requires cleansing to prevent environmental pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362318</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inert Minerals">
  <data key="d0">Inert Minerals</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Inert minerals are components within phosphogypsum that are targeted for activation in future calcination research.&lt;SEP&gt;Inert minerals are components within phosphogypsum that can be activated to improve material performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362318</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pure Phosphogypsum">
  <data key="d0">Pure Phosphogypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Pure phosphogypsum is the refined product resulting from purification processes, with increased purity percentage compared to raw ore.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362318</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Future Calcination Research">
  <data key="d0">Future Calcination Research</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Future calcination research focuses on developing new admixtures, lowering temperatures, and activating inert minerals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362318</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphogypsum Ore">
  <data key="d0">Phosphogypsum Ore</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphogypsum ore is the raw material processed through ball-milling and flotation to remove microfine particles and organic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362318</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clinker">
  <data key="d0">Clinker</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Clinker is a primary component of cement paste that can be partially substituted with natural gypsum or phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A cement component combined with varying percentages of phosphogypsum to evaluate compressive strength changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinker is a substitution material in mortar mixes that is partially replaced by Phosphogypsum in specific studies.&lt;SEP&gt;A cement production material that can be substituted or combined with treated phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinker is a primary component in cement production that is partially replaced by alternative materials in composite cement.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinker is a raw material in cement manufacturing that can utilize phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinker is the intermediate product formed during cement clinker firing, where liquid phases can wrap phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792096029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium sulphoaluminate cement">
  <data key="d0">Calcium sulphoaluminate cement</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium sulphoaluminate cement is a specialized cement type whose setting period increases with the addition of phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphogypsum dihydrate (Di-PG)">
  <data key="d0">Phosphogypsum dihydrate (Di-PG)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphogypsum dihydrate is a mineral phase of phosphogypsum employed in hydraulic binder formulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PG hemihydrate (a-PG and b-PG)">
  <data key="d0">PG hemihydrate (a-PG and b-PG)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">PG hemihydrate is a mineral phase of phosphogypsum utilized in hydraulic binder formulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insoluble anhydrite (A-PG II)">
  <data key="d0">Insoluble anhydrite (A-PG II)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Insoluble anhydrite is a mineral phase of phosphogypsum used in hydraulic binder formulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GGBFS">
  <data key="d0">GGBFS</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">GGBFS is a material combined with insoluble anhydrite to form hydraulic binder systems with variable setting times.&lt;SEP&gt;A supplementary material combined with phosphogypsum to produce mortar with high compressive strength over time.&lt;SEP&gt;GGBFS is a supplementary material used in quick lime-GGBFS-cement systems alongside PG.&lt;SEP&gt;GGBFS is a component in the cement-PG system that contributes to network stability during carbonization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Slag lime system">
  <data key="d0">Slag lime system</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Slag lime system is a hydraulic binder system composed of insoluble anhydrite and slag lime with specific setting times.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrationrecrystallization mechanism">
  <data key="d0">Hydrationrecrystallization mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrationrecrystallization mechanism is the primary process responsible for the properties of phosphogypsum unfired bricks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Smadi Et Al.">
  <data key="d0">Smadi Et Al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Smadi Et Al. documented that substituting cement with phosphogypsum extends both initial and final setting times.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shen Et Al.">
  <data key="d0">Shen Et Al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Shen Et Al. observed that adding phosphogypsum to calcium sulphoaluminate cement increases the setting period.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791362752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Altun And Sert">
  <data key="d0">Altun And Sert</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Altun And Sert compared natural gypsum and phosphogypsum as partial substitutes for clinker in cement pastes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017</data>
