<node id="Wang et al.">
  <data key="d0">Wang et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wang et al. is a research group that has conducted extensive investigations into the sustainable valorization of phosphogypsum and the controlled synthesis of advanced nanomaterials for environmental remediation. In their work on phosphogypsum processing, the researchers combined lime treatment with flotation techniques to effectively purify the industrial byproduct. They also documented the material's high reactivity and rapid carbonation rate, emphasizing its potential for carbon dioxide capture applications. Additionally, Wang et al. developed key technologies for the low-temperature decomposition of phosphogypsum to generate high concentrations of sulfur dioxide, successfully establishing a demonstration facility to validate this process.

Beyond phosphogypsum utilization, the group has made significant contributions to the precise synthesis of hydroxyapatite. Through microwave irradiation synthesis, they observed that crystal growth is directly proportional to both reaction time and temperature. To achieve targeted morphological control, Wang et al. successfully regulated hydroxyapatite crystal growth by incorporating a cationic surfactant, specifically hexadecyltrimethylammonium bromide. Building upon this controlled synthesis methodology, the researchers fabricated a hydroxyapatite-biochar nanocomposite engineered for the efficient removal of multiple heavy metal ions from aqueous systems. Collectively, these studies highlight Wang et al.'s broad expertise in material processing, crystal engineering, and pollution control technologies.&lt;SEP&gt;Wang et al. are researchers who investigated the strength properties of CPGFM with specific HPG and activator ratios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791723719</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohammad Salem Al-Hwaiti">
  <data key="d0">Mohammad Salem Al-Hwaiti</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Mohammad Salem Al-Hwaiti is a researcher who successfully leached 226Ra from phosphogypsum using chemical combinations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-010</data>
