<node id="Mullite">
  <data key="d0">Mullite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A mineral phase that forms during the crystallization process of bricks at temperatures around 1000 °C.&lt;SEP&gt;Mullite is a primary mineral component of fly ash detected in the concrete samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Mullite is a mineral phase identified by peak 2, appearing in clusters around 29°-30° and scattered between 35°-60°.&lt;SEP&gt;Mullite is an aluminum silicate compound (AlSiO9) identified in the mineralogical composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791938226</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hematite">
  <data key="d0">Hematite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A mineral phase whose proportion decreases as the percentage of Phosphogypsum in the brick samples increases.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematite is a mineral phase identified in phosphogypsum samples from an abandoned fertilizer industry.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791417933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Illite">
  <data key="d0">Illite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">An initially abundant clay mineral that disintegrates between 450 and 900 °C during the brick baking process.&lt;SEP&gt;Illite is a potassium aluminum magnesium silicate mineral identified in the mineralogical composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791938112</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-immersing Method">
  <data key="d0">Non-immersing Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific analytical technique employed during the Scanning Electron Microscopy examination of brick samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-immersing Method is a technique used to form semi-hydrated gypsum into standard bricks for quality control.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791364767</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Larnite">
  <data key="d0">Larnite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Larnite is a mineral phase that carbonate clays have the potential to generate during the brick baking process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791364898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anhydrous Gypsum">
  <data key="d0">Anhydrous Gypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Anhydrous gypsum is a stable product formed alongside hemihydrate gypsum during the calcination of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Anhydrous gypsum is a mineral phase that maintains its crystalline shape in brick samples containing Phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Anhydrous gypsum is produced by calcining beta-hemihydrate gypsum and is utilized as a chemical filler.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791772783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Waste River Sludge">
  <data key="d0">Waste River Sludge</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Waste river sludge is a material used as a predominant adhesion agent in cement pastes containing PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Waste river sludge is an industrial byproduct recycled into unfired green bricks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791364898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hemihydrate PG">
  <data key="d0">Hemihydrate PG</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hemihydrate PG is a form of phosphogypsum used as a predominant adhesion agent in cement pastes.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemihydrate PG is a form of phosphogypsum containing coarse particles that negatively impacts the water retention of filling slurry.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
