<node id="Tensile Strength">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Strength</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tensile strength is a mechanical property that improves with up to 10% phosphogypsum substitution but declines at higher replacement levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791364898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Selenite Gypsum">
  <data key="d0">Selenite Gypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Selenite gypsum is a form of gypsum whose strength development and setting time are negatively affected by soluble phosphate contaminants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791364898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gypsum Hemihydrate">
  <data key="d0">Gypsum Hemihydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Gypsum hemihydrate is a material whose functionality is negatively impacted by contaminants such as soluble phosphorus and organic substances in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791364898</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soluble Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Soluble Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Soluble phosphate is a contaminant that negatively impacts the strength development and setting time of selenite gypsum when present above 1.0%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365237</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Singh et al.">
  <data key="d0">Singh et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Singh et al. conducted a study indicating that soluble phosphate additions negatively impact selenite gypsum properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Singh et al. treated phosphogypsum with citric acid and ammonium hydroxide, and also calcined it at 800 degrees Celsius.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365237</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Min et al.">
  <data key="d0">Min et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Min et al. researched how soluble phosphorus and organic substances in phosphogypsum increase material gelling time.&lt;SEP&gt;Min et al. are researchers who provided chemical composition data for phosphogypsum samples in China.&lt;SEP&gt;Min et al. are researchers who considered that increasing calcium hydroxide content improves strength evolution by reducing porosity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Liu et al.">
  <data key="d0">Liu et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Liu et al. compared raw and calcined phosphogypsum, noting their similar chemical compositions.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers who summarized the profitability and sustainability of PG in various applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers who investigated the Hydrothermal Method, examining how pH levels affect particle morphology and agglomeration.&lt;SEP&gt;Liu et al. are researchers who provided chemical composition data for phosphogypsum samples in China.&lt;SEP&gt;Liu et al. are researchers who found that water washing pretreatment optimizes particle size distribution and reduces shear thinning.&lt;SEP&gt;Liu et al. concluded that gamma alumina promotes AFt precipitation and enhances fluorine retention.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-024</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Guiyang">
  <data key="d0">Guiyang</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Guiyang is a location where phosphogypsum samples with high sulfur trioxide content were sourced.&lt;SEP&gt;Guiyang is the city where Guizhou University is located.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516547</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hubei">
  <data key="d0">Hubei</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Hubei is a Chinese province that plays a significant role in the nation's phosphogypsum sector, contributing substantially to China's total phosphogypsum production. It is prominently represented in aggregate data distributions regarding phosphogypsum usage and has been a central focus of multiple studies analyzing the phosphogypsum recycling industry. These investigations frequently examine prominent companies engaged in recycling within the region and involve the collection and chemical analysis of phosphogypsum samples sourced from Hubei, which are characterized by a moderate sulfur trioxide content.

In addition to its research significance, Hubei is strategically targeted for the development of large-scale and demonstration phosphogypsum projects. The province has also actively advanced its recycling infrastructure, with CPGB technology being successfully adopted by large-scale mining operations. Through these initiatives, Hubei continues to serve as a critical hub for phosphogypsum management, technological innovation, and industrial recycling efforts in China.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791723719</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recycling">
  <data key="d0">Recycling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Recycling is a waste management process identified as a risk-free and environmentally beneficial solution for phosphogypsum stockpiling.&lt;SEP&gt;Recycling is a process used to repurpose phosphogypsum, though contaminants can degrade the quality of the recycled product.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of reprocessing PG into usable products and materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791451491</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Treatment Methods">
  <data key="d0">Treatment Methods</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Treatment methods are applied to phosphogypsum to remove contaminants and improve its suitability for engineering applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365237</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gelling Time">
  <data key="d0">Gelling Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gelling time is a material property that increases due to the presence of soluble phosphorus and organic substances in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365237</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strength Development">
  <data key="d0">Strength Development</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strength development refers to the process by which gypsum gains mechanical strength, which is hindered by contaminants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365237</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Loss on Ignition">
  <data key="d0">Loss on Ignition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Loss on ignition is a chemical analysis metric recorded in Table 1 to assess the composition of phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Loss on Ignition represents the volatile components lost during heating, comprising 20.8% of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Loss on ignition is a chemical property measured in phosphogypsum indicating volatile content.&lt;SEP&gt;Loss on ignition is a measured property indicating volatile content or moisture loss, recorded in weight percentages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791938112</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Raw Phosphogypsum">
  <data key="d0">Raw Phosphogypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Raw Phosphogypsum is an unprocessed industrial byproduct whose chemical composition closely resembles that of its calcined counterpart. In analytical and spectroscopic studies, it is consistently identified as the untreated sample corresponding to spectrum (a) and is routinely evaluated using infrared spectroscopy.

As a construction and composite material, Raw Phosphogypsum serves as a primary base material and the principal constituent of PGCM. Due to its inherently low cementitious properties, it is frequently combined with β-HPG in experimental mixtures at a fixed 60% to 40% ratio to enhance overall performance. The hydration of Raw Phosphogypsum within these composites is significantly influenced by the addition of cement and silica fume, and the material is typically cured using hemihydrate phosphogypsum to develop functional cementitious products. Despite its utility in mixtures, Raw Phosphogypsum exhibits notable mechanical vulnerabilities, as it tends to degrade and flake off when subjected to cyclic stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Raw Phosphogypsum is the untreated material baseline, containing no detectable sodium prior to leaching.&lt;SEP&gt;Raw Phosphogypsum is the unprocessed industrial byproduct evaluated for compatibility with conventional road construction materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791936533</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcined Phosphogypsum">
  <data key="d0">Calcined Phosphogypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcined phosphogypsum is a processed form of the byproduct that retains a chemical composition similar to the raw material.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcined phosphogypsum is mixed with slag cement, fly ash, and quicklime to enhance matrix compressive strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516427</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sieve Technique">
  <data key="d0">Sieve Technique</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Sieve technique is a contamination removal method that relies on particle size separation, effective when contaminants are widely dispersed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365237</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wet Sieve Cyclone Treatment">
  <data key="d0">Wet Sieve Cyclone Treatment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Wet sieve cyclone treatment is a refined purification procedure combining wet sieving and hydrocyclone processing to achieve high purity levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365237</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ajam et al.">
  <data key="d0">Ajam et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Researchers who developed fired bricks using a mixture of phosphogypsum, sand, and clay.&lt;SEP&gt;Ajam et al. are researchers who documented that calcium sulfate accounts for approximately 77% of phosphogypsum's composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365348</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Brickmaking Process">
  <data key="d0">Brickmaking Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Brickmaking process is an industrial application where treated phosphogypsum is used to reduce overall energy consumption and heat requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365348</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Methodical Crushing">
  <data key="d0">Methodical Crushing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Methodical crushing is a processing step recommended to eliminate the residual fluorescence effect caused by calcium sulphate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365348</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clean Gypsum Recovery">
  <data key="d0">Clean Gypsum Recovery</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Clean gypsum recovery refers to the estimated 75% yield of purified gypsum achieved through the sieve technique.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365348</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soluble Contaminants">
  <data key="d0">Soluble Contaminants</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Soluble contaminants are impurities in phosphogypsum, including fluorine and phosphorus, that are targeted for removal during treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365348</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PG3">
  <data key="d0">PG3</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">PG3 is one of four phosphogypsum samples analyzed using X-ray Diffraction to compare mineral phases.&lt;SEP&gt;PG3 is a sample represented by a blue line, displaying a peak pattern similar to PG2.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365348</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PG4">
  <data key="d0">PG4</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">PG4 is one of four phosphogypsum samples analyzed using X-ray Diffraction to compare mineral phases.&lt;SEP&gt;PG4 is a sample represented by a green line, characterized by a broad amorphous hump and distinct mineral peaks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038</data>
