<node id="Scanning Electron Microscope Micrograph">
  <data key="d0">Scanning Electron Microscope Micrograph</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A high-magnification grayscale image revealing the dense, complex microstructure of a cementitious composite or phosphogypsum reaction product.&lt;SEP&gt;A high-magnification, black-and-white image used to visualize the surface topography and morphology of the phosphogypsum material.&lt;SEP&gt;A high-magnification grayscale image revealing the surface morphology of a material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516319</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plate-like Crystals">
  <data key="d0">Plate-like Crystals</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Plate-like crystals are the dominant feature in the micrograph, appearing as flat, irregular, overlapping scales with smooth surfaces and sharp edges.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365348</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Needle-like Structures">
  <data key="d0">Needle-like Structures</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Needle-like structures are long, thin, parallel clusters resembling fibers, characteristic of hydration products like ettringite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365348</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The matrix is a rough, granular background in the micrograph, suggesting a solidified paste or binding agent.&lt;SEP&gt;Matrix is the continuous phase of PGCM that water penetrates along micro-cracks during the immersion stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Matrix is the structural base of PGCM containing free water and undergoing hydration reactions.&lt;SEP&gt;The matrix is the internal structure of PGCM where hydration, erosion, and ion balance processes occur.&lt;SEP&gt;The material structure within which air voids and aggregate particles are embedded.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516319</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="28d">
  <data key="d0">28d</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">28d is an annotation indicating a curing age of 28 days in the context of materials science and cement.&lt;SEP&gt;Represents a curing age of 28 days in scientific contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-040</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365348</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="5µm">
  <data key="d0">5µm</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">5µm is a scale bar annotation representing a length of 5 micrometers in the micrograph.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365348</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bhadauria">
  <data key="d0">Bhadauria</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Bhadauria is a researcher who observed varying improvements and declines in mortar compressive strength at different PG concentrations and curing ages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365515</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mun">
  <data key="d0">Mun</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Mun is a researcher who demonstrated that mortar compressive strength increases with PG concentration up to 12% before deteriorating.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365515</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hou">
  <data key="d0">Hou</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Hou is a researcher who reported that early PG addition enhances AFt development and that PG and fly ash have a beneficial synergistic impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Hou synthesized calcium sulfate whiskers from washed phosphogypsum and compared their purity to unwashed samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791772487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CeSeH Gels">
  <data key="d0">CeSeH Gels</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">CeSeH Gels are non-crystalline geopolymer system products anticipated to form from cement hydration in the presence of PG.&lt;SEP&gt;CeSeH Gels are cementitious gel formations in the mortar microstructure noted for their compact composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365515</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AFm">
  <data key="d0">AFm</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">AFm is a hydration compound produced when PG concentration reaches 5%, replacing other diffraction peaks.&lt;SEP&gt;AFm is a mineral phase produced in the mortar mixture, with its quantity dependent on the available sulphate content.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365515</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AFt">
  <data key="d0">AFt</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">AFt is a hydration product whose development is enhanced by early PG addition.&lt;SEP&gt;AFt is a hydration product in PGCM that increases with prolonged curing age.&lt;SEP&gt;AFt is a cementitious gel that interlaces with C-S-H to form a dense network structure beneficial for immobilization.&lt;SEP&gt;AFt is a hydration product whose precipitation is promoted by the incorporation of gamma alumina.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791722536</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moist Curing">
  <data key="d0">Moist Curing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Moist curing is a curing condition under which mortar compressive strength decreases between 13 and 71% with PG addition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C2AH8">
  <data key="d0">C2AH8</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">C2AH8 is a hydration compound that predominated in PG-free samples but disappeared at 5% PG concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AH3">
  <data key="d0">AH3</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">AH3 is a hydration compound present in PG-free samples and emphasized in diffraction diagrams due to PG.&lt;SEP&gt;AH3 is a chemical compound present in the mortar mixture that surrounds columnar ettringite when Plaster Of Paris reaches 20%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CAH10">
  <data key="d0">CAH10</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">CAH10 is a hydration compound that predominated in PG-free samples but disappeared at 5% PG concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PG-free Samples">
  <data key="d0">PG-free Samples</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">PG-free samples refer to mortar mixtures without PG addition, where certain hydration compounds predominated.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365693</data>
