</node>
<node id="Purification Process">
  <data key="d0">Purification Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The purification process removes contaminants from washed phosphogypsum to produce pure phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment method whose effectiveness can be evaluated through microstructural analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791470547</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kandil et al.">
  <data key="d0">Kandil et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Kandil et al. conducted research purifying phosphogypsum with sulfuric acid to produce an insoluble residue.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lutke et al.">
  <data key="d0">Lutke et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Lutke et al. reported that switching to sulfuric acid, increasing concentration and temperature, and decreasing the solid/liquid ratio improved leaching efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Garg et al.">
  <data key="d0">Garg et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Garg et al. used calcined phosphogypsum at 150 to 160 degrees Celsius to attempt contaminant removal.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Taher">
  <data key="d0">Taher</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Taher calcined Egyptian phosphogypsum at various temperatures and determined 600 degrees Celsius to be optimal.&lt;SEP&gt;Taher is a researcher who explored phosphogypsum decomposition reacting with various forms of carbon in 2007.&lt;SEP&gt;Researcher cited regarding the microstructure of calcium sulfate dihydrate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791674254</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leskeviciene and Nizeviciene">
  <data key="d0">Leskeviciene and Nizeviciene</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Leskeviciene and Nizeviciene calcined phosphogypsum at 800 and 900 degrees Celsius for 30 minutes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Removal Rate">
  <data key="d0">Removal Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Removal rate measures the percentage of contaminants like phosphorus pentoxide and fluoride eliminated during purification.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Specific Gravity">
  <data key="d0">Specific Gravity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Specific gravity is a physical property of phosphogypsum that increases as calcination temperature increases.&lt;SEP&gt;A soil property measured at 2.44, representing the ratio of the density of soil solids to the density of water.&lt;SEP&gt;A density metric for phosphogypsum, measured at 2.516, falling within the standard range of 2.3-2.6.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical property representing the ratio of a material's density to a reference density, measured for both samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791938112</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Organophosphorus">
  <data key="d0">Organophosphorus</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Organophosphorus is a trace contaminant eliminated through volatilization during the calcination process.&lt;SEP&gt;Organophosphorus concentration in solution after adsorption indicates the stability of 20HPA-HAP.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791656147</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="L Neutralization">
  <data key="d0">L Neutralization</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">L neutralization is a process combined with calcination to avoid secondary contamination and fluoride volatilization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791366062</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Solid/Liquid Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Solid/Liquid Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The solid/liquid ratio is a parameter that, when decreased, improves leaching efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791366062</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Egyptian PG">
  <data key="d0">Egyptian PG</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Egyptian PG is a specific regional variant of phosphogypsum studied for calcination at various temperatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791366062</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insoluble Residue">
  <data key="d0">Insoluble Residue</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Insoluble residue is the product formed when phosphogypsum is purified with sulfuric acid.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791366062</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acid Circulation System">
  <data key="d0">Acid Circulation System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An acid circulation system is required when using nitric acid for leaching due to its operational characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791366062</data>
