  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="B-type Carbonate Substitution">
  <data key="d0">B-type Carbonate Substitution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">B-type carbonate substitution is a structural phenomenon where carbonate ions replace phosphate ions in the apatite lattice.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791486667</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium Phosphorus Molar Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Phosphorus Molar Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium phosphorus molar ratio is a quantitative measure (approximately 1.7) indicating the stoichiometry and carbonate substitution in the sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791486667</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Atmospheric Carbon Dioxide">
  <data key="d0">Atmospheric Carbon Dioxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Atmospheric carbon dioxide is a gas that reacts with the synthesis solution to introduce carbonate ions into the hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Atmospheric Carbon Dioxide is a gas entrapped during preparation that facilitates carbonate substitution in the Hydroxyapatite lattice.&lt;SEP&gt;Atmospheric Carbon Dioxide is a gas entrapped during preparation that causes carbonate substitution in the HAp lattice.&lt;SEP&gt;Atmospheric carbon dioxide is a gas adsorbed onto the sample, causing carbonate substitution in the hydroxyapatite structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide captured from the atmosphere during preparation, leading to carbonate substitution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791965185</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Human Bone">
  <data key="d0">Human Bone</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Human bone is a biological tissue whose structural similarity to carbonate-containing hydroxyapatite explains its enhanced bioactivity.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Bone serves as a biological reference for the biocompatibility of carbonate-substituted Hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Bone is a biological reference material containing 4–6 wt% carbonates, used to indicate the biocompatibility of prepared HAp.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791627879</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mesopores">
  <data key="d0">Mesopores</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Mesopores are nanoscale pores distributed on the surfaces of the rod-shaped hydroxyapatite particles.&lt;SEP&gt;Mesopores are pore structures present in the analyzed hydroxyapatite samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791749076</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lattice Fringes">
  <data key="d0">Lattice Fringes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lattice fringes are well-resolved structural patterns observed in high-magnification TEM images, confirming high crystallinity.&lt;SEP&gt;Structural details visible in Panel (b) and Panel (e) representing atomic arrangements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791986136</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
