<node id="Anhydrite III">
  <data key="d0">Anhydrite III</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Anhydrite III, also recognized as gamma-anhydrite, is a hexagonal crystalline phase of anhydrous calcium sulfate. It represents a fully dehydrated chemical compound that forms through the thermal processing and dehydration of gypsum, semihydrate, and phosphogypsum. The emergence of this specific calcium sulfate phase occurs within a defined thermal window, with documented formation temperatures ranging from approximately 66°C to 280°C, frequently cited around 133°C to 140°C depending on the precursor material and exact heating protocols. Structurally, it constitutes a distinct anhydrous state that develops after the initial dehydration stages of its hydrated precursors.

Upon continued thermal exposure, Anhydrite III remains stable until it reaches 428°C, at which point it undergoes a phase transformation into Anhydrite II. This temperature-dependent behavior underscores its role as an intermediate crystalline phase in the systematic dehydration sequence of calcium sulfate hydrates. As a product of controlled thermal treatment, Anhydrite III is characterized by its complete water loss and its specific hexagonal lattice structure, which persists until the specified transformation threshold is met.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791586442</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anhydrite II">
  <data key="d0">Anhydrite II</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Anhydrite II is an insoluble calcium sulfate phase resulting from the transition of soluble anhydrite III at 428 degrees Celsius.&lt;SEP&gt;Anhydrite II is an orthorhombic crystalline form of anhydrite formed from anhydrite III and transforming into anhydrite at 1184°C.&lt;SEP&gt;An orthorhombic crystalline phase of anhydrite that forms from the transformation of anhydrite III.&lt;SEP&gt;A resulting phase of calcium sulfate, also known as beta-anhydrite, formed from the transformation of Anhydrite III.&lt;SEP&gt;Anhydrite II is a hexagonal crystalline form resulting from the crystallographic transformation of anhydrite III between 320°C and 450°C.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791586251</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anhydrite I">
  <data key="d0">Anhydrite I</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Anhydrite I is a calcium sulfate phase formed from the transition of anhydrite II at 1184 degrees Celsius.&lt;SEP&gt;A high-temperature crystalline phase of anhydrite that forms from the transformation of anhydrite II.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791498049</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
