<node id="Synthesis Time">
  <data key="d0">Synthesis Time</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Synthesis Time is a critical, controlled experimental and reaction parameter that dictates the duration of material fabrication processes, encompassing both conventional heating and microwave-assisted techniques. As a fundamental variable in crystallization and nanoparticle synthesis, it directly governs the kinetics of crystal growth, phase composition development, and carbonate absorption. The specific duration required for Synthesis Time varies considerably depending on the chosen methodology, typically spanning from 55 to 1200 seconds in microwave heating applications, and extending from several minutes up to 15 hours in other synthesis protocols.

Adjusting this parameter significantly alters the physicochemical characteristics of the resulting materials. Variations in Synthesis Time have been shown to directly influence particle size, the calcium-to-phosphorus (Ca/P) ratio, material density, and specific surface area. Notably, when Synthesis Time is increased in conjunction with elevated temperatures, it consistently reduces the specific surface area of nanopowders. Consequently, precise regulation of Synthesis Time is essential for tailoring the final structural, compositional, and functional properties of synthesized crystalline and nanomaterials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791854849</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
