<node id="Stack Disposal">
  <data key="d0">Stack Disposal</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Stack Disposal is a phosphogypsum waste management method that functions as the most common and primary approach for handling this industrial byproduct, currently accounting for approximately 85% of phosphogypsum storage worldwide. The technique involves placing untreated phosphogypsum into designated stacks of varying heights, which are governed by specific height parameters and require extended land occupation times. Although frequently categorized as an alternative management strategy, it remains the most widely practiced option due to its significant economic advantages, making it the least expensive method available. In practice, this approach often manifests as uncontrolled landfilling.

Despite its economic efficiency and widespread adoption, Stack Disposal is ranked fourth in environmental footprint assessments and is consistently identified as the least preferred management method. This low preference is directly attributed to the substantial environmental damage caused by the prolonged, untreated storage of large phosphogypsum volumes. Consequently, the method represents a clear dichotomy between practical ubiquity and ecological unsustainability, highlighting the need for more sustainable alternatives despite its current dominance in waste management practices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791876904</data>
