  <data key="d5">1791719139</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GB/T 39489–2020">
  <data key="d0">GB/T 39489–2020</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">GB/T 39489–2020 is a national standard that defines the strength requirements for cementitious composite materials.&lt;SEP&gt;GB/T 39489–2020 is a standard that defines the strength requirements met by the optimal phosphogypsum content in cementitious composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719139</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Performance">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Performance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanical performance refers to the strength and durability of cementitious materials, which is influenced by porosity, hydration products, and impurity levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanical performance refers to the required engineering properties, specifically (E, Rt), achieved by the phosphogypsum-based mixes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791937342</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CCM">
  <data key="d0">CCM</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">CCM refers to cementitious composite materials produced by mixing PG with pozzolanic blending materials and activators.&lt;SEP&gt;CCM refers to cemented composite materials developed using phosphogypsum and other binders to improve mechanical performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719139</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Refining Slag">
  <data key="d0">Refining Slag</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Refining slag is a raw material used in early-strength CCM formulations alongside slag and phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719139</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GB 8978–1996">
  <data key="d0">GB 8978–1996</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">GB 8978–1996 is a regulatory standard specifying that phosphate concentration in Class I wastewater must not exceed 0.5 mg/L.&lt;SEP&gt;GB 8978–1996 is a regulatory standard that sets the maximum allowable fluoride concentration at 10 mg/L in Class I wastewater.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GB 11893–89">
  <data key="d0">GB 11893–89</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">GB 11893–89 is a technical standard outlining the analytical method for determining various phosphate species in water.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPGFM">
  <data key="d0">CPGFM</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">CPGFM (Cemented Paste Filling Material) is a specialized cementitious composite designed primarily for industrial and mining applications, particularly goaf backfill and open-pit restoration. It is formulated as a slurry that incorporates phosphogypsum (PG) and high-phosphorus gypsum (HPG) as primary aggregates, combined with quicklime, cement, slag, alkaline activators, phosphorus tailings, water, and various admixtures. As a calcium phosphogypsum filling composite, its composition is engineered to optimize mechanical properties and effectively utilize solid waste materials.

The material's mechanical strength and flow characteristics are highly sensitive to the precise proportions of its components, as well as to environmental conditions and specific processing treatments. Beyond its structural utility, CPGFM serves a critical environmental function by immobilizing harmful substances, notably phosphorus and fluorine. Leaching tests demonstrate that the composite significantly reduces phosphorus concentration in water, making it a sustainable construction material that balances enhanced performance with environmental safety for widespread application in Chinese mining operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CDW">
  <data key="d0">CDW</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">CDW refers to construction demolition waste, which was analyzed alongside PG for its phosphorus leaching behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;CDW is a material whose leachate fluoride content was tracked alongside PG and CPGFM in Fig. 13.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GB 5086.1">
  <data key="d0">GB 5086.1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">GB 5086.1 is a standard detailing leaching test procedures for identifying waste characteristics for storage and disposal facilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HJ/T 299-2007">
  <data key="d0">HJ/T 299-2007</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">HJ/T 299-2007 is a standard specifying leaching test conditions to simulate the release of hazardous components from solid wastes.&lt;SEP&gt;HJ/T 299-2007 is a Chinese standard detailing the sulphuric acid and nitric acid method for extracting leaching toxicity from solid waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leaching Test">
  <data key="d0">Leaching Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Leaching test is a standardized procedure used to measure the release of major and trace elements from materials under varying pH conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="F-">
  <data key="d0">F-</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">F- represents the soluble fluoride ion whose concentration in leachate is affected by pH, dissolution, and adsorption processes.&lt;SEP&gt;F- represents the fluoride ion, which is quantified using specific volumetric and pH-controlled procedures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792069208</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C-S-H Matrix">
  <data key="d0">C-S-H Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The C-S-H matrix is a component in the CPGFM model with an assumed Ca/Si ratio of 1.5, used to simulate dissolution reactions.&lt;SEP&gt;The C-S-H matrix provides binding sites that adsorb soluble fluorine, reducing its concentration in leachate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yellow Phosphorus Slag-Based Binder">
  <data key="d0">Yellow Phosphorus Slag-Based Binder</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A composite material composed of yellow phosphorus slag, fly ash, cement clinker, and lime used as a structural binder.&lt;SEP&gt;The addition of this binder to PG causes stabilized fluorine to be re-released into the filling slurry as pH rises.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPGFM Backfill">
  <data key="d0">CPGFM Backfill</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">CPGFM backfill is a composite material engineered to immobilize phosphorus and fluorine, minimizing their environmental leaching.&lt;SEP&gt;CPGFM backfill is a cemented composite material where heavy metal ions and fluorine are immobilized through physical and chemical processes.&lt;SEP&gt;CPGFM backfill is a material used for immobilizing toxic and harmful elements through gel interlacing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Copper Ion">
  <data key="d0">Copper Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Copper ions are toxic heavy metal ions that can replace calcium ions in the C-S-H matrix through ion exchange.&lt;SEP&gt;Copper ion (Cu²⁺) is an ion that may replace calcium ions in C-S-H.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chromium Ion">
  <data key="d0">Chromium Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Chromium ions are toxic heavy metal ions that can replace calcium ions in the C-S-H matrix through ion exchange.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromium ion (Cr³⁺) is an ion that may replace silicon ions in C-S-H.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Physical Adsorption">
  <data key="d0">Physical Adsorption</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical adsorption relies on molecular attraction forces, such as Van der Waals forces, to immobilize toxic metal ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical adsorption is a mechanism for pollutant stabilization that is weaker than chemical combination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chemical Combination">
  <data key="d0">Chemical Combination</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Chemical combination involves the formation of stable chemical bonds between harmful ions and hydration products to immobilize contaminants.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical combination is a mechanism for pollutant stabilization that has a stronger solidification effect than physical adsorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SPLP Tests">
  <data key="d0">SPLP Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">SPLP tests are standardized procedures used to measure leaching concentrations of heavy metal ions in the filling body.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Secondary Release">
  <data key="d0">Secondary Release</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Secondary release refers to the risk of previously immobilized fluorine being re-released into the slurry due to pH changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chronic Poisoning">
  <data key="d0">Chronic Poisoning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Chronic poisoning is a severe health consequence for humans if heavy metal elements from PG enter the food chain.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Van der Waals Force">
  <data key="d0">Van der Waals Force</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Van der Waals force is the molecular attraction responsible for the physical adsorption of toxic ions in the backfill.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Xu et al.">
  <data key="d0">Xu et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Xu et al. highlighted the health risks of heavy metals from PG entering the human food chain.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers who investigated the effects of ball milling time on phosphogypsum particle size and hardened body strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791773516</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chen and Wu">
  <data key="d0">Chen and Wu</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Chen and Wu described how toxic and harmful elements enter hydration products through ion exchange.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719874</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fig. 15">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 15</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 15 depicts the mechanisms of physical adsorption and chemical combination for immobilizing toxic metal ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 15 is a figure providing a schematic diagram of toxic metal ion immobilization by CPGFM backfill.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719874</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Al-F Complex">
  <data key="d0">Al-F Complex</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Al-F Complex is a chemical form that fluorine mainly exists in when pH is less than 5.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719874</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Food Chain">
  <data key="d0">Food Chain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Food Chain is the pathway through which heavy metal elements from PG can enter the human body and cause poisoning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kidney Failure">
  <data key="d0">Kidney Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Kidney Failure is a severe health consequence for humans resulting from chronic heavy metal poisoning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphorus Immobilization">
  <data key="d0">Phosphorus Immobilization</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorus Immobilization is a process noted as relatively easy compared to fluorine immobilization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Closed Dynamic Leaching">
  <data key="d0">Closed Dynamic Leaching</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Closed Dynamic Leaching is a testing condition under which equilibrium fluoride concentrations were measured.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Closed Static Leaching">
  <data key="d0">Closed Static Leaching</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Closed Static Leaching is a testing condition under which equilibrium fluoride concentrations were measured.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cementing Material">
  <data key="d0">Cementing Material</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Cementing Material provides the cementation that allows heavy metal elements to exist in different chemical forms within the filling body.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Filling Body">
  <data key="d0">Filling Body</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Filling Body is the cured cementitious structure that contains heavy metal elements and is exposed to groundwater.&lt;SEP&gt;Filling Body is the prepared material structure made from CPGFM and other solid wastes for mine filling applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Water Body">
  <data key="d0">Water Body</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Water Body is the receiving environment where heavy metal contaminants risk migrating from the filling area.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Curing Ages">
  <data key="d0">Curing Ages</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Curing Ages refer to the different time periods of the filling body that affect heavy metal leaching concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leachates">
  <data key="d0">Leachates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Leachates are the liquid solutions extracted from the filling body that contain measured concentrations of heavy metal ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Leachates are</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792039718</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Toxic Metal Ions">
  <data key="d0">Toxic Metal Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Toxic Metal Ions are heavy metal contaminants immobilized in CPGFM backfill through physical adsorption and chemical combination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Harmful Ions">
  <data key="d0">Harmful Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Harmful Ions are toxic elements that form stable chemical bonds with hydration products during immobilization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Toxic Ions">
  <data key="d0">Toxic Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Toxic Ions react with calcium and hydroxide ions to form precipitates like Cadmium Hydroxide.</data>
