  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">PGI slag is the molten byproduct whose composition, viscosity, and homogeneity are analyzed to optimize desulfurization performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095416</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="XPS Characterization">
  <data key="d0">XPS Characterization</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">XPS characterization is a method used to confirm the sulfur content and chemical forms present in the slag.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095416</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbothermal Smelting Reduction">
  <data key="d0">Carbothermal Smelting Reduction</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbothermal smelting reduction is the high-temperature process used to reduce phosphogypsum and recycle sulfur.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095416</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Slag Homogeneity">
  <data key="d0">Slag Homogeneity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Slag homogeneity is a prerequisite for good fiber-forming performance and is negatively impacted by excessive sulfide formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095578</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eutectic Mixtures">
  <data key="d0">Eutectic Mixtures</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Molten phases formed at high temperatures that encapsulate solid materials and impede the reaction kinetics.&lt;SEP&gt;Eutectic mixtures are formed by adding silicon dioxide, aluminum oxide, and magnesium oxide to lower the melting point of the PGI system.&lt;SEP&gt;Lighter layer surrounding the sintered slag core during the reaction stages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="slag wool">
  <data key="d0">slag wool</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">slag wool is a fiber-forming product that requires homogeneous slag for optimal production.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C/S Molar Ratio">
  <data key="d0">C/S Molar Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The C/S Molar Ratio denotes two distinct compositional parameters depending on the specific industrial or chemical context. In the majority of applications, particularly during carbothermal reduction and phosphogypsum processing, the C/S Molar Ratio represents the molar proportion of carbon to sulfur within the reaction mixture or raw material blend. This parameter typically ranges from 0 to 2.0 and is commonly evaluated at discrete set points of 0.5, 1.0, and 2.0, with 0.5 specifically designated for formulations such as PGI-1.4. As a critical process variable, it fundamentally governs desulfurization efficiency, sulfur escape, and the overall desulfurization rate. Additionally, it directly dictates the phase structure, homogeneity, and sulfur content within the resulting slag, controlling the relative distribution of sulfides and sulfates, the formation of sulfide residues, the specific content and chemical form of sulfur in the slag, and the generation of characteristic reaction peaks and products during slag formation and reaction process studies.

In a separate metallurgical application, the C/S Molar Ratio refers specifically to the designed carbon-to-silicon molar ratio within the raw material mixture utilized for smelting operations. While sharing the identical abbreviation, this variation targets the carbon-silicon balance rather than the carbon-sulfur balance, serving as a foundational compositional input for smelting processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Desulfurization Rate">
  <data key="d0">Desulfurization Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Desulfurization Rate is a quantitative performance metric and key indicator used to evaluate the efficiency of sulfur removal from a reaction system, particularly during the thermal processing of phosphogypsum. Calculated to represent both the sulfur distribution ratio and overall removal efficiency, this metric tracks the percentage of sulfur eliminated over time, typically plateauing near 99 percent. It is primarily measured and assessed during reduction smelting experiments, including carbothermal reduction smelting, to optimize industrial processes and evaluate slag performance.

The effectiveness of the desulfurization rate is fundamentally constrained by several operational and chemical factors, including the formation of liquid phases, the addition of specific ingredients, and the residual sulfide content remaining in the resulting slag. By integrating these parameters, the metric serves as a critical tool for monitoring reaction dynamics and refining metallurgical operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="amorphous-structured slag">
  <data key="d0">amorphous-structured slag</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">amorphous-structured slag is the proposed structural outcome of the PGI desulfurization mechanism.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="disulfide">
  <data key="d0">disulfide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">disulfide is a compound formed when divalent iron binds with sulfide ions during the reaction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="polysulfides">
  <data key="d0">polysulfides</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">polysulfides are compounds formed alongside iron sulfide when residual sulfides combine with divalent iron.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="iron oxide">
  <data key="d0">iron oxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">iron oxide is a compound that undergoes reduction to form divalent iron during the high-temperature process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="residual sulfides">
  <data key="d0">residual sulfides</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">residual sulfides are unreacted sulfide compounds in the slag that combine with divalent iron to form iron sulfide and polysulfides.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="S^2-">
  <data key="d0">S^2-</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">S^2- represents sulfide ions that bind with divalent iron to form disulfide compounds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fe^2+">
  <data key="d0">Fe^2+</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fe^2+ is an iron species bonded to sulfide, identified by a binding energy peak at 707.76 eV.&lt;SEP&gt;Fe^2+ represents divalent iron produced by the carbon reduction of iron trioxide.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FT">
  <data key="d0">FT</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">FT is a characteristic melting temperature point representing the flow temperature of the system.&lt;SEP&gt;FT refers to the fusion temperature, serving as a baseline for determining the required carbothermal smelting reduction temperature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="uneven material mixing">
  <data key="d0">uneven material mixing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">uneven material mixing is a practical limitation that prevents the actual reaction from achieving an ideal state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ideal state">
  <data key="d0">ideal state</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">ideal state represents the theoretical optimal condition for the reaction, which is unattainable in practice due to mixing limitations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792095693</data>
