<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Gypsum">
  <data key="d7">41.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Gypsum and Phosphogypsum centers on their shared chemical composition, industrial origins, and distinct processing pathways. Phosphogypsum is an industrial byproduct and waste material generated primarily during phosphoric acid manufacturing, with gypsum produced in this specific process often referred to directly as phosphogypsum. Chemically, both materials consist of the same fundamental calcium sulfate compound, predominantly in dihydrate form, alongside minor hemihydrate and anhydrite variants. Gypsum constitutes the vast majority, approximately 90 to 95 percent, of phosphogypsum’s mineralogical makeup, serving as its dominant and primary crystalline phase across multiple treatment conditions. Within the phosphogypsum matrix, gypsum forms the primary building blocks, characterized by elongated, prismatic, and needle-shaped crystals that agglomerate into a lattice structure. This structure accommodates trace elements and serves as the primary host for weakly bound substances, including rare earth elements.

Despite sharing an identical chemical foundation, the two materials are distinguished by purity and industrial application. Gypsum typically denotes refined or naturally extracted material, whereas phosphogypsum represents the recycled or waste-derived form. Nevertheless, the gypsum matrix within phosphogypsum is frequently recovered and transported to recycling facilities. Through specialized industrial treatments, such as radionuclide extraction and rare earth element recovery processes, phosphogypsum undergoes recrystallization, purification, and recovery to synthesize pure gypsum, which may occasionally emerge as a secondary byproduct.

In practical applications, both materials exhibit versatile utility across manufacturing sectors. Phosphogypsum is commonly utilized in soil amendments, construction, and building materials, functioning as a setting regulator and a versatile replacement for natural gypsum. Conversely, pure gypsum is highly valued as a sole calcium oxide source in cement production, where it can substitute for or complement phosphogypsum. Due to their chemical similarity, gypsum also serves as a critical reference and benchmark for sulfur salvage studies, agricultural effectiveness assessments, and micrograph analyses when evaluated against phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Novel processes are developed to recover pure gypsum from raw phosphogypsum.</data>
