<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Calcination">
  <data key="d7">19.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Calcination and Phosphogypsum describes a thermal treatment process applied to raw phosphogypsum to modify its physical, chemical, and mineralogical properties. This processing method induces significant dehydration, alters the crystal structure, and drives phase changes that primarily transform the material into anhydrite and calcium sulphate hemihydrate. The thermal conversion is extensively analyzed to understand the resulting dehydration patterns and impurity transformations, which are fundamental to optimizing the treatment.

As a critical pretreatment and waste management strategy, calcination effectively purifies phosphogypsum and activates it for enhancing fly ash-lime systems, while also preparing the material for gypsum plaster production. The process reduces potentially toxic elements, eliminates volatile contaminants, and removes specific impurities such as fluoride, organic phosphates, and eutectic phosphorus, simultaneously converting soluble phosphate into stable forms. By transforming hazardous components into stable compounds, calcination significantly enhances the material's suitability for industrial reuse, particularly in construction applications like brick manufacturing, and supports broader recycling initiatives. Despite its demonstrated success as a laboratory-scale purification technology, the process currently lacks large-scale industrial scalability.</data>
  <data key="d9">activation method,application,chemical alteration,contaminant removal,dehydration,impurity reduction,impurity removal,material treatment,modifies,performance enhancement,phase transformation,pretreatment,process application,processing,processing method,processing step,purification,purification technology,scalability challenge,structural modification,system enhancement,thermal process,thermal processing,thermal treatment,treatment,waste modification</data>
