<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="pH">
  <data key="d7">10.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The (Phosphogypsum, pH) relation characterizes pH as a fundamental chemical property and parameter of phosphogypsum. pH is recognized as the primary factor governing the chemical behavior and reactivity of the material, making it a key metric for characterizing its acidity. Consequently, pH is routinely measured in phosphogypsum samples to assess its chemical profile and ensure compliance with established guidelines.

Beyond its intrinsic properties, the pH of phosphogypsum significantly influences its environmental and agricultural interactions. The dumping or application of phosphogypsum introduces highly acidic conditions to surrounding ecosystems, notably reducing the pH of both seawater and soil. In agricultural contexts, introducing phosphogypsum to alkaline, saline, and saline-sodic soils leads to substantial pH reductions, thereby altering soil chemistry and potentially affecting land management practices.

Given its pronounced effects on acidity and environmental systems, the pH of phosphogypsum is subject to regulatory oversight. Established standards explicitly define acceptable pH ranges for phosphogypsum, ensuring that its handling, application, and disposal remain within parameters that mitigate adverse ecological and agricultural impacts while maintaining material quality.&lt;SEP&gt;Raw phosphogypsum has a measured pH of 4.17, which varies based on depth and leaching.&lt;SEP&gt;The phosphogypsum sample exhibits this measure of acidity, ranging from 2 to 3.</data>
  <data key="d9">acidity,acidity increase,causes decrease,chemical property,chemical_property,exhibits,explanatory factor,measures property of,parameter,property,quality parameter,reduces</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-76e07d75a9f5124d07b9dafa2dacfba7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1791938545</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Salt Marshes">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Salt marsh sediment creates a barrier effect that rapidly decreases the activity concentration of radionuclides originating from phosphogypsum stacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Salt marsh sediment creates a barrier effect that rapidly decreases the activity concentration of radionuclides originating from phosphogypsum stacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Salt marsh sediment creates a barrier effect that rapidly decreases the activity concentration of radionuclides originating from phosphogypsum stacks.</data>
