<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Tunisia">
  <data key="d7">17.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphogypsum in Tunisia represents a major industrial byproduct and a significant contributor to global production, accounting for approximately 6.24% of worldwide phosphogypsum production or reserves. The country generates roughly 10.0 million metric tons of this material annually, a substantial volume that presents ongoing management challenges and establishes Tunisia as a primary geographic source for scientific research. Consequently, Tunisian phosphogypsum is frequently cited as the origin for samples analyzed in academic literature, where researchers extensively characterize its chemical composition and identify associated impurities. Beyond compositional studies, the material has been characterized and valorized for industrial applications, notably in the production of clay bricks.

The environmental footprint of phosphogypsum storage and disposal in Tunisia has been a focal point of ecological investigation. Scientific assessments have specifically evaluated the impact of phosphogypsum piles on local soil bacterial communities and wild plant species, as well as the effects of phosphogypsum leachate on regional groundwater systems. Together, these factors underscore Tunisia’s dual role as a leading global producer of phosphogypsum and a critical reference location for understanding its chemical properties, environmental consequences, and potential for material reuse.&lt;SEP&gt;Tunisia stockpiles phosphogypsum and partly uses it in cement manufacture.</data>
  <data key="d9">cement manufacture,chemical analysis,environmental impact,environmental impact study region,environmental study,geographic context,geographic origin,geographic source,global distribution,groundwater assessment,high volume,management challenges,material characterization,origin,production,production location,production share,production volume,regional comparison,regional source,sample origin,sample source,source location,stockpiling,waste storage</data>
