<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Phosphoric Acid">
  <data key="d7">34.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Phosphogypsum and Phosphoric Acid is fundamentally defined by an industrial production pathway in which the wet manufacturing process of Phosphoric Acid yields Phosphogypsum as a major chemical and solid byproduct. This generation occurs at a substantial ratio, producing approximately five tonnes of Phosphogypsum for every single tonne of Phosphoric Acid manufactured. Chemically, Phosphogypsum is intrinsically linked to this production cycle as it inherently retains residual Phosphoric Acid as a primary impurity. This residual acid contributes to the material's acidic nature and exists as a water-soluble component that is typically eliminated during standard treatment procedures.

Beyond its role as a manufacturing waste product, Phosphogypsum participates in a cyclical industrial dynamic with Phosphoric Acid. It serves as a viable source for Phosphoric Acid recovery through targeted chemical processing, where phosphate impurities within the Phosphogypsum can react with aqueous citric acid to regenerate Phosphoric Acid. Conversely, in certain processing stages, Phosphoric Acid is deliberately added to Phosphogypsum slurry to facilitate specific chemical reactions. While the primary industrial consensus and production data establish Phosphogypsum as the byproduct of Phosphoric Acid manufacturing, some contextual or recovery-focused descriptions identify Phosphoric Acid as a byproduct or recovered output from Phosphogypsum processing. Together, these interactions highlight a bidirectional relationship where Phosphoric Acid production continuously generates Phosphogypsum, while the resulting byproduct contains recoverable Phosphoric Acid that can be extracted or manipulated under controlled industrial conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is generated as a by-product during the production of phosphoric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is generated as a by-product of phosphoric acid manufacturing.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains impurities of phosphoric acid and its salts.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is generated as a waste byproduct during the industrial production of phosphoric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphoric Acid was removed from Phosphogypsum samples through rinsing with pure water.</data>
