<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="China">
  <data key="d7">37.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between China and phosphogypsum is defined by the nation's position as the world's largest annual producer of this industrial byproduct, generating approximately 70 to 80 million metric tons annually, which accounts for roughly 19.93% of global production and reserves. This massive output is highly concentrated in the Yangtze River Basin and is driven by the rapid expansion of China's phosphorus chemical sector. Chemically, Chinese phosphogypsum features specific ranges of sulfur trioxide and calcium oxide, alongside generally low radioactivity and heavy metal concentrations that typically remain below national safety standards.

Despite its substantial production, China faces significant environmental and logistical challenges due to the massive accumulation of this solid waste, which currently relies heavily on stockpiling and low-value product development. While specific storage conditions are implemented, a notable gap remains in achieving zero-emission goals. To address this, Chinese authorities have established formal policy targets, aiming to elevate the comprehensive utilization rate from its current level of approximately 50% (recorded at 55.6% in 2023) to 75% by 2025. This transition underscores the critical intersection of industrial growth, environmental management, and regulatory strategy.

Consequently, China serves as the primary geographic and policy focus for extensive scientific research and industrial analysis concerning phosphogypsum. Domestic efforts and international studies extensively cover generation, disposal, stockpile management, pollutant retention, and resource reuse, with Chinese entities actively supplying typical samples for detailed chemical and impurity analysis. This ongoing research and regulatory push highlight China's central role in advancing sustainable handling and large-scale recycling practices for this widespread industrial byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;China experiences massive annual discharge of phosphogypsum, driving the need for cyclic utilization research.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum was obtained from Yunnan Province, China.</data>
