<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Hydroxyapatite">
  <data key="d7">19.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Hydroxyapatite and Phosphogypsum establishes a chemical synthesis and resource recovery pathway where phosphogypsum, a calcium- and phosphorus-rich industrial byproduct of the phosphate industry, functions as the primary feedstock and foundational raw material for producing hydroxyapatite. Recognized as a sustainable secondary resource, phosphogypsum is systematically repurposed to synthesize nanocrystalline hydroxyapatite, providing a cost-effective alternative to conventional mineral sources. The transformation typically relies on hydrothermal synthesis or general chemical processing to extract essential elemental components and yield solid hydroxyapatite nanoparticles.

Beyond conventional synthesis routes, hydroxyapatite has also been identified as a distinct phase within phosphogypsum samples subjected to alkaline conditions, indicating that treatment at higher pH levels can similarly facilitate its formation or precipitation. Collectively, this relation highlights a targeted environmental and industrial strategy that valorizes phosphate industry waste into a valuable nanomaterial. By converting an abundant industrial byproduct into a targeted solid compound, the process underscores a sustainable chemical conversion framework that achieves both ecological remediation and efficient material production.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum serves as the primary raw material for synthesizing hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum serves as the primary reactant that is chemically converted into hydroxyapatite during the experimental process.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum waste is reacted with phosphoric acid and ammonia to synthesize nano-hydroxyapatite.</data>
