<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Phosphorus">
  <data key="d7">20.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Phosphogypsum and Phosphorus encompasses significant industrial, chemical, and environmental dimensions. Phosphogypsum is an industrial byproduct characterized by highly enriched phosphorus levels, which serve as a definitive identifier for the material. Within this context, phosphorus functions as both a primary component and a significant impurity, creating a complex duality across different applications. Industrially, phosphorus is frequently classified as a harmful, acidic, and toxic impurity that compromises the material's filler strength and reduces overall product integrity. Consequently, soluble forms of phosphorus are actively targeted for removal or efficient precipitation during treatment and recycling processes to ensure safe handling, proper solidification, and high-quality end products.

Environmentally, the leaching behavior of phosphorus from phosphogypsum is a major focus of scientific study. When the material is stockpiled or improperly disposed of, precipitation events can facilitate the leaching and export of soluble phosphorus into adjacent waterways, leading to ecological concerns such as eutrophication and alterations to authigenic phosphorus content at disposal sites. Conversely, from an agricultural and resource recovery perspective, phosphogypsum serves as a valuable direct source of phosphorus for soils, with high application rates significantly boosting available soil phosphorus. Furthermore, the material functions as a key industrial source for recovering phosphorus, a critical mineral that underpins global supply chains. Ultimately, the Phosphogypsum-Phosphorus relation highlights the material's dual nature as both a hazardous waste requiring careful environmental management and a valuable resource with substantial agricultural and economic potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorus is a major chemical species analyzed in the phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorus concentration in phosphogypsum decreased by 43% following the leaching process.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorus is present in scattered spots within the phosphogypsum matrix.</data>
