<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Fluorine">
  <data key="d7">25.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Fluorine and Phosphogypsum characterizes fluorine as a naturally occurring, toxic, and hazardous impurity derived from phosphate ore that constitutes a significant minor constituent of phosphogypsum. Its concentration varies depending on production parameters, particularly citric acid levels utilized during processing, with reported values ranging from approximately 0.79% to 1.39% by weight. Within the material, fluorine primarily exists as fluoride forms, manifests as a distinct mineral phase, and exhibits a very strong, sharp ring structure in specific particles. It functions as a volatile chemical species that transfers during chemical processing and can volatilize as hydrogen fluoride gas, while also persisting in soluble forms.

As a major environmental contaminant, fluorine poses significant ecological and public health risks. It can migrate into surrounding ecosystems through soil and water pollution, atmospheric deposition, and agricultural inputs, potentially leading to groundwater contamination and fluorosis. Beyond environmental concerns, fluorine acts as an acidic impurity that adversely affects the physical properties of phosphogypsum. Specifically, it compromises filler strength and reduces the overall structural integrity of the gypsum, thereby hindering its safe and effective reuse in construction materials.

To mitigate these harmful effects and enable industrial application, comprehensive treatment protocols are essential. Volatile fluorine compounds are typically eliminated during calcination, while soluble forms are targeted for removal during purification stages. Residual fluorine is managed through solidification techniques to prevent environmental migration. These combined measures underscore the necessity of rigorous control strategies to safely manage fluorine levels in phosphogypsum and ensure its responsible utilization.</data>
