<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Heavy Metals">
  <data key="d7">29.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Heavy Metals and Phosphogypsum characterizes heavy metals as inherent, toxic, and harmful impurities within phosphogypsum, typically occurring in trace amounts alongside radioactive elements. These contaminants significantly alter the chemical properties of phosphogypsum, notably causing its solubility to differ from that of natural gypsum. Due to their high solubility and environmental availability, heavy metals readily leach, diffuse, and permeate into surrounding ecosystems when phosphogypsum is discharged, stored for extended periods, or undergoes decomposition. This migration facilitates the accumulation of contaminants in adjacent soils, their subsequent entry into nearby water bodies, and their uptake into agricultural products, thereby posing substantial threats to ecological systems and human health.

Consequently, the resource utilization and industrial applications of phosphogypsum must strictly adhere to environmental standards governing heavy metal content. To mitigate these risks, targeted management protocols are essential, employing stabilization, passivation, and immobilization treatments to effectively trap contaminants and prevent their further migration into soil and water systems. Furthermore, continuous environmental monitoring is strongly recommended to track contaminant transformation and movement, ensuring long-term agricultural safety and broader ecological preservation.&lt;SEP&gt;Heavy metals are typically found in very low concentrations within phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is associated with heavy metals that can leach into the environment and cause pollution.</data>
