<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Uranium">
  <data key="d7">21.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between phosphogypsum and uranium describes the presence of uranium as a naturally occurring trace radioactive impurity within the phosphogypsum matrix. During phosphate rock processing, approximately 86 percent of the uranium transfers to the phosphoric acid product, while the remaining portion is retained in the phosphogypsum byproduct. Scientific studies have systematically determined uranium concentrations in phosphogypsum samples, with specific measurements recording trace levels such as 14 ppm, and have further documented its distribution across various grain fractions.

Because of its inherent radioactivity, uranium in phosphogypsum poses potential radiation hazards that require continuous monitoring and strict management. Monitoring efforts primarily target phosphogypsum waste piles to evaluate exposure risks for nearby workers and to ensure environmental compliance, while systematic analysis tracks both total concentration and binding percentages. Beyond its hazardous characteristics, uranium is also recognized as a valuable associated resource. Consequently, recovery protocols are implemented to extract this element from phosphogypsum, a process that simultaneously prevents long-term environmental pollution and maximizes the material's overall utility.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum retains a portion of Uranium radioisotopes, primarily U-238.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains trace amounts of uranium that may be released during environmental exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Uranium is released from phosphogypsum at a concentration of 17.29 µg/L.&lt;SEP&gt;Uranium is present in phosphogypsum and preferentially leaches during experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains uranium with concentrations ranging from 9.3 to 27.4 ppm.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum releases uranium alongside rare earth elements and cadmium during leaching processes.</data>
