<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Magnesium Oxide">
  <data key="d7">9.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Magnesium Oxide and Phosphogypsum describes the presence of magnesium oxide within the chemical composition of phosphogypsum. Available descriptions present conflicting perspectives regarding its abundance. Several sources characterize magnesium oxide as a major or primary chemical component, indicating it forms a significant part of the material's main composition. Conversely, other descriptions classify it as a trace impurity or minor constituent, with one specific reference quantifying its concentration at exactly 0.02 wt%. 

Despite these discrepancies in reported abundance, the descriptions consistently note that magnesium oxide remains stable within the phosphogypsum matrix. It is present to a lesser extent in certain contexts and exhibits no significant changes even after treatment processes. Consequently, the relationship underscores magnesium oxide as an integrated constituent of phosphogypsum, though its exact proportion varies across sources, ranging from a major component to a trace impurity depending on the specific sample or analytical context.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains magnesium oxide as part of its initial mixture.</data>
