<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Potassium Oxide">
  <data key="d7">8.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphogypsum and potassium oxide are connected by a chemical relationship that describes the presence of potassium oxide within phosphogypsum. Available descriptions present varying perspectives regarding its abundance. Some sources characterize potassium oxide as a major or primary chemical component of phosphogypsum. Conversely, other descriptions quantify it as a minor or trace constituent, with specific measurements indicating concentrations of 0.01 wt% and 0.08 wt%. 

Despite these discrepancies in reported abundance, it is consistently noted that potassium oxide is an inherent component of phosphogypsum. Furthermore, the available data indicates that the presence of potassium oxide in phosphogypsum remains stable, showing no significant changes following treatment processes. The variation in characterization likely reflects differences in source material, analytical methods, or specific industrial processing conditions, though the exact concentration remains subject to contextual variation.</data>
