<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Florida">
  <data key="d7">13.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Florida and phosphogypsum encompasses significant industrial production, large-scale storage, and extensive scientific research. Florida operates as a major global producer of phosphogypsum, generating over 30 million tons annually. Consequently, the state functions as a primary storage location for contaminated phosphogypsum, which is accumulated in massive stacks and ponds. Geographically, Florida is consistently identified in scientific literature as a key source for phosphogypsum samples. These materials are routinely collected from the region to serve as reference points for various analytical studies.

Scientific investigations utilizing Florida-sourced phosphogypsum focus on both material characterization and practical applications. Samples are systematically analyzed and compared to determine their chemical composition, identify specific impurities, and document physical properties such as monoclinic crystal structures. Beyond laboratory analysis, the phosphogypsum from this region is actively studied for industrial processing, particularly for rare earth element recovery and gypsum upgrading techniques. Additionally, the state's extensive phosphogypsum storage infrastructure serves as a critical site for environmental research. Researchers extensively study these stacks to understand environmental radiochemistry, with a specific emphasis on tracking nuclide migration and assessing the ecological behavior of the accumulated material.&lt;SEP&gt;Florida is a region studied for nuclide migration and the environmental radiochemistry of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum from Florida contains natural radioactive elements that limit its application.</data>
  <data key="d9">application limitation,chemical analysis,environmental radiochemistry,environmental study,geographic source,high volume,location,nuclide migration,origin,production location,radioactivity,radiochemistry,regional comparison,resource recovery,sample origin,site of study,source location,storage location,study location,waste management</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-003</data>
