<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Morocco">
  <data key="d7">31.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Morocco-Phosphogypsum relation encompasses significant industrial, environmental, and scientific dimensions, centered on Morocco's status as a leading phosphate mining region and a major global producer of phosphogypsum. Driven by its extensive phosphate industry, the country generates approximately 15 million tonnes of the byproduct annually. To manage this massive output, Morocco employs extensive terrestrial containment infrastructure, including numerous stacks and ponds, while also discharging the majority of the material directly into the Atlantic Ocean, a practice facilitated by its coastal geography. Given the scale of production, alternative disposal and utilization pathways have become economically practical.

Chemically, Moroccan phosphogypsum is characterized by relatively low sulfur trioxide and calcium oxide content, but it exhibits high radioactivity due to elevated natural radionuclides, particularly radium. This radiological hazard necessitates rigorous pretreatment, prioritized radium removal technologies, and comprehensive hazard assessments for the material and related phosphate products. Fortunately, treatment processes can effectively reduce its radioactivity. Due to its widespread accumulation and distinct properties, the material has become a primary subject of scientific investigation. Samples sourced from Moroccan facilities are routinely analyzed for chemical composition, impurities, and mean weight percentages of various oxides, often serving as comparative benchmarks in geographic and environmental research. Studies have extensively examined its thermal behavior and the migration patterns of heavy metals and radionuclides throughout the carbonation process.

Beyond environmental monitoring and hazard management, the phosphogypsum waste generated in Morocco is actively processed for resource recovery. Industrial and scientific initiatives focus on extracting valuable compounds, including calcium hydroxide, calcium carbonate, and sodium sulfate, as well as converting the waste into advanced materials such as nano-calcium fluoride. Together, these factors underscore Morocco's central role in both the large-scale generation and the comprehensive environmental, chemical, and industrial evaluation of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;The studied phosphogypsum samples were collected from a phosphate treatment plant in Morocco.</data>
