<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Cadmium">
  <data key="d7">22.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Cadmium and Phosphogypsum characterizes cadmium as a prominent heavy metal impurity and trace chemical constituent within phosphogypsum, a byproduct of phosphoric acid production that contains over sixty other elements. Scientific analyses consistently verify the presence of cadmium in this material, with measured concentrations typically ranging from 3.50 to 7.40 ppm and occasionally reported around 6 ± 1 ppm. However, analytical outcomes exhibit significant variability; some studies report non-detection due to differences in sampling locations, matrix composition, or detection thresholds, while others note that concentrations in certain samples exceed probable effect thresholds. This variability directly influences cadmium release, which fluctuates significantly between samples based on initial concentrations, temperature conditions, and high liquid/solid fractionation during leaching processes.

As a toxic heavy metal, cadmium poses substantial environmental and agricultural risks when associated with phosphogypsum. Its persistent presence as a hazardous contaminant facilitates accumulation in reclaimed marsh soils following long-term application as a soil amendment, and it readily enters the food chain through crop uptake, prompting ongoing food safety research. Furthermore, phosphogypsum can carry cadmium and other pollutants that are capable of volatilizing into the atmosphere during steam emission processes. Consequently, the association between cadmium and phosphogypsum is defined by cadmium’s consistent detection as a toxic impurity, its variable but notable concentrations, and its multifaceted potential to drive soil-crop pollution cycles and atmospheric contamination.</data>
