<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Soil">
  <data key="d7">13.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Phosphogypsum and Soil encompasses both regulated agricultural applications and significant environmental impacts. When intentionally applied to soil for agricultural purposes, phosphogypsum is mixed into the ground under strictly regulated permissible content limits that vary by land type. In this controlled context, it can improve soil conditions by mechanically breaking apart fine structural elements, thereby enhancing physical properties.

However, unregulated dumping or long-term accumulation of phosphogypsum leads to substantial ecological and chemical disruptions. The presence of phosphogypsum alters soil acidity, reduces microbial activity, depletes organic matter, and compromises structural integrity. Furthermore, its accumulation disrupts the natural balance of water, air, and heat within the soil matrix. Toxic emissions and contaminated runoff associated with phosphogypsum exacerbate these issues, directly causing soil acidification and widespread degradation. Additionally, the application or accumulation of this material introduces radioactive elements, specifically increasing the concentration of Radium-226 in the affected soil. Together, these factors illustrate a dual-nature relationship where controlled use offers mechanical benefits, while unmanaged accumulation poses severe chemical, physical, and radiological risks to soil health.&lt;SEP&gt;Surface storage of phosphogypsum disrupts soil functioning and causes serious environmental contamination.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is applied to soil to treat acidity, improve structure, and enhance nutrient availability.&lt;SEP&gt;Inadequate storage and disposal of phosphogypsum lead to significant Soil contamination due to high toxicity levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil and phosphogypsum are mixed in a dry state for 3 minutes before water addition.&lt;SEP&gt;Impurities from phosphogypsum can seep into soil, causing environmental pollution.</data>
  <data key="d9">acidification,agricultural treatment,alters properties,application,contamination,contamination source,degrades quality,environmental impact,landscape alteration,material combination,mechanical division,mixing process,pollution,regulation,remediation,soil degradation,soil improvement,structural disruption</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-004</data>
