<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Fluoride">
  <data key="d7">21.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Fluoride and Phosphogypsum is defined by fluoride's status as a primary, soluble impurity and contaminant within the material. Phosphogypsum consistently contains elevated concentrations of fluoride, which poses significant environmental and health risks. During processing and storage, fluoride can be released into the surrounding environment as gas or dust. When water interacts with phosphogypsum, it frequently generates leachate with fluoride levels that exceed established drinking water safety standards, although some studies note variable or low leaching rates depending on specific environmental conditions. Once introduced into ecosystems, fluoride readily adsorbs into topsoil and is absorbed by vegetation. Classified as a primary pollutant responsible for causing fluorosis, this contamination risk severely restricts the material's potential for agricultural application, making fluoride management a critical factor in phosphogypsum handling and disposal.

From an industrial and analytical perspective, fluoride acts as a soluble impurity that negatively affects processing by weakening the compressive strength of gypsum and necessitates specific elimination protocols to meet quality standards. Due to its toxicity and pervasive presence, rigorous monitoring is essential. Analytical methods are routinely employed to determine fluoride content in phosphogypsum samples, including those sourced from regions such as Egypt, highlighting the ongoing scientific and regulatory focus on quantifying and mitigating this hazardous component.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride is a characteristic pollutant contained in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride is a primary pollutant contained within phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains fluoride as a primary characteristic pollutant, existing in multiple chemical forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains fluorides that easily migrate in groundwater and soil.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride is a key pollutant component in phosphogypsum requiring strict control to prevent environmental contamination.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride is a trace element found in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d9">analysis,characteristic pollutant,chemical analysis,chemical content,composition,contains,contaminant,contamination,contamination risk,determination,environmental concern,impurity,leachate composition,pollutant composition,presence,primary pollutant,toxicity,trace element source,waste composition</data>
