<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Phosphates">
  <data key="d7">8.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphates and phosphogypsum are linked by the presence of phosphates as significant chemical impurities within the phosphogypsum matrix. Phosphogypsum contains phosphates as residual, undesirable, and potentially toxic contaminants that are classified among its primary harmful chemical impurities. These phosphate compounds do not merely reside on the surface; they actively adhere to gypsum crystals and substitute directly within the crystal lattice structure. This integration makes them an inherent and persistent component of the solid material.

From a processing and environmental standpoint, these embedded phosphates function as mobile contaminants. During chemical or industrial treatment procedures, the phosphates readily transfer from the solid phosphogypsum into the surrounding solution. Their classification as both primary toxic elements and lattice-substituting impurities underscores their dual role as a structural feature of phosphogypsum and a notable challenge for material processing and environmental management.</data>
  <data key="d9">chemical composition,composition,contains,contains impurity,contaminant,contamination,impurity,impurity content,primary component,removal,toxicity</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-006</data>
