<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Egypt">
  <data key="d7">8.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Egypt and phosphogypsum encompasses significant industrial production, extensive scientific analysis, and notable environmental considerations. Egypt is a major producer of phosphogypsum, contributing substantially to global output with an annual production volume of 5.4 million tonnes. Given this substantial output, phosphogypsum samples originating from Egypt have been the focus of detailed chemical investigations. These studies analyze the mean weight percentages of various chemical oxides, assess overall chemical composition, and identify specific impurities. Researchers have also conducted comparative analyses of these samples across different geographic locations within Egypt to establish comprehensive compositional baselines.

In addition to chemical profiling, the material has been extensively studied regarding contamination and radiological properties. Egyptian phosphogypsum is characterized by elevated radionuclide levels, which necessitate specific pretreatment protocols prior to safe handling or utilization. Furthermore, Egypt serves as a critical geographic source for evaluating the broader radiological impact associated with both phosphogypsum and the phosphate fertilizers derived from it. Collectively, these factors establish Egypt as both a key production hub and a focal region for comprehensive environmental and chemical research on phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d9">chemical analysis,geographic origin,geographic source,high radionuclides,production,production volume,radiological study,regional comparison,sample origin,study location</data>
