<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Spain">
  <data key="d7">11.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphogypsum in Spain is defined by the country's substantial role in global production and the distinct geochemical profile of the material generated domestically. Spain stands as a major worldwide producer of phosphogypsum, accounting for approximately 10.84% of global production or reserves. The nation generates roughly 2.5 million metric tons of the byproduct annually, necessitating the management of large quantities through extensive industrial stockpiles.

The phosphogypsum produced in Spain is characterized by elevated radionuclide concentrations, prompting extensive scientific scrutiny. Samples sourced from this region have been rigorously analyzed to determine their mean weight percentages of various chemical oxides, overall chemical composition, and impurity levels. Furthermore, environmental case studies focusing on Spanish phosphogypsum stockpile sites have demonstrated that geogenic weathering serves as the dominant mechanism governing the distribution of trace pollutants within these deposits.&lt;SEP&gt;Spain exhibits significantly higher Radium-226 radioactivity in its phosphogypsum compared to other nations.</data>
  <data key="d9">case study,chemical analysis,geographic origin,global distribution,high volume,higher radioactivity,major producer,production,production location,production share,production volume,radionuclide content,radionuclide presence,regional comparison,sample origin,spatial distribution</data>
