<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Secondary Pollution">
  <data key="d7">5.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Recycling phosphogypsum presents a risk of secondary pollution due to impurity leaching.&lt;SEP&gt;Certain valorization approaches for phosphogypsum carry the risk of causing secondary pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Reusing phosphogypsum poses potential risks of secondary pollution that require assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Secondary pollution is a challenge that hinders the large-scale, high-value-added resource utilization of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Secondary pollution is a key challenge that limits the large-scale utilization of phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d9">environmental challenge,environmental risk,limitation,reuse impact,risk,utilization challenge</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1791774155</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Leaching">
  <data key="d7">10.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Leaching applied to Phosphogypsum is a hydrometallurgical process primarily utilized to extract and recover various chemical species, with a significant focus on rare earth elements and radionuclides such as radium isotopes. This technique typically employs a range of mineral or organic acids to facilitate the dissolution and separation of target materials from the phosphogypsum matrix. As a key method in resource recovery, leaching enables the efficient extraction of valuable components while simultaneously addressing the removal of radioactive impurities.

Beyond its extractive applications, leaching serves as a critical processing step that influences the material's internal composition. During this phase, crystal structure substitutions originally present in the phosphate ore are transferred into the phosphogypsum. Furthermore, after resource products are generated through leaching, residual impurities within the phosphogypsum may continue to leach out over time. Collectively, these processes highlight the dual role of leaching in both valorizing phosphogypsum as a source of critical minerals and managing its chemical and radiological characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;The phosphogypsum matrix must be destroyed to enable Rare Earth Element recovery during leaching.&lt;SEP&gt;Leaching evaluates the release of elements from phosphogypsum, demonstrating high fractionation for certain metals.</data>
  <data key="d9">element release,element transfer,evaluation,experimental application,experimental condition,extraction process,extraction target,matrix destruction,process,process application,processing step,purification method,radium removal,risk mechanism,source material,treatment</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1792042812</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
