<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Radium">
  <data key="d7">14.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Phosphogypsum and Radium describes how phosphogypsum inherently concentrates and retains radium, a naturally occurring radioactive element and isotope. During production, phosphogypsum captures approximately 80% of the naturally occurring radium from the processing stream. Once incorporated, scientific studies estimate that roughly 89% of this radium remains bound within the material's crystal structure. The distribution of the element is size-dependent, with radium being preferentially retained in the smaller grain size fractions of the phosphogypsum.

Because radium is a radioactive component, its presence in phosphogypsum poses notable environmental risks and serves as a key factor in leaching dynamics and environmental impact assessments. Managing this radioactive content requires specific removal processes alongside continuous environmental monitoring. Consequently, the radioactive isotopes of radium within phosphogypsum are frequently characterized in environmental studies to evaluate their behavior and develop effective mitigation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Radium co-crystallizes with phosphogypsum and approximately 80% migrates into it during phosphate decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum may contain radium as a radioactive element impurity.&lt;SEP&gt;Radium concentration is measured in phosphogypsum to assess its radiological properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains radium, with approximately 80% of naturally occurring radium from phosphate rock ending up in it.&lt;SEP&gt;Radium is predominantly transferred into phosphogypsum during the production process.&lt;SEP&gt;Radium is removed during production and ends up in phosphogypsum waste.</data>
  <data key="d9">co-crystallization,composition,containment,contains,distribution,hazard,impurity,leachability,migration,preferential retention,radioactive concentration,radioactive content,radioactive element,radiological analysis,radionuclide content,radionuclide transfer,retention</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
