<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Natural Gypsum">
  <data key="d7">12.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Natural gypsum and phosphogypsum are fundamentally linked through a relationship defined by industrial substitution, comparative material analysis, and resource utilization. Phosphogypsum exhibits physical properties similar to natural gypsum, which enables it to function as a cost-effective and renewable substitute across multiple sectors. It is widely utilized as a replacement for natural gypsum in the production of plaster, wallboard, and cement, and it effectively serves as a cement retarder. Despite these functional parallels, natural gypsum consistently serves as the standard baseline, highlighting that phosphogypsum possesses distinct physical and chemical qualities primarily due to the presence of impurities, such as heavy metals.

These compositional differences result in natural gypsum demonstrating a higher curing effect compared to phosphogypsum. Consequently, products derived from phosphogypsum are currently considered inferior in quality to those manufactured from natural gypsum. To better understand and account for these impurity-driven variations, the two materials are frequently combined in solubility studies. Nevertheless, phosphogypsum remains a primary industrial substitute for natural gypsum, and it is recognized as a fully viable replacement following appropriate purification processes that aim to mitigate current quality limitations and enhance its overall performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum and Natural Gypsum are compared in the scientific paper's data table regarding their chemical composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is used as a substitute for natural gypsum in the preparation of calcium sulfate whiskers.</data>
  <data key="d9">chemical comparison,comparative baseline,construction materials,curing effect,industrial substitution,market competition,material comparison,material replacement,performance comparison,physical similarity,quality comparison,quality difference,raw material,replacement,resource substitution,solubility study,substitute,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1791774648</data>
