<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Stack Disposal">
  <data key="d7">8.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphogypsum and Stack Disposal represent a widely utilized yet environmentally problematic waste management practice. Stack disposal is currently the most common and frequently cited alternative method for handling phosphogypsum waste. The process fundamentally involves the storage and physical containment of the waste material within designated, purpose-built stacks. Despite its routine application across the industry, this approach is consistently ranked as the least preferred management strategy due to its inherent ecological drawbacks.

Environmental considerations heavily dictate the assessment of this method, as approximately 85% of all phosphogypsum is disposed of in stacks without any prior treatment. This untreated deposition causes significant environmental damage, with the severity of ecological impacts being heavily influenced by the physical height of the stacks. Consequently, while stack disposal remains the dominant standard for phosphogypsum waste management, its widespread use is directly counterbalanced by its recognized environmental hazards and its status as the industry's least favored disposal option.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative method,application,disposal,disposal method,environmental damage,environmental performance,management,primary disposal,ranking,waste handling,waste management</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1791881210</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
