<edge source="Calcium Sulfate" target="Calcium Sulfide">
  <data key="d7">9.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Calcium Sulfate and Calcium Sulfide characterizes a chemical interconversion primarily driven by thermal reduction and redox mechanisms. During thermal reduction processes, calcium sulfate decomposes and is reduced by various agents, including carbon, iron compounds, or general reducing atmospheres, to produce calcium sulfide. In this primary pathway, calcium sulfide consistently functions as a transient intermediate compound or phase. The generation of calcium sulfide is directly tied to the presence of these reducing conditions, establishing it as a key transitional species in the system.

As the reduction proceeds, the newly formed calcium sulfide participates in a secondary redox reaction with any remaining, undecomposed calcium sulfate. This interaction between the two compounds continues until the calcium sulfate is entirely exhausted. The relationship is not strictly unidirectional; under oxidizing conditions or through specific chemical interactions, calcium sulfate can be regenerated when calcium sulfide undergoes oxidation or reacts with existing calcium sulfate. Consequently, the dynamic between these two substances is defined by a reversible reduction-oxidation cycle where calcium sulfide acts as both a reduction intermediate and a reactant in secondary transformations.</data>
  <data key="d9">chemical interaction,chemical reaction,decomposition pathway,decomposition product,intermediate formation,oxidation product,produces,product,reacts with,redox reaction,reduction reactant,reduction reaction,reduction target,solid-solid reaction,stoichiometry</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1792099180</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Calcium Sulfate" target="Calcium Oxide">
  <data key="d7">6.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Calcium sulfate decomposes at high temperatures to produce calcium oxide.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate decomposes to form calcium oxide during the reaction with coke.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate decomposes into calcium oxide and sulfur dioxide at high temperatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate decomposes into calcium oxide at approximately 1250 degrees Celsius.&lt;SEP&gt;The secondary reaction produces calcium oxide.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate undergoes further decomposition to yield calcium oxide.</data>
  <data key="d9">chemical product,decomposition,intermediate product,produces,product,production,reaction output,thermal decomposition</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-023</data>
