<edge source="Rare Earth Elements" target="Yttrium">
  <data key="d7">5.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Yttrium is one of the predominant rare earth elements found in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Yttrium is one of the chemical elements classified under Rare Earth Elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Yttrium follows the lanthanide series in the table's ordering of rare earth elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Yttrium shares similar chemistry with rare earth elements and is often discussed alongside them.&lt;SEP&gt;Rare earth elements include yttrium as part of their definition.</data>
  <data key="d9">atomic number,chemical similarity,classification,composition,comprises,definition,elemental classification,includes,sequence</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1791814610</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Rare Earth Elements" target="Lanthanum">
  <data key="d7">12.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Lanthanum is a chemical element formally classified as a Rare Earth Element, specifically recognized as a light rare earth element. It occupies a foundational position in chemical classification, as it initiates the sequence of rare earth elements when ordered by increasing atomic number. In environmental and industrial matrices, particularly within phosphogypsum and dried phosphogypsum, lanthanum is identified as a predominant and primary component that dominates the overall rare earth element composition. It is routinely analyzed in phosphogypsum samples and exists as a primary light rare earth element ion in leach liquor and nitrate leach solutions.

Due to its abundance and chemical properties, lanthanum is consistently targeted in scientific studies and processing operations. It is specifically selected for selective extraction from nitrate leach solutions, as well as for broader extraction, stripping, and recovery processes. This focus establishes lanthanum as a key rare earth element of interest, bridging its natural occurrence in phosphogypsum with its practical application in targeted resource recovery and chemical analysis.</data>
  <data key="d9">atomic number,classification,component,composition,comprises,constituent element,dominant component,elemental classification,elemental composition,includes,member,primary component,sequence,subset</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1791813996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Rare Earth Elements" target="Neodymium">
  <data key="d7">11.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Within the (Neodymium, Rare Earth Elements) relation, Neodymium is classified as a specific member of the rare earth element group, specifically identified as a light rare earth element among chemical elements. In resource processing and analytical contexts, Neodymium is consistently recognized as a predominant and primary component that dominates the rare earth element composition in phosphogypsum, including dried phosphogypsum samples. It is also present as a primary light rare earth element ion in leach liquor and nitrate leach solutions. Due to its abundance and value, Neodymium is routinely analyzed in phosphogypsum samples and is specifically targeted for selective extraction, recovery, and stripping processes. Consequently, within studied frameworks, Neodymium stands out as a key light rare earth element prioritized for extraction and stripping operations from both solid byproducts and liquid leach streams.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,component,composition,comprises,constituent element,dominant component,elemental classification,elemental composition,includes,member,primary component,subset</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1791814300</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Rare Earth Elements" target="Cerium">
  <data key="d7">12.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Cerium, as defined within the (Cerium, Rare Earth Elements) relation, is a chemical element formally classified as a light rare earth element. Within this classification, cerium serves as a definitive indicator, as the detection of cerium clusters typically confirms the broader presence of rare earth elements. In geological and industrial byproducts, particularly phosphogypsum and dried phosphogypsum, cerium is consistently identified as a predominant and primary component that dominates the overall rare earth element composition. It is routinely analyzed in phosphogypsum samples to quantify and characterize rare earth element distribution.

In hydrometallurgical and recovery contexts, cerium is a primary light rare earth element ion found in leach liquor and nitrate leach solutions. Due to its abundance and chemical properties, cerium is consistently targeted in various processing studies and industrial operations. These efforts focus on its selective extraction, extraction and stripping procedures, and overall recovery from solution matrices. Consequently, cerium is explicitly classified and prioritized as one of the key targeted rare earth elements in research and resource recovery initiatives, underscoring its significant role within the rare earth element category.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,component,composition,comprises,constituent element,dominant component,elemental classification,elemental composition,includes,indicator,member,primary component,subset</data>
