<edge source="Hydrothermal Synthesis" target="Hydroxyapatite">
  <data key="d7">24.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Hydrothermal Synthesis is a primary and widely employed technique utilized for the production of synthetic hydroxyapatite and its various derivatives. This method relies on the precipitation and subsequent crystal growth of the material under controlled hydrothermal conditions, typically conducted at temperatures around 200 °C. By carefully regulating the reaction environment, the process consistently yields high-quality, phase-pure nanocrystalline structures with precise and well-defined morphological forms, making it highly valued for advanced material applications.

The versatility of Hydrothermal Synthesis allows it to be tailored to produce a diverse array of hydroxyapatite architectures depending on specific synthesis parameters and additives. Documented forms include nanoparticles, nanopowders, nanorods, needlelike crystals, flaky structures, and microspheres. Furthermore, the strategic use of cationic surfactant templates enables the directed formation of nanopowders. The technique is also highly advantageous for utilizing various starting materials, demonstrating significant adaptability in material fabrication.

Notably, Hydrothermal Synthesis serves as an effective strategy for synthesizing hydroxyapatite from alternative and waste-derived raw materials. It is specifically employed to convert waste sources, such as phosphogypsum, into phase-pure nanocrystalline hydroxyapatite. This capability bridges sustainable waste valorization with high-precision crystalline production, establishing the method as a robust and adaptable approach in both academic research and industrial applications.&lt;SEP&gt;The hydrothermal synthesis method is employed to fabricate hydroxyapatite microspheres with specific structural properties.</data>
  <data key="d9">advantages,chemical synthesis,crystal growth,experimental process,fabrication,material production,method,methodology,nanomaterial production,nanopowder production,particle formation,phase transformation,preparation,produces,production,reaction environment,synthesis,synthesis method,synthesis technique</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-001</data>
